2 CONT
2 CONT ## 1. CONSTANTINO IX MONÓMACO
2 CONT ## Datos Filiatorios de la Persona
2 CONT
2 CONT * Nombre completo: Constantino Monómaco (en griego: Konstantinos Monomakhos).
2 CONT * Título o cargo principal: Emperador Bizantino (gobernó entre 1042 y 1055 como el 54.º emperador del Imperio Romano de Oriente).
2 CONT * Fecha de nacimiento: c. 1000 (estimada).
2 CONT * Lugar de nacimiento: Constantinopla (actual Estambul, Turquía).
2 CONT * Fecha de muerte: 11 de enero de 1055.
2 CONT * Lugar de muerte: Monasterio de San Jorge de Manganas, Constantinopla.
2 CONT * Motivo de su muerte: Causa natural (complicaciones graves derivadas de una artritis y gota crónica que le impidieron caminar en sus últimos años). [1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ## Matrimonios y Parejas
2 CONT ## Vínculo Matrimonial que dio origen a Anastasia:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Nombre de la cónyuge: Helena Esclerina (Helene Skleraina).
2 CONT * Título: Noble de la alta aristocracia militar de Anatolia (hija de Basilio Escleros y nieta del legendario general Bardas Escleros).
2 CONT * Fecha de casamiento: c. 1032 / 1034 (este fue el segundo matrimonio de Constantino IX, celebrado años antes de que este fuera coronado emperador).
2 CONT * Nacimiento de la cónyuge: c. 1015.
2 CONT * Muerte de la cónyuge: c. 1035 / 1036 (falleció joven, antes del ascenso de su esposo al trono).
2 CONT * Sub-lista de hijos:
2 CONT    * Anastasia Monómaca (Princesa Bizantina): n. c. 1035 – m. 1067. (Se casó en 1046 con el Gran Príncipe Vsevolod I de Kiev; madre de Vladimiro II Monómaco).
2 CONT
2 CONT ## Tercer Matrimonio (Matrimonio de Estado Imperial):
2 CONT
2 CONT * Nombre de la cónyuge: Zoe Porfirogéneta. Emperatriz reinante de Bizancio (dinastía Macedónica). Casaron en 1042 únicamente para legitimar el acceso de Constantino al trono imperial. Ella tenía 64 años, por lo que no tuvieron hij
2 CONC os.
2 CONT
2 CONT ## Progenitores
2 CONT
2 CONT * Padre: Teodosio Monómaco (Theodosios Monomachos). Título: Magistrado Supremo y alto funcionario judicial en los tribunales de Constantinopla. n. c. 970 – m. c. 1020-1030.
2 CONT * Madre: María Tornikaina. Título: Noble dama perteneciente a la dinastía de los Tornikios/Taronitas, príncipes soberanos de origen armenio incorporados a la corte bizantina. n. c. 975 – m. s. XI.
2 CONT
2 CONT ## Actos o Hechos Principales y Contexto
2 CONT
2 CONT * Exilio y regreso triunfal: Durante su juventud, la influencia de su familia se derrumbó cuando su padre Teodosio cayó bajo la sospecha de conspirar contra el emperador Basilio II. Constantino fue desterrado a la isla de Lesbos. 
2 CONC Sin embargo, su atractivo físico e inteligencia fascinaron a la anciana emperatriz Zoe Porfirogéneta. En 1042, Zoe lo llamó del exilio, se casó con él y lo coronó como coemperador.
2 CONT * El Cisma de Oriente (1054): Durante su reinado ocurrió uno de los hechos más trascendentales de la historia universal: la ruptura definitiva e irreversible entre la Iglesia Católica de Roma y la Iglesia Ortodoxa de Constantinopl
2 CONC a (el Gran Cisma de 1054). Aunque Constantino intentó mediar políticamente para evitar la división, no pudo frenar la excomunión mutua entre los legados papales y el patriarca Miguel Cerulario.
2 CONT * El Tratado de Paz con Kiev: En 1043, la Rus de Kiev lanzó un masivo ataque naval contra Constantinopla. Tras repeler la invasión, Constantino demostró una enorme visión diplomática y en 1046 entregó a su hija Anastasia en matrim
2 CONC onio al príncipe Vsevolod I de Kiev, sellando una paz duradera que integró definitivamente la cultura bizantina en las estepas rusas. Fue un gran mecenas de las artes, refundando la Universidad de Constantinopla.
2 CONT
2 CONT ## Posesiones y Títulos
2 CONT
2 CONT * Títulos: Emperador y Autócrata de los Romanos (Basileus kai Autokrator Romaion).
2 CONT * Posesiones: El dominio absoluto de los tesoros imperiales del Palacio de Blanquerna, las provincias de Grecia, Anatolia y los Balcanes, y el patronato del monumental complejo arquitectónico de Manganas.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantine_IX_Monomachos '''Constantine IX Monomachos''']
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine IX
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor of the Byzantine Empire
2 CONT
2 CONT A mosaic in Hagia Sophia showing Constantine IX Monomachos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Byzantine Emperor
2 CONT * Reign 	11 June 1042 - 11 January 1055
2 CONT * Coronation 	12 June 1042
2 CONT * Predecessor 	Zoe
2 CONT * Successor	Theodora
2 CONT * Consort to	
2 CONT #Helena Skleraina
2 CONT #Empress Zoe
2 CONT #Maria Skleraina
2 CONT #Guarandukht of Georgia
2 CONT * Dynasty 	Macedonian
2 CONT *Father	Theodosios Monomachos
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Constantine IX Monomachos''' (Greek: Κωνσταντίνος Θ΄ Μονομάχος, Kōnstantinos IX Monomakhos), c. 1000–January 11, 1055, reigned as Byzantine emperor from June 11, 1042 to January 11, 1055. He had been chosen by Zoe as a husband 
2 CONC and co-emperor in 1042, although he had been exiled for conspiring against her previous husband, Emperor Michael IV the Paphlagonian. They ruled together until Zoe died in 1050.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Life
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Architecture and Art
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine Monomachos was the son of Theodosios Monomachos, an important bureaucrat under Basil II and Constantine VIII. At some point Theodosios had been suspected of conspiracy and his son's career suffered accordingly. Constan
2 CONC tine's position improved after he married, as his second wife, a niece of Emperor Romanos III Argyros. Catching the eye of the Empress Zoe, Constantine was exiled to the island of Lesbos by her second husband, Michael IV. He was r
2 CONC etrieved from exile in 1042, when he was appointed judge in Greece, but before he undertook his appointment, Constantine was summoned to Constantinople as Zoe's choice for husband. The pair were married on June 11, 1042, without t
2 CONC he participation of Patriarch Alexius I of Constantinople, who refused to officiate over a third marriage (for both spouses). On the following day Constantine was formally proclaimed emperor together with Zoe and her sister Theodo
2 CONC ra.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine purged the relatives of Michael IV from the court. The new emperor was pleasure-loving and prone to violent outbursts on suspicion of conspiracy. He was heavily influenced by his mistress, Maria Skleraina, a niece of h
2 CONC is second wife, and Maria's relatives. In August 1042, under the influence of the Skleroi, the emperor relieved General George Maniakes from his command in Italy, and Maniakes rebelled, declaring himself emperor in September. He t
2 CONC ransferred his troops into the Balkans and was about to defeat Constantine's army in battle, when he was wounded and died on the field, ending the crisis in 1043.
2 CONT
2 CONT Immediately after the victory, Constantine was attacked by a fleet from Kievan Rus'; it is "incontrovertible that a Rus' detachment took part in the Maniakes rebellion".[1] They too were defeated, with the help of Greek fire. Cons
2 CONC tantine married his daughter Anna (see below) to the future Prince Vsevolod I of Kiev, the favorite son of his dangerous opponent Yaroslav I the Wise by Ingegerd Olofsdotter.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1045 Constantine annexed the Armenian kingdom of Ani, but this expansion merely exposed the empire to new enemies. In 1046 the Byzantines came into contact for the first time with the Seljuk Turks. They met in battle in Armenia
2 CONC  in 1048, and settled a truce the following year. However, Constantine foolishly disbanded the Armenian troops to save money in 1053, leaving the eastern frontier poorly defended at precisely the moment when its defences should ha
2 CONC ve been strengthened. Even if Seljuk rulers were willing to abide by the treaty, their unruly Turcoman allies showed much less restraint. Thus Constantine weakened the Byzantine forces, which in turn led to their cataclysmic defea
2 CONC t at the battle of Manzikert in 1071.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leo Tornikios attacks Constantinople, Skylitzes chronicle.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1047 Constantine was faced by the rebellion of his nephew Leo Tornikios in Adrianople. Tornikios gained support in most of Thrace and vainly attempted to take Constantinople. Forced to retreat, Tornikios failed in another siege
2 CONC , and was captured during his flight. The revolt had weakened Byzantine defenses in the Balkans and in 1048 the area was raided by the Pechenegs, who continued to plunder it for the next five years. The emperor's efforts to contai
2 CONC n the enemy through diplomacy merely exacerbated the situation, as rival Pecheneg leaders clashed on Byzantine ground, and Pecheneg settlers were allowed to live in compact settlement in the Balkans, making it difficult to suppres
2 CONC s their rebellion. Faced with such difficulties, Constantine may have sought Hungarian support.
2 CONT
2 CONT Internally, Constantine sought to secure his position by favoring the nobility (dynatoi) and granted generous tax immunities to major landowners and the church. Similarly, he seems to have taken recourse to the pronoia system, a s
2 CONC ort of Byzantine feudal contract in which tracts of land (or the tax revenue from it) were granted to particular individuals in exchange for contributing and maintaining military forces. Both expedients gradually compromised the e
2 CONC ffectiveness of the state and contributed to the development of the crisis that engulfed Byzantium in the second half of the 11th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1054 the centuries-old differences between the Greek and Roman churches led to their final separation. Legates from Pope Leo IX excommunicated the Patriarch of Constantinople Michael Keroularios when Keroularios would not agree
2 CONC  to adopt western church practises, and in return Keroularios excommunicated the legates. This sabotaged Constantine's attempts to ally with the Pope against the Normans, who had taken advantage of Maniakes' disappearance to take 
2 CONC over Southern Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine tried to intervene, but he fell ill and died on January 11 of the following year. Theodora, the elderly daughter of Constantine VIII who had ruled with her sister Zoe since 1042, was recalled from her retirement and na
2 CONC med empress.
2 CONT
2 CONT Overall, his reign was a disaster for the Byzantine empire; in particular, the military weakness for which he was largely responsible greatly contributed to the subsequent loss of Asia Minor to the Turks, and the ultimate fall of 
2 CONC Constantinople to the Muslim Ottomans in 1453.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Architecture and Art
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine IX was also a patron of the arts and literature, and during his reign the university in Constantinople expanded its juridical and philosophical programs. The literary circle at court included the philosopher and histor
2 CONC ian Michael Psellos, whose Chronographia records the history of Constantine's reign. Psellos left a physical description of Constantine in his Chronographia: he was "ruddy as the sun, but all his breast, and down to his feet... [w
2 CONC ere] colored the purest white all over, with exquisite accuracy. When he was in his prime, before his limbs lost their virility, anyone who cared to look at him closely would surely have likened his head to the sun in its glory, s
2 CONC o radiant was it, and his hair to the rays of the sun, while in the rest of his body he would have seen the purest and most translucent crystal."
2 CONT
2 CONT Immediately upon ascending to the throne in 1042, Constantine IX set about restoring the Church of the Holy Sepulchre in Jerusalem, which had been substantially destroyed in 1009 by Calif al-Hakim bi-Amr Allah. Permitted by a trea
2 CONC ty with al-Hakim's son Ali az-Zahir and Byzantine Emperor Romanus III, it was Constantine IX who finally funded the reconstruction of the Church and other Christian establishments in the Holy Land.[2] The reconstruction took place
2 CONC  during the reign of the Caliph Ma'ad al-Mustansir Billah.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine Monomachos was married three times:
2 CONT *1. to a wife of unknown identity.
2 CONT *2. to Helena Skleraina, daughter of Basil Skleros, great-granddaughter of Bardas Skleros, and niece of Emperor Romanus III.
2 CONT *3. to the Empress Zoe
2 CONT *After the death of his second wife, Constantine also took her first cousin '''''Maria Skleraina as his mistress.''''' 
2 CONT *At the time of Constantine's death in January 1055, the emperor had '''''another mistress, a certain "Alan princess", probably Irene''''', daughter of the Georgian Bagratid prince Demetrius.[33]
2 CONT
2 CONT He had no children with his first wife or with the aging Zoe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [Old information- With either Helena or Maria Sklerina he had a daughter named Anastasia, who married Vsevolod I of Kiev in 1046. Constantine's family name Monomachos ("one who fights alone") was inherited by his Kievan grandson, 
2 CONC Vladimir II Monomakh.]
2 CONT
2 CONT He also took Guarandukht, the daughter of King George I of Georgia, as a mistress.
2 CONT
