1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia This article is about the 11th-century Byzantine nobleman. For the fictional character, see Agent of Byzantium. Basil Argyros (Greek: Βασίλειος Ἀργυρός, c. 970 – after 1023) wa
2 CONC s a Byzantine nobleman of the Argyros family and a brother of the emperor Romanos III.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Synopsis Historion by John Skylitzes, Basil Argyros was the strategos of Samos who was sent to fight the Italian rebel Meles c. 1010–11.[2] It is possible that the account of Basil's career in Italy is the result 
2 CONC of Skylitzes' conflation of Argyros with another contemporary Basil, surnamed Mesardonites, who was the catapan of Italy.[3] On the other hand, he may have been a commander of the fleet sent to support Basil Mesardonites in his cr
2 CONC ackdown on the rebellion.[4] He was recalled from Italy c. 1017.[2] Modern scholars such as Guilou and Vannier consider Basil Argyros and Basil Mesardonites to have been the same person, a view not shared by Alexander Kazhdan.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT After a gap in his recorded career, Basil appears as the first Byzantine commander of Vaspurakan, an Armenian kingdom, which was surrendered by its king Senekerim-John to the emperor Basil II in 1021/22. He was removed from the of
2 CONC fice for incompetence soon after his appointment.[6] The gap in Basil's career can tentatively be filled by the information provided on a seal discovered at Preslav, Bulgaria, on which Basil is named a patrikios and strategos of T
2 CONC hrace.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Basil and his family members played a role in the Byzantine interaction with the empire's eastern neighbors. The marriage of Basil's daughter Helena to the Georgian king Bagrat IV was part of a peace deal negotiated by Bagrat's mo
2 CONC ther Queen Maria, daughter of the former king John-Senekerim of Vaspurakan, in 1032.[7] Skylitzes also speaks of the sons of Basil as archons who lived in the Anatolic Theme in the mid-11th century.[1] Basil's other daughter, whos
2 CONC e name has not survived, was married to the general Constantine Diogenes and became the mother of the future emperor Romanos IV Diogenes.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes[edit] ^ Jump up to: a b Kazhdan (1987), p. 69. ^ Jump up to: a b c Cheynet & Vannier (2003), p. 72. Jump up ^ Holmes (2005), p. 190. Jump up ^ Holmes (2005), p. 505. Jump up ^ Kazhdan (1987), p. 70. Jump up ^ Holmes (2005), 
2 CONC p. 367. Jump up ^ Skylitzes, p. 357 Jump up ^ Cheynet & Vannier (2003), p. 78. References[edit] Cheynet, J.-C.; Vannier, J.-F. (2003). "Les Argyroi" (PDF). Zbornik Radova Vizantološkog Instituta (in French). 40: 57–90. Archived fr
2 CONC om the original (PDF) on 2011-07-23. Holmes, Catherine (2005). Basil II and the Governance of Empire (976–1025). Oxford: Oxford University Press. ISBN 978-0-19-927968-5. Kazhdan, Alexander. Some Notes on the Byzantine Prosopograph
2 CONC y of the Ninth through the Twelfth Centuries. Byzantinische Forschungen 12 (1987): 63–76 Wortley, John, ed. (2010). John Skylitzes: A Synopsis of Byzantine History, 811-1057. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. ISBN 978-0-521-7
2 CONC 6705-7.
