1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/GEORGIA.htm#_Toc278647985
2 CONT
2 CONT H.      '''KINGS of OSSETIA (ALANIA)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.        ''' --- .  King of Ossetia'''.  m ---.  [Three] children:  
2 CONT
2 CONT *a)         DURGHULEL .  The Georgian Chronicle (18th century) records that "Bagrat" married "la reine Boréna, fille du roi des Osses et sœur de Dourghoulel"[306].  
2 CONT *b)         [ALDA] .  The Georgian Chronicle (13th century) records that "Georgi" died leaving "two sons Bagrat and Demetre and two daughters", a later passage stating that Demetre was born "not of the same mother" as Bagrat[307].
2 CONC   As no record has been found to indicate that King Giorgi repudiated his wife named Mariam, it is assumed that the mother of Demetre was therefore the king's concubine.  The question of her identity is less certain.  Her possible
2 CONC  name and origin are indicated by Cedrenus who records that "Alda, Georgii Abasgorum regali quondam uxor, gente Alana" submitted to Emperor Romanos Argyros and brought "Anacuphen", dated to [1033][308].  However, this must have be
2 CONC en at the same time as the alleged visit to Constantinople of Mariam, mother of King Bagrat IV and the other reported wife of King Giorgi, from which she returned with a wife for her son.  As noted in the Introduction to this docu
2 CONC ment, references in Byzantine sources to the "Alans" often refer to Georgians (see, for example, the references to the alleged "Alan" origin of Maria, wife of Emperor Mikhael VII, who is assumed to have been the daughter of King B
2 CONC agrat IV, see below).  It seems likely that there was only one visit to Constantinople around this time by one widow of King Giorgi and that the reference in Skylitzes is to Mariam's visit.  The Georgian Chronicle (18th century) n
2 CONC ames "un autre fils du roi Giorgi, né d'une seconde femme, fille du roi des Osses…encore très jeune…Démétré"[309].  It is assumed that this reference can be traced to the indirect allusion to King Giorgi's concubine which can be d
2 CONC educed from the Georgian Chronicle (13th century) quoted above, supplemented by the additional information provided by Skylitzes, so it is doubtful whether this source has any additional historical value on this point.  [Mistress 
2 CONC of: GIORGI I King of Georgia, son of BAGRAT III King of Abkhazi, of Kartveli and Kartli & his wife --- ([995/96]-[Mqinwarni or Itaroni 16 Aug] 1027, bur Kothathis).]  
2 CONT *c)         BORENA .  The Georgian Chronicle (13th century) records the marriage of Bagrat and "Borena, daughter of the Ossetian king" after the death of his first wife[310].  The Georgian Chronicle (18th century) records that "Ba
2 CONC grat" married "la reine Boréna, fille du roi des Osses et sœur de Dourghoulel"[311].  m ([1033/40]) as his first wife, BAGRAT IV King of Abkhazia and Kartvelia, son of GIORGI I King of Georgia & his first wife Alda of Ossetia ([10
2 CONC 17/18]-24 Nov 1072).  
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ossetians
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jazones, or Jász, an Ossetic people who migrated to Hungary, are first mentioned in Hungarian records in the year 1318, and their name, spelled in Greek means "Jasons" (Ιάσωνες). The Jász in Hungary maintained their language u
2 CONC ntil the 18th century. While they have become linguistically Hungarian, descendants in the Jász area of Hungary still maintain some original culture and have folk consciousness of their origins.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://hu.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osz%C3%A9tok
2 CONT
2 CONT Velük rokon törzs volt a Magyarországra a 13. században betelepült jászoké (az oroszok eredetileg jaszoknak nevezték az oszéteket, az oszét név grúz eredetű.)[1] Az oszétok legközelebbi rokonai a magyarországi jászok és a tádzsiki
2 CONC sztáni jagnobi nép. A Magyarországra a 13. században betelepült jászok az oszét nyelv egyik dialektusát beszélték a 16. századig.
