2 TITL Constantine & Zoe
1 NOTE Zoe, daughter of Constantine VIII married in 1028 to Romanus III Argyrus, and ruled as empress from 1028-1034. She conspired with Michael Paphlagonian to murder her husband, Romanus.  Then married Michael IV, and adopted his nephe
2 CONC w Michael Kalaphates in 1040.  There was a revolution in 1040, and Michael Kalaphates put her in a cloister.  He ruled for 4 months, then she was released, and regained the throne.  She then ruled as co-empress with her sister, Th
2 CONC eodora in 1042.  Then in 1042 Zoe married her 3rd husband, Constantine Monomachus, who became Emperor Constantine IX.     The three of them were then co-rulers until Zoe died in 1050.
2 CONT
2 CONT Empress Zoe of the Eastern Roman Empire was shut up in a cloister by her 2nd husband's nephew Michael V Kalaphates.  But the nobles of Constantinople rise up and release her, and she is restored to the throne.  Michael then is put
2 CONC  in a monastary.  Zoe then married Contantine Monomachus and made him emperor.
1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT En slektning of Michael Cerularius.
2 CONT apparently a descendant of the Argyros and Skleros families of the Byzantine
2 CONT Empire -- Wikipedia (8.3.2008)
1 NOTE Foreldre ukjent.
1 NOTE Co ruler. Her name means, Life.
2 CONT Speculation said that she and her lover killed her husband while he took a bath either by strangulation or drowning. She and her lover married the day after Zoe's husband's death.Constantine VIII forbade his daughter's getting mar
2 CONC ried while he was alive. Before his death he married his daughter off to Romanos III. Zoe's lover, Michael became Michael IV.
2 CONT The Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium: Oxford Univ Press 1991.
2 CONT Michael Psellus, Chronographia, New Haven: Yale University Press, 1953 Vol 1,2,3,4,5,6,7
2 CONT http://www.fordam.edu/halsall/basis/psellus-chrono-02.html
1 NOTE Zoe (in Greek: ???, Zoe, meaning "Life"), (c. 978-June 1050) was Empress of the Byzantine Empire with co-rulers November 15, 1028-1050, and senior reigning Empress from April 19 to June 11, 1042.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT Zoe was one of the few Byzantine empresses who was Porphyrogenita, or "born into the purple" (that is, as the child of a reigning emperor). She was the daughter of Constantine VIII and Helena, daughter of Alypius. Her father had b
2 CONC ecome co-emperor in 962, and sole emperor in 1025. He reigned for only three years between December 15, 1025 and November 15, 1028.
2 CONT
2 CONT Worried by the prospect of associating another man with the imperial house, Constantine VIII prevented his daughters from marrying until the very end of his life. Before dying, Constantine had married Zoe to his chosen heir Romano
2 CONC s III Argyros, the prefect (eparch) of Constantinople, on November 12, 1028. By this time, Zoe had reached 50 and had failed to produce any heir. Romanos III succeeded to the throne together with Zoe three days after they married,
2 CONC  but this failure helped alienate the couple. Romanos incurred his wife's animosity by paying little attention to her and limiting her spending, while Zoe herself became enamoured of her courtier Michael.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT As an Empress
2 CONT On April 11, 1034, Romanos III was found dead in his bath, and there was speculation that Zoe and Michael had had him strangled or drowned. Zoe married Michael later the same day, and he reigned as Michael IV until his death in 10
2 CONC 41. Although Michael proved to be a more uxorious husband than Romanos, Zoe remained excluded from politics by the monopoly on government enjoyed by Michael's brother John the Eunuch. The disgruntled empress conspired in vain agai
2 CONC nst John in 1037 or 1038.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Gold histamenon of Zoe and Theodora, 1042.Shortly before the death of Michael IV in December 1041, the couple adopted as their son Michael V, the son of Michael IV's sister. After several months of rule, the new emperor exiled his
2 CONC  adoptive mother to a convent on Principus (one of the Princes' Islands near Constantinople). The population of the city, loyal to Zoe, quickly forced him to recall the empress and her younger sister Theodora in April 1042. The si
2 CONC sters deposed Michael V, blinded him, and exiled him to a monastery, where he died later the same year. For two months, Zoe shared power with Theodora, until she could find yet another husband, her third, and the last she was perm
2 CONC itted according to the rules of the Orthodox Church. Her choice was Constantine IX Monomachos (reigned 1042-1055), who outlived her by four years. Zoe died in 1050.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zoe was fifty when she first married. Despite her age, she married twice more. Ironically, the most capable of her husbands was the one who was least well prepared to be emperor, Michael IV. It is said she was stunningly beautiful
2 CONC , and one writer even commented that, like a well baked chicken, "every part of her was firm and in good condition." She was aware of her charms and meant to keep and use them for as long as possible. With typical Byzantine ingenu
2 CONC ity, she had many rooms in her chambers converted into laboratories for the preparation of secret ointments, and she was able to keep her face free of wrinkles until she was sixty.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT (primary source) Michael Psellus, Chronographia. 
2 CONT The Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium, Oxford University Press, 1991. 
