1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://books.google.com/books?id=lQjkJOUcVIwC&pg=PA35&lpg=PA35&dq=Zubeida+Khatun,+wife+of+Malik-Shah&source=bl&ots=5h6pZ2rimG&sig=reGhqSBaBsqKwuYCVdSrdzoi_6I&hl=en&sa=X&ei=vWwyVdW8EMnksAT_mIDwBQ&ved=0CDEQ6
2 CONC AEwAw#v=onepage&q=Alp%20Arslan&f=false '''Continuity and Change in Medieval Persia'''] by Ann K.S. Lambton:
2 CONT
2 CONT Nizam al-Mulk also advised Malik-Shah to kill his aunt Gohar Khatun, the sister of Alp-Arslan. Apparently, after she quarreled with Nizam al-Mulk over some financial transaction, she set out for Azerbaijan to seek help against Niz
2 CONC am al-Mulk from the Navakiyya, who were on their way back to Anatolia. Another reason for Nizam al-Mulk’s dislike of her was that she had been opposed to the murder of Qavurt. Nizam al-Mulk urged the sultan to nip her conspiracy w
2 CONC ith the Navakiyya in the bud. Two hundred ghulams were accordingly sent after her to kill her, which they did. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not clear from Sibt b. al-Jauzi’s account exactly what was the reason for their quarrel. On p. 176 he states that Gohar Khatun had some small debt and when Alp-Arslan died Nizam al-Mulk had borrowed from her 50,000 dinars fo
2 CONC r the sultan. Whatever was the reason for her murder, it aroused ill-feeling against Nizam al-Mulk in Baghdad. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Navakiyya were a group of Turks first heard of in 462/1069-70 under Erishgi (Erisghen), to whom Gohar Khatun was married. They are mentioned in Syria and Anatolia during the reign of Alp-Arslan... ''Navaki'' also means an arch
2 CONC er, from ''navak'', a bow which a number of small arrows are shot simultaneously.
