1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ## FICHA FILIATORIA Y DOCUMENTAL: ANASTASIA MONÓMACA
2 CONT ## Datos Filiatorios de la Persona
2 CONT
2 CONT * Nombre completo: Anastasia Monómaca (en griego: Anastasia Monomache; registrada en crónicas eslavas alternativas bajo el nombre de Marija Monomaj).
2 CONT * Título o cargo principal: Princesa de la Familia Imperial Bizantina y Gran Princesa consorte de la Rus de Kiev (1054–1067).
2 CONT * Fecha de nacimiento: c. 1035 (fecha estimada a partir del segundo matrimonio de su padre y la fecha de su propio enlace en 1046).
2 CONT * Lugar de nacimiento: Constantinopla (actual Estambul, Turquía).
2 CONT * Fecha de muerte: 1067 (asentada de forma unánime en las crónicas de los monasterios de Kiev).
2 CONT * Lugar de muerte: Kiev, Rus de Kiev (actual Ucrania).
2 CONT * Motivo de su muerte: Causa natural.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------
2 CONT ## Matrimonios y Parejas
2 CONT
2 CONT * Nombre del cónyuge: Vsevolod I de Kiev (Vsevolod Yaroslávich).
2 CONT * Título: Gran Príncipe de Kiev (hijo legítimo de Yaroslav I el Sabio y de la princesa Ingegerd Olofsdotter de Suecia, cuyas fichas validamos previamente).
2 CONT * Fecha de casamiento: 1046 (matrimonio de Estado de altísima trascendencia internacional para sellar el tratado de paz definitivo entre el Imperio Bizantino y la Rus de Kiev).
2 CONT * Nacimiento del cónyuge: 1030.
2 CONT * Muerte del cónyuge: 13 de abril de 1093.
2 CONT
2 CONT ## Sub-lista de hijos del matrimonio:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Vladimiro II Monómaco (Volodymyr Monomakh): n. 1053 – m. 1125. (Gran Príncipe de Kiev, unificador y estadista eslavo que heredó con orgullo el apellido imperial de su abuelo materno; eslabón directo que conecta con la línea de G
2 CONC éza II de Hungría y la reina Violante de Aragón de su árbol).
2 CONT * Yanka Vsevolodovna (Santa Ana de Kiev): n. c. 1055 – m. 3 de noviembre de 1112. (Célebre princesa, monja y educadora que fundó escuelas para mujeres y introdujo el refinamiento de la caligrafía e industria textil bizantina en Ki
2 CONC ev; canonizada por la Iglesia Ortodoxa).
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------
2 CONT ## Progenitores
2 CONT ## Padre			
2 CONT
2 CONT * Nombre completo: Constantino IX Monómaco.
2 CONT * Título o cargo: Emperador Bizantino (Autócrata de los Romanos, reinó de 1042 a 1055).
2 CONT * Fecha de nacimiento: c. 1000.
2 CONT * Fecha de muerte: 11 de enero de 1055.
2 CONT
2 CONT ## Madre
2 CONT
2 CONT * Nombre completo: Helena Esclerina (Helene Skleraina).
2 CONT * Título o cargo: Aristócrata de la alta nobleza militar de Anatolia (segunda esposa de Constantino IX, fallecida antes de que este ascendiera al trono).
2 CONT * Fecha de nacimiento: c. 1015.
2 CONT * Fecha de muerte: c. 1035/1036.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------
2 CONT ## Actos, Hechos Principales y Contexto de su Vida
2 CONT
2 CONT * El Tratado de Paz y Alianza (1046): En 1043, la Rus de Kiev liderada por Vladímir de Nóvgorod (cuñado de Anastasia) lanzó un masivo y violento ataque naval contra los muros de Constantinopla. Tras la destrucción de la flota rusa
2 CONC  por el fuego griego, ambos estados decidieron zanjar el conflicto de forma diplomática. En 1046, Anastasia fue entregada en un fastuoso matrimonio político al príncipe Vsevolod I (el hijo predilecto de Yaroslav el Sabio). Esta un
2 CONC ión selló la sumisión espiritual y cultural de Kiev a la influencia del Imperio Bizantino.
2 CONT * Introducción de la Cultura de la Seda y el Arte: Al trasladarse desde los lujosos palacios de Constantinopla hacia las cortes de madera y piedra de Kiev, Anastasia viajó acompañada de una inmensa comitiva de sacerdotes, copistas
2 CONC , médicos y artistas bizantinos. Introdujo de forma institucional el protocolo de la corte romana, el uso de la seda imperial y sentó las bases para la posterior helenización del arte y la arquitectura eclesiástica en la Rusia med
2 CONC ieval.
2 CONT * La Semilla del Nombre Monómaco: Su mayor impacto histórico fue la crianza y educación de su hijo primogénito, Vladimiro II Monómaco. Ella se encargó de transmitirle la legitimidad dinástica de los emperadores de Constantinopla, 
2 CONC al punto de que su hijo adoptó oficialmente el apellido materno (Monómaco), gobernó bajo los códigos jurídicos bizantinos y sus descendientes utilizaron este lazo como argumento para considerar a Kiev (y posteriormente a Moscú) co
2 CONC mo la herencia legítima de la "Tercera Roma".
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------
2 CONT ## Posesiones y Títulos
2 CONT
2 CONT * Títulos: Princesa Imperial de Bizancio (Porphyrogenita por asimilación honorífica de su linaje), Gran Princesa consorte de la Rus de Kiev.
2 CONT * Bienes e Ingresos: Administró las rentas e impuestos asignados a las tierras principescas de Pereyáslavl (el feudo original de su esposo) y poseyó una de las colecciones privadas de joyería bizantina, manuscritos iluminados y re
2 CONC liquias sagradas más ricas documentadas en la Europa Oriental del siglo XI, las cuales pasaron a formar parte del tesoro real de Kiev tras su fallecimiento en 1067.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ======================OLD =====================================================
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://ru.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9C%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%85%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%8F Russian Wikipedia] google translation, retrieved January 2021
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Monomakhina''' (c. 1030/35 [3] -1067 [4] ) - a representative of the Byzantine imperial dynasty of Monomakhs, unknown personal name , married in 1046 [before 1053] to Prince Vsevolod Yaroslavich of Kiev (1030-1093), mother of V
2 CONC ladimir Monomakh .
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal name options: ''Anastasia, Maria, Irina, Theodora or Anna'' . The synodicists of the Vydubitsky monastery in Kiev call Vsevolod's wife Anastasia [5] . The version about "Mary" is based on the preserved seals. Perhaps one 
2 CONC of the names is a dying monastic. The name Monomakhini may indicate the St. Sophia Cathedral in Kiev , erected in the middle of the 11th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known exactly who her parents were. It is indicated that Emperor Constantine IX Monomakh was her relative, possibly her father, since his family was generally extremely small. Constantine was the only representative of t
2 CONC he Monomakh family on the Byzantine throne, and received the throne thanks to his third marriage - in 1042 with the already elderly Empress Zoya , whose marriage remained childless. Before that, he was married twice - the name of 
2 CONC his first wife is unknown (also childless), the second was [https://www.geni.com/people/Helena-Pulcheria-Skleraina/6000000006101358064?through=385935664970005621 Elena Sklirena], the granddaughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/B
2 CONC ardas-Skleros/6000000010974364252?through=6000000001190136654 General Barda Sklira], niece of Emperor Roman III Argir (the previous husband of Princess Zoe).
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Helena Sklirena, the emperor Constantine took her cousin Maria Sklirena (d. 1044) as his mistress and settled her in the imperial palace in parallel [8] with his marriage to Zoe. Also known is his mistress "Alan
2 CONC " princess - perhaps Irina, daughter of the Bagratid prince Dmitry (d. 1042, son of George I ).
2 CONT
2 CONT It is believed that the Russian bride was either a legal daughter from Elena Sklirena's wife, or illegitimate from https://www.geni.com/people/Maria-Skleraina/6000000017809711154?through=385935664970005621; however, there is no me
2 CONC ntion of the birth of children by https://www.geni.com/people/Constantine-IX-Monomachos-Byzantine-Emperor/385935664970005621. The origin of Theophano , her contemporary and the wife of the Emperor of the Holy Roman Empire is also 
2 CONC mysterious - before it was believed that she was the daughter of the emperor, but now it is assumed that she also belonged to the Sclera family.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage'''	
2 CONT
2 CONT To https://www.geni.com/people/Vsevolod-Yaroslavich-Grand-Prince-of-Kiev/6000000001610448055?through=6000000003858589577 between 1046 and 1053.
2 CONT
2 CONT Background 
2 CONT
2 CONT [In August 1042, the emperor relieved General George Maniakes from his command in Italy, and Maniakes rebelled, declaring himself emperor in September.[11] He transferred his troops into the Balkans and was about to defeat Constan
2 CONC tine's army in battle, when he was wounded and died on the field, ending the crisis in 1043.[12] Immediately after the victory, Constantine was attacked by a fleet from Kievan Rus';[12] it is "incontrovertible that a Rus' detachme
2 CONC nt took part in the Maniakes rebellion".[13] They too were defeated, with the help of Greek fire.[14] 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1043, the last campaign of Kievan Rus (the only one after baptism) to Constantinople took place - the Russian-Byzantine war of 1043... It was commanded by the son ofYaroslav the Wise  - Vladimir Yaroslavich... There is debate a
2 CONC bout the reasons for the campaign. During the battle, the Russian fleet, damaged by Greek fire and destroyed by the storm, was defeated and forced to retreat. Constantine IX received an indemnity. Probably, as a sign of the conclu
2 CONC sion of peace with Russia, Constantine gave the "princess" in marriage to another son of Yaroslav, Vsevolod, the son of Princess Ingegerda of Sweden... The father of Yaroslav the Wise -Vladimir Saint  - half a century earlier (c. 
2 CONC 988) married Anna, the sister of the Byzantine emperor, and the above-mentioned Empress Zoya was her niece.
2 CONT
2 CONT >A researcher of Russian-Byzantine relations writes: “The position of Byzantium is also poorly explained. If we admit that “in 1043 Byzantium was victorious and in the year of the conclusion of the peace nothing threatened it from
2 CONC  anywhere”, then what forced Konstantin Monomakh to marry his daughter to Vsevolod?  ....”
2 CONT
2 CONT At the same time, any princess from the imperial house was at that moment an extremely important person in the line of succession to the throne of Byzantium, because Constantine IX Monomakh, his wife Empress Zoya and her sister Em
2 CONC press Theodora were childless, and on them the Macedonian dynasty eventually ended, Michael VI Stratioticascended the throne . The fact that Constantine had a legitimate daughter would have allowed her to marry a suitable candidat
2 CONC e and continue the dynasty, but since this did not happen, this confirms the assumptions either about the illegitimacy of Monomakhini, or about her very distant relationship with Constantine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Information about this marriage is extremely scarce. "TheTeaching of Vladimir Monomakh "reads: “I am thin, my grandfather Yaroslav, blessed, glorious, named in baptism Vasily, the Rus name Volodymyr, my beloved father and my mothe
2 CONC r Mnomakhi [12] ..." - [13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Actually, historians know about this marriage only from a brief entry under 6567/1053 in the " Tale of Bygone Years"that " oh Vsevolod, son Volodymyr was born from the buckwheat tesarina " (Ip., 149) [14] .
2 CONT
2 CONT The young age of Monomakhini at the time of marriage is evidenced by the fact that their firstborn with Vsevolod was born only 7 years after the marriage. From the appearance of her alleged father Constantine, one can conclude abo
2 CONC ut her beauty: she, like him, could be white-skinned, with thick red hair, bright blue eyes, and graceful physique [15][ unauthorized source? ] .
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vsevolod_I_of_Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT To back up an armistice signed with the Byzantine Emperor Constantine IX Monomachos in 1046, his father married Vsevolod to a Byzantine princess, who according to tradition was named Anastasia or Maria. That the couple's son Vladi
2 CONC mir Monomakh bore the family name of the Byzantine emperor, suggests she was a member of his close family, but no contemporary evidence attests to a specific relationship and accounts of the Emperor give him no such daughter.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1067, Vsevolod's Greek wife died and he soon married a Kypchak princess, Anna Polovetskaya. She bore him another son, who drowned after the Battle of the Stugna River, and daughters, one becoming a nun and another, Eupraxia of 
2 CONC Kiev, marrying Emperor Henry IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children'''
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/people/Vladimir-Monomakh/6000000002968624707?through=6000000003858589577 Vladimir Monomakh] (1053-1125) - inherited the dynastic nickname on the maternal side. Married Gytha of Wessex, Eufemia of Constantin
2 CONC ople.
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/people/Yanka-Anna-of-Kiev/6000000002135641710?through=6000000003858589577 Yanka Vsevolodovna] Ianka or Anna Vsevolodovna (d. 3 November 1112) who was engaged to Constantine Dukas in 1074, but she never marr
2 CONC ied[20] She became a nun and started a school for girls.[21]
2 CONT
2 CONT After her death, Vsevolod married a second time to an unknown (possibly Polovtsian princess).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins and family 
2 CONT -https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BYZANTIUM.htm#MariaMonomachedied1067<br/>
2 CONT '''Possible relative of [https://www.geni.com/people/Constantine-IX-Monomachos-Byzantine-Emperor/385935664970005621 Emperor Konstantinos IX], the precise relationship (if any) is not known:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         [MARIA] [Irena] ([1030/35])-1067).  The Primary Chronicle refers to the wife of Vsevolod as "the Greek princess" but does not name her or give her origin[1694].  The primary source which states her name has not so far b
2 CONC een identified.  Apparently she and her marriage are not referred to in Greek sources.  '''No doubt her belonging to the Monomachos family has been assumed, firstly because her son is generally known as "Vladimir Monomakh", and se
2 CONC condly because Emperor Konstantinos IX Monomachos ruled in Byzantium at the date of her marriage, but no information has been found to corroborate this.  It is unlikely that she was the daughter of the Emperor Konstantinos himself
2 CONC  as he is not recorded in Greek sources as having had children by any of his wives or mistresses, although her birth date range (estimated from the birth of her son in 1053) would be consistent with her having been the daughter of
2 CONC  his second marriage.'''  In particular, Psellos mentions no children in his detailed review of the events of his reign.  The primary source which corroborates the date of her marriage has not so far been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (1046) as his first wife, VSEVOLOD Iaroslavich of Kiev, son of IAROSLAV I Vladimirovich "Mudriy/the Wise" Grand Prince of Kiev & his second wife Ingigerd Olafsdottir of Sweden (1030-13 Apr 1093, bur Kiev St Sofia).  In acc
2 CONC ordance with the terms of his father's testament, he succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Pereiaslavl.  He was appointed to succeed his brother Sviatoslav as Prince of Chernigov 1073.  He succeeded his brother in 1076 as VSEVOLOD I Gran
2 CONC d Prince of Kiev, but was deposed in 1077 by his older brother Iziaslav, restored in 1078 after Prince Iziaslav's death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT https://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00027062&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT >Maria, also known as Irene, was born about 1030, daughter of Constantine IX Monomachus, emperor of Byzantium, and NN Skleraina. In 1046 she married Vsevolod I, grand duke of Kiev, and they had two children, of whom their son Vlad
2 CONC imir would have progeny. Maria died in 1067 and her name, as Anastasia, is attested in the ancient synodic of the Caves Monastery in Kiev where she was buried.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT -https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Monomacha-1<br/>
2 CONT ''Daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Constantine-IX-Monomachos-Byzantine-Emperor/385935664970005621  Constantinos Monomachos] and [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000006101358064 Helena Skleraina]''
2 CONT
2 CONT >Annals of Vsevolod wife was called "the queen of the Greek," "monomahinya", "grekinya" [3] , but her name and origin not certain. Her parents are unknown but she has been identified with many names : Anastasia, Mary, Irene, Theod
2 CONC ora , or Anna[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.berndjosefjansen.de/monomachos/monomachos.htm#BM5077
2 CONT
2 CONT Мономахиня (ок. 1030/35[1]—1067) — неизвестная по личному имени представительница византийской императорской династии Мономахов, в 1046 году выданная замуж за киевского князя Всеволода Ярославича (1030—1093), мать Владимира Монома
2 CONC ха.
2 CONT
2 CONT Имя
2 CONT
2 CONT Варианты личного имени: Анастасия, Мария, Ирина, Феодора или Анна. Синодики Выдубицкого монастыря в Киеве называют супругу Всеволода Анастасией. Версия о «Марии» опирается на сохранившиеся печати. Возможно, одно из имён является п
2 CONC редсмертным монашеским.
2 CONT
2 CONT Происхождение
2 CONT
2 CONT Константин IX и Зоя, венчаемые Христом. Мозаика.
2 CONT Летопись называет жену Всеволода «греческая царица», «мономахиня», «грекиня»[3], но не называет её имени и происхождения. О ней и её браке не упоминается в византийских источниках. Бесспорно, к роду Мономахов она принадлежала, так
2 CONC  как это прозвание унаследовал её сын, а также поскольку именно представитель этого рода правил империей в год её брака[4].
2 CONT
2 CONT Точно неизвестно, кто был её родителями. Указывается, что император Константин IX Мономах был её родственником, возможно, отцом, т.к. его семья была вообще крайне малочисленна. Константин являлся единственным представителем рода М
2 CONC ономахов на византийском престоле, и получил трон благодаря своему третьему браку — в 1042 году с немолодой уже императрицей Зоей, брак с которой оставался бездетным. До этого он был женат дважды — имя первой его супруги неизвестн
2 CONC о (также бездетна), второй же была Елена Склирена, внучка генерала Варды Склира, племянница императора Романа III Аргира (предыдущего мужа принцессы Зои).
2 CONT
2 CONT После смерти Елены Склирены император Константин взял в любовницы её кузину Марию Склирену (ум. 1044) и поселил её в императорском дворце параллельно[5] со своим супружеством с Зоей. Также известна его любовница «аланская» принцес
2 CONC са — возможно, Ирина, дочь багратидского князя Дмитрия (ум. 1042, сын Георгия I). Предполагают, что русская невеста была либо законной дочерью от жены Елены Склирены, либо внебрачной от Марии Склирены; однако никаких упоминаний о 
2 CONC рождении у Константина детей нет. Происхождение Феофано, её современницы и супруги императора Священной римской империи тоже загадочно — прежде считалось, что она дочь императора, теперь же предполагают, что она также принадлежала
2 CONC  к роду Склиров.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
