1 NOTE HMA a un décès le 2 août 1090 et un mariage en 1059 à Melfi
1 NOTE 867789491. Prinsesse Sikelgatia WAIMARSDTR av Salerno (20661)(20662) was born about 1040.(20663) She was a Prinsesse. (20664) Hun var en skjønn og heltemodig lagobardisk prinsesse. Hennes navn skrives forskjellin. Sycheligaita, Si
2 CONC chelgaita, Sigelgaita. Hennes barn med Robert var, foruten sønnene Roger, Guido og Robert: Helene, forlovet m Constantin, Mabille g.m Wilh. av Gantmesnil, Sibille g.m Ebles av Roucy, datter g.m Hugo, en sønn av Azzo av Este, Mathi
2 CONC lde g.m Raimund Berengar II og senere m Aymeri I, Cecilie, samt Gaitelgrima g.m Dreux Anfroi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hun forstod å øve stor inflytelse på Guiscard og synes å ha vært netopp den kone som passet ham, Mere enn en gang nevner kronistene at hun deltok i sin manns kamper, og hun oppflammet hans soldater til kamp. Orderic Vital besky
2 CONC lder henne foo å ha forgiftet Guiscard, hvilket ikke kan være riktig, da hun ikke var hos ham dengang han ble syk. Hun kom derimot til han dødsleie sammen med sønnen Robert.
1 NOTE Sikelgaita (also Sichelgaita or Sigelgaita) (1040 - 16 April 1090) was a Lombard princess, the daughter of Guaimar IV, Prince of Salerno, and second wife of Robert Guiscard, Duke of Apulia.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Robert in 1058, after Robert divorced his first wife Alberada, due to supposed consanguinity. Her sister Gaitelgrima had earlier married Robert's half-brother Drogo. The divorce from Alberada and the marriage of Sikelg
2 CONC aita were probably part of a strategy of alliance with the remaining Lombard princes, of whom Guaimar was chief. Alberada, for her part, appears to have had no qualms about dissolving her marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sikelgaita tried to mediate between her brother Gisulf II of Salerno and husband when their relations went sour, but her pleas went unheeded and she accepted her brother's lot in the war with Guiscard (1078).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sikelgaita frequently accompanied Robert on his conquests. Although at first she tried to persuade him not to attack the Byzantine Empire, she accompanied him on his campaign against them nevertheless. At the Battle of Dyrrhachium
2 CONC  she fought in full armour, rallying Robert's troops when they were initially repulsed by the Byzantine army. According to the Byzantine chronicler Anna Comnena, she was "like another Pallas, if not a second Athena," and, in the A
2 CONC lexiad, Anna attributes to her a quote from the Iliad.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1083, Sikelgaita returned to Italy with Robert to defend Pope Gregory VII against the Emperor Henry IV. She accompanied him on a second campaign against the Byzantines, during which Robert died on Kefalonia in 1085 with Sikelga
2 CONC ita at his side. Early in 1086, Sikelgaita was in Salerno making a donation of the town of Centraro in his honour to Montecassino, which the couple had endowed well throughout their married life.[1] Sikielgaita donated a large amo
2 CONC unt of silver for her health while she was ill on another occasion.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Supposedly, she tried to poison Robert's son Bohemond by his first wife, although the two eventually came to an agreement by which her son Roger Borsa was allowed to succeed Robert in the duchy. With her son she put the Jews of Ba
2 CONC ri under that city's archbishop.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT On her death, she was, at her own request, buried in Montecassino.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT With Robert, Sikelgaita had eight children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mafalda (1059/1060 - 1108), married Raymond Berengar II of Barcelona and then Aimeric I, Viscount of Narbonne 
2 CONT Roger Borsa (1060/1061 - 1111) 
2 CONT Guy (died 1107) 
2 CONT Robert Scalio (died 1110) 
2 CONT Sibilla (Sybil), married Ebles II, Count of Roucy 
2 CONT Mabillia (Mabel), married William de Grandmesnil 
2 CONT Heria, married Hugh V, Count of Maine 
2 CONT Olympias, betrothed to Constantine Doukas, son of Michael VII Ducas and Maria Bagrationi, in 1074 
2 CONT Emma, married Odo the Good Marquis 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT ^ a b Bloch, 214. 
2 CONT ^ Loud, 823. 
2 CONT ^ Ibid, 828. 
2 CONT
