1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Guiscard  ''[49 languages]''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Robert Guiscard''' (c. 1015 – 17 July 1085), also referred to as Robert de Hauteville, was a Norman adventurer remembered for his conquest of southern Italy and Sicily in the 11th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was born into the Hauteville family in Normandy, the sixth son of Tancred de Hauteville and his wife Fressenda. He inherited the County of Apulia and Calabria from his brother in 1057, and in 1059 he was made Duke of Apulia
2 CONC  and Calabria and Lord of Sicily by Pope Nicholas II. He was also briefly Prince of Benevento (1078–1081), before returning the title to the papacy. 
2 CONT {{https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Tomba_degli_Altavilla.jpg/1280px-Tomba_degli_Altavilla.jpg|right|200px}}
2 CONT
2 CONT #[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Guiscard#Name  Name]
2 CONT #[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Guiscard#Historical_background  Historical background]
2 CONT #[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Guiscard#Arrival_in_Italy Arrival in Italy]
2 CONT #[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Guiscard#Rule Rule]
2 CONT #[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Guiscard#Succession Succession]
2 CONT #[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Guiscard#Religion Religion]
2 CONT #[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Guiscard#Marriage_and_issue Marriage and issue]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Guiscard d'Hauteville, Count and Duke of Apulia, Calabria and Sicily
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Guiscard was of Norman descent, the sixth son of Tancred of Hauteville and eldest by his second wife Fressenda of Normandy. Grandson of Richard I, the 3rd Duke of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Albereda of Buonalbergo, the daughter of Reginald I, Count of Burgundy and Adelais of Nornmandy, and granddaughter of Othon Guillaume de Bourgogne and Ermetrude de Roucy, Richard II the 4th Duke of Normandy and Judith d
2 CONC e Bretagne. They were married about 1051 and had two children:
2 CONT Emma, mother of Tancred Prince of Galilee
2 CONT Prince Bohemond I of Antioch
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert divorced Albereda about 1058 and married Sikelgaita, the daughter of Guaimar IV, Prince of Salerno. They had eight children:
2 CONT * Mafalda, wife of Ramon Berenguer II, Count of Barcelona
2 CONT * Roger Borsa
2 CONT * Mabile, wife of William de Grantmesnil
2 CONT * Gersent, wife of Hugh of Maine
2 CONT * Robert Scalio
2 CONT * Guy, Duke of Amalfi d 1107
2 CONT * Sibylla, wife of Ebles de Ramerupt, Count of Roucy
2 CONT * Olympias, betrothed to Constantine Doukas
2 CONT
2 CONT According to legend in 1047 he left Normandy with five mounted riders and foot soldiers for Langobardia where he became the chief of a roving robber band. Anna Comnena gave a physical description of Guiscard that included a tyrann
2 CONC ical temperament, a cunning mind, brave in action, clever in attack, most obstinate in achievement and he did not allow any obstacle to prevent his executing his desire. The tallest of his peers, ruddy complexion, flaxen hair and 
2 CONC broad shoulders with eyes that "all but emitted sparks of fire." His cry, "Is said to have put thousands to flight." Then, "equipped by fortune, physique and character, he was naturally indomitable, and subordinate to no one in th
2 CONC e world."
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert would be involved in local feuding before being granted the command of the fortress at Scribula from his brother, Drogo. Robert settled at the castle at San Marco Argentano instead.
2 CONT
2 CONT While in Calabria and still a robber baron, Robert married Albereda of Buonalbergo. Albereda's dowry consisted of two hundred knights arranged by her uncle Lord Girard of Buonalbergo, looking for Robert's support.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert distinguished himself at the Battle of Civitate in 1053 fighting with his brother Humphrey and his father-in-law against Pope Leo's forces who wanted Normans removed from Italy. Robert was unseated and remounted thrice, the
2 CONC n honored for his actions by succeeding Humphrey as Count of Apulia in 1057.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Pope Nicholas had strengthened law prohibiting consanguinity, which gave Robert the ability to easily repudiate Albereda about 1058. Evidently the 'divorce' was amicable. Soon after, Robert would marry Sichelgaita, the 
2 CONC sister of a previous adversary's successor, Gisulf II of Salerno. In return for her hand in marriage, Robert agreed to destroy his brother, William's castle on Guisulf's territory.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Papacy reversed their policy and made the Normans their allies, investing Robert as Duke of Apulia, Calabria and Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert and his brother Roger captured Messina in 1061 and Palermo in 1072. A campaign in 1064 failed as his campsite became infested with tarantulas to where it had to be abandoned. During this time he became known as "Black Shirt
2 CONC  Robert" as his elegant clothing with imported dyes ran together to make black. In 1076 he took Salerno, expelling his wife's brother, Gisulf.
2 CONT
2 CONT His campaign against the Byzantine empire included an infamous sword fight where Robert faced his most worthy adversary and nemesis, Emperor Alexius, at the Battle of Dyrrhachium, in October of 1081. His wife, Sikelgaita fought al
2 CONC ong side him in full armour.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert died of a fever along with 500 of his Norman knights at Corfu on the 17th of July, 1085. He was seventy years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT The town of Fiscardo on Kefalonia is named after him, he was succeeded by his sons Roger Borsa. Robert is credited with bringing Latin Christianity to the Byzantine territory.
2 CONT
2 CONT *went on to become Count of Apulia and Calabria (1057–1059), 
2 CONT *and then Duke of Apulia and Calabria and lord of Sicily (1059–1085), 
2 CONT *and briefly Prince of Benevento (1078–1081) before returning the title to the Pope.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Guiscard (c. 1015 – 17 July 1085) was a Norman adventurer remembered for the conquest of southern Italy and Sicily. Robert was born into the Hauteville family in Normandy, went on to become Count of Apulia and Calabria (105
2 CONC 7–1059), and then Duke of Apulia and Calabria and Duke of Sicily (1059–1085), and briefly Prince of Benevento (1078–1081) before returning the title to the Pope.
2 CONT
2 CONT His sobriquet, in contemporary Latin Viscardus and Old French Viscart, is often rendered "the Resourceful", "the Cunning", "the Wily", "the Fox", or "the Weasel". In Italian sources he is often Roberto il Guiscardo or Roberto d'Al
2 CONC tavilla (from Robert de Hauteville)
2 CONT
2 CONT
