1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Grandson of  https://www.geni.com/people/Theodosios-Monomachos/6000000002188110606
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodosios Monomachos was an important bureaucrat under Basil II and Constantine VIII. He was president of the Senate when he attempted to usurp the throne of emperor on the accession of Michael VI Stratiotikos in 1056. However, M
2 CONC ichael VI managed to survive the conspiracy organized by Theodosios, who was a nephew of the former emperor Constantine IX Monomachos.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BYZANTIUM.htm#TheodosiosMonomachos
2 CONT
2 CONT Two brothers, parents not known: 
2 CONT
2 CONT *1.         THEODOSIOS Monomachos 
2 CONT *2. [--- Monomachos . His parentage is confirmed by Cedrenus who records that "Theodosius præses, patruo Monomachi natus" opposed the succession of "Michaelum Stratioticum" as emperor (in 1056)[1689], on the assumption that "patru
2 CONC us" should be interpreted in its strict sense of paternal uncle, although this is not beyond all doubt. It is should be noted that neither of the sources cited below refer to his son as "Monomachos".] m ---. One child: 
2 CONT **a) '''THEODOSIOS [Monomachos'''] (-after 1056). m ---. The name of Theodosios's wife is not known. Theodosios & his wife had one child:
2 CONT *** i) --- [Monomachos] (-after 1056). Cedrenus records that "Theodosius præses, patruo Monomachi natus" sought refuge "cum filio" in Hagia Sofia (in 1056)[1693
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodosios Monomachos
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodosios_Monomachos
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Theodosios Monomachos'''
2 CONT * Usurper of the Byzantine Empire
2 CONT * Reign	1056
2 CONT * Predecessor	Theodora
2 CONT * Successor	Michael VI
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodosios Monomachos, Latinized as Theodosius Monomachus (Greek: Θεοδόσιος Μονομάχος), was a usurper who attempted to become emperor on the accession of Michael VI Stratiotikos in 1056.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Career===
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodosios Monomachos was the grandson of Theodosios Monomachos, an important bureaucrat under Basil II and Constantine VIII, and the nephew of the emperor Constantine IX Monomachos.[1] In 1056 he was serving as president of the S
2 CONC enate[2] when he heard of the results of the election of Michael VI Stratiotikos upon the death of the empress Theodora. Claiming a link to the throne (his uncle Constantine IX had been married to Theodora's sister, Zoe), he proce
2 CONC eded to arm his household slaves, and with the help of some supporters in the imperial court,[3] he rallied a mob behind him and marched through the streets of Constantinople.[2] To increase his numbers, he threw open some prisons
2 CONC  and recruited the prisoners with the hope of successfully storming the palace.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Varangian Guard heard of the revolt, they marched out to stop him, whereupon all of his armed followers vanished at the sight of the imperial guards.[4] Seeking asylum at Hagia Sophia, Theodosios found the church doors lo
2 CONC cked against him, and he together with his son were captured sitting on the outside steps of the church.[1] Michael VI banished him to Pergamum[1] while the citizens of the city openly mocked him, composing insulting verses about 
2 CONC his failed coup, calling out "The moron Monomachus did whatever he thought of".[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources===
2 CONT
2 CONT Primary Sources
2 CONT * John Zonaras, Extracts of History
2 CONT * John Skylitzes, Synopsis of Histories
2 CONT
2 CONT Secondary Sources
2 CONT * Norwich, John Julius (1993), Byzantium: The Apogee, Penguin, ISBN 0-14-011448-3
2 CONT * Canduci, Alexander (2010), Triumph & Tragedy: The Rise and Fall of Rome's Immortal Emperors, Pier 9, ISBN 978-1-74196-598-8
2 CONT * George Finlay, History of the Byzantine Empire from 716 – 1057, William Blackwood & Sons, 1853
2 CONT
