1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petrislav_of_Rascia
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petrislav_of_Rascia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Petrislav''' (Serbian Cyrillic: Петрислав[A]; fl. 1060–1083) was the Prince of Raška (Latin: Rascia; later anachronism for the Principality of Serbia), a province under the Grand Principality of Doclea, from 1060 to 1083. He wa
2 CONC s appointed to govern Raška by his father, Grand Prince Mihailo I, who had reunited Raška (the Zagora region of the former early medieval Serbian Principality) into the Serbian realm after decades of Byzantine annexation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Background
2 CONT Bosnia, Zahumlje, and Rascia (i.e. Serbia) never were incorporated into an integrated state with Doclea.[1] Each principality had its own nobility and institutions, simply requiring a member of the royal family to rule as Prince o
2 CONC r Duke.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Petrislav was the last son of Mihailo I and his Greek second wife.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mihailo I reconquered Rascia from the Byzantines between 1060 and 1074.[2] He appointed Petrislav as Prince of Rascia.[2] Mihailo I died in 1081, and Constantine Bodin succeeded as Prince.[2] By 1085, the Vojislavljević brothers s
2 CONC uppressed the revolt in the župa of Zeta, staged by their cousins, the sons of Radoslav. Constantine Bodin ruled unchallengedly.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by his two sons, Vukan and Marko, in 1083.[1]
