1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://books.google.com/books?id=lQjkJOUcVIwC&pg=PA35&lpg=PA35&dq=Zubeida+Khatun,+wife+of+Malik-Shah&source=bl&ots=5h6pZ2rimG&sig=reGhqSBaBsqKwuYCVdSrdzoi_6I&hl=en&sa=X&ei=vWwyVdW8EMnksAT_mIDwBQ&ved=0CDEQ6
2 CONC AEwAw#v=onepage&q=Zubeida%20Khatun%2C%20wife%20of%20Malik-Shah&f=false '''Continuity and Change in Medieval Persia'''] by Ann K. S. Lambton:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Abu Tahir Sa'd b. Ali b. Isa Qummi, who was wazir to Sanjar from 515/1121-2 until his death in 516/ 1122-3, had formerly been wazir to '''Taj al-Din Khatun Safariyya''', the mother of Muhammad and Sanjar. Several of the Saljuq wo
2 CONC men held large estates, and the duties of the wazir to the wife of the sultan were probably mainly connected with the management of their personal estates."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note''': a wazier is a vizier or a chancellor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also from the same source:
2 CONT
2 CONT When Mahmud b. Muhammad rebelled against Sanjar in 513/1119-20, his mother, who was also Mahmud’s grandmother, apparently persuaded Sanjar to make peace with his nephew. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Khatun Safariyya is mentioned on page 224 of the [https://www.scribd.com/doc/138326336/Cambridge-History-of-Iran-Vol-5-The-Saljuq-and-Mongol-Periods-pdf '''Cambridge History of Iran, Volume 5'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of the wives had their own divans and viziers, and were women of substance; Sanjar's mother, for instance, had her own mamluks. It appears to have been usual for the sultans' wives to have personal estates.
