1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * From the [http://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/alp-arslan-saljuq-sultan '''Encyclopaedia Iranica page'''] of his brother, Alp Arslan:
2 CONT
2 CONT Contrary to what some of the late sources say, Ṭoḡrel chose Čaḡrī Beg’s son Solaymān as his successor; when he died at Ray on 8 Ramażān 455/4 September 1063 his vizier, ʿAmīd-al-molk Kondorī, proclaimed Solaymān sultan. Alp Arslān
2 CONC , who had been the malek of Khorasan since his father’s death, began to move west on hearing rumors of Ṭoḡrel’s demise (Sebṭ b. al-Jawzī, Merʾāt, pp. 99-102); he pulled back when they proved false, then moved decisively when defin
2 CONC itive news arrived. Some of Ṭoḡrel’s officers, notably Erdem the ḥāǰeb, refused to accept Kondorī’s proclamation of Solaymān and announced for Alp Arslān. Kondorī reversed himself as Alp Arslān approached and relegated Solaymān to
2 CONC  the position of heir-apparent. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * From the [http://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/cagri-beg-dawud-b '''Encyclopaedia Iranica'''] page of his father, Čaḡrī Beg:
2 CONT
2 CONT It is difficult to account for the fact that Ṭoḡrel before his death in 455/1063, in spite of Alp Arslān’s strong position, named as his successor Solaymān, another of Čaḡrī sons who was a virtual nonentity. In the event, however,
2 CONC  it was Alp Arslān with his extensive military experience who became sultan over the united Saljuq empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Solayman's brief accession to the sultanate is mentioned on page 145 of [https://books.google.com/books?id=BAN6ONlDkgIC&printsec=frontcover&source=gbs_ge_summary_r&cad=0#v=onepage&q&f=false '''The Annals of the Saljuq Turks: Sel
2 CONC ections from Al-Kamil Fi'l-Ta'rikh of Ibn Al-Athir'''], translated by D.S. Richards:
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Sultan Tughril Beg, Amid al-Mulk al-Kunduri set up on the throne of the sultanate Sulayman, the son of Da'ud Chaghri Beg, whom his uncle Sultan Tughril Beg had recognized as heir to his power, and whose mother T
2 CONC ughril Beg had married. When he was named in the khutbah as sultan, the emirs were at odds. Yaghi Siyan and Ardam went to Qazvin and pronounced the khutbah in the name of Adud al-Dawla Alp Arslan Muhammad ibn Da'ud Chaghri Beg, wh
2 CONC o was at that time lord of Khurasan and had with him his vizier, Nizam al-Mulk. The people inclined towards him. When Amid al-Mulk al-Kunduri saw that the situation was turning against him, he ordered the khutbah in Rayy to be mad
2 CONC e for the Sultan Alp Arslan, and after him for his brother, Sulayman.
