1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leo_Argyros_(10th_century)_
2 CONT
2 CONT Leo Argyros (Greek: Λέων Ἀργυρός) was a Byzantine aristocrat and general active in the first decades of the 10th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of the magistros Eustathios Argyros, droungarios of the Watch under Leo VI the Wise (ruled 886–912). In ca. 910, Leo and his brother Pothos Argyros were serving at court as manglabites (personal bodyguards of the em
2 CONC peror), when their father was poisoned after being suspected by Leo for plotting against him. The two brothers brought their father's body for burial to their ancestral monastery of Saint Elizabeth in the Charsianon district.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pothos and Leo both followed military careers. According to Constantine VII Porphyrogennetos, already in 911, Leo, despite his youth, became military governor (strategos) of the theme of Sebasteia, with the rank of protospatharios
2 CONC . Both brothers played a distinguished role during the regency of Empress Zoe Karbonopsina (913–919). Leo and a younger brother, Romanos, participated in the campaign against Bulgaria that ended in the disastrous Battle of Achelou
2 CONC s on 20 August 917.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Romanos I Lekapenos (r. 920–944), Leo reached the highest offices and attained the ranks of patrikios and eventually magistros. In April 922 he commanded, along with his brother Pothos, who was then Domestic of the Schools, 
2 CONC the rhaiktor John, and the droungarios of the imperial fleet Alexios Mosele, the army that confronted a Bulgarian raid under the kavkhan Menikos, that had reached the outskirts of Constantinople. The subsequent Battle of Pegae was
2 CONC  a rout for the Byzantines, but the two Argyroi managed to escape to safety in a nearby fort. At some point, Leo too served as Domestic of the Schools, but it is unclear when. Jean-François Vannier suggested the period between Peg
2 CONC ae and June 922, when John Kourkouas held the post, but this seems too brief. Rodolphe Guilland also suggested a brief tenure sometime before 922, or perhaps after Romanos Lekapenos' fall in 944. On the other hand, it is also poss
2 CONC ible that Byzantine historians confused Leo with his brother Pothos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leo Argyros had two sons, Marianos Argyros and Romanos Argyros. Both were firm supporters of Romanos Lekapenos and enjoyed high court titles; Romanos Argyros even married the emperor's daughter, Agathe. Through Romanos, Leo Argyro
2 CONC s was probably the great-grandfather of Emperor Romanos III Argyros (r. 1028–1034).
