1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helena,_daughter_of_Alypius '''Helena, daughter of Alypius''']
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena, daughter of Alypius was the wife of Constantine VIII.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronographia by Michael Psellos mentions her only briefly:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Constantine while still a young man, had married a lady called Helena. She was a daughter of the renowned Alypius, then the leading man in the city and member of a noble family held in high repute. This lady, who was not only bea
2 CONC utiful but also virtuous, bore him three daughters before she died." [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Their three daughters were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudokia. Eldest daughter. According to the Chronographia "in childhood she had been attacked by some infectious illness, and her looks had been marred ever since" [2]. She later became a nun.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zoe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of their marriage Constantine VIII was the co-ruler of his older brother Basil II. Basil reigned as senior Byzantine Emperor from 976 to 1025 but never married. Which would make Helena the only Augusta during his reign
2 CONC . Constantine VIII would only become senior Emperor in 1025. Psellos does not mention if she was still alive by that point.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Possible descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Ronald Wells, a modern genealogist, has suggested that Eudokia did not remain a nun for life. He has theorised an identification of Eudokia with the otherwise unnamed wife of Andronikos Doukas, a Paphlagonian nobleman who may have
2 CONC  served as governor of the theme of Moesia. This theory would make Helena a maternal grandmother of Constantine X and John Doukas. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Wells has further suggested two daughters of the above proposed union. The first suggested daughter is Marija, the wife of Ivan Vladislav. The second daughter is "Sophia", an alleged wife of Manuel Erotikos Komnenos. [4]Manuel was
2 CONC  the father of Isaac I Komnenos and John Komnenos, the latter being the father of Alexios I Komnenos.
2 CONT
2 CONT The theory apparently serves as a way to trace the ancestry of the Doukas and Komnenos families to the Macedonian dynasty. However if such a descent exists, primary sources are silent about it.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Michael Psellos, "Chronographia", Book 2, chapter 4
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Michael Psellos, "Chronographia", Book 2, chapter 5
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Ronald Wells, "Ancient Ancestors"
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Ronald Wells, "Ancient Ancestors"
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Helena, daughter of Alypius (in Greek: Ελένη) was a Byzantine Empress consort as the wife of Byzantine Emperor Constantine VIII.
2 CONT
2 CONT Very little is known about her, as she is only briefly mentioned in the Chronographia of Michael Psellos, followed in the works of John Skylitzes and the Zonaras. Psellos writes of her only that:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Constantine while still a young man, had married a lady called Helena. She was a daughter of the renowned Alypius, then the leading man in the city and member of a noble family held in high repute. This lady, who was not only bea
2 CONC utiful but also virtuous, bore him three daughters before she died."
2 CONT
2 CONT Apart from this reference in Psellos, her father Alypius (Αλυπίου) is otherwise unknown. The marriage probably took place ca. 976, and Helena died at some unknown point, apparently long before her husband became sole emperor in 10
2 CONC 25. The historian Gunther G. Wolf theorized that she died ca. 989, possibly during the birth of her third daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their three daughters were: Eudokia (Ευδοκία). Eldest daughter. According to the Chronographia "in childhood she had been attacked by some infectious illness, and her looks had been marred ever since".[4] She later became a nun. Z
2 CONC oë Porphyrogenita. Theodora.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of their marriage, Constantine VIII was the co-ruler of his older brother Basil II. Basil reigned as senior Byzantine Emperor from 976 to 1025 but never married, which would make Helena the only Augusta during his reig
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ronald Wells, a modern genealogist, has suggested that Eudokia did not remain a nun for life. He has theorised an identification of Eudokia with the otherwise unnamed wife of Andronikos Doukas, a Paphlagonian nobleman who may have
2 CONC  served as governor of the theme of Moesia. This theory would make Helena a maternal grandmother of Constantine X and John Doukas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wells has further suggested two daughters of the above proposed union. The first suggested daughter is Maria, the wife of Ivan Vladislav. Christian Settipani has however posited a more robustly argued descent of Maria from Tsar Bo
2 CONC ris II of Bulgaria.[6] The second daughter is "Sophia", an alleged wife of Manuel Erotikos Komnenos. Manuel was the father of Isaac I Komnenos and John Komnenos, the latter being the father of Alexios I Komnenos.
2 CONT
2 CONT The claim, which provides no references or argumentation, apparently serves as a way to trace the ancestry of the Doukas and Komnenos families to the Macedonian dynasty. There is however no proof for any such relationship of Eudok
2 CONC ia in primary sources or contemporary sigillography and modern historical and prosopographical authorities remain equally silent on the matter, making the asserted descent appear entirely unlikely.
