2 CONT [https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%90%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_(%D0%B6%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%92%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0_%D0%AF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D
2 CONC 0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0)_ Russian Wikipedia] retrieved January 2021.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Anna Polovetskaya''' (died October 7, 1111 ) - Polovtsian princess, the second wife of the Grand Duke of Kiev Vsevolod Yaroslavich .
2 CONT
2 CONT According to popular belief, she was the daughter of the Polovtsian Khan, which is not confirmed by sources, although probably from the point of view of the political situation of the late 1060s. In baptism she received the name A
2 CONC nna. The date of birth, the names of her parents and her Polovtsian name are unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1068 she became the second wife (after the death of her first wife in 1067) of the [https://www.geni.com/people/Vsevolod-Yaroslavich-Grand-Prince-of-Kiev/6000000001610448055?through=6000000017917237051 Grand Duke of Kiev] (from
2 CONC  1076) Vsevolod Yaroslavich . [4] [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT After Vsevolod's death in 1093, she stayed in Kiev , where, apparently, she was respected by the local residents. When in 1097 her stepson Vladimir Monomakh together with the Chernigov princes besieged Prince Svyatopolk Izyaslavic
2 CONC h in Kiev, it was her and Metropolitan Nicholas who were asked by the Kievans to become mediators in negotiations between the warring parties. ChronicleShe cites the entire speech with which Anna turned to Vladimir Monomakh: “Pray
2 CONC , to the prince, to you and your brother, you do not want to destroy the Rus lands. Even more will you take up the army among you, the rottenness has to rejoice and disturb our land, your fathers and grandfathers like that, your f
2 CONC athers and grandfathers have acquired great labor and courage, sweeping across the Russian lands, and other lands to seek out. And you want to destroy the Rus land. " The chronicle notes that Vladimir Monomakh revered Anna as a mo
2 CONC ther, so he took her words with tears and promised to fulfill the request. As a result, the strife ended in peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the end of her life, Anna went to the Andreevsky monastery , whose abbess was her stepdaughter Anna-Yanka . She died on October 7, 1111, and was buried in this monastery. [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess "Vsevolozh" appears in the graffiti on the wall of the St. Sophia Cathedral , where it is said that the widow bought the land of a certain Boyana with 12 witnesses.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 
