2 CONT John Sloan and Micha Jelisavic write:  He was the sixth son of Vladimir I Svyatoslavich, prince of Kyiv. He married Ingigerd, daughter of Olaf, King of Sweden. His sons were Ilya (Elias), prince of Novgorod, (d 1020); Vladimir, pr
2 CONC ince of Novgorod (d 1043); Izyaslav I, prince of Kyiv (d 1107); Svyatoslav II, usurper prince of Kyiv and prince of Chernigiv (d 1076); Vsyevolod I, prince of Kyiv (d 1093); Vyacheslav, prince of Smolensk (d 1057); and Igor, princ
2 CONC e of Vladimir-in-Volynia (d 1060).
2 CONT
2 CONT  On the death of their father, Vladimir, Svyatopolk I seized the throne and had the half-brothers, Boris, Gleb, and Vsyevelod, killed. Yaroslav was ruling in Novgorod. He marched south with a combined Novgorodian-Varangian army. S
2 CONC ee Svyatopolk I. In 1017 Svyatopolk set the Polish King, Boleslaw Khrabryi, against the Russians. On the 22nd of July, a Polish army under the command of the Polish king reached the Bug river, then the border dividing Russian hold
2 CONC ings from those Polish. Here they met Yaroslav and his cohorts. With a surprise attack the Poles routed the Russians and unopposed came upon Kiev. Here they captured the wife and sisters of Yaroslav, one of whom became Boleslaw's 
2 CONC concubine. In the "Kronika Thietmara," it is recorded that the Polish bishop Rhinebern in the 1020s found himself in Rus' as a choir master for the daughter of Boleslaw who was marrying the son of Vladimir of Kiev--Svyatopolk. Pag
2 CONC e 150, Priniatiye Khristianstva Narodami Tsentral'noi I Yugo-Vostochnoi Yevropy I Kreshchenie Rusi, 1988. After several campaigns Yaroslav was again and firmly ensconced on the throne of Kiev and generously rewarded to Novgorodian
2 CONC s before he allowed them to go home. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  The most important monument, (or better, a part of a monument) that dates from the reign of Yaroslav, is the Cathedral of Saint Sophia in Kiev, built in 1037 to commemorate the Russian victory over the Patzinaks (Pechenegs) in th
2 CONC e previous year. Originally the cathedral was conceived on the Greek cross plan and built in line with Yaroslav's fondness for monumental forms, solemn dispositions and very richly decorated frescoes and mosaics. At that time the 
2 CONC Cathedral had five aisles terminated in semicircular apses and six smaller cupolas, dominated by the seventh central large cupola over the central square. Four piers each of about 23 feet in diameter mark the square and support fo
2 CONC ur arched vaults that carry the central cupola. The drum of this cupola has several (12) tall, large windows that bring light in from all sides and lavishly enlighten the frescoes and mosaics that are under the main cupola or the 
2 CONC nearby apse, in contrast with other parts of the cathedral which are rather dark. When the Tatars invaded Kiev they spared the cathedral but when Lithuanians and Poles moved in after them, Saint Sophia was seriously damaged and an
2 CONC  entire wall collapsed. Since then the cathedral was restored and renovated several times. Its exterior received an entirely different appearance when in the seventeenth century Metropolitan Moghila and Hetman Mazepa decided to en
2 CONC large and restore the cathedral. Two more aisles were added, making the cathedral 176 feet wide, and six more cupolas were put on. At this time the ancient hemispheric Byzantine cupolas were replaced by pear-shaped cupolas that be
2 CONC came popular in Ukraine in the seventeenth and the eighteenth centuries. Later several more cupolas were added and the entire outline of the edifice, regretfully, turned into a sort of Ukrainian baroque. To make the exterior of th
2 CONC e cathedral even more exotic, in 1953-1954 Soviet authorities gilded the cupolas. On a few occasions excavations were made inside and around the cathedral by the Soviet archaeologists. According to some, the ancient walls and foun
2 CONC dations show that the original building had many details in common with old churches built in Armenia during or before the eleventh century. These findings prompted some Soviet historians to conclude that first Russian monuments w
2 CONC ere more influenced by Armenian and Georgian masters than by the Greeks. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Chronology: 
2 CONT 1016-1019 AD - Yaroslav organizes two campaigns with Varangian and Novgorodian troops to unseat Svyatopolk from the throne at Kyiv 
2 CONT 1020 AD - Yaroslav's son Vladimir is born. Elias was born while Yaroslav was still at Novgorod and was placed on command at Novgorod when Yaroslav left, but died that same year.
2 CONT 1021 AD - Bryacheslav Izyaslavich, grandson of Vladimir I captures Novgorod and then returns to Polotsk. Yaroslav marches north and defeats Bryacheslav at the Sudomir River and frees the Novgorodian prisoners, forcing Bryacheslav 
2 CONC to flee to Polotsk. 
2 CONT 1022 AD - Yaroslav marches on Brest. Mstislav, ruler of Tmutorokan, attacks the Kasogians. He defeats the Kasogian ruler, Rededya, in single combat.
2 CONT 1023 AD - Mstislav marches north with combined Khazar, Kasogian and personal druzhina to attack Yaroslav.
2 CONT 1024 AD - Yaroslav is still at Novgorod when Mstislav arrives at Kyiv. The Kyivans refuse him entry so he marches on to take Chernigiv on the other side of the Dniper. The chronicler reports that in the same year magicians appeare
2 CONC d at Suzdal and than Yaroslav had to march there to expel them before organizing a campaign to Kyiv. He follows the standard practice by sending for Varangian troops. The two armies meet at Listven. Mstislav deploys his Severians 
2 CONC in the center of his line opposite Yaroslav's Varangians. He keeps his personal druzhina out of the fray during a night battle in the midst of a tremendous thunder storm. The result is a victory for Mstislav after which he is doub
2 CONC ly pleased that the Severians and Varangians had taken all the casulaties while his personal troops remained unscathed. Meanwhile Yaroslav flees again to Novgorod. Mstislav proposes a peace treaty by which he will have the left ba
2 CONC nk Dniper from Chernigiv to Murom and Yaroslav will have the right bank Dniper including Novgorod. Yaroslav is warry and stays at Novgorod.
2 CONT 1026 AD - Yaroslav has a new army of sufficient strength to venture south and enter Kyiv. He accepts Mstislav's proposal for split domain. They manage to live in peace until Mstislav dies.
2 CONT 1027 AD - Yaroslav's third son, Svyatoslav, is born. 
2 CONT 1030 AD - Yaroslav captures Bel'z. His fourth son, Vsyevolod, is born. He founds Yur'yev. There is a revolt in Poland after the death of Boleslaw the Great. Many priests and bishops are killed. 
2 CONT 1031 AD - Yaroslav and Mstislav lead a joint campaign into Poland. They retake the Cherven towns and resettle many Poles along the southern Russian frontier. 
2 CONT 1032 AD - Yaroslav expands Russian towns and fortified line south along the Ros River.
2 CONT 1033 AD - Mstislav's son, Eustathius, dies, leaving his without heir.
2 CONT 1036 AD - Mstislav dies while hunting and his part of Rus reverts to Yaroslav. Yaroslav goes to Novgorod and installs his son, Vladimir, at prince. This year Yaroslav has another son, Vyacheslav. Yaroslav also imprisoned his broth
2 CONC er, Sudislav, at Pskov.
2 CONT 1036 AD - While Yaroslav is in Novgorod the Pecheneg again launch a major offensive to besiege Kyiv. Yaroslav sails to Kyiv. He enters the city and then sallies out toward the Pecheneg force in the field where the St Sophia cathed
2 CONC ral is now. Yaroslav deploys his forces with Kyivans in right, Varangians in center and Novgorodians on left. The struggle lasts most of the day before the Pecheneg are routed and flee in all directions.
2 CONT 1037 AD - Yaroslav expands the city fortress and also the outer city walls. He builds the great St Sophia cathedral and the Church of the Annunciation over the Golden Gate, the most impressive of the three gates in the new city wa
2 CONC ll. 
2 CONT 1038 AD - Yaroslav attacks the Yatvingians (southern Lithuania).
2 CONT 1039 AD - The Church of the Blessed Virgin, (The tithe church) started by Vladimir in the inner part of the city, which was damaged in the city fire of 1017, is re-consecrated.
2 CONT 1040 AD - Yaroslav again attacks Lithuania. (Probably trying to open a more direct route to the Baltic via the Dvina river.
2 CONT 1041 AD - Yaroslav campaigns by river against the Masovians, who live along the Northern Bug and Vistula.
2 CONT 1042 AD - Yaroslav's son, Vladimir, attacks the Yam district (between Novgorod and the coast).
2 CONT 1043 AD - Yaroslav sends his son, Vladimir, to attack Byzantium with a very large naval expedition. The Byzantine emperor, Constantine IX, is surprised but eventually victorious by use of "Greek Fire". On the return voyage they at
2 CONC tempt an attack at Varna and are again defeated. 
2 CONT 1043 AD - Yaroslav arranges the marriage of his sister, Dobronega-Maria, to Casimir, prince of Poland. Yaroslav's son, Izyaslav, marries Casimir's sister. Casimir returns 800 prisoners captured by Boleslav.
2 CONT 1044 AD - Bryachislav Izyaslavich, prince of Polotsk, dies and is succeeded by his son, Vseslav.
2 CONT 1045 AD - Vladimir Yaroslavich founds the church of St Sophia in Novgorod, which is largely intact to the present time.
2 CONT 1046-7 AD - Yaroslav conquers the Mazovians and gives the region to Casimir. 
2 CONT 1048-50 Yaroslav's wife, Ingigerd, dies.
2 CONT 1051 AD - Yaroslav appoints Hilarion as Metropolitan of Rus with his chair at the new cathedral of St Sophia. The Cave Monastery (Pechersky Lavra) just south of Kyiv and the Church at Berestovo were also founded during Yaroslav's 
2 CONC reign. 
2 CONT 1052 AD - Vladimir, Yaroslav's eldest son, dies at Novgorod. 
2 CONT 1054 AD - Yaroslav dies at Vyshgorod and is succeeded by his eldest remaining son, Izyaslav I, who is at Novgorod at the time. The Chronicle contains Yaroslav's testament in which he urges his sons to live in peace with each other
2 CONC . He designates Izyaslav to be prince of Kyiv, Svyatoslav to be prince of Chernigiv, Vsyevolod to Pereyaslavl, Igor to Vladimir-in-Volynia, and Vyacheslav to Smolensk. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
1 NOTE Storfurste i Kiev och Novgorod
1 NOTE Burke calls him Great Duke of Russia. Snorri Sturlasson call himPrince of
2 CONT Holmgarth and shows his children as Holti-Nimble, Vissivald, Ellisif.
2 CONT
2 CONT Donald Lines Jacobus (1883-1970), the "Founder of Scientific
2 CONT Genealogy in America" wrote an article in The American Genealogist(TAG)
2 CONT 9:13-15 entitled "The House of Rurik."  I quote:  "To correct the many
2 CONT errors that have appeared in print, and to aid those who follow thepastime
2 CONT of tracing "royal ancestry," the following condensed account of theearly
2 CONT Rurikides is here printed.  It is based in large part on "Genealogieset
2 CONT Mariages Occidentaux des Rurikides Russes du Xe au XIIIe Siecle,"published
2 CONT at Rome in 1927 as Vol. IX, No. 1, of *Orientalia Christiana.* Theauthor,
2 CONT N. de Baumgarten, is probably the best living authority on earlyRussian
2 CONT history, and every statement made on the fourteen genealogical tablesof
2 CONT his monograph is fully supported by the citation of contemporarydocuments
2 CONT and chronicles."
2 CONT
2 CONT I am not attacking Jacobus, who is a giant among genealogistsand
2 CONT certainly needs no defenders.  Neither am I disagreeing with Alexander
2 CONT Agamov, in Moscow, who has pointed out that there is no credibleevidence
2 CONT that Rurik was ever "Prince of Kiev" and progenitor of the linebeginning
2 CONT with Igor, Grand Prince of Kiev, who married Olga. I hope AlexanderAgamov
2 CONT is reading this.  Some historians and genealogists, Russians inparticular,
2 CONT have taken sharp issue with the theory that the Kievan Rus wasfounded by
2 CONT a "Dane" rather than a "Slav"----and the evidence for "The Varangian
2 CONT Theory" seems fragmentary and inconclusive, at best.
2 CONT
2 CONT G. Andrews Moriarty and Walter L. Sheppard in TAG 28:91-95also
2 CONT quote the N. de Baumgarten material as authoritative [specifically
2 CONT "Orientalia Christiana, No. 119, N. de Baumgarten, "Aux Origines de la
2 CONT Russie," p. 79.  Both Jacobus and Moriarty/Sheppard headline theircharts
2 CONT with "Rurik (d. 879) Grand Prince of Kiev."  Jacobus probably did notread
2 CONT 10th to 13th century Russian. But--- some of us may.
2 CONT
2 CONT For anyone who might conceivably have access to the original,1927,
2 CONT N. de Baumgarten source---is it provable that, "every statement madeon the
2 CONT fourteen genealogical tables of his monograph is fully supported bythe
2 CONT citation of contemporary documents and chronicles?"
2 CONT
2 CONT Or, is it possible that Jacobus and the other experts simply
2 CONT trusted in N. de Baumgarten's scholarship and professionalism---anddid not
2 CONT really check out the facts themselves. The Editor of a journal, suchas
2 CONT TAG, certainly cannot check out every fact and document himself. But,in
2 CONT this particular case, Jacobus gives the N. de Baumgarten material his
2 CONT personal imprimatur, as cited above [TAG 9:13, Paragraph 2]
2 CONT
2 CONT So----has N. de Baumgarten in his "OrientaliaChristiana"---dealing
2 CONT with "The House of Rurik"---been totally discredited by subsequentrigorous
2 CONT scholarship---or does his judgment still seem credible to some serious
2 CONT scholars----or are there alternate explanations?
2 CONT
2 CONT This is an intriguing question of interest to many folks whoare
2 CONT descended from Anne of Kiev (c. 1024-c.1066) [Anna Yaroslavna] whomarried
2 CONT Henry I, King of France.
1 NOTE Jaroslav I "de Wijze" van Kiev, geb. 978, ovl. 20.02.1054, ref. nr. 25.03.2004 ES II-128, KHO, NOW.6,24,[39]                         Vorst van Rostow, grootvorst van Kiew 1019/54. Hij trouwde (1) N.N. Jaroslav zou aanvankelijk vor
2 CONC st van Nowgorod geweest zijn, waar hij de kathedraal van de Heilige Sophia (heden de oudste kerk van Rusland) naar Byzantijns voorbeeld liet bouwen. Ook bouwde hij daar de nog heden bestaande Jaroslov Hof. Nadat hij Swjatopolk de 
2 CONC Vervloekte uit Kiev verdreven had, werd Jaroslov heerser van het rijk. Hij nam een Zweedse prinses tot vrouw en zijn nakomelingen werden door de Europese vorstenhuizen als goede huwelijkspartners beschouwd. Na zijn dood viel het K
2 CONC ievsche Rijk in onafhankelijk vorstendommen uiteen.                         Hij trouwde met Ingegerd (Anna) van Zweden, getrouwd febr 1019 in Sarpsborg.
1 NOTE "WLADIMIROWWITSCH"; GRAND PRINCE OF KIEV 1017-1018 AND 1019-1054; GRAND PRINCE
2 CONT OF ROSTOV 988; GRAND PRINCE OF NOVGOROD 988-1036
1 NOTE Yaroslav I el Sabio1 (Yaroslav Mudryi) (c. 978, Kiev - 20 de febrero de 1054, Kiev)2 (eslavo oriental: Ярослав Мудрый nombre cristiano: Jorge nórdico antiguo: Jarizleifr) fue tres veces Gran Príncipe de Nóvgorod y Kiev, uniendo te
2 CONC mporalmente ambo
2 CONT s principados. Durante su largo reinado, el Rus de Kiev alcanzó el apogeo de su florecimiento cultural y poder militar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contenido ocultar
2 CONT 1 Su camino al trono
2 CONT 2 Su reino
2 CONT 3 Vida familiar y posteridad
2 CONT 4 Bibliografía
2 CONT 5 Referencias
2 CONT 6 Enlaces externos
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT  Su camino al trono  editarLa vida temprana de Yaroslav está cubierta de misterio. Fue uno de los numerosos hijos de Vladimir el Grande, probablemente el segundo con Rogneda de Polotsk, aunque su edad real (según indica la Crónica
2 CONC  de Néstor y lo
2 CONT  corrobora la examinación de su esqueleto en los años 1930) lo ubicaría entre los hijos más jóvenes de Vladimir. Se sugirió que este era un hijo extramatrimonial tras el divorcio de Vladimir de Rogneda y su casamiento con Anna Por
2 CONC phyrogeneta, la
2 CONT  hermana menor de Basilio II, o incluso que éste era hijo de la misma Anna Porphyrogeneta. Yaroslav aparece en la Saga nórdica con el nombre de Jarisleif el Cojo su legendaria cojera (queprovenía probablemente de un flechazo) fue 
2 CONC corroborada por
2 CONT  los científicos que examinaron sus restos.
2 CONT
2 CONT En su juventud, Yaroslav fue enviado por su padre para gobernar las tierras del norte de Rostov pero fue transferido a Nóvgorod, como heredero mayor del trono, en 1010. Mientras vivía allí, encontró la ciudad de Yaroslavl en el Vo
2 CONC lga. La relación
2 CONT  con su padre era aparentemente tensa, por lo que Vladimir legó el trono de Kievan a su hijo menor, Boris. En 1014, Yaroslav se negó a pagar tributo a Kiev y sólo la muerte de Vladimir evitó la guerra.
2 CONT
2 CONT Durante los próximos cuatro años Yaroslav hizo una complicada y sangrienta guerra por Kiev contra su medio hermano Sviatopolk, que tenía el apoyo de su suegro, el duque Boleslao I el Bravo.3 Durante el curso de esta lucha varios d
2 CONC e sus hermanos (
2 CONT Boris y Gleb, Svyatoslav) fueron brutalmente asesinados.3 La Crónica de Néstor acusa a Svyatopolk de haber planeado estos asesinatos, mientras que la Saga de Eymund es a menudo interpretada como una narración de la historia del as
2 CONC esinato de Boris
2 CONT  por los Varegos a cargo de Yaroslav.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yaroslav derrotó a Svyatopolk en su primera batalla, en 1016, y este huyó a Polonia. Pero Svyatopolk regresó con tropas polacas, suministradas por su suegro, el duque Boleslao I el Bravo,tomó Kiev y repelió a Yaroslav a Nóvgorod.3
2 CONC  En 1019, Yaros
2 CONT lav eventualmente prevaleció sobre Svyatopolk y estableció su dominio en Kiev.4 Una de sus primeras acciones como Gran Príncipe fue otorgar a los novgorodenses leales (quienes lo ayudarona recuperar el trono) varias libertades y p
2 CONC rivilegios. De
2 CONT  esta manera se estableció la fundación de la República de Nóvgorod. Los novgorodenses respetaban a Yaroslav más que a otros príncipes kieveños y la espléndida residencia de la ciudad (donde a menudo se reunía el Veche), situada a
2 CONC l lado del merca
2 CONT do, recibió el nombre de Yaroslavovo Dvorishche. Se piensa que en este período Yaroslav promulgó el primer código de leyes, la Justicia de Yaroslav o mejor conocida como Russkaya Pravda, en las tierras eslavas orientales.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT  Su reino  editar
2 CONT Una de muchas estatuas de Yaroslav sosteniendo el Russkaya Pravda en su mano.
2 CONT Monumento a Yaroslav en Yaroslavl representado en el billete de banco de 1000 rublos rusos.Dejando a un lado la legitimidad de los reclamos de Yaroslav al trono de Kiev en su acusación por el asesinato de sus hermanos, Néstor y po
2 CONC steriormente var
2 CONT ios historiadores rusos a menudo lo representan como un modelo de Sabio. Un lado menos atractivo de su personalidad puede ser revelado a través del hecho de que encarceló de por vida a suhermano menor, Sudislav. Otro de sus herman
2 CONC os, Mstislav de
2 CONT  Tmutarakan, cuyo reino bordeaba el Cáucaso Norte y el Mar Negro, se apresuró a Kiev y venció a Yaroslav en 1024. Inmediatamente Yaroslav y Mstislav dividieron el Principado de Kiev: mientras que Yaroslav permaneció con la ribera 
2 CONC oriental del Dni
2 CONT éper, cuya capital es Chernigov, la ribera occidental fue cedida a Mstislav, hasta su muerte en 1036, año en el que Yaroslav reunió nuevamente todo el territorio del Estado de Kiev bajo su mando.3
2 CONT
2 CONT En su política exterior, Yaroslav contó con la alianza de Escandinavia e intentó debilitar la influencia del Imperio Bizantino en Kiev. En 1030 reconquistó Rutenia Rojo, y concluyó una alianza con el rey Casimiro I el Restaurador,
2 CONC  cerrada con el
2 CONT  posterior casamiento de la hermana de Yaroslav, María. En otro exitoso asalto militar, ese mismo año, conquistó el hipotético fuerte estoniano de Tarbatu, donde construyó su propio fuerte, que recibió el nombre de Yuriev (por San
2 CONC  Jorge, o Yury,
2 CONT  santo patrono de Yaroslav). Además forzó a la provincia circundante de Ungannians a pagar un tributo anual.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1043 Yaroslav dirigió un asalto naval contra Constantinopla inducido por su hijo Vladimir Vyshata. Aunque el ejército Rus fue derrotado en la Guerra Rus-Byzantina (1043), Yaroslav decidió terminar la guerra con un tratado favor
2 CONC able y el casami
2 CONT ento prestigioso de su hijo Vsevolod con la hija del emperador. Se sugirió que la paz fue ventajosa debido a que los kieveños tomaron posesión del Imperio Bizantino en Crimea, Quersoneso.
2 CONT
2 CONT Para defender a su estado de los Pechenegos y de otras tribus nómadas amenazadoras del sur, Yaroslav construyó una línea de fuertes, que abarcaban Yuriev, Boguslav, Kanev, Korsun y Pereyaslav. Para celebrar su decisiva victoria so
2 CONC bre los Pecheneg
2 CONT os en 1036 (quienes nunca trataron con Kiev),5 este patrocinó la construcción de la catedral de Santa Sofía de Kiev, que fue uno de los edificios más representativos del siglo XI, en 1037.6 7 8 Otros celebrados monumentos de su re
2 CONC ino, tales como
2 CONT  la Puerta de Oro de Kiev, sufrieron un grave deterioro.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yaroslav fue un destacado patrocinador de libros de cultura y del aprendizaje. En 1051 tuvo un monje ruso, Ilarion (Hilario), que proclamó a Kiev metropolitana, cambiando de esta manera la antigua tradición bizantina de ubicar a l
2 CONC os griegos en se
2 CONT des episcopales. El discurso de Ilarion sobre Yaroslav y su padre Vladimir es a menudo citado como el primer trabajo de la literatura rusa.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT  Vida familiar y posteridad  editarEn 1019, Yaroslav se casó con Ingegerd Olofsdotter, hija del rey de Suecia Olaf el Tesorero,9 y le dio la ciudad y condado de Ladoga como regalo de casamiento y parte del trato.10 Se presume que 
2 CONC Yaroslav había e
2 CONT stado casado anteriormente con una mujer llamada Anna.
2 CONT
2 CONT En la catedral de Santa Sofía de Kiev uno puede ver un fresco que representa a la familia entera: Yaroslav, Irene (o Ingigerd, como era conocida en Rus) y sus cinco hijas y cinco hijos. Yaroslav casó a tres de sus hijas con prínci
2 CONC pes extranjeros,
2 CONT  exiliados, que vivieron en su palacio: Isabel (Elizaveta) con Harald III de Noruega (que obtuvo su mano por sus hazañas militares en el Imperio Bizantino) Anastasia (Agmunda) con el futuro Andrés I de Hungría y su hija menor, Ana
2 CONC  de Kiev, se cas
2 CONT ó con Enrique I de Francia,9 que rigió en este país durante la minoría de edad de su hijo. Otra hija puede haber sido la Ágata que se casó con Eduardo el Exiliado, heredero del trono de Inglaterra, y que fue madre de Edgar Athelin
2 CONC g y Santa Margar
2 CONT ita. Su hija mayor Dobroniega o Dobroñeva (o segun otros fuentes su hermana) se casó con Casimiro I el Restaurador, rey de Polonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarcófago de Yaroslav I el Sabio.Yaroslav tuvo un hijo de su primer matrimonio con Anna, hija de Conrad de Swabia(?), (cuyo nombre cristiano era Ilya (1020†). Yaroslav le casó con Estrid-Margarita (?), hija de Svend I de Dinamarca
2 CONC ), y seis hijos
2 CONT  de su segundo matrimonio. Teniendo en cuenta el daño que podría sobrevenir de las divisiones entre hermanos, les recomendó enfáticamente vivir en paz entre ellos. El mayor de estos, Vladimir de Nóvgorod, más conocido por haber co
2 CONC nstruido la cate
2 CONT dral de Santa Sofía de Nóvgorod, falleció antes que su padre (1020-1052). Tres de sus otros hijos—Iziaslav, Sviatoslav (1027-1076) y Vsevold (1030-1093)—reinaron en Kiev uno tras otro. Los hijos más jóvenes de Yaroslav eran Igor d
2 CONC e Volinia (1036-
2 CONT 1060) y Vyacheslav de Smolensk (1036-1057).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT  Bibliografía  editarMartin, Janet (1995). Medieval Russia, 980-1584. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. ISBN 0-521-36276-8.
2 CONT Nazarenko, A. V. (2001). Drevniaia Rus’ na mezhdunarodnykh putiakh: mezhdistsiplinarnye ocherki kul’turnykh, torgovykh, politicheskikh sviazei IX-XII vekov (en Russian). Moscow: Russian History Institute. ISBN 5-7859-0085-8.
2 CONT Polons’ka-Vasylenko, N. Kyïv chasiv Volodymyra ta Iaroslava (Prague 1944)
2 CONT Tolochko, Petro. Sviatyi Volodymyr Iaroslav Mudryi (Kyiv 1996)
1 NOTE The Grand Duke or Czar of Russia. [BROOKES.GED]
1 NOTE Burke calls him Great Duke of Russia. Snorri Sturlasson call him Prince of
2 CONT Holmgarth and shows his children as Holti-Nimble, Vissivald, Ellisif.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Donald Lines Jacobus (1883-1970), the "Founder of Scientific
2 CONT Genealogy in America" wrote an article in The American Genealogist (TAG)
2 CONT 9:13-15 entitled "The House of Rurik." I quote: "To correct the many
2 CONT errors that have appeared in print, and to aid those who follow thepastime
2 CONT of tracing "royal ancestry," the following condensed account of the early
2 CONT Rurikides is here printed. It is based in large part on "Genealogies et
2 CONT Mariages Occidentaux des Rurikides Russes du Xe au XIIIe Siecle,"published
2 CONT at Rome in 1927 as Vol. IX, No. 1, of *Orientalia Christiana.* The author,
2 CONT N. de Baumgarten, is probably the best living authority on early Russian
2 CONT history, and every statement made on the fourteen genealogical tables of
2 CONT his monograph is fully supported by the citation of contemporary documents
2 CONT and chronicles."
2 CONT
2 CONT     I am not attacking Jacobus, who is a giant among genealogists and
2 CONT certainly needs no defenders. Neither am I disagreeing with Alexander
2 CONT Agamov, in Moscow, who has pointed out that there is no credible evidence
2 CONT that Rurik was ever "Prince of Kiev" and progenitor of the line beginning
2 CONT with Igor, Grand Prince of Kiev, who married Olga. I hope Alexander Agamov
2 CONT is reading this. Some historians and genealogists, Russians in particular,
2 CONT  have taken sharp issue with the theory that the Kievan Rus was founded by
2 CONT a "Dane" rather than a "Slav"----and the evidence for "The Varangian
2 CONT Theory" seems fragmentary and inconclusive, at best.
2 CONT
2 CONT     G. Andrews Moriarty and Walter L. Sheppard in TAG 28:91-95 also
2 CONT quote the N. de Baumgarten material as authoritative [specifically
2 CONT "Orientalia Christiana, No. 119, N. de Baumgarten, "Aux Origines de la
2 CONT Russie," p. 79. Both Jacobus and Moriarty/Sheppard headline their charts
2 CONT with "Rurik (d. 879) Grand Prince of Kiev." Jacobus probably did not read
2 CONT 10th to 13th century Russian. But--- some of us may.
2 CONT
2 CONT     For anyone who might conceivably have access to the original, 1927,
2 CONT N. de Baumgarten source---is it provable that, "every statement made onthe
2 CONT fourteen genealogical tables of his monograph is fully supported by the
2 CONT citation of contemporary documents and chronicles?"
2 CONT
2 CONT     Or, is it possible that Jacobus and the other experts simply
2 CONT trusted in N. de Baumgarten's scholarship and professionalism---and didnot
2 CONT really check out the facts themselves. The Editor of a journal, such as
2 CONT TAG, certainly cannot check out every fact and document himself. But, in
2 CONT this particular case, Jacobus gives the N. de Baumgarten material his
2 CONT personal imprimatur, as cited above [TAG 9:13, Paragraph 2]
2 CONT
2 CONT     So----has N. de Baumgarten in his "Orientalia Christiana"---dealing
2 CONT with "The House of Rurik"---been totally discredited by subsequentrigorous
2 CONT scholarship---or does his judgment still seem credible to some serious
2 CONT scholars----or are there alternate explanations?
2 CONT
2 CONT     This is an intriguing question of interest to many folks who are
2 CONT descended from Anne of Kiev (c. 1024-c.1066) [Anna Yaroslavna] who married
2 CONT Henry I, King of France.
2 CONT [Custer February 1, 2002 Family Tree.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT [merge G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Burke calls him Great Duke of Russia. Snorri Sturlasson call him Prince of
2 CONT Holmgarth and shows his children as Holti-Nimble, Vissivald, Ellisif.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Donald Lines Jacobus (1883-1970), the "Founder of Scientific
2 CONT Genealogy in America" wrote an article in The American Genealogist (TAG)
2 CONT 9:13-15 entitled "The House of Rurik." I quote: "To correct the many
2 CONT errors that have appeared in print, and to aid those who follow thepastime
2 CONT of tracing "royal ancestry," the following condensed account of the early
2 CONT Rurikides is here printed. It is based in large part on "Genealogies et
2 CONT Mariages Occidentaux des Rurikides Russes du Xe au XIIIe Siecle,"published
2 CONT at Rome in 1927 as Vol. IX, No. 1, of *Orientalia Christiana.* The author,
2 CONT N. de Baumgarten, is probably the best living authority on early Russian
2 CONT history, and every statement made on the fourteen genealogical tables of
2 CONT his monograph is fully supported by the citation of contemporary documents
2 CONT and chronicles."
2 CONT
2 CONT     I am not attacking Jacobus, who is a giant among genealogists and
2 CONT certainly needs no defenders. Neither am I disagreeing with Alexander
2 CONT Agamov, in Moscow, who has pointed out that there is no credible evidence
2 CONT that Rurik was ever "Prince of Kiev" and progenitor of the line beginning
2 CONT with Igor, Grand Prince of Kiev, who married Olga. I hope Alexander Agamov
2 CONT is reading this. Some historians and genealogists, Russians in particular,
2 CONT  have taken sharp issue with the theory that the Kievan Rus was founded by
2 CONT a "Dane" rather than a "Slav"----and the evidence for "The Varangian
2 CONT Theory" seems fragmentary and inconclusive, at best.
2 CONT
2 CONT     G. Andrews Moriarty and Walter L. Sheppard in TAG 28:91-95 also
2 CONT quote the N. de Baumgarten material as authoritative [specifically
2 CONT "Orientalia Christiana, No. 119, N. de Baumgarten, "Aux Origines de la
2 CONT Russie," p. 79. Both Jacobus and Moriarty/Sheppard headline their charts
2 CONT with "Rurik (d. 879) Grand Prince of Kiev." Jacobus probably did not read
2 CONT 10th to 13th century Russian. But--- some of us may.
2 CONT
2 CONT     For anyone who might conceivably have access to the original, 1927,
2 CONT N. de Baumgarten source---is it provable that, "every statement made onthe
2 CONT fourteen genealogical tables of his monograph is fully supported by the
2 CONT citation of contemporary documents and chronicles?"
2 CONT
2 CONT     Or, is it possible that Jacobus and the other experts simply
2 CONT trusted in N. de Baumgarten's scholarship and professionalism---and didnot
2 CONT really check out the facts themselves. The Editor of a journal, such as
2 CONT TAG, certainly cannot check out every fact and document himself. But, in
2 CONT this particular case, Jacobus gives the N. de Baumgarten material his
2 CONT personal imprimatur, as cited above [TAG 9:13, Paragraph 2]
2 CONT
2 CONT     So----has N. de Baumgarten in his "Orientalia Christiana"---dealing
2 CONT with "The House of Rurik"---been totally discredited by subsequentrigorous
2 CONT scholarship---or does his judgment still seem credible to some serious
2 CONT scholars----or are there alternate explanations?
2 CONT
2 CONT     This is an intriguing question of interest to many folks who are
2 CONT descended from Anne of Kiev (c. 1024-c.1066) [Anna Yaroslavna] who married
2 CONT Henry I, King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Yaroslav I, byname YAROSLAV THE WISE, Russian YAROSLAV MUDRY (b. 980--d.Feb. 2, 1054), grand prince of Kiev from 1019 to 1054.
2 CONT A son of the grand prince Vladimir, he was vice-regent of Novgorod at thetime of his father's death in 1015. Then his eldest surviving brother,Svyatopolk the Accursed, killed three of his other brothers and seizedpower in Kiev. Ya
2 CONC roslav, with the active support of the Novgorodians andthe help of Varangian (Viking) mercenaries, defeated Svyatopolk andbecame the grand prince of Kiev in 1019.
2 CONT Yaroslav began consolidating the Kievan state through both cultural andadministrative improvements and through military campaigns. He promotedthe spread of Christianity in the Kievan state, gathered a largecollection of books, and
2 CONC  employed many scribes to translate Greekreligious texts into the Slavic language. He founded churches andmonasteries and issued statutes regulating the legal position of theChristian Church and the rights of the clergy. With the 
2 CONC help of Byzantinearchitects and craftsmen, Yaroslav fortified and beautified Kiev alongByzantine lines. He built the majestic Cathedral of St. Sophia and thefamous Golden Gate of the Kievan fortress. Under Yaroslav, thecodificatio
2 CONC n of legal customs and princely enactments was begun, and thiswork served as the basis for a law code called the Russkaya Pravda("Russian Justice").
2 CONT Yaroslav pursued an active foreign policy, and his forces won severalnotable military victories. He regained Galicia from the Poles,decisively defeated the nomadic Pechenegs on the Kievan state's southernfrontier, and expanded Kie
2 CONC van possessions in the Baltic region,suppressing the Lithuanians, Estonians, and Finnish tribes. His militarycampaign against Constantinople in 1043 was a failure, however.
2 CONT Trade with the East and West played an important role in Kievan Rus inthe 11th century, and Yaroslav maintained diplomatic relations with theEuropean states. His daughters Elizabeth, Anna, and Anastasia weremarried respectively to
2 CONC  Harald III of Norway, Henry I of France, andAndrew I of Hungary.
2 CONT In his testament, Yaroslav sought to prevent a power struggle among hisfive sons by dividing his empire among them and enjoining the youngerfour sons to obey the eldest, Izyaslav, who was to succeed his father asgrand prince of Ki
2 CONC ev. This advice had no lasting effect, and civil warensued after Yaroslav's death. {Britannica CD, 1997, YAROSLAV I]AncestralFile Number:<AFN> 952M-GVYaroslav I the Wise, Grand Prince of Kiev
2 CONT Born in 978
2 CONT Acceded in 1019
2 CONT Died on Ferburary 20, 1054 at Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT   Kievan Rus achieved its greatest power and splendor under Yaroslav theWise in the 11th century. Yaroslav made Kiev a great city and builtmagnificent buildings, including the notable Cathedral of Saint Sophia orHagia Sophia of Ki
2 CONC ev. Yaroslav did much to develop Kievan Rus educationand culture. He also revised the first Russian law code, the so-calledRusskaya Pravda or Russian Justice. After his death in 1054, Kievan Rusdeclined. Yaroslav's grandson, Vladi
2 CONC mir II Monomachus, made the finalattempt to unite Kievan Rus, but after his death in 1125 thefragmentation continued as other Kievan Rus principalities challengedKiev's supremacy.
2 CONT   By the 13th century, the East Slavic lands became a loose federation ofcity-states, held together by common language, religion, traditions, andcustoms. Although ruled by members of the house of Rurik, thesecity-states were often
2 CONC  at war with one another. The area became an easytarget for bands of invading Asiatic Mongols.
2 CONT
2 CONT .....Microsoft® Encarta® Encyclopedia 99. © 1993-1998 MicrosoftCorporation.
2 CONT Yaroslav had a son, but the name of the mother is unknown:
2 CONT Elias, Prince of Novgorod
2 CONT Yaroslav married 1019 to Ingeborg Olafsdottir, a daughter of OlafSkötkonung, King of Sweden.
2 CONT Yaroslav and Ingeborg had the following children:
2 CONT Anne of Kiev
2 CONT Izyaslav I, Prince of Kiev, 1054 - 1078, deposed 1068 - 1069 and 1073 -1076.
2 CONT Svyatoslav II, Prince of Kiev, 1073 - 1076
2 CONT Vsevolod I, Prince of Kiev, 1076 - 1093, deposed 1077 - 1078.
2 CONT Ellisif ( or Elizabeth) of Kiev, married first in 1045 to Harald IIIHardrada, King of Norway, who was slain on September 25, 1066 at theBattle of Stamford Bridge. Ellisif married second in 1067 to Svend II,King of Denmark.
2 CONT Valdimar ( or Holti) the Nimble, Prince of Novgorod, 1036 - 1052.
2 CONT Anastasia Agmunda of Kiev, married circa 1046 to Andrew I, King ofHungary.
2 CONT Viacheslav, Prince of Smolensk 1054 - 1056
2 CONT Igor of Vladimir, Prince of Vladimir 1054 - 1060
2 CONT Dobronega (or Maria) of Kiev, married 1038 to Casimir I, King of Poland.
