1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Vladimir Yaroslavich (Russian: Владимир Ярославич)  Prince of Novgorod
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 1020 Died October 4, 1052 reigned as prince of Novgorod
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Yaroslav I the Wise Mother:  Ingigerd
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir Yaroslavich reigned as prince of Novgorod from 1036 until his death. He was the eldest son of Yaroslav I the Wise of Kiev by Ingigerd, daughter of king Olof Skötkonung of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT IZIASLAV [Vladimirovich], son of VLADIMIR I "Velikiy/the Great" Grand Prince of Kiev & his first wife Rogneda of Polotsk (-1001).  Prince of Polotsk.  The Primary Chronicle records the death of Izyaslav in 1001. m ---.   The name 
2 CONC of Iziaslav´s wife is not known. Iziaslav & his wife had two children, Vseslav Iziaslavich and  Bryacheslav Iziaslavich.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_of_Novgorod
2 CONT
2 CONT In the state affairs he was assisted by the voivode Vyshata and the bishop Luka Zhidiata. In 1042, Vladimir may have been in conflict with Finns, according to some interpretations even making a military campaign in Finland. In the
2 CONC  next year he led the Russian armies together with Harald III of Norway against the Byzantine emperor Constantine IX. He predeceased his father by two years and was buried by him in St Sophia Cathedral he had built in Novgorod. Hi
2 CONC s sarcophagus is in a niche on the south side of the main body of the cathedral overlooking the Martirievskii Porch. He is depicted in an early twentieth century fresco above the sarcophagus and on a new ephigial icon on top of th
2 CONC e sarcophagus. The details of his death is unknown, however his son Rostislav and his descendants were in unfriendly relationship with the descendants of the Yaroslaviches triumvirate (Iziaslav, Sviatoslav, and Vsevolod). Three of
2 CONC  Vladimir's younger brothers Izyaslav I, Svyatoslav II and Vsevolod I all reigned in Kiev, while other two (Igor and Vyacheslav) died in their early twenties after which their lands were split between the Yaroslaviches triumvirate
2 CONC . Coincidentally, the Vyshata of Novgorod pledged his support to Rostislav in the struggle against the triumvirate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir's only son, Rostislav Vladimirovich, was a landless prince who usurped power in Tmutarakan. His descendants were dispossessed by their uncles and were proclaimed as izgoi (outcast), but gradually managed to establish them
2 CONC selves in Halychyna, ruling the land until 1199, when their line went extinct. In order to downplay their claims to Kiev, the records of Vladimir's military campaigns seem to have been obliterated from Kievan chronicles. As a resu
2 CONC lt, medieval historians often confuse him with two more famous namesakes — Vladimir the Great and Vladimir Monomakh. The name of Vladimir's consort is uncertain either. According to Nikolai Baumgarten Vladimir was married to the d
2 CONC aughter of count Loepold of Staden, Ode. Others (Aleksandr Nazarenko) disregard that assumption or claim a different person. Vladimir's memory was better preserved in foreign sources. In Norse sagas he frequently figures as Valdem
2 CONC ar Holti (that is, "the Nimble"). George Cedrenus noticed Vladimir's arrogance in dealing with the Byzantines.pp
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Vladimir Yaroslavich / Владимир Ярославич
2 CONT
2 CONT (1020 – October 4, 1052)
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Novgorod from 1036 until his death 1052. He was the eldest son of Yaroslav I the Wise of Kiev by Ingegerd Olofsdotter also known as Anna of Novgorord, daughter of king Olaf Skötkonung of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Vladimir of Novgorod:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_of_Novgorod
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir Yaroslavich (Russian: Владимир Ярославич) (1020 – October 4, 1052) reigned as prince of Novgorod from 1036 until his death. He was the eldest son of Yaroslav I the Wise of Kiev by Ingigerd, daughter of king Olaf Skötkonun
2 CONC g of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1042, Vladimir may have been in conflict with Finns, according to some interpretations even making a military campaign in Finland.[1] In the next year he led the Russian armies against the Byzantine emperor Constantine IX.
2 CONT
2 CONT He predeceased his father by two years and was buried by him in St Sophia Cathedral he had built in Novgorod. His younger brothers Izyaslav I, Svyatoslav II and Vsevolod I all reigned in Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir's only son, Rostislav Vladimirovich, was a landless prince who usurped power in Tmutarakan. His descendants were dispossessed by their uncles but gradually managed to establish themselves in Halychina, ruling the land unt
2 CONC il 1199, when their line went extinct.
2 CONT
2 CONT In order to downplay their claims to Kiev, the records of Vladimir's military campaigns seem to have been obliterated from Kievan chronicles. As a result, medieval historians often confuse him with two more famous namesakes — Vlad
2 CONC imir the Great and Vladimir Monomakh.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir's memory was better preserved in foreign sources. In Norse sagas he frequently figures as Valdemar Holti (that is, "the Nimble"). George Cedrenus noticed Vladimir's arrogance in dealing with the Byzantines.
2 CONT
2 CONT Further reading
2 CONT
2 CONT Volkoff, Vladimir. Vladimir, the Russian Viking. Overlook Press, 1985.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ The first indisputable Novgorodian expedition to Finland was done in 1191. Suomen varhaiskeskiajan lähteitä. Gummerus Kirjapaino Oy, 1989. ISBN 951-96006-1-2. See also online description of the conflict from Laurentian Codex as 
2 CONC hosted by the National Archive of Finland. In Swedish.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#AnnaIaroslavnadied1075
2 CONT
2 CONT VIACHESLAV Iaroslavich ([1033/36]-Smolensk [1055/57]).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Vyacheslav son of Vladimir in [1034/36][173].
2 CONT
2 CONT In accordance with the terms of his father's testament, he succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Smolensk.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of Vyacheslav son of Yaroslav at Smolensk in [1056/57][174].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---.  The name of Viacheslav´s wife is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Viacheslav & his wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Boris Viacheslavich (b. c.1056, d. 3 October 1078 at the Battle of Nezhatina Meadows, Prince of Chernigov in 1077, Prince of Tmutorokan 1077-1078)
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Russian Wikipedia page on Vyacheslav Yaroslavich:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%92%D1%8F%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2_%D0%AF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87
2 CONT
2 CONT In English:
2 CONT
2 CONT Vyacheslav Yaroslavich (1036-1057) was Prince of Smolensk (1054-1057), and one of the younger sons of Yaroslav the Wise and Ingegerd.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born probably in Kiev, where he lived until adulthood. During his life as a young father, Vyacheslav was married to Audet, daughter of Count Leopold Shtadenskiy. In 1054, after the death of Yaroslav, he received from his ol
2 CONC der brother Iziyaslav the Smolensk Principality. Three years later, however, Vyacheslav died, leaving the principality to his infant son, Boris.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#AnnaIaroslavnadied1075
2 CONT
2 CONT VLADIMIR Iaroslavich (1020-Novgorod 1052, bur Novgorod Church of St Sophia).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Vladimir son of Iaroslav in 1020[154].  Snorre names "Valdemar, Vissivald and Holte the Bold" as the children of "King Jarisleif" & his wife[155].
2 CONT
2 CONT His father installed him in 1043 as Prince of Novgorod.
2 CONT
2 CONT He subjugated the Finnic tribe of Yam to secure Novgorod's control over the Gulf of Finland[156].
2 CONT
2 CONT Psellos records that Vladimir led an attack on Constantinople in [1045], apparently in revenge for the death of some Scythian noble in a brawl, but was defeated and forced to retreat[157].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of Vladimir "Yaroslav's eldest son" at Novgorod in 1052 and his place of burial[158].  As he predeceased his father, his descendants were ignored in the latter's testament and were subsequen
2 CONC tly considered ineligible to succeed as Grand Princes of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT m --- ([1022]-1066).  The name of Vladimir´s wife is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baumgarten identifies the wife of Vladimir as Oda, daughter of Luitpold Graf von Stade & his wife Ida von Elstorf [Brunswick][159].  This is based partially on the Annales Stadenses which name "Odam sanctimonialem de Rinthelen" as
2 CONC  daughter of "Lippoldo filio domiine Glismodis" and "Ida [de Elsthorpe]", specifying that she married "regi Ruzie" by whom she had a son "Warteslaw", returned to Saxony with her son after her husband died[160].
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir & his wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT a) ROSTISLAV Vladimirovich ([1045]-Tmutorakan 3 Feb 1067, bur Tmutorakan Church of the Holy Virgin).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Rostislav, son of Vladimir and grandson of Yaroslav, recording that in 1064 he fled to Tmutorakan from where he expelled Gleb and occupied the throne himself, only to be expelled the following year by h
2 CONC is uncle Svyatoslav who re-established his son Gleb, whom Rostislav expelled again[161].
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Rostov, Novgorod and Volynia 1056-1064.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Tmutorokan 1064-1065.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records that Rostislav was poisoned by the Greeks 3 Feb 1067 at Tmutorakan and his place of burial[162].
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page on Vladimir of Novgorod:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_of_Novgorod
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir Yaroslavich (Russian: Владимир Ярославич) (1020 – October 4, 1052) reigned as prince of Novgorod from 1036 until his death. He was the eldest son of Yaroslav I the Wise of Kiev by Ingigerd, daughter of king Olof Skötkonun
2 CONC g of Sweden.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the state affairs he was assisted by the voivode Vyshata and the bishop Luka Zhidiata.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1042, Vladimir may have been in conflict with Finns, according to some interpretations even making a military campaign in Finland.[2] In the next year he led the Russian armies together with Harald III of Norway against the Byz
2 CONC antine emperor Constantine IX.
2 CONT
2 CONT He predeceased his father by two years and was buried by him in St Sophia Cathedral he had built in Novgorod. His sarcophagus is in a niche on the south side of the main body of the cathedral overlooking the Martirievskii Porch. H
2 CONC e is depicted in an early twentieth century fresco above the sarcophagus and on a new ephigial icon on top of the sarcophagus.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The details of his death is unknown, however his son Rostislav and his descendants were in unfriendly relationship with the descendants of the Yaroslaviches triumvirate (Iziaslav, Sviatoslav, and Vsevolod). Three of Vladimir's you
2 CONC nger brothers Izyaslav I, Svyatoslav II and Vsevolod I all reigned in Kiev, while other two (Igor and Vyacheslav) died in their early twenties after which their lands were split between the Yaroslaviches triumvirate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coincidentally, the Vyshata of Novgorod pledged his support to Rostislav in the struggle against the triumvirate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir's only son, Rostislav Vladimirovich, was a landless prince who usurped power in Tmutarakan. His descendants were dispossessed by their uncles and were proclaimed as izgoi (outcast), but gradually managed to establish them
2 CONC selves in Halychyna, ruling the land until 1199, when their line went extinct.
2 CONT
2 CONT In order to downplay their claims to Kiev, the records of Vladimir's military campaigns seem to have been obliterated from Kievan chronicles. As a result, medieval historians often confuse him with two more famous namesakes — Vlad
2 CONC imir the Great and Vladimir Monomakh.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Vladimir's consort is uncertain either. According to Nikolai Baumgarten Vladimir was married to the daughter of count Loepold of Staden, Ode. Others (Aleksandr Nazarenko) disregard that assumption or claim a different 
2 CONC person.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir's memory was better preserved in foreign sources. In Norse sagas he frequently figures as Valdemar Holti (that is, "the Nimble"). George Cedrenus noticed Vladimir's arrogance in dealing with the Byzantines.
2 CONT
2 CONT Further reading
2 CONT
