2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#_Toc198014254
2 CONT
2 CONT ANASTASIA Iaroslavna ([1023]-[1074/1096], bur Admont Abbey).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Baumgarten names the second wife of King András and gives her origin but only cites one secondary source in support[163].  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.   
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband's death in 1060, she and her sons took refuge at the court of Heinrich IV King of Germany but, leaving her elder son there, she and her younger son went to Austria[164].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Annals of Lambert record that "regina Ungariorum, mater Salomonis regis" presented the sword of "rex Hunnorum Attila" to "duci Baioriorum Ottoni" after her son was restored as king of Hungary[165].  
2 CONT
2 CONT She became a nun at Admont in 1074 as AGMUNDA.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([1039]) as his second wife, ANDRÁS of Hungary, son of VÁSZOLY [Vazúl] Prince of Hungary Duke between March and Gran & --- of the Bulgarians ([1014]-Zirc autumn 1060, bur Tihany, Abbey of St Anian).  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded in 1047 as ANDRÁS I "the Catholic" King of Hungary.  
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page on Anastasia of Kiev:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anastasia_of_Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia of Kiev (c. 1023 – 1074/1096) was Queen of Hungary as the wife of King Andrew I the White. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Hungarian Wikipedia says that she was born in Kiev in 1023, and died in Admont, Styria, Austria, in 1096.)
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the eldest daughter of Grand Prince Yaroslav I the Wise of Kiev and Ingigerd of Sweden, and the older sister of Anne of Kiev, Queen consort of Henry I of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1039 (Hungarian Wikipedia says 1037-1038), Anastasia was married to Duke Andrew of Hungary, who had settled down in Kiev after his father Vazul took part in a failed assassination attempt aimed at King Stephen I of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1046, her husband returned to Hungary and ascended the throne as King Andrew I after defeating King Peter I Orseolo. Anastasia followed her husband to the kingdom. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It was probably she who persuaded her husband to set up a lavra in Tihany for hermits who had come to Hungary from the Kievan Rus'. (Hungarian Wikipedia says that she lived on the Tihany peninsula on Lake Balaton. She also founded
2 CONC  the Bihari Tormova monastery for Russian nuns.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The royal couple did not have a son till 1053, when Anastasia gave birth to Solomon. However, Solomon's birth and later coronation caused an embittered conflict between King Andrew I and his younger brother Duke Béla, who had been
2 CONC  the heir to the throne til the child's birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Duke Béla rose in open rebellion against King Andrew in 1060, the king sent his wife and children to the court of Adalbert, Margrave of Austria. King Andrew was defeated and died shortly afterwards, and his brother was crowne
2 CONC d King of Hungary on 6 December 1060.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia sought the help of King Henry IV of Germany (future Holy Roman Emperor), whose sister, Judith had been engaged to Solomon when he was a child in 1058. By the time Imperial troops entered Hungary to give assistance to Sol
2 CONC omon against his uncle, King Béla I died on 11 September 1063 and his sons, Géza, Ladislaus, and Lampert fled to Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The young Solomon was crowned around 27 September 1063. On the occasion of her son's coronation, Anastasia presented the alleged sword of Attila the Hun to Duke Otto II of Bavaria who was the leader of the Imperial troops. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1060 and 1073 King Solomon governed his kingdom in collaboration with his cousins, Dukes Géza, Ladislaus, and Lampert who had returned to Hungary and accepted his rule. However, in 1074 the three brothers rebelled against 
2 CONC their cousin, and defeated him on 14 March 1074. King Solomon fled to the Western borders of Hungary where he maintained his rule only over the counties of Moson and Pozsony (Slovak: Bratislava).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia followed her son, but they began to argue with each other and she moved to Admont Abbey (in 1074) where she lived as a nun till her death (in 1096). She was buried in the Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Hungarian Wikipedia says that the Thuroczy Chronicle recorded where Anastasia admonished her son: "My dear boy! Several others did not listen to me either, nor supported my advice. On the contrary, they were silent. What happened
2 CONC  to you? Rally yourself and your men. Do not say that you have had enough of the Hungarian crown, that in order to live in peace you must give up part of your kingdom. We can no longer watch, Prince, while you cannot keep your pow
2 CONC er." Solomon almost hit his mother after she said these words, and only Judith, his wife, prevented him. Anastasia never resolved the conflict between Bela and Solomon.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT King Andrew I of Hungary (c. 1015 – before 6 December 1060):
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Adelaide (c. 1040 – 27 January 1062), wife of king Vratislaus II of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. King Solomon of Hungary (1053 – 1087 or after)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. David of Hungary (after 1053 – after 1094)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Kristó, Gyula - Makk, Ferenc: Az Árpád-ház uralkodói (IPC Könyvek, 1996)
2 CONT
2 CONT Korai Magyar Történeti Lexikon (9-14. század), főszerkesztő: Kristó, Gyula, szerkesztők: Engel, Pál és Makk, Ferenc (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1994)
2 CONT
2 CONT Magyarország Történeti Kronológiája I. – A kezdetektől 1526-ig, főszerkesztő: Benda, Kálmán (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1981)
2 CONT
