2 CONT [205] Russian Primary Chronicle (1973), 1102, pp. 199 and 200.
2 CONT
2 CONT [206] Annalista Saxo 1062.
2 CONT
2 CONT [207] Russian Primary Chronicle (1973), 1103, p. 200, and 1104, p. 202.
2 CONT
2 CONT [208] Russian Primary Chronicle (1973), 1069, p. 150.
2 CONT
2 CONT [209] Annales Capituli Cracoviensis 1088, MGH SS XIX, p. 588.
2 CONT
2 CONT [210] Baumgarten (1927), p. 11, citing Sommersberg, Silesiacarum Rerum Scriptores T. I, p. 300, Dlugosz Lib. IV, p. 312, and Monum. Polon. II 773, 796 and 874.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Dynastic Burials in Kiev before 1240" by Martin Dimnik
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.history.org.ua/JournALL/ruthenica/7/5.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years later, on 3 October 1078, Izyaslav was killed in battle fighting against Svyatoslav’s son Oleg and the Polovtsy. His body was brought to Kiev and laid to rest in the Tithe Church [35].
2 CONT
2 CONT Izyaslav was the first of Yaroslav’s descendants to die in battle and whose body was brought from the field for burial in a church. In retrieving his brother’s body Vsevolod adopted the practice initiated with the burials of SS. B
2 CONC oris and Gleb whose bodies had also been collected from the spots where they had been murdered.
2 CONT
2 CONT Izyaslav was not entombed in the Cathedral of St. Sophia that his father Yaroslav had built. As we shall see, Vsevolod, who succeeded Izyaslav to the throne of Kiev and was responsible for his burial, was probably reserving that h
2 CONC onour for himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT Even more surprisingly, however, Vsevolod did not inter Izyaslav in the Church of St. Dmitry, in the monastery that he had founded in his district of Kiev [36]. It has been suggested that Izyaslav’s Church of St. Dmitry was built 
2 CONC of wood [37]. If this was the case it may explain why Vsevolod did not bury him there but in the Tithe Church which, being built of stone, was a more lasting structure. What is more, since it contained the remains of their grandfa
2 CONC ther Vladimir the Christianizer of Rus’, it was also the more prestigious edifice.
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT 35 PSRL 1: 201–202; PSRL 2: 193.
2 CONT
2 CONT 36 See under the year 1051, PSRL 1: 159; PSRL 2: 147. Karger points out that the Monastery of St. Dmitry was built by 1062 when Izyaslav appointed Abbot Varlaam to the institution (Karger, Drevniy Kiev, 2, 262). Concerning the loc
2 CONC ation of the monastery, see Tolochko, Kiev i Kievskaya zemlya, 54–55, nr. 23.
2 CONT
2 CONT 37 Aseev, Arkhitektura drevnego Kieva, 93–94.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Russian Wikipedia page (English below):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%98%D0%B7%D1%8F%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2_%D0%AF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT In English:
2 CONT
2 CONT Iziyaslav Yaroslavich (baptized as Dimitry, born in Novgorod in 1025, died October 3, 1078 in Nezhatina Niwa, near Chernihiv, Ukraine), was Grand Prince of Kiev 1054-1068, 1069-1073, and in 1077; and Prince of Novgorod in 1052-105
2 CONC 4.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Yaroslav
2 CONT
2 CONT Iziyaslav was born in 1025 in Novgorod, where at the time his father was Prince Yaroslav the Wise and his mother was Irinia (Swedish princess Ingegerd). He was their second son, after Vladimir.
2 CONT
2 CONT He received from his father a lordship in Turov (southern Belarus). After the death of his brother, Prince Vladimir of Novgorod in 1052, he succeeded him, and by dynastic rules, became heir to the throne of Kiev (even though Vladi
2 CONC mir left behind a son) on February 20, 1054. After the death of his father, he was Grand Prince of Kiev, and he left as Prince of Novgorod his son Mstislav.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Yaroslavich Triumvirate
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of the reign of Iziyaslav was characterized by equal participation in the governance of the Grand Principality alongside his younger brothers - Prince of Chernigov Svyatoslav and Prince of Pereyaslav Vsevolod. The brothers to
2 CONC gether revised the legal code "Russkaya Pravda" (renaming it the "Yaroslavichskaya Pravda"), which required the brothers to jointly fill princely seats and set up separate Metropolitans in their principalities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Historians call this the Yaroslavich Triumvirate. Together, they marched on the Torks after they raided Peryaslavl in 1055. The Torks were defeated, but the clash marked for the first time that the Rus confronted to Polovtsy Khan 
2 CONC Bolusha - they forced him to sign a peace treaty to keep him outside of the borders, installing a 50 kilometer wide no-man's land between the neutral Rus and the Polovtsians.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1057, the Rus provided Byzantium with military assistance to protect Armenia from the Turkish Seljuks.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1058, Iziyaslav conquered the Baltic tribe of the Golyad on the Protva River.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1060, they marched against the Torks, and against the wizard Vseslav, Prince of Polotsk, in 1067.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first overthrow
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1068, Iziyaslav along with his brothers suffered a defeat in the Alta region, and were overthrown in a rebellion that probably started in a popular uprising in Kiev. Insurgent leaders were freed from dungeons (a cell built arou
2 CONC nd the prisoner without a door), who were arrested earlier by Prince Iziyaslav. Vseslav of Polotsk was elevated to the throne of Kiev. Iziyaslav fled to Poland to the safety of his nephew Prince Boleslaw II. With the assistance of
2 CONC  Polish troops, he returned in 1069. He sent ahead of him his son Mstislav, who brutally suppressed the instigators of the uprising, and had those who sent Iziyaslav into exile executed or blinded.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Second Exile: Wandering through Europe
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1073 (most likely somewhat earlier), the Yaroslavich Triumvirate disintegrated, as Iziyaslav's younger brothers Svyatoslav and Vsevelod conspired against him after reconciling with Vseslav of Polotsk.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1073, Svyatoslav of Chernigov captured Kiev, and Iziyaslav again fled to Poland where this time he was expelled by Polish authorities (who concluded an alliance with Svyatoslav and Vsevolod).
2 CONT
2 CONT In exile, Iziyaslav went to Germany to the Holy Roman Emperor Henry IV and asked for help to fight against his brothers, along with a large amount of money. The Emperor, however, was facing internal struggles on his own, and could
2 CONC  not help.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1075, Iziyaslav sent his son, Duke Yaropolk of Volhynia, to Rome where he visited Pope Gregory VII, an antagonist of Emperor Henry IV. The Pope limited himself to general exhortations addressed against the Russian princes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Return and death
2 CONT
2 CONT Iziyaslav's wanderings came to an end with the sudden death of Svyatoslav on December 27, 1076, leaving behind Vsevolod as sole successor. The new Grand Prince reconciled with his older brother and gave him back the Kiev principal
2 CONC ity, while he himself went to Chernigov.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the next year, however, a civil war started. The son of Prince Svyatoslav, Oleg of Chernigov (claiming the Chernigov throne) and exiled Prince Borish Vyacheslavich, rose up against their uncles. In the battle of Nezhatina Niwa 
2 CONC near Chernigov on October 3, 1078,, the Yaroslavich coalition won - Oleg was forced to flee and Boris was killed. However, before the end of the battle, Iziyaslavich also died when an enemy rider hit him with a spear on his should
2 CONC er.
2 CONT
2 CONT Iziyaslav Yaroslavich was buried in the Sophia Cathedral in Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT It is known that Iziyaslav was married to Gertrude, daughter of Polish King Mieszko II Lambert.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Yaropolk, Prince of Volyn and Turovsky. It is known that Gertrude called Yaropolk in his prayer book (the so-called Code Gertrude) as his "only son." Nazarenko assumes that from him came the Vsevolodkovich rulers of the Princip
2 CONC ality of Hrodno.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps from another unknown mistress, perhaps wife, Iziyaslav had two more famous sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Svyatopolk II Iziyaslavich (1050-1113), Prince of Polotsk (1069-1071), Prince of Novgorod (1078-1088), Prince of Turov (1088-1093), and Grand Prince of Kiev (1093-1113). His descendants in the 12th and 13th centuries continued 
2 CONC to reign in Turov.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Mstislav, Prince of Novgorod (1054-1067).
2 CONT
