2 CONT From the Russian Wikipedia page on the Battle of Nezhatin Niva:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%91%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9D%D0%B5%D0%B6%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%9D%D0%B8%D0%B2%D0%B5
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT In English:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Nezhatin Niva was fought in the vicinity of Chernigov in 1078. Grand Prince Izyaslav Yaroslavich died in that battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1078, the sons of Yaroslav the Wise, Grand Prince of Kiev Izyaslav and Prince of Chernigov Vsevolod, faced a rebellion by their nephews, Oleg Svyatoslavovich and Boris Vyacheslavich. The nephews first captured Chernigov from Vs
2 CONC evolod, who fled to Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod turned to his brother for help. Four princes - Izyaslav, his son Yaropolk, Vsevolod, and his son Vladimir - collected an army and laid siege to Chernigov. Oleg and Boris were not in the city, but nonetheless, the city res
2 CONC isted capture. Vladimir invested the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon learning that his nephew Oleg was marching upon Chernigov, Izyaslav set out to meet him. Oleg did not expect to win against the army of the four princes, and advised his brother to join in peace talks. Boris replied: "Stay ca
2 CONC lm and watch me fight them." Boris was killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Izyaslav stood amongst his infantry as it was attacked, and an enemy rider hit him with a spear in the shoulder, inflicting a mortal wound on the Grand Prince. Oleg fled with a small number of men to Tmutarakan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle was described in "Words about Igor's Campaign" ("Слове о полку Игореве").
2 CONT
2 CONT External links:
2 CONT
2 CONT Article in the encyclopedia "Words about Igor's Campaign" on the FEB (Fundamental Electronic Library) server:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://feb-web.ru/feb/slovenc/es/es3/es3-3091.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT "Nezhatina Niva" in the Encyclopedic Dictionary of Brockhaus and Efron:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://be.sci-lib.com/article071085.html
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Encyclopedic Dictionary of Brockhaus and Efron:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://be.sci-lib.com/article071085.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Nizhatina Niva:
2 CONT
2 CONT Known as the site of a battle between the Russian Princes and the Polovtsy in 1078, during which fell Grand Prince Izyaslav Yaroslavich. It was probably on the Left Bank of the Dnieper River near Gorodets, a port from which the de
2 CONC ad Grand Prince was brought back to Kiev by boat.
2 CONT
2 CONT The city of Nezhatina was supposed to have been standing in the 12th century. The assumption that Nezhatina Neva was on the site of the present city of Nezhyn is not probable.
2 CONT
2 CONT (No sources cited)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the entry on "Words about Igor's Campaign" on the FEB (Fundamental Electronic Library) server:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://feb-web.ru/feb/slovenc/es/es3/es3-3091.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT In English:
2 CONT
2 CONT Nezhatina Niva (Fields of Nezhatin) - located in the vicinity of Chernigov. In 1078, there was a battle there between an army under Oleg Svyatoslavich and Boris Vyacheslavich on one side, and a coalition of princes into which ente
2 CONC red Izyaslav Yaroslavich Grand Prince of Kiev, his son Yaropolk, Vsevolod Yaroslavich, and lastly Vladimir Monomakh.
2 CONT
2 CONT "And the opposing sides met on a field at a village near Nezhatin, and both sides inflicted great harm on the other" - informs the PVL (Primary Chronicle, C. 133). In this fight was lost Izyaslav Yaroslavich and Borish Vyacheslavi
2 CONC ch: "The first to die was Boris, son of Vyacheslav. Izyaslav stood his ground without waivering, and took a spear in his shoulder. Thus died Izyaslav, son of Yaroslavl" (at the same location).
2 CONT
2 CONT Continuing to fight were significant forces, in the opinion of some researchers, basic participants of which included: Oleg Svyatoslavich, Boris Vyacheslavich, Vsevolod Yaroslavich, and Vladimir Monomakh, and the unnamed person wh
2 CONC o killed Izyaslav, whose body, according to texts that contradict the annals, was not carried to Kiev by Yaropolk and others, but rather by Svyatopolk Izyaslavich, his son. (Text in Slavonic or Old Russian follows.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Researchers have tried to define the location of the fight. There were attempts to correlate this with the modern location of Nezhin (Gerbel), but already Maximovich has refuted this assumption because of the distance from Chernig
2 CONC ov and the reference to Nezhin by an earlier name of Unezh.
2 CONT
2 CONT In order to define the location of N.N., it helps to understand the C. and the lavr. let. before 1152 river Kanin (see Kanin), which flows near Chernigov. Kudryashov places it between Svinya and Chernigov, 2 kilometers east of Che
2 CONC rnigov where the small river Desna runs. He considers that this geographical position is closely related to the Kanin annals: "By Nezhin, determined to be to the south of Chernigov over the Desna on a road to Nezhin, it is possibl
2 CONC e to imagine that the Nezhatin field was located between Nezhin and Kanin. (From Igor Seversky C. 55).
2 CONT
2 CONT (Ben M. Angel notes: A close inspection by Google Earth shows none of these locations as I've translated them. Nizhyn/Nezhin is located 65 kilometers southeast of Chernihiv/Chernigov. The Desna flows about 2-3 kilometers from Cher
2 CONC nihiv town center - it acts as a city border for part of its stretch. The closest towns east of Chernihiv are Enkiv, Anysiv, and Pidhirne, all located about 5-8 kilometers away.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Лит./Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT Maximovich M. A. Zamechaniya on Pech about Igor's Campaign in poetic translation Gerbelya/Moscow 1855.
