2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid (covering his married family):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#SviatoslavIaroslavichdied1076B
2 CONT
2 CONT SVIATOSLAV Iaroslavich, son of IAROSLAV I "Mudriy/the Wise" Grand Prince of Kiev & his second wife Ingigerd Olafsdottir of Sweden (1027-27 Dec 1076, bur Chernigov, Church of the Redeemer).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Svyatoslav "a third son" to Vladimir in 1027[231].  
2 CONT
2 CONT In accordance with the terms of his father's testament, he succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Chernigov, the territory including the land of the Viatichians and Radimichians and the Murom region[232].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed to succeed his brother Igor in 1060 as Prince of Smolensk.  He was forced out of Smolensk to Chernigov by the Kuman [Cumans], but defeated the latter in 1068, capturing their leader[233].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He deposed his elder brother Iziaslav in Kiev in 1073, succeeding as SVIATOSLAV II Grand Prince of Kiev, whereupon he transferred Chernigov to his younger brother Vsevolod.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of Svyatoslav son of Yaroslav 27 Dec 1076 "from the cutting of a sore" and his place of burial[234]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m [KILIKIA] von Dithmarschen, daughter of ETHELER von Dithmarschen & his wife Ida von Elsdorf [Braunschweig].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Baumgarten refers to the marriage of "[Kilikia] de Dithmarschen, fille du Comte Etheler" and Sviatoslav II Grand Prince of Kiev, adding that she was a different person from "Oda von Stade" who married Sviatoslav´s brother Vladimir
2 CONC  (see above)[235].  This marriage is also discussed by Bloch[236].  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Sviatoslav II & his wife had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. VIZESLAVA Sviatoslavna.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Baumgarten names the wife of Bolesław II and gives her origin, citing primary sources in support[237].   If this is correct, she must have been one of the older children in view of the date of birth of her son.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (before 1069) BOLESŁAW II "Szczodry/the Liberal" Prince of Poland, son of KAZIMIERZ I KAROL "Odnowiciel/the Renewer" Prince of Poland & his wife Dobronega Maria Vladimirovna of Kiev ([1039]-1081).  King of Poland 1076. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. GLEB Sviatoslavich (-killed in battle 30 May 1078[238], bur Chernigov, Church of the Saviour).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Gleb, son of Sviatoslav, recording that he was expelled from Tmutorakan by Rostislav in 1064, restored by his father in 1065, before being expelled again by Rostislav[239].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Tmutorokan 1064-1065/69.  
2 CONT
2 CONT His father installed him as Prince of Novgorod in 1068, in succession to Mstislav Iziaslavich who had been forced out by Vseslav Prince of Polotsk, while Mstislav's father Iziaslav Grand Prince of Kiev was in exile in Poland.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He successfully defended Novgorod against a raid by the Kuman [Cumans] in Oct 1069, but was forced out in 1073, restored in 1077, but forced out again in 1078 and killed[240].  The Primary Chronicle records that Gleb, son of Sviat
2 CONC oslav, was killed in "Zavaloche"[241]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. ROMAN Sviatoslavich "the Red" (-killed 2 Aug 1079).  Baumgarten names him and cites a primary source in support[242].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Murom.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Tmutorokan 1069-1079.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed on the orders of his uncle Vsevolod Grand Prince of Kiev while advancing to take Pereyaslavl with a force of Kuman [Cumans][243].
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. DAVID Sviatoslavich (-Jan 1123).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names David, son of Svyatoslav, recording that he left Novgorod for Smolensk "toward the close of the year" 1095[244].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Novgorod.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was installed as Prince of Smolensk 1094[245].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the family accord at Liubech 1097, he became Prince of Chernigov.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m FEODOSIA, daughter of ---.  Baumgarten records her marriage and cites a primary source in support[246].  
2 CONT
2 CONT David & his wife had five children.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. OLEG MIKHAIL Sviatoslavich "Gorislavich" ([1058]-1 Aug 1115[258]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Oleg, son of Sviatoslav, recording that he and Vladimir, son of Vsevolod, fought the Czechs with the Poles in 1076[259].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Volynia [1073].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He claimed Chernigov in 1076 on the death of his father, but was expelled in Apr 1078 by his uncles Iziaslav and Vsevolod to Tmutorokan.  He returned with support from the Kuman [Cumans] to take Chernigov by force and defeated his
2 CONC  uncle Vsevolod on the river Sozh, east of Pereyaslavl, 25 Aug 1078[260].  Prince Vsevolod sought support from Prince Iziaslav.  They defeated Oleg 3 Oct 1078 at Nezhatin Meadow near Chernigov, where Prince Iziaslav was killed.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Oleg was kidnapped from Tmutorokan on the orders of his uncle Vsevolod Grand Prince of Kiev and taken to Constantinople (from where he went to Rhodos where he spent two years and married his first wife), being replaced by Vsevolod
2 CONC 's agent Ratibor, who in turn was expelled by Oleg's cousins David Igorevich and Volodar Rostislavich, before returning himself to take control of Tmutorokan in [1083].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He captured Chernigov in 1094, after expelling Prince Vladimir "Monomakh".  He was expelled in 1095 by Princes Vladimir and Sviatopolk, but waged continual war against them 1096/97[261].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He installed himself as Prince of Murom and Suzdal in 1096, but was obliged to withdraw by the armed forces of Mstislav Vladimirovich Prince of Novgorod (later Mstislav II Grand Prince of Kiev)[262].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the terms of the family accord at Liubech in 1097, he became Prince of Novgorod-Severskiy and Riazan.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (Rhodos [1081/83]) THEOPHANO Musalonissa, from Rhodos.  Baumgarten records this marriage, citing one secondary source in support[263].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly --- Kuman princess, daughter of OSSOLUR Asaduk Khan of Kumans.  Baumgarten records this marriage and cites a primary source in support[264].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Oleg & his first wife had three children.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.  IAROSLAV Sviatoslavich (-1130).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Iaroslav brother of Oleg, recording that the latter sent him as an outpost while he was attempting to seize Novgorod[271].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Chernigov 1123/27.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Riazan and Murom 1127/29.
2 CONT
