1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid (covering her birth family):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#AnnaIaroslavnadied1075
2 CONT
2 CONT IAROSLAV Vladimirovich, son of VLADIMIR I "Velikiy/the Great" Grand Prince of Kiev & his first wife Rognoda of Polotsk ([978]-Vyshgorod 20 Feb 1054, bur Kiev Church of St Sophia).
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Rostov 988-1010.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Novgorod 1013-1015.
2 CONT
2 CONT He rebelled against his father and refused to pay tribute from Novgorod in 1014. Challenged by his cousin Sviatopolk I Grand Prince of Kiev, following the latter's accession in Kiev, he defeated Sviatopolk at Liubech [1016] and fo
2 CONC rced him to flee to Poland, succeeding as IAROSLAV I "Mudriy/the Wise" Grand Prince of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk returned in Jul 1018 with an army led by his father-in-law, Bolesław I King of Poland, and defeated Iaroslav at the Western Bug and forced him to retire to Novgorod. Sviatopolk entered Kiev again 14 Aug 1018 and resumed
2 CONC  his rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT After King Bolesław returned to Poland, Iaroslav advanced once more on Kiev. Sviatopolk fled south to raise another force with the Pechenegs but was finally defeated by the river Alta in 1019, when Iaroslav resumed power in Kiev[1
2 CONC 40].
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1024, his half-brother Mstislav Prince of Tmutorokan moved his headquarters north to Chernigov to challenge Iaroslav, whom he defeated at Listven. The half-brothers agreed a division of territories, Iaroslav taking the land on 
2 CONC the western bank of the Dnieper including Kiev and Novgorod.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Iaroslav was absent in Novgorod, the Pechenegs laid siege to Kiev, but were defeated after Iaroslav returned with a formidable army to relieve the siege[141].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded in the territories of his half-brother Mstislav on the latter's death in 1036, becoming sole ruler or "Autocrat of the land of the Rus"[142].
2 CONT
2 CONT His greatest project was the conquest of Constantinople but his armies were defeated in decisive battles in 1043[143].
2 CONT
2 CONT He supervised the rapid expansion of the city of Kiev, modelled on Constantinople. His law code Rus'ka Pravda also contributed to the development and consolidation of his administration especially in the area around Kiev, although
2 CONC  it appears to have had little impact in the north[144].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of Iaroslav 19 Feb 1054 at Vyshgorod aged 76 and his burial place[145]. He left a testament dividing his territories between his sons, the substance of which is recorded in the Primary Chron
2 CONC icle[146] although no original text has survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT [m firstly ---. The fact of Iaroslav´s first marriage is indicated by the chronology of his oldest son, although as noted below no primary source has yet been identified which refers to this oldest son. Nevertheless, Iaroslav´s bi
2 CONC rth date, if accurate as shown above, does suggest that an earlier marriage is likely.]
2 CONT
2 CONT m [secondly] (1019) INGIGERD Olafsdottir of Sweden, daughter of OLOF "Skotkonung" King of Sweden & his wife Estrid of the Obotriten ([1000/03]-10 Feb 1050).
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam of Bremen names "filius Iacobus et filia Ingrad" as the children of "Olaph rex Sueonum" & his wife Estred, specifying that Ingrad married "rex sanctus Gerzlef de Ruzzia"[147]. Her birth date range is estimated based on the bi
2 CONC rth of her oldest child in 1020, and her youngest known child in [1036]. Morkinskinna names “Queen Ingigerđr the daughter of King Óláfr the Swede” as wife of “King Yaroslav [of] Russia”[148]. Snorre records the betrothal of "Ingeg
2 CONC erd the king's daughter" and "King Jarisleif…from Russia"[149]. The Historia Norwegie records the marriage of "sororem Olaui Sueonensis…Margaretam" and "rex Iarezlafus de Ruscia" at her brother's instigation, after her betrothal t
2 CONC o Olav of Norway was terminated[150]. It is more probable that she was the daughter rather than sister of King Olof if it is correct that she had ten children by her husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT She is referred to as IRINA in Russian sources[151].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of "the Princess wife of Yaroslav" 10 Feb [1048/50][152].
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Iaroslav & his first wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. [ILIYA Iaroslavich (-1020). Prince of Novgorod 1015.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Iaroslav & his second wife had ten children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. VLADIMIR Iaroslavich (1020-Novgorod 1052, bur Novgorod Church of St Sophia).
2 CONT
2 CONT His father installed him in 1043 as Prince of Novgorod. He subjugated the Finnic tribe of Yam to secure Novgorod's control over the Gulf of Finland[156].
2 CONT
2 CONT m --- ([1022]-1066). The name of Vladimir´s wife is not known. Baumgarten identifies the wife of Vladimir as Oda, daughter of Luitpold Graf von Stade & his wife Ida von Elstorf [Brunswick][159].
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir & his wife had one child.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. ANASTASIA Iaroslavna ([1023]-[1074/1096], bur Admont Abbey).
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband's death in 1060, she and her sons took refuge at the court of Heinrich IV King of Germany but, leaving her elder son there, she and her younger son went to Austria[164].
2 CONT
2 CONT Became a nun at Admont in 1074 as AGMUNDA.
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([1039]) as his second wife, ANDRÁS of Hungary, son of VÁSZOLY [Vazúl] Prince of Hungary Duke between March and Gran & --- of the Bulgarians ([1014]-Zirc autumn 1060, bur Tihany, Abbey of St Anian). He succeeded in 1047 as ANDRÁ
2 CONC S I "the Catholic" King of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. IZIASLAV Iaroslavich (1024-killed in battle Nezhatinaia Niva 3 Oct 1078 bur Kiev).
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in 1054 as IZIASLAV I Grand Prince of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. IELIZAVETA Iaroslavna (-after 25 Sep 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1044) HARALD Sigurdson, son of SIGURD Syr King of Ringeringe & his wife Asta Gudbrandsdatter (1015-killed in battle Stamfordbridge 25 Sep 1066). He succeeded in 1047 as HARALD III "Hardråde" King of Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. SVIATOSLAV Iaroslavich (1027-27 Dec 1076, bur Chernigov, Church of the Saviour).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Svyatoslav "a third son" to Vladimir in 1027[170].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Chernigov, and in 1073 as SVIATOSLAV II Grand Prince of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. VSEVOLOD Iaroslavich (1030-13 Apr 1093, bur Kiev St Sofia).
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Pereyaslavl.
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in 1076 as VSEVOLOD I Grand Prince of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. VIACHESLAV Iaroslavich ([1033/36]-Smolensk [1055/57]).
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Smolensk.
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---. The name of Viacheslav´s wife is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Viacheslav & his wife had one child.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.  ANNA Iaroslavna (1036-5 Sep ([1075/78], bur Abbaye Villiers near La-Ferté-Alais).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Liber Modernorum Regum Francorum records the marriage of "filiam regis Russorum Annam" with King Henri[176].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Anna filia Georgii regis Sclavonum" as wife of King Henri[177].
2 CONT
2 CONT She was consecrated Queen Consort at Reims on her wedding day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Anna caused a scandal in France by her second marriage and was forced to leave the court, although she returned after his death in 1074[178].  The Liber Modernorum Regum Francorum records the marriage of "Anna, Henrici relic
2 CONC ta" and "Rodulfo comitis"[179].
2 CONT
2 CONT "Rodulphus…Ambianensis comes" donated property to Sainte-Marie d'Amiens, with the consent of "Simon filius meus et Gualterus, Gualteri Tyrelli natus", by charter dated 1069, subscribed by "Anna uxor eius"[180].
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (Reims 19 May 1051) as his second wife, HENRI I King of France, son of ROBERT II "le Pieux" King of France & his third wife Constance d'Arles [Provence] ([end 1009/May 1010]-Palais de Vitry-aux-Loges, forêt d’Orléans, Lo
2 CONC iret 4 Aug 1060, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([1061]) as his third wife, RAOUL III “le Grand” Comte de Valois, son of RAOUL II Comte de Valois & his wife Aliénor de Champagne (-Péronne 23 Feb or 8 Sep 1074, bur Montdidier, later transferred to Crépy-en-Valois, égl
2 CONC ise collégiale Saint-Arnoul).
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. IGOR Iaroslavich ([1036]-1060).
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Volynia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in 1057 as Prince of Smolensk.
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---. The name of Igor´s wife is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Igor & his wife had two children.
2 CONT
