2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gytha_of_Wessex updated June 2020
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gytha of Wessex''' (born c. 1053/1061 - died 1098 or 1107;[1] Old English: Gȳð) was one of several daughters of Harold Godwinson, the last Anglo-Saxon King of England, by his consort, Edyth Swannesha. Through marriage to Vladim
2 CONC ir II Monomakh Gytha became a Grand Princess consort of Kievan Rus.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the thirteenth-century chronicler Saxo Grammaticus, after the death of their father Harold Godwinson (d. 1066), Gytha and two of her brothers (probably Magnus, son of Harold Godwinson and one of Godwin and Edmund) esc
2 CONC aped to the court of their first cousin once-removed, King Sweyn Estridsson of Denmark.[3] The two brothers were treated by Sweyn with hospitality, Magnus entering into high-level service with Bolesław II the Generous while their 
2 CONC sister was married to Waldemar, King of Ruthenia, i.e. Vladimir II Monomakh, one of the most famous rulers of Kievan Rus.[4] This took place in 1069/1070 when Bolesław restored Grand Prince Iziaslav I of Kiev and Gertruda (Bolesła
2 CONC w's aunt) to power after they had been deposed. Gytha's role in Vladimir’s rule isn't documented. Vladimir explained in a book of 'Instructions' (Pouchenie) for his sons, written in the twelfth-century: “Love your wives, but grant
2 CONC  them no power over you.”[5] In his book, Vladimir also mentioned Yuri mother's recent death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gytha was the mother of Mstislav the Great, the last ruler of united Kievan Rus. In the Norse sagas, Mstislav is called Harald, after his grandfather. The patericon of St. Pantaleon Cloister in Cologne says that "Gytha the Queen" 
2 CONC (Gida regina) died as a nun on 10 March.[6] A year later Vladimir Monomakh married another woman.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children===
2 CONT
2 CONT With Vladimir, Gytha had several children, including:[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Mstislav the Great (1076–1132)
2 CONT * Izyaslav Vladimirovich, Prince of Kursk († 6 September 1096)
2 CONT * Svyatoslav Vladimirovich, Prince of Smolensk and Pereyaslav († 16 March 1114)
2 CONT * Yaropolk II of Kiev († 18 February 1139)
2 CONT * Viacheslav I of Kiev († 2 February 1154)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Legacy===
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her son Yuri Dolgorukiy she was an ancestor of all major Rurik rulers in Russian history, including Alexander Nevsky, Ivan I Kalita, Dmitry Donskoy, Ivan III the Great and Ivan IV the Terrible.
2 CONT Through her son Mstislav the Great she was an ancestor of both Philippa of Hainault and King Edward III of England, hence of all subsequent English and British monarchs. According to Russian politician and historian Vladimir Medin
2 CONC sky, Gytha was a significant influence on Monomakh's public relations: "Knyaz's English wife wasn't wasted". As a source, Medinsky quotes M. P. Akekseev's comparative analysis between Monomakh writings' and Alfred the Great's, and
2 CONC  other anonymous then contemporary Anglo-Saxon texts.[8]
2 CONT
