2 CONT
2 CONT Tancred de Hauteville was an eleventh-century Norman petty lord about whom little is known. His historical importance comes entirely from the accomplishments of his sons and later descendants. He was a minor noble near Coutances i
2 CONC n the Cotentin, but it is not even certain which of the three villages called Hauteville he held, though Hauteville-la-Guichard is most often cited. Various legends later arose about him which have no supporting contemporary evide
2 CONC nce.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had 12 sons by his two wives, and several daughters, almost all of whom left Normandy for southern Italy and acquired some prominence there.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his first wife Muriella he had five sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''William Iron-Arm, count of Apulia''' (1042-1046)  
2 CONT * '''Drogo, count of Apulia''' (1046-1051)  
2 CONT * '''Humphrey, count of Apulia''' (1051-1057)  
2 CONT * '''Geoffrey, count of Loritello'' 
2 CONT * '''Serlo (stayed in Normandy), lord of Hauteville''' (1041) 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Italian chronicler of the Norman feats in the south, Amatus of Montecassino, Tancred was a morally upright man who would not carry on a sinful relationship and so remarried, being unable also to live out his life 
2 CONC in perfect celibacy. By his second wife '''Fressenda (or Fredesenda)''' he had seven sons and at least one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Robert Guiscard, count of Apulia''' (1057), then duke of Apulia and Sicily (1059-1085) 
2 CONT * '''Mauger, count of the Capitanate''' (1056-1059) 
2 CONT * '''William (Willerm or Guillerm, Willermus in Latin), count of the Principate''' 
2 CONT * '''Aubrey''' (Alberic or Alvared, Alveredus in Latin; sometimes called Alvred or Alfred) (stayed in Normandy) 
2 CONT * '''Humbert (Hubert)''' (stayed in Normandy) 
2 CONT * '''Tancred''' (stayed in Normandy) 
2 CONT * '''Roger, count of Sicily''' from 1062 
2 CONT * '''Fressenda, who married Richard I (dead in 1078), count of Aversa and prince of Capua'''
2 CONT
