2 CONT Tancred of Hauteville (980-1041) was an eleventh-century Norman petty lord about whom little is known. His historical importance comes entirely from the accomplishments of his sons and later descendants.
2 CONT He had 12 sons by his two wives (both of them have been said to be daughters of Duke Richard I of Normandy, but no primary sources back up this claim and several daughters, almost all of whom left Normandy for southern Italy and a
2 CONC cquired some prominence there.
2 CONT By his first wife Muriella he had five sons:
2 CONT Serlo (stayed in Normandy)
2 CONT Beatrix (d. 1101), married first Armand de Mortain and second Roger
2 CONT Geoffrey, lord of Hauteville, count of Loritello (d. 1063)
2 CONT William Iron Arm, count of Apulia (d. 1046)
2 CONT Drogo, count of Apulia (d. 1051)
2 CONT Humphrey, count of Apulia (d. 1057)
2 CONT According to the Italian chronicler of the Norman feats in the south, Amatus of Montecassino, Tancred was a morally upright man who would not carry on a sinful relationship and so remarried, being unable also to live out his life 
2 CONC in perfect celibacy. By his second wife Fressenda (or Fredesenda) he had seven sons and at least one daughter:
2 CONT Robert Guiscard, count of Apulia (1057), then duke of Apulia and Sicily (d. 1085)
2 CONT Mauger, count of the Capitanate (d. 1064)
2 CONT William, count of the Principate (d. 1080)
2 CONT Aubrey (Alberic or Alvared, Alveredus in Latin; sometimes called Alvred or Alfred) (stayed in Normandy)
2 CONT Humbert (Hubert) (stayed in Normandy)
2 CONT Tancred (stayed in Normandy)
2 CONT Roger, count of Sicily from 1062 (d. 1101)
2 CONT Fressenda, who married Richard I (dead in 1078), count of Aversa and prince of Capua
