2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Bulgaria (d. after 1081), protovestiaria, was the wife of protovestiarios Andronikos Doukas and mother of Irene Doukaina.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''NOTE:  Protovestiarios: Honorific title given to high-ranking officials'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria was a daughter of Troian of Bulgaria by an unnamed Byzantine noblewoman descended from the families of Kontostephanos and Phokas. Her paternal grandparents were Ivan Vladislav of Bulgaria and his wife Marija. Her paternal un
2 CONC cles included Presian II of Bulgaria and Alusian of Bulgaria. Maria married Andronikos Doukas well before 1066. Her husband was a son of the Caesar John Doukas and Eirene Pegonitissa. He was also a nephew of Constantine X and firs
2 CONC t cousin of Michael VII. Maria was endowed with an inheritance of vast land holdings around Lake Ohrid, and her considerable income was used to support her husband's lavish lifestyle and political ambitions. Her prominent marriage
2 CONC  is another evidence to the integration of descendants of the Kometopouloi into the court nobility in Constantinople. As mother of the Empress Irene Doukaina, Maria was a woman of some influence in the early years of the reign of 
2 CONC Alexios I Komnenos. Her granddaughter Anna Komnene praises her beauty and wisdom in the Alexiad.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Bulgaria and Andronikos Doukas had five children:
2 CONT # Michael Doukas.
2 CONT # John Doukas.
2 CONT # Irene Doukaina, who married Emperor Alexios I Komnenos.
2 CONT # Anna Doukaina, who married George Palaiologos.
2 CONT # Theodora Doukaina, a nun.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT D.I. Polemis, The Doukai, London, 1968. Roderick W. Stuart, Royalty for Commoners
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Maria of Bulgaria (d. after 1081), protovestiaria, was the wife of protovestiarios Andronikos Doukas and mother of Irene Doukaina.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Life Maria was a daughter of Troian of Bulgaria by a Byzantine noblewoman, whose given name is not known, descended from the families of Kontostephanos and Phokas, and was granddaughter of Ivan Vladislav of Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria married Andronikos Doukas well before 1066. Her husband was a son of the Caesar John Doukas, a major power player in Byzantine politics of the era, and Eirene Pelagonitissa. He was also a nephew of Constantine X and first co
2 CONC usin of Michael VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria was endowed with an inheritance of vast land holdings around Lake Ohrid, and her considerable income was used to support her husband's lavish lifestyle and political ambitions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her prominent marriage is another evidence to the integration of descendants of the Kometopouloi into the court nobility in Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT As mother of the Empress Irene Doukaina, Maria was a woman of some influence in the early years of the reign of Alexios I Komnenos. Her granddaughter Anna Komnene praises her beauty and wisdom in the Alexiad. Maria's other daughte
2 CONC r, Anna, married into the powerful Palaiologos family.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family Maria of Bulgaria and Andronikos Doukas had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Doukas.  John Doukas.  Irene Doukaina, who married Emperor Alexios I Komnenos.  Anna Doukaina, who married George Palaiologos.  Theodora Doukaina, a nun. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Maria of Bulgaria (d. after 1081), protovestiaria, was the wife of protovestiarios Andronikos Doukas  and mother of Irene Doukaina .
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria was a daughter of Troian of Bulgaria by an unnamed Byzantine noblewoman descended from the families of Kontostephanos and Phokas, and was granddaughter of Ivan Vladislav of Bulgaria .
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria married Andronikos Doukas  well before 1066. Her husband was a son of the Caesar , John Doukas  a major power player in Byzantine politics of the era, and Eirene Pelagonitissa. He was also a nephew of Constantine X  and firs
2 CONC t cousin of Michael VII .
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria was endowed with an inheritance of vast land holdings around Lake Ohrid , and her considerable income was used to support her husband's lavish lifestyle and political ambitions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her prominent marriage is another evidence to the integration of descendants of the Kometopouloi  into the court nobility in Constantinople .
2 CONT
2 CONT As mother of the Empress Irene Doukaina, Maria was a woman of some influence in the early years of the reign of Alexios I Komnenos . Her granddaughter Anna Komnene  praises her beauty and wisdom in the Alexiad . Maria's other daug
2 CONC hter, Anna, married into the powerful Palaiologos family.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Bulgaria and Andronikos Doukas had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Doukas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Doukas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene Doukaina , who married Emperor Alexios I Komnenos . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Doukaina, who married George Palaiologos. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora Doukaina, a nun.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Maria of Bulgaria (d. after 1081), protovestiaria, was the wife of protovestiarios and domestikos ton scholon Andronikos Doukas and mother of Irene Doukaina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents  [hide]  1	Life 2	Family 3	References 4	External links Life[edit] Maria was a daughter of Troian of Bulgaria by an unnamed Byzantine noblewoman descended from the families of Kontostephanos and Phokas, and a granddaughter
2 CONC  of Ivan Vladislav of Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria married Andronikos Doukas well before 1066. Her husband was a son of the Caesar John Doukas, a major power player in Byzantine politics of the era, and Eirene Pelagonitissa. He was also a nephew of Constantine X and first co
2 CONC usin of Michael VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria was endowed with an inheritance of vast land holdings around Lake Ohrid, and her considerable income was used to support her husband's lavish lifestyle and political ambitions. As the last descendants of the ruling family of
2 CONC  Bulgaria, Maria and her daughters Irene and Anna, who married the first notable member of the Palaiologos family, carried not only immense wealth but also legitimisation of Byzantine authority over the Bulgarian population: her (
2 CONC and her daughters') prominent marriages are evidence for the eventual integration of the descendants of the Cometopuli dynasty into the court nobility in Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT As mother of the Empress Irene Doukaina, Maria was a woman of some influence in the early years of the reign of Alexios I Komnenos, although she, as a widow, shunned the Imperial court and chose to live in her Lake Ohrid estate. H
2 CONC er granddaughter Anna Komnene praises her beauty and wisdom in the Alexiad.
