2 CONT Curator Note, the previous version of this page cited Gaza’s name as Arpad Házi.  Árpád Házi (20 September 1908 – 13 November 1970) was a Hungarian communist politician, who served as Interior Minister between 1951 and 1952.  The 
2 CONC name Hazi has been removed.  What was probably meant was Árpád-ház, which is the Hungarian language for the House of Arpad.  Geza likely had no surname, Arpad is a reference to the House of Arpad to which he and his family belonge
2 CONC d… The Árpád dynasty consisted of the members of the royal House of Árpád (Hungarian: Árpád-ház), also known as Árpáds (Hungarian: Árpádok, Croatian: Arpadovići). They were the ruling dynasty of the Principality of Hungary in the 
2 CONC 9th and 10th centuries and of the Kingdom of Hungary from 1000 to 1301. The dynasty was named after the Hungarian Grand Prince Árpád who was the head of the Hungarian tribal federation during the conquest of the Carpathian Basin, 
2 CONC c. 895. Previously, it was referred to as the Turul dynasty or kindred.
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000221853370826&size=medium}}They bore this Coat of Arms
2 CONT …..
2 CONT '''GÉZA, son of BÉLA I King of Hungary & his wife [Ryksa] of Poland ([in Poland]''' [1044/45]-25 Apr 1077, bur Vac).  The Gesta Hungarorum names "Geichæ et Ladislai" as sons of "fratris sui Belæ" when recording that King András ob
2 CONC tained their agreement to the future succession of his son Salomon[519].  The Kronika Węgiersko-Polska names "Geyzam et Ladislaum" as the two older sons of "Bela" and his wife "rex Polonie filiam", adding that they were both born 
2 CONC in Poland[520].  He was sent as a hostage to the imperial court in [1062/63][521], at which time he must have been unmarried in line with the custom of not sending married men as hostages to foreign courts.  He sought refuge in Po
2 CONC land after his father's death in 1063, but later returned to Hungary, made peace with King Salamon, and was appointed Duke between March and Gran[522].  This must have occurred in [1064/67] if it is correct that Géza's second marr
2 CONC iage took place before 1067, as suggested below.  The Chronicon Posoniense records disputes in 1071 between "Salomon rex" and "duce magno Geyza Ungarorum"[523].  Relations deteriorated and Géza, possibly with at least financial su
2 CONC pport from Emperor Mikhael VII[524], defeated King Salamon at Mogyorod, forcing the king to withdraw to the western border and from there to Germany.  Géza succeeded his cousin in 1074 as GÉZA I King of Hungary.  The Chronicon Pos
2 CONC oniense records that "Salomon" was deposed in 1074 and "Magnus rex" crowned in 1075[525].  "Magnus qui et Geysa supremus Hungarorium Dux postea…rex consecratus, Belæ regis filius" founded the monastery of St Benedict, Gron, in the
2 CONC  presence of "Ladislao Duce germano meo…Iula Comite Palatino", by charter dated 1075[526].  The Gesta Hungarorum records that Géza succeeded King Salomon but died after a reign of three years and was buried at "Waciæ [Vác]"[527]. 
2 CONC  The Chronicon Varadiense records the death "VIII Kal Mai" in 1077 of "Geysa primogenitus Belæ regis" and his burial "in ecclesia Vaciensi quam ipse construxit"[528]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m firstly ([1062]) [SOPHIE de Looz], daughter of [EMMO Comte de Looz] & his wife [Suanehildis of Holland]''' ([1044/46]-[1065]).  She is named as the first wife of King Géza in Europäische Stammtafeln[529].  The primary source 
2 CONC on which this is based has not yet been identified.  Kerbl, in his analysis concerning Géza I's [second] Byzantine marriage, does not mention this supposed first marriage[530].  If it is correct, the marriage presumably took place
2 CONC  while Géza was a hostage at the imperial court, which Kerbl dates to [1062/63][531].  This is consistent with Sophie having been born in [1044/46].  The Vita Arnulfi names "Arnulfum comitem de Lo et Sophiam ducissam de Hungaria…e
2 CONC t ducissam de Hui" as the children of Emmo Comte de Looz, adding that Sophie was the mother of "regem de Hungaria"[532].  This manuscript, written at Oudenbourg abbey, is dated to 1220[533].  This is late to be reliable.  In addit
2 CONC ion, the document represents the ancestors of Comte Emmo in a way which is inconsistent with earlier primary sources.  As the county of Looz was among the temporal possessions of the Bishop of Liège and, as such, part of the duchy
2 CONC  of Lower Lotharingia under the suzerainty of the German emperor, it would not be improbable for a daughter of the comte de Looz to have been staying at the imperial court and for her marriage to have been arranged with another no
2 CONC ble visitor.  The Vita Andreæ, first abbot of Averboden, in the Chronicle written by Nicolas Hogeland Abbot of Middelburg, records that "Sophia de Los, Hungariæ regina, comitis Arnoldi Lossensis soror" sent letters to her brother 
2 CONC after hearing that he intended to found Averboden abbey[534].  This report is clearly anachronistic as the abbey in question was founded in 1135, when Sophie de Looz could not possibly have been queen of Hungary.  The question rem
2 CONC ains whether Sophie´s supposed marriage to King Géza I is based on speculation, suggested by an as yet unidentified secondary source which was trying to make sense of the passages in the Vita Arnulfi and the Vita Andreæ by identif
2 CONC ying the most likely Hungarian king who could have been her husband.  Until further sources come to light, it has been decided to show Sophie de Looz in square brackets.  Whatever the truth of the matter, the chronology of the bir
2 CONC ths of King Géza´s older children suggests that their mother could not have been the Byzantine wife whom he married in [1066/75]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m secondly ([1066/75]) --- Synadene, daughter of THEODULOS Synadenos & his wife --- Botaneiatissa'''.  Skylitzes records that Emperor Nikephoros Botaneiates married "sororis suæ filiam Synadenen, Theodulo Synadeno genitam" ("τή
2 CONC ν αεψιάν αυτου ο βασιλευς") the daughter of Theodoulos Synadenos ("την Συναδηνην, θυγατέρα ουσαν Θεοδουλου του Συναδηνου") to "crali Ungariæ" ("τω κράλη Ουγγρίας είς γυναικα") and that she returned to Byzantium after her husband d
2 CONC ied[535].  The passage does not name the Hungarian king in question.  Kerbl says that Horvát suggested that her husband was Lambert, son of Béla I King of Hungary[536], although it is unclear how Lambert could have been described 
2 CONC as "krali" of Hungary as no other record has been identified indicating that he ever reigned as king.  Kerbl also cites Wertner as the first source which proposed that her husband was Géza of Hungary[537].  The narrative of Skylit
2 CONC zes Continuatus ends during the reign of Emperor Nikephoros (who reigned from 1078 until his forced abdication in 1081).  This suggests that the husband of --- Synadene must have died before that date, which supports his identific
2 CONC ation as King Géza.  However, it is not impossible that the text was written some years later, and that her return to Constantinople was mentioned because it was of recent date at the time of writing.  Nevertheless, it is unlikely
2 CONC  that her husband was King László I (who appears to be the only other realistic candidate) as his marriage to Adelheid von Rheinfelden appears to be certain as discussed above.  The remaining potential difficulty is with the date 
2 CONC of the marriage.  Wertner suggested that the marriage took place in [Oct 1073/Oct 1074][538].  Nikephoros Botaneiates (later Emperor Nikephoros III) was Byzantine military commander along the Danube, adjacent to Hungarian territor
2 CONC y, from 1064 to before 1067 when he was reassigned as governor of Antioch[539].  Kerbl therefore assesses this as the more likely period during which the marriage took place[540].  However, if it is correct, as stated by Skylitzes
2 CONC , that --- Synadene returned to Byzantium after her husband's death, it is probable that she had no surviving children.  If she had had children, it is reasonable to expect that she would have remained with them to protect their i
2 CONC nterests, especially as the chronology suggests that King Géza's son Kálmán could not in any case have been her son and would therefore have had a superior claim to the throne than any half-brothers.  If this is correct, all of Ki
2 CONC ng Géza's children must have been born from his first marriage, which would date his second marriage to --- Synadene to the early 1070s at the earliest. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Géza & his first wife had two children:'''
2 CONT *1.         '''daughter ([1064]-)'''.  She and her son are shown in Europäische Stammtafeln[541], but the primary source on which this is based has not been identified.  Assuming that her brother Kálmán was born in 1065, this daug
2 CONC hter must have been the older child as their mother died in [1065], the same year in which their father remarried.  The other possibility is that they were twins, which appear to have run in the family: King Kálmán's first cousin,
2 CONC  Empress Piroska [Eirene] gave birth to at least one set of twins.  m ---, from Hungary.  One child:
2 CONT **a)         IVAN (-beheaded [1120/25]).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.   Pretender to the throne of Hungary 1120/25.  The Chronica Ungarorum records that "Bors et Juan" claimed the t
2 CONC hrone, but that "Juan" was beheaded and "Bors" sent back to Greece, dated from the context to the lifetime of István II King of Hungary[542]. 
2 CONT *2.         '''KÁLMÁN ([1065]-3 Mar 1116, bur Székesfehérvár)'''.  The Gesta Hungarorum names "filius Geichæ regis, Kalomannus" when recording that he succeeded King László I[543].  Kerbl states that Kálmán was the son of Géza I's
2 CONC  Byzantine wife, although as noted above he does not mention Géza's supposed first marriage[544]. On the death of his father in 1077, he was passed over in the succession by his uncle King László who intended Kálmán for the church
2 CONC , possibly appointing him as Bishop of Eger in northern Hungary[545].  Subsequently László I designated Kálmán's younger brother Álmos as his successor, but Kálmán seized the throne in 1095 when the king died[546], possibly with h
2 CONC elp from Poland[547], succeeding as KÁLMÁN "Könyves/the Bookman" King of Hungary.  The Chronicon Posoniense records that "Colomannus et frater eius Almus" succeeded after the death of "Ladislaus rex" in 1097, "Colomannus rex" bein
2 CONC g crowned and "frater eius Almus" receiving "diadema" in 1098[548].  According to Lázár, he was "dishevelled, hirsute, half-blind, hunchbacked and lame"[549].  Albert of Aix records "domno Kalomanno rege…Ungarorum" welcoming the f
2 CONC irst crusaders led by Pierre l´Hermite during their passage through Hungary in 1096[550].  During the course of this journey, the travellers occupied the castle of Zimony [Zemun][551].  King Kálmán recaptured Pannonian Croatia in 
2 CONC 1096, killed Peter King of Croatia on the Gvozd mountain, and occupied Beograd in 1097[552].  The Gesta Hungarorum records that the forces of King Kálmán killed "in regnum Dalmatiæ…regem Petrum in montibus…Gozd"[553].  The Histori
2 CONC a Salonitanorum of Thomas Archdeacon of Split records that Kálmán King of Hungary annexed the remaining parts of Croatia in 1102/03[554].  A charter dated 1102 records that "Colomannus…Rex Ungariæ, Croatiæ atque Dalmatiæ…postquam 
2 CONC coronatus fui Belgradi super mare" donated property to "Monasterio S. Mariæ Monialium…in Iadera", signed by "Isaac comes, Amec comes, Thomas comes, Andreas comes, Cosmas comes, Bocan comes, Dyonisius comes"[555].  By 1107, he had 
2 CONC also taken possession of all the Byzantine-controlled towns in Dalmatia, accepted by Byzantium in a complex series of agreements which included the marriage in [1104/05] of his (supposed) cousin Piroska to Ioannes Komnenos, heir t
2 CONC o the Byzantine throne[556].  The Chronicon Posoniense records that "Colomannus rex" accepted "civitatem Zader" in 1108[557].  He was elected King of Croatia and Dalmatia, which was to remain a separate kingdom from Hungary[558], 
2 CONC but he and his successors appointed Bans (usually from among the members of the Croatian nobility) who took charge of Croatian internal affairs.  The Gesta Hungarorum records that King Kálmán sent a Hungarian army to Apulia where 
2 CONC it captured the cities of Monopoli and Brindisi for the Venetians and raided the plain of Apulia[559].  In order to secure the succession for his son, he blinded his brother Álmos and the latter's son Béla[560].  He enacted a law 
2 CONC forbidding the trial of witches[561].  The Gesta Hungarorum records that "Qunwes [Könyves]…Kalman" reigned for eighteen years and was buried at Székesfehérvár[562].  The Chronicon Varadiense records the death "III Non Feb" in 1113
2 CONC  of "Colomannus rex" and his burial "Albæ"[563].  The Annales Gradicenses record the death in 1116 of "Colomannus rex"[564].  The Chronicon Posoniense records the death in 1117 of "Colomannus rex"[565].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m firstly (1097) [FELICIA] of Sicily, daughter of ROGER I Count of Sicily & his second wife Eremburge de Mortain [Normandy]''' ([1078]-[1102]).  Malaterra records the marriage in 1097 of "Colomannus…rex Ungarorum" and "comitis 
2 CONC Rogerii…filiam suam" but does not name her[566].  Given that she gave birth to four known children before her death, it is unlikely that she was born much later than [1078], in which case she would have been the oldest child by Co
2 CONC unt Roger's second marriage.  Her marriage was arranged, with the help of Pope Urban II, to seal King Kálmán's alliance with the Normans of Sicily against Byzantium.  King Kálmán's wife is named Felicia in Europäische Stammtafeln[
2 CONC 567], but the primary source on which this is based has not been identified.  She is sometimes referred to as "Buzilla", but according to Kerbl this is simply a corruption of the French "pucelle"[568]. According to Houben, her nam
2 CONC e is unknown[569].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m secondly (1104, repudiated 1113) IEVFEMIA Vladimirovna of Kiev, daughter of VLADIMIR II Vsevolodich "Monomach" Prince of Pereiaslavl [later Grand Prince of Kiev]''' & his second wife --- (-4 Apr 1139).  Baumgarten names the s
2 CONC econd wife of King Kálmán and gives her origin but only cites one secondary source in support[570].  She was repudiated by her husband and sent back to Suzdal before giving birth to her son[571].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Kálmán & his first wife had four children:''' 
2 CONT *a)         '''ZSÓFIA''' .  Her parentage and marriage are confirmed by the Chronica Ungarorum which records that "rex Stephanus" appointed as his heir "filium sororis sue…Saul"[572].  The primary source which confirms her name ha
2 CONC s not yet been identified.   m SAUL Count of Bihar, son of --- (-after 1118). 
2 CONT *b)         '''daughter'''.  Baumgarten refers to the wife of Prince Vladimirko as the daughter of King Kálmán but only cites one secondary source in support[573].  m ([1117]) VLADIMIRKO Volodarovich, son of VOLODAR Rostislavich P
2 CONC rince of Peremysl & his wife [Anna] von Pommern (-1153).  He became the first Prince of Galich in 1144. 
2 CONT *c)         '''LÁSZLÓ''' (1101-1112).  He is named in Europäische Stammtafeln[574], but the primary source on which this is based has not been identified. 
2 CONT *d)         '''ISTVÁN''' (1101-1 Mar 1131, bur Oradea).  The Chronicon Varadiense records that "Stephanus…secundus filius Colomanni" succeeded his father as king[575].  The Chronicle of Otto of Freising names "rege Stephano, Colom
2 CONC anni filio" when recording his death[576].  He was named as heir to the throne in 1105 and succeeded his father in 1116 as ISTVÁN II King of Hungary and Croatia.  The Chronicon Posoniense records the death in 1117 of "Colomannus r
2 CONC ex" and the succession of "filius eius Stephanus"[577].  "Stephanus…Hungariæ, Dalmatiæ, Croatiæ, Gallitiæ, Bulgariæque…rex" confirmed the donation to "monasterii S. Benedicti de Gron" by "Geyzæ regis avi nostri" by charter dated 1
2 CONC 124, which names "Ottonem Borsuensem comitem" and quotes the original charter of King Géza dated 1075 which was witnessed by "Ladislao duce germano meo…Iula comite meo palatino"[578].  He launched two attacks on Byzantine territor
2 CONC y, firstly against Beograd in 1125 and secondly against Braničevo/Barancs in 1126, but was obliged to withdraw by a Byzantine counter-attack[579].  The Chronicon Posoniense records that "Stephanus rex" captured "civitatem Nis" in 
2 CONC 1127[580].  A group of Pechenegs fleeing from Kuman suzerainty sought refuge at the court of King István in [1122][581].  He named his nephew Saul as his successor[582], but in 1130 recognised his first cousin Béla as his heir[583
2 CONC ].  The Chronicon Dubnicense records that "Stephanus" died in 1131 and was buried "Varadini"[584].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m firstly [CRISTIANA] di Capua, daughter of ROBERTO I Prince of Capua & his wife''' --- (-before 1121).  She is shown as the first wife of King István in Europäische Stammtafeln[585], but the primary source on which this is bas
2 CONC ed has not been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m secondly (1121) ADELHEID von Riedenburg, daughter of STEFAN von Riedenburg Burggraf von Regensburg & his wife''' ---.  She is shown as the second wife of King István in Europäische Stammtafeln[586], but the primary source on 
2 CONC which this is based has not been identified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Kálmán & his second wife had one child:'''
2 CONT *e)         '''BORS KONRAD''' (Suzdal 1113-killed in battle [1155/56]).  The Chronicle of Otto of Freising names "Boricius, qui et ipse Colomanni sed ex alia quam Stephanus matre, Rutenorum seu Chyos Regis filia" when recording th
2 CONC at he claimed the succession after the death of his half-brother King István II[587].  Kreibl cites other contemporary sources which do not refer to this alleged illegitimacy and points out that, if his legitimacy had really been 
2 CONC in doubt, it is unlikely that both Emperor Heinrich III and Heinrich "Jasomirgott" Duke of Austria would have supported his candidature for the Hungarian throne[588].  Panhypersébastos of Byzantium.  The Chronica Ungarorum records
2 CONC  that "Bors et Juan" claimed the throne, but that "Juan" was beheaded and "Bors" sent back to Greece, dated from the context to the lifetime of István II King of Hungary[589].  He gained support from Bolesław III Duke of Poland [1
2 CONC 132/35], who installed him as Lord of Zips [Spiš][590].  After he failed to secure the throne, he returned to Constantinople but received no support as Hungarian/Byzantine relations had by then improved.  The Chronica Ungarorum re
2 CONC cords that "Borith" rebelled during the reign of King Béla II, claiming to be "filium Colomanni", was supported by "magnum exercitu Ruthenorum et Polonorum" who later abandoned him "knowing his cause to be unjust", and was killed 
2 CONC by his own people when fleeing[591].  In 1146, he received the support of Emperor Heinrich III and Heinrich "Jasomirgott" Duke of Austria but his forces were again defeated 11 Sep 1146 between the rivers Fischa and Leitha[592].  O
2 CONC don de Deuil records that "Boris qui réclamait ses droits héréditaires sur [le] royaume [de Hongrie]" had sent letters to the French king at Etampes, setting out his claim, hid among the crusading army to cross Hungary in 1147 and
2 CONC  that, when found, Louis VII King of France refused to hand him over to the king of Hungary[593].  He was killed fighting the Kumans[594].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m (before 8 Apr 1143) ANNA Dukaina''', daughter of ---.  The Chronicle of Otto of Freising records that Bors, while in Greece, married "consanguineam sibi imperatoris Kaloioannis" but does not specify her name or her precise pa
2 CONC rentage[595].  Odon de Deuil records that "Boris qui réclamait ses droits héréditaires sur [le] royaume [de Hongrie]" had married "l´empereur de Constantinople…une nièce"[596].  According to Sturdza[597], she was the daughter of K
2 CONC onstantinos Dukas sébastos, son of Mikhael Dukas protostator and sébastos, but the basis for this is not known.  According to Kerbl[598], the marriage likely took place before the death of Emperor Ioannes II with whom Boris enjoye
2 CONC d good relations.  She became a nun as ARETE.  Bors & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT *i)          KÁLMÁN ([1137/1145]-after 1173).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  He is assumed to have been born when his father was living in Constantinople.  He adopted the name KONSTA
2 CONC NTINOS Dukas Kalamános in Byzantium[599], where he settled.  Ioannes Kinnamos names "Constantino Ciliciæ duce, quem Calamanum iuvenem vocabant"[600].  He became a General in the Byzantine army and was awarded the title sébastos by
2 CONC  Emperor Manuel I. In 1163, Constance Pss of Antioch appealed to Konstantinos Kalamános, Byzantine Governor of Cilicia, for military support to maintain her position in Antioch[601].  General Kalamános joined forces with Bohémond 
2 CONC III Prince of Antioch, Raymond III Count of Tripoli and Hugues de Lusignan to relieve the siege of the castle of Krak by Nur ed-Din in 1163[602].  He joined the same group in Aug 1164 to relieve another attack on Harenc, but was c
2 CONC aptured in an ambush at Artah, together with the other leaders, and taken bound to Aleppo, although Kalamános was released almost immediately in return for 150 silken robes[603].  He was appointed Governor of Cilicia in 1167, and 
2 CONC also charged with the mission of ending the relationship between Andronikos Komnenos (his predecessor as Governor of Cilicia) and Philippa, sister of Bohémond III Prince of Antioch, although Philippa refused his proposal of marria
2 CONC ge[604].  He suppressed the revolt of Thoros II Lord of the Mountains [Armenia-Rupen] who had attacked Byzantine garrisons after accusing Andronikos Komnenos, while Governor, of complicity in the murder in 1165 of his brother Step
2 CONC hané[605].  He was captured in Armenia in 1172/73, after which he was probably succeeded as Governor of Cilicia by Isaakios Dukas (later emperor in Cyprus)[606].  It is not clear whether General Kalamános was captured on two separ
2 CONC ate occasions, or whether the two occasions described above were two versions of the same event which, if the latter is correct, would have to be re-dated.  His descendants were the Kalomanoi family in Byzantium. 
