2 CONT
2 CONT * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesud_I '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also known as [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=johnneville&id=I52475 '''Masud Temirek'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Masud I''' or '''Ma'sud I''' (Modern Turkish: I. Rükneddin Mesud, Persian: ركن الدین مسعود‎, Rukn al-Dīn Mas'ūd;) was the sultan of the Seljuqs of Rûm from 1116 until his death in 1156.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Reign=
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the defeat and death of his father Kilij Arslan I by Fakhr al-Mulk Radwan of Aleppo at the battle of Khabur river in 1107,[1] Masud lost the throne in favor of his brother Malik Shah. With the help of the Danishmends, Ma
2 CONC sud captured Konya and defeated Malikshah in 1116, later blinding and eventually murdering him. Masud would later turn on the Danishmends and conquer their lands. In 1130, he started construction of the Alaeddin mosque in Konya, w
2 CONC hich was later completed in 1221.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Masud, towards the end of his reign, fought against the armies of the Second Crusade. There were actually two armies, one led German emperor Conrad III and the other led by the French king Louis VII. Masud defeated both of them; t
2 CONC he first army in Dorylaeum near modern Eskişehir in 1147[3] and the second army in Laodicea near modern Denizli in 1148.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he died, Masud was succeeded by his son Kilij Arslan II.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Masud's daughters married John Tzelepes Komnenos, a member of the royal house of Byzantium who had converted to Islam.[4]
2 CONT
