1 OCCU Turkic chieftain
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali-Tegin '''Wikipedia page'''] of his ally, Ali-Tegin:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ali-Tegin is first mentioned as being thrown in prison under the orders of his opponent Mansur Arslan Khan, but quickly managed to escape and receive help from a group of Oghuz Turks under the Seljuq chief Arslan Isra'il. With the
2 CONC se Oghuz Turks under his grasp, Ali-Tegin seized Bukhara and soon occupied all of Sogdia; after his conquest of the region, he took the title of "Yïgan-tigin" and another title of "Arslan Ilig".[4] He then gave his daughter in mar
2 CONC riage to Arslan Isra'il.[5] With the possession of the wealthy and important cities of Bukhara and Samarkand, Ali-Tegin became a powerful and influential figure in Central Asia; however, this made his relations with his jealous br
2 CONC other Yusuf Qadir Khan hostile, and resulted in Qadir Khan allying himself with the Ghaznavid Sultan Mahmud, who had received expression of discontent from Ali-Tegin's subjects and was himself annoyed by Ali-Tegin, who did not all
2 CONC ow him to send envoys to Qadir Khan, whose territories consisted of the important cities of Khotan and Kashgar.[6] Ali-Tegin, after having received the news of his brother's alliance with the Ghaznavids, responded by allying himse
2 CONC lf with his other brother Muhammad Toghan Khan.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1024/5, a combined army under Mahmud and Qadir Khan invaded Ali-Tegin's territories and completely defeated him and his Seljuq supporters.[7] Ali-Tegin then fled to the steppes, while Mahmud and Qadir Khan made a marriage allia
2 CONC nce at Samarkand. Mahmud also managed to take the family of Ali-Tegin captive, which was fleeing towards the steppes, but was captured by an officer of Mahmud.[8] Meanwhile Arslan Isra'il fled to Ghaznavid territory in Khorasan an
2 CONC d asked for permission to settle in the region in return for protecting the Ghaznavid borders from incursions by the other Turks of Transoxiana. Mahmud, who did not trust him, had him and his followers imprisoned.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Fortunately for Ali-Tegin, however, Mahmud had to withdraw from Transoxiana in order to prepare another expedition in India, which gave Ali-Tegin the opportunity to make a counter-attack against Qadir Khan and re-conquer his forme
2 CONC r territories. Ali-Tegin, although not with the support of Arslan Isra'il any longer, had support from the latter's nephews Tughril and Chaghri Beg. Although Ali-Tegin quarreled with the Seljuqs in 1029, the Seljuqs still continue
2 CONC d to serve and support Ali-Tegin.[9]
2 CONT
