1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eustathios_Argyros_(general_under_Leo_VI)
2 CONT
2 CONT Eustathios Argyros (Greek: Εὐστάθιος Ἀργυρός; died ca. 910) was a Byzantine aristocrat and one of the most prominent generals under Emperor Leo VI the Wise (r. 886–912). The first member of the Argyros family to rise to high posts
2 CONC , he fought with distinction against the Arabs in the east, before being disgraced ca. 907, possibly in connection with the flight of Andronikos Doukas to the Arabs. Rehabilitated soon after, he was appointed as strategos of Chars
2 CONC ianon, from which post he oversaw the settlement of Armenian lords as march-wardens along the Empire's eastern frontier. Promoted to commander of the imperial bodyguard in late 908, he again fell into disgrace shortly after and di
2 CONC ed of poison (apparently a suicide) on his way to his estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents 1	Life 2	Family 3	Identity 4	References 5	Sources Life Eustathios Argyros was the son of the tourmarches Leo Argyros, the founder of the noble Argyros family.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nothing is known of his life or prior to the turn of the 10th century, although he may have been in imperial service as early as 866, when a man of the same name is recorded as protostrator of the Caesar Bardas in connection with 
2 CONC the latter's murder on 21 April.[2][3][4] The Byzantine historians praise Eustathios Argyros as an intelligent, valiant, prudent and just man, and account him, along with Andronikos Doukas, as the best of Leo VI's generals.[5][6] 
2 CONC The historians Jean-Claude Cheynet and Jean-François Vannier, experts on Byzantine prosopography, consider him "the true founder of the family's glory".[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Map of the Arab–Byzantine frontier zone Although he is sometimes identified with an admiral active in ca. 902–904 (see below), his life is only securely attested after 904.[3][4] At this time, evidently after a succession of—unkno
2 CONC wn—military commands, Eustathios had reached, according to Theophanes Continuatus, the rank of patrikios and hypostrategos of the Anatolic Theme. The significance of the term "hypostrategos" has been debated; normally it would des
2 CONC ignate the second-in-command to the strategos or military governor of a theme, but Vannier suggested that on account of his high title of patrikios, Argyros was actually the strategos.[2][3][4] Theophanes Continuatus further extol
2 CONC s Argyros and mentions that he scored several successes against the Arabs in the east, most likely a reference to the great Byzantine victory over the Arabs of Tarsus and Mopsuestia at Germanikeia in December 904, under the overal
2 CONC l command of Andronikos Doukas.[2][3] He then fell into disgrace and was exiled. Although no details or reasons are offered for his exile, this has been interpreted by modern scholars as being connected to the failed rebellion and
2 CONC  flight of Andronikos Doukas to the Arabs in 906–907. Eustathios was rehabilitated soon after; if indeed his disgrace was related to the flight of Andronikos Doukas, this happened likely in 907/8, when Andronikos Doukas' son Const
2 CONC antine escaped from his Arab exile and returned to Constantinople, where he was pardoned by Emperor Leo.[3][5][7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Eustathios was then appointed as strategos of the border theme of Charsianon, a position notably inferior in rank to that of the Anatolics that he had held previously.[3] The Argyros family, however, had strong connections with Ch
2 CONC arsianon, from which it hailed.[8] There he received the return to imperial service of a number of Armenian lords, Melias, the three brothers Baasakios, Krigorikios and Pazounes, and Ismael, who were established as march-wardens a
2 CONC long the Empire's eastern border.[2][3][9] Of them, Melias in particular would go on to become the founder of the theme of Lykandos and one of the main Byzantine leaders in the wars against the Arabs over the next 30 years.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT In late 908 Eustathios Argyros was promoted to the rank of magistros—the highest court dignity open to someone not a member of the imperial family—and the post of Drungary of the Watch, i.e. commander of the imperial bodyguard, an
2 CONC d replaced as strategos of Charsianon by Constantine Doukas.[2][11] About a year later, he fell again under Leo's suspicion, and was ordered to return to his family's estates at Charsianon. Again the reason is unknown; perhaps, ac
2 CONC cording to Cheynet and Vannier, it was a result of the unreliability of the Armenian lords he had welcomed into the Empire, who frequently went over to the Arabs.[2][12] On the way, he died after taking poison by one of his servan
2 CONC ts, and was buried on Spynin, the summit of Mount Aran.[2][9][12] While historian Romilly James Heald Jenkins has suggested that Argyros' poisoning was done through an agent of the powerful and scheming court eunuch Samonas, it mo
2 CONC re likely was suicide.[13] His two sons, Pothos and Leo, who served in the palace as manglabites (personal bodyguards of the emperor), arranged to have their father's body transferred for burial to the monastery of Saint Elizabeth
2 CONC  in the Charsianon district, founded by Eustathios' father.[2][9][12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Eustathios' sons Pothos and Leo would go on to hold senior military commands, including the post of Domestic of the Schools (commander-in-chief).[9][14] Another son, Romanos, is known only from his participation in the Batt
2 CONC le of Achelous in 917.[15] Leo Argyros married a daughter of Emperor Romanos I Lekapenos (r. 920–944), and was probably the grandfather or great-grandfather of Emperor Romanos III Argyros (r. 1028–1034).[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT Identity Some modern scholars like R. J. H. Jenkins (The 'Flight' of Samonas), R. H. Dolley (The Lord High Admiral Eustathios Argyros and the betrayal of Taormina to the African Arabs in 902) and Ekkehard Eickhoff (Seekrieg und Se
2 CONC epolitik zwischen Islam und Abendland) consider Eustathios Argyros to have been identical with the contemporary admiral Eustathios, active in the years before 904, mainly due to the reference by the 11th-century historian John Sky
2 CONC litzes that Argyros pursued a career in the army as well as in the fleet. This identification is rejected by other scholars like J.-F. Vannier (Familles byzantines: les Argyroi (IXe–XIIe siècles)) and R. Guilland (Recherches sur l
2 CONC es institutions byzantines). Furthermore, the admiral is given the surname "Argyros" in some modern works that distinguish him from the general, while others reject the surname entirely. According to the Prosopographie der mittelb
2 CONC yzantinischen Zeit, "a definitive decision can no longer be made", and the main argument for the two being different persons is the incompatibility of their careers: the admiral Eustathios likewise experienced a tumultuous career 
2 CONC with accusations of treason, rehabilitation, and renewed disgrace, and it is unlikely that such a man would again be entrusted with senior posts.[17]
