1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basil_Lekapenos
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BYZANTIUM.htm#LekapeneMMosele
2 CONT
2 CONT i)           BASILEIOS (-985).  Theophanes Continuatus names "Basilium protovestiarium…Romanique decessoris imperatoris naturalem ex pellice filium"[1310].  Psellos states that the "parakoimomenos…Basil" was "born of the same fath
2 CONC er as the father of [Emperors] Basil [II] and Constantine [VIII]", which suggests incorrectly that he was the son of Emperor Konstantinos VII, and "on his mother's side he came from different stock"[1311].  Psellos records that "i
2 CONC n early infancy he had suffered '''castration''' - a natural precaution against a concubine's offspring"[1312].  Zonaras records that Emperor Konstantinos VII ordered the castration of "Romani senioris filium Basilium, ex ancilla 
2 CONC procreatum"[1313].  Cedrenus records that, after his restoration (944), Emperor Konstantinos VII had "Basilium, Romano seni e serva natum" castrated[1314].  He was in charge of civil administration under Emperor Nikeforos Fokas in
2 CONC  963, and granted the new title proedros.  Leo Diaconus records that "Basilius Nothus, Romani quondam Augusti ex Scythica femina filius" was granted "præsidis dignitate" by Emperor Nikeforos[1315].  In 976, he became chief adviser
2 CONC  [parakoimomenos] to the restored Emperor Basileios II[1316].  Psellos records that In 985, the emperor assumed personal rule and banished Basileios Lekapenos who soon after died "his limbs…paralysed and he a living corpse"[1317].
2 CONC
