1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 2-2438
1 NOTE Constantine VII, known as Porphyrogenitus (born in the purple), son of Leo VI and Zoe II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine VII succeeded to the throne of the Eastern Roman Empire in 912 after the death of his father Leo VI.  Under the regency of his Uncle Alexander from 912-913, then under the regency of his mother, Zoe, and the Patriarch 
2 CONC Nikolas and John Eladas,  from 913 to 919, then became co-ruler with his stepfather, Romanus I Lecapenus from 919-944. He ruled on his own from 944 to 959.  He was practically excluded from government administration in the early y
2 CONC ears, and later left government to capable men in his kingdom; and was later poisoned by his son Romanus II  in 959.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Constantine VII of Macedonia , author of a life of Basil I. Also wrote politan treatises as "On the Themes (DeThermatibus) in 935; On th eCeremonies at the Court of Constantinople (De Cerimoniis alulae Byzantinae) in 953; 
2 CONC and On th eAdministration of the Empire (De Adminstrando Imperio) 953.   Caused many works to be written that included excerpts from the classics, scientific collections, and collections of laws.  He died 959.
2 CONT
2 CONT The armies of Constantine VII waged a successful war with the Arabs in Syria and in 915 at Garigliano in Italy.
