1 NOTE Wladimir I "de Heilige" van Kiev, (zie dezelfde persoon hierboven in generatie 31) geb. ca. 960, ovl. 15.07.1015 in Berestiow, ref. nr. 25.03.2004 ES II-128, KHO, NOW.8,5,9  Hij trouwde met (1) Rogneda van Polock, ook bekend als R
2 CONC agnild, getrouwd 980, ovl. 1002, ref. nr. 25.03.2004 ES II-128.8  Hij trouwde met (2) NN, ref. nr. 14.11.2004 ES II-128.8  Partner NN, ref. nr. 22.01.2007 EGS I.1-286.10
1 NOTE OR "OLEG"; PRINCE OF DRUSTIANA
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
2 CONT Original individual @P2447683498@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@) merged with @P2442109209@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@)
2 CONT Original individual @P2447683498@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@) merged with @P2308141583@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@)
2 CONT Original individual @P2447683498@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@) merged with @P2442110467@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@)
1 NOTE His baptism made Orthodox Christianity the official religion of Russia.  
2 CONT Vladimir was a pagan at the beginning of his reign, which was at first 
2 CONT devoted to consolidating his territories into a unified Russian state. In 
2 CONT exchange for helping the Byzantine emperor Basil II suppress a rebellion, 
2 CONT Vladimir was allowed to marry the emperor's sister, Anne, at which time he 
2 CONT accepted Christianity. Allied to Byzantium by religious and family ties, 
2 CONT Vladimir introduced Byzantine civilization into Russia by building 
2 CONT churches, suppressing paganism, and making social reforms. Nonetheless, he 
2 CONT remained open to Western influences, which are reflected in his 
2 CONT legislation.
1 NOTE     Line 3426 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
2 CONT     NAME Vladimir I "The Great" /Kiev/
1 NOTE OR "WLADIMIR""BASIL I (THE SAINT)""VASSILLI""THE GREAT""SWJATOSLAWITSCH"; GRAND
2 CONT PRINCE OF KIEV 977-1015; BAPTIZED A CHRISTIAN 988 (AS VASSILI) AT KHERSON, AND
2 CONT SOON "CONVERTED" THE ENTIRE COUNTRY, AFTER THE MANNER OF MEDIEVAL PRINCES; HAD
2 CONT MANY "PAGAN" WIVES AND CONCUBINES
1 NOTE He ruled from 980 to 1015. He converted to Greek Orthodoxy in 988 and made it the State religion, with himself at its head. He was canonized after his death.
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Vladimir was the son of Svjatoslav I Igorjevitch, grand duke of Kiev, and Predslava. When his father was killed in 972 he was a contender for the rule of Kiev, together with his elder two brothers. The eldest brother Jaropolk I Sv
2 CONC jatoslavitsch was already established in Kiev, and he disposed of the middle brother Oleg and forced Vladimir to flee the country. Vladimir went from Novgorod to Scandinavia, from where he returned with an army of Varangians. He a
2 CONC ttacked and killed Jaropolk and in doing so became the sole ruler of Rus.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his early years as ruler he was brutal, bloodthirsty and dissolute. By his first wife Ragneda of Polatsk, whom he abducted before killing her parents and two brothers, he had three sons and two daughters; by a Greek woman he fa
2 CONC thered one son; by a Czech woman one son; two more sons by a different mother; and by a Bulgarian woman two more sons. He was said to have had three hundred concubines at Vyshegorod, three hundred at Belgorod, and two hundred at B
2 CONC erestovo.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the beginning of his long reign he continued attacking the Byzantine empire. However he soon realised that it was better to be on good terms with his neighbours and around 988 he adopted Christianity for himself and his people.
2 CONC  He also took as his third wife Anna Porphyrogenita, the sister of the Byzantine emperors Basilus II and Constantine VIII. However, the Pechenegs continued to harass him and he had to fight them until the end of his reign. After h
2 CONC is conversion Vladimir became a changed man; he became mild towards criminals, generous to the poor and supported the Greek missionaries. This led later generations to look on Vladimir and his grandmother Olga as the first-born of
2 CONC  the new Christian people of Russia and her borderland. They were both esteemed as saints and St. Vladimir became the subject of a cycle of folklore and heroic poems.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 15 July 1015, and was succeeded in Kiev by his son Jaroslav I Vladimirovitch, one of the two sons by Ragneda who would have progeny.
1 NOTE He ruled from 980 to 1015. He converted to Greek Orthodoxy in 988 and made it the State religion, with himself at its head. He was canonized after his death.
1 NOTE Han hadde minst tre  friller og gitet seg 2. med Anna av By zantz. Var
2 CONT konge fra 980-1015. Mange kjente etterkommere. M ed sin først frille
2 CONT hadde han blant annet sønnen Vsevolod ( 970) som ble  innebrendt sammen
2 CONT med Harald Grenske av Sigri d Storråde i 995 i Sverige.
1 NOTE Rurikovich; SWJATOSLAWITSCH; Saint; Grand Duke of KIEV (UKRAINE); `the Great';
2 CONT « Soleil Clair » (en ukrainien Volodymyr Volodymer)
2 CONT A fait assassiner son frère Iaropolk Ier
2 CONT
2 CONT Il reçut le baptême en 988 et imposa à son peuple le christianisme de rite byzantin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Epouses:
2 CONT Malfrida une tchèque (Bohème)= 
2 CONT - Vycheslav
2 CONT
2 CONT Rogneda (Rognieda) Princess of POLOTZK = quatre fils et deux filles:
2 CONT - Iaroslav le Sage prince de Rostov puis de Novogorod, 
2 CONT - Iziaslav prince de Polotzk, épouse Gertruda, la sœur de Kazimir I pologne.
2 CONT - Mstislav prince de Tmoutorakan puis de Tchernigov, 
2 CONT - Vsevolod prince de Vladimir 
2 CONT
2 CONT de diverses femmes =
2 CONT - Sviatoslav prince des Drevlianes tué en 1015 
2 CONT - Stanislav prince de Smolensk 
2 CONT - Pozvizd 
2 CONT - Soudislav mort en 1065 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adela or Milolika (?) a Bulgarian = 
2 CONT - Boris prince de Rostov assassiné en 1015 
2 CONT - Gleb prince de Mourom assassiné en 1015
2 CONT
2 CONT Ste.Anna PORPHYROGENITA (963-1011), sœur de Basile II empereur de Constantinople. (no kids)
2 CONT
2 CONT (Ragneda ?)  von OHNINGEN = fille  du Cte Kuno (fils d'OTTO I du St.Empire Germanique.)
2 CONT - Dobroniega épouse Kazimierz I de Pologne (le restaurateur)
