1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rogneda_of_Polotsk Rogneda of Polotsk]==
2 CONT
2 CONT * The Primary Chronicle names Rogned, daughter of Rogvolod Prince of Polotsk, recording that she at first refused to marry Vladimir, preferring his half-brother Yaropolk[80].
2 CONT * Rogneda was NOT the daughter of Kuno (Count) von OHNINGEN, as some have claimed.
2 CONT * Rogneda is not a Slavic name. Her father was Ragnvald of West Gotland, then of Polotsk (killed 970), the brother of Truggvi Olafson of Norway (died 965) and the illegitimate son of Olaf Haraldson of Norway (died 934). As you may
2 CONC  see, Yaroslav was a great grandson of Harald I Fairhair (died 931), the first Yngling king of Norway. This explains why in Norse sagas Vladimir is called a kinsman of Norse King Olaf Traggvison (who was brought up in Kiev) more t
2 CONC han once.
2 CONT
2 CONT * After Vladimir married Anna and became a Christian he had Rogneda christened also, after which she became a nun.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Rogneda died circa 1002 in a monastic cell as a nun named Anastasia.
2 CONT * After Vladimir converted to Christianity and took Anna Porphyrogeneta as his wife, he had to divorce all his previous wives, including Rogneda. After that, she entered the convent and took the name Anastasia.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Spouse==
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir I Married firstly ([977], divorced 986) as her second husband, ROGNED of Polotsk, widow of --- Jarl in Sweden, daughter of ROGVOLOD Prince of Polotsk & his wife --- ([956]-[998/1000]).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Rogned, daughter of Rogvolod Prince of Polotsk, recording that she at first refused to marry Vladimir, preferring his half-brother Yaropolk[80].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of Rogned in [998/1000][81].
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Grand Prince Vladimir & his first wife Rogneda had five children:==
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. Iziyaslav Vladimirovich (d. 1001, Prince of Polotsk)
2 CONT
2 CONT * 2. Yaroslav Vladimirovich "The Wise" (b. c.978, d. 20 February 1054, succeeded as Grand Prince of Kiev in 1016)
2 CONT
2 CONT * 3. Mstislav Vladomirovich "The Brave" (b.978, d. c.1034/36 during a hunting expedition, b. Church of the Redeemer, Chernigov, Prince of Tmutorokan 988-1024, Prince of Chernigov and East Bank 1024-1034)
2 CONT
2 CONT * 4. Vsevolod Vladimirovich (d. 1015, Grand Prince of Volynia and Pereyaslavl, proposed to Sigrid the Haughty who sought his assassination in 995)
2 CONT
2 CONT * 5. Unknown daughter (d. after 1018, possibly Predslava Vladimirovna, married Boleslaw III Duke of the Bohemians)
2 CONT
2 CONT * 6. Unknown duaghter (d. after 1018)
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Russian Wikipedia page on Rogneda Rogvolodovna 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==History==
2 CONT
2 CONT Under year 980 ￼''Nestor's Chronicle''￼ says that Vladimir, who was at war with his half-brother Yaropolk, sent his men to Rogvolod, Prince of Polotsk, and proposed marriage to his daughter Rogneda. Rogneda answered that she didn'
2 CONC t want to marry son of a slave girl and she prefered Yaropolk. However Vladimir took Polotsk, murdered Rogvolod and two his sons (their names aren't recorded) and married Rogneda by force. She became a nun (989).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 979 going from Novgorod to Kiev, with the intent to take power from his older brother, Vladimir was under influence of Dobrynia, who wanted to take away Yaropolk's bride - Rogneda, the daughter of the ruler of Polotsk Rogvolod,
2 CONC  and to give her to Vladimir. Rogneda answered to this: "I do not want to go for the slave's son" - this strongly offended Dobrynya, whos sister was named a slave, and after the victory over Rogvolod, as remarked in Chronicle, Dob
2 CONC rynia told to his nephew, "be with her (Rogneda) in front of her father and her mother." That is, at the call of Dobrynia Vladimir raped Rogneda in front of her parents, and then killed her father and two brothers. When Vladimir b
2 CONC ecame Grand Duke of Kiev, he forcibly took Rogneda as his wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rogneda of Polotsk married Saint Vladimir I Grand Duke of Kiev, son of Svyatoslav Igorevich and Malusha of Lubech, 978. Possibly as late as 980. Her father, Rogvolod, watched the skirmishes between Vladimir and his brothers trying
2 CONC  to remain neutral.
2 CONT
2 CONT Both wanted and needed the support of Rogvolod. Rogvolod had to make a choice of which brother to support. Both brothers sent Matchmakers to Rogvolod to secure his daughter Rognieda. The Matchmakers of brother Yaropolk decided it 
2 CONC was not suitable. But Rognieda is recorded as answering the Matchmakers from Vladimir with "I do not want to marry the lesser born. I want Yaropolk."
2 CONT
2 CONT This made Vladimir extremely angry. He collected an army and went to Polotsk, captured the town, ravenaged Rognieda, who was but about 12 years old, in front of her parents, then killed her parents and her brothers. Vladimir renam
2 CONC ed Rognieda.....Gorislava.4,8,2,7
2 CONT
2 CONT Tho' Rogneda had not wanted to marry Vladimir she had 5 children by him in about 10 years. She may have forgiven him for having killed her father and brothers but she could not abide by his unfaithfulness.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Rogenda was about 22 years old, and son Izyaslav was about 7 she had had enough. While Vladimir was sleeping she took his knife and drove it into his heart. But he lived!!!!
2 CONT
2 CONT Rogneda expected to pay the penalty of death for murder but Vladimir chose a worse sentence. She should never see her children again. Son Izyaslav put up such a real fuss that he was deported with her to a "deaf" (that is what the
2 CONC  translation from the Russian says!!) place at the source of the Svislock river.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his way to Kiev he sent ambassadors to Rogvolod (Norse: Ragnvald), prince of Polotsk, to sue for the hand of his daughter Rogneda
2 CONT
2 CONT Rogneda of Polotsk is the Slavic name for Ragnhild, whose father Ragnvald came from overseas and established himself at Polatsk in the mid-10th century....
2 CONT
2 CONT The well-born princess refused to affiance herself to the son of a bondswoman, but Vladimir attacked Polotsk, slew Rogvolod, and took Ragnhild by force. Actually, Polotsk was a key fortress on the way to Kiev, and the capture of P
2 CONC olotsk and Smolensk facilitated the taking of Kiev (980), where he slew Yaropolk by treachery, and was proclaimed konung, or Khagan or Great Khan Khagan or Great Khan Khagan or Great Khan, of all Kievan Rus.
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been speculated that Ragnvald belonged to the Ynglings royal family of Norway. In or about 980, Vladimir of Novgorod, on learning that she was betrothed to his half-brother Yaropolk I of Kiev, took Polotsk and forced Rogned
2 CONC a to marry him. Having raped Rogneda in the presence of her parents, he ordered them to be killed, along with two of Rogneda's brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rogneda gave him several children. The four sons were Yaroslav the Wise, Vsevolod, Mstislav of Chernigov, and Izyaslav of Polotsk. She also bore two daughters, one of whom is named by Nestor the Chronicler as Predslava (taken as a
2 CONC  concubine of Boleslaus I of Poland, according to Gallus).
2 CONT
2 CONT A later chronicle tells a story, most likely taken from a Norse saga, of Rogneda plotting against Vladimir and asking her elder son, Izyaslav, to kill him. As was the Norse royal custom, she was sent with her elder son to govern t
2 CONC he land of her parents, i.e. Polotsk. Izyaslav's line continued to rule Polotsk and the newly-found town of Izyaslavl until the Mongol invasion.
2 CONT
