2 CONT Noteringar 
2 CONT
2 CONT Konung ca 1050 – 1061. Frilloson till Olof Skötkonung, modern hette Edla. Född efter år 1000. Döpt samtidigt med brodern Anund, därefter uppfostrad i Venden. Blev kung efter Olof III Skötkonungs äktfödde son Anund II Jacobs död ef
2 CONC tersom denne dog barnlös. Men troligen hade Anund redan tagit honom som medregent 1047. Han låg i fejd med Bremens ärkestift och ville upprätta en oavhängig nationalkyrka. Han lär ha varit en ståtlig, handlingskraftig och frikosti
2 CONC g monark. Dog sotdöden, troligen i Sigtuna. Han sägs ibland vara den siste kungen av Ynglingaätten, vilket inte stämmer. Möjligen begravd i Linköping.  
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Emund the Old, Emund den gamle, Old Swedish: Æmunðær gamlæ or Æmunðær gammal also Emund the Evil Old Swedish: Æmunðær slemæ (king of Sweden 1050–1060)[1] was an illegitimate son of Olof Skötkonung. Emund succeeded his brother Anun
2 CONC d Jakob c. 1050. He was also called Slemme, "the bad," because he actively opposed the priests from the Archbishopric of Bremen in favour of the English missionary Osmundus. The English equivalent of his name is Edmund.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Westrogothic law says that he was a disagreeable man when wanting to pursue a goal, and that he marked the border between Sweden and Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the last king of the House of Munsö. Adam of Bremen relates in his work Gesta Hammaburgensis Ecclesiae Pontificum (Deeds of Bishops of the Hamburg Church) that his son Anund Emundsson died when leading a Swedish attack agai
2 CONC nst Terra Feminarum and the attack ended in Swedish defeat. Emund was succeeded by Stenkil and his house.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hervarar saga says that Emund was king only a short time:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eymundr hét annarr sonr Óláfs sænska, er konungdóm tók eptir bróður sinn. Um hans daga heldu Svíar illa kristnina. Eymundr var litla hríð konungr.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Olaf the Swede had another son called Eymund, who came to the throne after his brother. In his day the Swedes neglected the Christian religion, but he was King for only a short time.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The cognomen "Gamle" is known from Adam of Bremen, although he mistakes it as a proper name and mentions in one episode a "King Gamle" when it is in fact Emund. The name means "old" and could signify that he was old when he became
2 CONC  king or that he was the older brother to his predecessor Anund Jakob.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://images.google.no/imgres?imgurl=http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Fredrik_I,_king_of_Sweden.jpg/100px-Fredrik_I,_king_of_Sweden.jpg&imgrefurl=http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Swedish_monarchs&usg=_
2 CONC _ZE5Q-WnBc2PEvVu-HxrKIqth8SM=&h=138&w=100&sz=6&hl=en&start=17&sig2=LmnfAbxH3xw22rI2oupapw&um=1&tbnid=SChtoe5968whOM:&tbnh=93&tbnw=67&prev=/images%3Fq%3DSteinkil,%2BKing%2Bof%2BSweden%26hl%3Den%26rlz%3D1T4GGLT_enNO348NO348%26um%3D1
2 CONC &ei=nJE8S_jTE8n3-AbpmqmWDg
