1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/HUNGARY.htm#AndrasI
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_I_of_Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew I the White or the Catholic (Hungarian: I. (Fehér/Katolikus) András/Endre; c. 1015 – before 6 December 1060) was King of Hungary from 1046/1047 until his death. He descended from a younger branch of the Árpád dynasty. After
2 CONC  spending fifteen years in exile, he ascended the throne during an extensive revolt of the pagan Hungarians. He strengthened the position of Christianity in his kingdom, while preserving the independence of the Kingdom of Hungary 
2 CONC from the Holy Roman Empire. He tried to ensure the succession of his son which resulted in the open revolt of his brother who dethroned him by force.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew was the second son of Duke Vazul, who was a cousin of Stephen I, the first King of Hungary. His mother was probably the concubine (a daughter of a member of the Hungarian gens Tátony) of his father.[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT On 2 September 1031, King Stephen's only surviving son, Emeric was killed by a boar while hunting. The king wanted to secure the position of Christianity in his semi-pagan kingdom and therefore he planned to name his sister's son,
2 CONC  Peter Orseolo as his successor. However, Duke Vazul, who was suspected of following pagan-customs, took part in a conspiracy to murder the king. The assassination attempt failed and Duke Vazul had his eyes gouged out, molten lead
2 CONC  poured in his ears, and his three sons exiled.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Background
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hungarian tribal society of the eleventh century still believed in exclusive inheritance through the male line and was not in favor of primogeniture, favoring instead agnatic seniority for determining the order of succession. 
2 CONC This made other males of the Árpád dynasty's cadet lines dangerous to the incumbent king. Andrew's branch of the dynasty had long been rivals to the elder branch, to which Stephen I of Hungary and his father belonged. For the prev
2 CONC ious half century, the familial rivalry had centered mostly on the conflict between Christianity and paganism, respectively represented by the elder and the younger branches. In 1038, the extinction of the male line of the elder b
2 CONC ranch opened new opportunities for the younger, surviving male branch.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] In exile
2 CONT
2 CONT After their father's tragic death, the three brothers were obliged to leave the country. Fleeing first to Bohemia, they continued to Poland where Béla married a member of the Piast dynasty. Andrew and Levente, possibly feeling ove
2 CONC rshadowed by their brother, continued on, settling in Kiev. There, Andrew married Anastasia of Kiev, a daughter of Grand Duke Yaroslav I the Wise.
2 CONT
2 CONT The British prelates, led by Bishop Gerard of Csanád, decided to call back Andrew and his brothers to Hungary and wrote them a letter. By the time when Andrew and Levente arrived to Hungary, an extensive revolt of the pagan Hungar
2 CONC ians had broken out. The two brothers made an alliance with the pagan rebels in Abaújvár, who accepted their leadership. King Peter tried to escape to the Holy Roman Empire, but he was arrested and blinded by the followers of the 
2 CONC two princes.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Contest for the throne
2 CONT
2 CONT Tihany abbey, burial place of Andrew.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hungarian chronicles related that following the downfall of King Peter, Andrew agreed with his elder brother, Levente, who was a committed pagan, that Andrew would rule over Hungary. Andrew, however, was crowned only in 1047, 
2 CONC after his brother's death. Nevertheless, he continued the policies of Christianisation that had already been taking place. After his coronation, he confirmed King Stephen's decrees and invited foreign priests to Hungary, because t
2 CONC he pagan rebels had murdered several members of the Christian clergy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relations with the Holy Roman Empire remained tense, because King Peter had been not only a close ally of the Emperor Henry III, but he also had become a vassal of the Holy Roman Empire. Andrew refused to accept the suzerainty of 
2 CONC the Emperor, ruled Hungary independently and prepared for the approaching war.[2] He invited his younger brother, Béla, who had become a successful military leader in Poland, to his court and entrusted him with the government of t
2 CONC he third part of the Kingdom of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1051, the Emperor Henry III undertook a campaign against Hungary, but the imperial troops were defeated at the Vértes Hills, while the imperial fleet was induced to turn back by a forged letter. At the end of the year, Abbot Hu
2 CONC gh of Cluny was mediating between the two rulers, but the emperor refused to accept the peace. Next year the emperor led a fleet against Pozsony (Bratislava), but his ships were sunk by Andrew's men. In this time, Pope Leo IX trie
2 CONC d to mediate a peace, but the emperor did not accept Andrew's offer. When the imperial troops were withdrawn, Andrew formed an alliance, in 1053, with Conrad II, Duke of Bavaria, supporting the opposition against the emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1055, Andrew founded the Benedictine Abbey of Tihany on the shores of Lake Balaton and he also set up a monastery for Orthodox nuns there.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Succession crisis
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1057, Andrew I tried to ensure his succession, by having his five-year-old son, Solomon of Hungary crowned as king. But the coronation of his son provoked his brother, Duke Béla who had been assigned as Andrew's successor, and 
2 CONC the displeased duke left the king's court and left for his domains. In September 1058, Andrew had a personal meeting with the new King of Germany, Henry IV, Holy Roman Emperor in Marchfeld, and they came to a peaceful agreement, m
2 CONC arked by the betrothal of the child Solomon to the Henry's sister, Judith of Swabia.
2 CONT
2 CONT After achieving peace with the Holy Roman Empire, Andrew tried to persuade Duke Béla to accept his son's succession, but the duke left for Poland to collect armies against his brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Andrew heard of his brother's open rebellion, he sent his family to Austria, and prepared for the struggle, although he had been so ill that he was not even able to walk. Shortly afterwards, Duke Béla returned to Hungary with
2 CONC  Polish troops and won a decisive victory over the king. Following his defeat, Andrew fled towards Austria, but he fell down of his horse at the Theben Pass. Andrew was arrested by Duke Béla's men and taken to Zirc where he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew was buried in the Tihany Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT # c. 1039: Anastasia of Kiev (c. 1023 – c. 1074/1096), daughter of Grand Duke Yaroslav I of Kiev and his wife, Ingigerd of Sweden
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Adelaide (c. 1040 – 27 January 1062), wife of king Vratislaus II of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT     * King Solomon of Hungary (1053 – 1087 or after)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * David (after 1053 – after 1094)
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew's son Solomon never properly managed to establish himself as king; the sons of Andrew's younger brother gradually took over, particularly since neither Solomon, nor David (Andrew's youngest son) left surviving male descenda
2 CONC nts. Thus, Andrew's line continued in the Piast dynasty but not in Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Killed At Winsselburg
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Hungarian:
2 CONT
2 CONT I. András magyar király [szerkesztés]
2 CONT
2 CONT A Wikipédiából, a szabad enciklopédiából.
2 CONT
2 CONT I. András (Endre) (1015. körül – Zirc, 1060 vége) Árpád-házi magyar király 1046–1060 között. Uralkodása alatt többször került szembe a Német-római Birodalommal, trónjától végül öccse, Béla fosztotta meg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tartalomjegyzék [elrejtés]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Élete 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.1 Származása, ifjúsága
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.2 Újra Magyarországon
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.3 Uralkodása 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.3.1 Viszály a németekkel
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.3.2 Belviszály
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Halála
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Családja
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Források
2 CONT
2 CONT  Élete [szerkesztés]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Származása, ifjúsága [szerkesztés]
2 CONT
2 CONT Édesapja Vászoly (Vazul) volt, akit az I. István ellen szervezett lázadása miatt megvakítottak és fülébe forró ólmot öntöttek, hogy uralkodásra alkalmatlanná tegyék. Vazul Géza testvérének, Mihálynak volt a fia. Apját későbbi krón
2 CONC ikások tévesen nevezték meg Szár Lászlóként, aki Vazul testvére volt. Édesanyja egy Tátony nembeli nő volt. Két testvéréről tesznek említést a források, bátyját Leventének, öccsét Bélának hívták.
2 CONT
2 CONT Apjuk megvakításakor fiainak menekülnie kellett. Először Csehországba kerültek, majd nemsokkal később tovább mentek Lengyelországba, ahol Mieszko fejedelem fogadta őket. Ideérkezésük 1031 és 1034 (Mieszko halála) közötti időszakra
2 CONC  tehető. A lengyel udvarban csak Béla maradt, a másik két testvér tovább állt Oroszország irányába. Itt Bölcs Jaroszláv vendégszeretetét élvezték. 1038 táján András elnyerte Jaroszláv lányának Anasztáziának kezét. Feltehetően enne
2 CONC k a házasságnak volt az előfeltétele, hogy Andrásnak fel kellett vennie az ortodox kereszténységet. Ekkor Oroszország védőszentjének Szent Andrásnak nevét kapta. Eredeti pogány nevét a források nem őrizték meg.
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Családja [szerkesztés]
2 CONT
2 CONT András oroszországi tartózkodása idején feleségül vette Jaroszláv Anasztázia nevű lányát, a kijevi Szofija-székesegyházban. Az esküvőt ortodox szertartás szerint kötötték. Feleségétől három gyermeke született:
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelhaid, aki a cseh Vratiszláv fejedelem felesége lett, aki később Csehország első királya lett, de felesége ezt már nem élte meg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Salamon, a későbbi magyar király
2 CONT
2 CONT Dávid
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://hu.wikipedia.org/wiki/I._Andr%C3%A1s_magyar_kir%C3%A1ly
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Andreas I Arpád, King of Hungary (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #221310, d. 1060
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=8 Mar 2007
2 CONT
2 CONT      Andreas I Arpád, King of Hungary was the son of unknown Arpád.1 He married Anastasia of Kiev, daughter of Jarislaus I, Grand Duke of Kiev and Ingegarde of Sweden. (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1060. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT      Andreas I Arpád, King of Hungary gained the title of King Andreas I of Hungary in 1046. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Andreas I Arpád, King of Hungary and Anastasia of Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT Salamon Arpád, King of Hungary d. 1087 (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p22131.htm#i221310
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT I. András
2 CONT
2 CONT (1015 - Zirc, 1060 ősze)
2 CONT
2 CONT magyar király 
2 CONT
2 CONT Vászoly (Vazul) másodszülött fia, Salamon apja. 1046-tól 1060-ig Magyarország királya. 1037-ben Bölcs Jaroszláv kijevi fejedelem leányával, Anasztáziával kötött házasságot. (Szobruk a tihanyi apátság sétányán látható.) Apja megvak
2 CONC ítása után testvéreivel, Leventével és Bélával a kijevi udvarba ment, ahol megkeresztelkedett.
2 CONT
2 CONT Uralkodásának első felét a királyi hatalom és a kereszténység helyreállítása, valamint a magyar-német viszony rendezése foglalta le. Fiát, Salamont 1057-ben megkoronáztatta. De az ellenségeskedések a német-római birodalommal csak 
2 CONC III. Henrik császár 1056-ban bekövetkezett halála után kötött békével szűntek meg, amikor a császár leányát Salamon herceggel jegyezték el. 1060-ban háború tört ki a király és öccse, Béla között. Ennek során csatát vesztett, lovak
2 CONC  tiporták el. Nem sokkal később Zircen, a vadászházában belehalt sérüléseibe. Az általa 1055-ben alapított tihanyi bencés apátságban temették el, ahol sírköve is látható. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tihanyban a Visszhang-dombon, az Echó Vendégház falán emléktáblája van. Az apátság épülete előtt álló, Alapító című szobor Varga Imre alkotása. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.vendegvaro.hu/I-Andras
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
