2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT (from wikipedia.fr) Il est fiancé en 1033 à Mahaut (Mathilda, v.1027 - 1034), fille en bas-âge de l'empereur Conrad II le Salique, mais celle-ci trouve la mort à l'âge de sept ans.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1034, il épouse en premières noces Mathilde de Frise, (v. 1025/1026 - 1044), fille de Luidolf de Frise. Le 19 mai 1051 à Reims, il épouse en secondes noces Anne de Kiev, fille de Iaroslav le Sage, grand-prince de Kiev, et de In
2 CONC gigerd de Suède.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ensemble ils eurent:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Philippe Ier (1052-1108) * Robert (1054-1063) * Emma (1055-1109) * Hugues (1057-1102), comte de Vermandois, époux d'Adélaïde de Vermandois. Leur fils aîné, associé au trône en 1059, succède à son père sous le nom de Philippe Ier
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on France Capetian Kings (covering his birth family):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CAPET.htm#HenriIdied1060A
2 CONT
2 CONT ROBERT de France, son of HUGUES Capet King of France & his wife Adelais [de Poitou] (Orléans ([27 Mar] 972-Château de Melun 20 Jul 1031, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Genealogiæ Scriptoris Fusniacensis names "Robertum regem et filiam Hadevidem…comitissam Hainonensium" as the children of King Hugues[148].
2 CONT
2 CONT He was invested as associate-king with his father 25 Dec 987, consecrated 1 Apr 988 at the cathedral of Sainte-Croix in Orléans[149]. He succeeded his father in 996 as ROBERT II "le Pieux"[150] King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT He claimed the duchy of Burgundy on the death of his paternal uncle Duke Henri in 1002, but took 12 years to complete its conquest in the face of opposition from Otto-Guillaume Comte de Mâcon[151].
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Emperor Heinrich II King of Germany in 1024, King Robert supported the rebels (led by Frédéric II Duke of Upper Lotharingia) opposed to King Konrad II but he refused the crown of Italy which they offered it to h
2 CONC im. Robert nevertheless sent troops to attack Metz, but was repulsed[152].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the death in 1031 of "rex Francorum Robertus"[153]. Rodolfus Glaber records the death of King Robert at Melun in July and his place of burial[154]. The necrology of Chartres cath
2 CONC edral records the death "XII Kal Aug" of "Rotbertus rex"[155]. The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Denis records the death "XIII Kal Aug" of "Rotbertus…Francorum rex"[156].
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (988, before 1 Apr, repudiated [991/92]) as her second husband, ROZALA [Suzanne] di Ivrea, widow of ARNOUL II “le Jeune” Count of Flanders, daughter of BERENGARIO II ex-King of Italy [Ivrea] & his wife Willa of Tuscany-A
2 CONC rles ([950/960]-13 Dec 1003 or 7 Feb 1004, bur Gent, church of the Abbey de Saint-Pierre du Mont-Blandin).
2 CONT
2 CONT Regino records that two of the daughters (unnamed) of ex-King Berengario were brought up in the imperial palace by the empress after being brought to Germany[157]. One of these two daughters was presumably Rozala, bearing in mind 
2 CONC that the emperor arranged her marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Genealogica Comitum Flandriæ Bertiniana names "filiam Berengeri regis Langobardorum, Ruzelam quæ et Susanna" as wife of Comte Arnoul[158]. The Annales Elnonenses Minores record the marriage [undated between 950 and 968] of "Ar
2 CONC nulfus iunior" and "filiam Beregeri regis Susannam"[159]. Her marriage was presumably arranged by Emperor Otto to increase his influence in Flanders at a time when Lothaire IV King of the West Franks was asserting his own control 
2 CONC over the county.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Nicholas, Count Arnoul II married Rozala di Ivrea when he reached the age of majority in 976[160], but the source on which this is based has not been located. "Baldwinus marchysus cum matre sua Susanna" donated "villa
2 CONC m Aflingehem…jacentem in pago Tornacinse" to Saint-Pierre de Gand, after the death of "Arnulfi marchysi", by charter dated 1 Apr 988, signed by "…Waldberto advocato, Theoderico comite, Arnulfo comite, Artoldo comite, Baldwino comi
2 CONC te, item Arnulfo comite…"[161]. The Vita Sancti Bertulfi names "Rozala filia…Berengarii Regis Italiæ", specifying that "post mortem Arnulfi [Balduini filius] principis, Roberto Regi Francorum nupsit et Susanna dicta"[162]. Kerrebr
2 CONC ouck, presumably basing his supposition on this passage from the Vita Sancti Bertulfi, says that she adopted the name Suzanne on her second marriage[163], but the sources quoted above show that she was referred to by this name ear
2 CONC lier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugues "Capet" King of France arranged her second marriage to his son and heir, apparently as a reward for Flemish help when he seized power in 987[164]. She was given Montreuil-sur-Mer by the county of Flanders as her dowry on he
2 CONC r second marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richer records that King Robert repudiated his wife "Susannam…genere Italicam eo quod anus esset" but refused to allow her to retake her castle at Montreuil, whereupon she constructed another nearby[165]. She returned to Flanders 
2 CONC after she was repudiated by her second husband, and became one of the principal advisers of her son Count Baldwin IV. France retained Montreuil-sur-Mer.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Susanna regina cum filio suo Baldwino" donated "alodem suum…Atingehem…et in Testereph" to Saint-Pierre de Gand, for the soul of "filie sue Mathildis", by charter dated 26 Jun 995[166]. "Susanna regina…cum filio suo Baldwino" dona
2 CONC ted "alodem suum…in pago Flandrensi…in Holtawa…in Fresnere…in Clemeskirca…in Jatbeka…in Sclefteta…" to Saint-Pierre de Gand by charter dated 1 Jun 1003[167]. The Annales Elnonenses Minores records the death in 1003 of "Susanna reg
2 CONC ina"[168]. The Memorial of "regina Susanna" records her death "VII Feb"[169].
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([late 996/early 997], divorced Sep 1001) BERTHE of Burgundy, widow of EUDES I Comte de Blois et de Chartres, daughter of CONRAD I “le Pacifique” King of Burgundy [Welf] & his wife Mathilde de France [Carolingian] ([964
2 CONC /965]-16 Jan after 1010).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Liber Modernorum Regum Francorum names "Berta filia Conradi regis Burgundiæ" as wife of "Odone comite Carnotensium"[170]. This origin is corroborated by Rodulfus Glauber who names "Odo natus ex filia Chuonradi regis Austrasior
2 CONC um, Berta nomine"[171]. Richer records that King Robert married "Berta Odonis uxor"[172].
2 CONT
2 CONT Pope Gregory V called on King Robert to repudiate his wife in 998 on grounds of consanguinity. The request was repeated in 1001 by the court of Rome. Robert at first refused and the kingdom of France was excommunicated[173].
2 CONT
2 CONT "Bertæ reginæ, Odonis comitis filii eius…" subscribed the charter dated 1004 under which "Gislebertus prepositus" recorded a donation[174].
2 CONT
2 CONT The king, in reaction to the 1108 assassination of his favourite Hugues de Beauvais who had served Queen Berthe, visited Rome in 1008 in an unsuccessful attempt to divorce his third wife in order to take back Berthe[175].
2 CONT
2 CONT "Odonis comitis, Ermengardis uxoris eius, Bertæ reginæ…" subscribed the charter dated after 1005 under which "comitem Odonem" donated property "in comitatu Dunensi…Boscus Medius" to "Sancti Petri"[176].
2 CONT
2 CONT The necrology of Chartres cathedral records the death "XVII Kal Feb" of "Berta mater Odonis comitis"[177].
2 CONT
2 CONT m thirdly (after Sep 1001 before 25 Aug 1003) CONSTANCE d'Arles, daughter of GUILLAUME II “le Libérateur” Comte d’Arles [Provence] et Marquis & his wife Adelais [Blanche] d’Anjou ([987/89]-Château de Melun 22 or 25 Jul 1032, bur é
2 CONC glise de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum names "Constantiam, filiam Guillelmi comitis Arelatensis, natam de Blanca sorore Gaufridi comitis Andegavensis" as wife of King Robert[178]. The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines also names "Constantia
2 CONC  filia fuit Blanche comitisse Arelatensis" as wife of "Robertus rex"[179]. The Chronicon Hugonis names "Constantiam" as wife of "Robertus", specifying that she was "cognatam Hugonis Autisiodorensis episcopi comitis Cabilonensis"[1
2 CONC 80]. This is presumably based on Rodulfus Glaber who states incorrectly that "Constantiam…filiam…prioris Willemi Aquitanie ducis" was wife of King Robert II, specifying that she was "cognatam" of Hugues Comte de Chalon Bishop of A
2 CONC uxerre[181]. The only relationship so far identified between the two is that Constance's maternal uncle, Geoffroy I Comte d'Anjou, was the second husband of the mother of Comte Hugues.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodulfus Glauber dates her marriage to "about the year 1000"[182].
2 CONT
2 CONT The king attempted to separate from Constance in 1008 in order to take back his second wife, according to Rodulfus Glaber through the influence of "Hugo dictus Beluacensis"[183], but he restored Constance's royal prerogatives end-
2 CONC 1009[184].
2 CONT
2 CONT She opposed her husband's proposal to crown their second son Henri as associate king in 1026, supporting the candidature of her third son Robert[185]. She organised two revolts against King Robert, and another against her son King
2 CONC  Henri I after his accession in 1031[186].
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodolfus Glaber records the death of Queen Constance in the same city as her husband [Melun] and in the same month [July] in the following year, as well as her place of burial[187]. The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Germain-des-
2 CONC Prés records the death "XI Kal Aug" of "regina Constancia"[188]. The necrology of Argenteuil Priory records the death "VIII Kal Aug" of "Constancia regina"[189].
2 CONT
2 CONT King Robert & his third wife had [seven] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. [CONSTANCE de France.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no proof that Constance, wife of Manassès de Dammartin, was the daughter of King Robert II, the affiliation being proposed for onomastic reasons only[190]. It is, however, supported by the presence of the king and queen a
2 CONC t a donation by Comte Manassès in 1031[191].
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodolfus Glaber records that King Robert had two daughters by his wife Constance[192], presumably referring to Hedwige and Adela. According to Europäische Stammtafeln[193], the wife of Manassès was "Constance [de Dammartin]", pres
2 CONC umably on the theory that she brought her husband the county of Dammartin.
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([1023 or before]) MANASSES Comte de Dammartin-en-Goële, son of HILDUIN II de Montdidier Seigneur de Ramérupt & his wife --- (-killed in battle Ornel, near Etain, Bar-le-Duc 15 Nov or 15 Dec 1037).]
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. HEDWIGE [Avoie] de France ([1003]-5 Jun after 1063).
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodolfus Glaber records that "Rainaldus…Landrici comitis filius" married a daughter of King Robert[194]. The Chronici Hugonis Floriacensis names "Adelaidem…Rainaldi comitis Nivernensis uxorem" as the daughter of King Robert and hi
2 CONC s wife Constance[195]. The Historia Nivernensium Comitum records that the wife of "Renaldum" was "sorori Regis Roberti, filii Hugonis Capitonis"[196]. The Annales Vizeliacenses also specifies that Renaud's wife was the sister not 
2 CONC daughter of King Robert II[197]. However, this is chronologically unlikely given that King Robert and his known sisters were born in the 970s, more than 20 years before the earliest possible date of birth of Comte Renaud.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her marriage was agreed by her father as part of his alliance with Landry Comte de Nevers after capturing Auxerre, which the king gave to his daughter as dowry[198]. "Rainaldus comes Nivernensis" donated property "Belmontis" to Cl
2 CONC uny, for the souls of "…uxoris mee Advise…" by charter dated to [1028/40][199].
2 CONT
2 CONT She founded the abbeys of Crisenon and Issenon.
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1006, soon after 25 Jan 1016) RENAUD de Nevers, son of LANDRY Comte de Nevers & his wife Mathilde de Bourgogne-Comté (-killed in battle Sainte-Vertu, Yonne 29 May 1040, bur Auxerre, Saint-Germain).
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 1028 as RENAUD I Comte de Nevers.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed in battle against Robert I Duke of Burgundy, his brother-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. HUGUES de France (1007-28 Aug 1025, bur Compiègne, church of the Abbaye de Saint-Corneille).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum names (in order) "Hugonem qui cognominatus est Magnus, Henricum, Robertum, Odonem" as the four sons of King Robert and Constance[200].
2 CONT
2 CONT He was consecrated associate-king 9 Jun 1017, at Compiègne, church of the Abbaye de Saint-Corneille, when he was "barely 10 years old" according to Rodolfus Glaber[201].
2 CONT
2 CONT He rebelled against his father claiming the full authority of his position as associate-king, but later submitted[202].
2 CONT
2 CONT The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Germain-des-Prés records the death "V Kal Sep" of "Hugo iuvenis rex Francorum"[203]. The necrology of Argenteuil Priory records the death "V Kal Sep" of "Hugo iuvenis rex"[204]. Rodolfus Glaber 
2 CONC records his place of burial[205].
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. HENRI de France ([end 1009/May 1010]-Palais de Vitry-aux-Loges, forêt d’Orléans, Loiret 4 Aug 1060, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum names (in order) "Hugonem qui cognominatus est Magnus, Henricum, Robertum, Odonem" as the four sons of King Robert and Constance[206].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 1031 as HENRI I King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. ROBERT de France ([1011/12]-church of Fleury-sur-Ouche, Côte d’Or 18 Mar 1076, bur Saint-Seine-l'Abbaye, Côte d’Or).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum names (in order) "Hugonem qui cognominatus est Magnus, Henricum, Robertum, Odonem" as the four sons of King Robert and Constance[207].
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodulfus Glauber names "Heinricus rex…germanium suum Rotbertum" when recording the latter's installation as duke of Burgundy by his brother[208]. His mother supported him as candidate to be consecrated associate king in 1027, in p
2 CONC lace of his older brother Henri who was supported by their father.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father named him heir to the duchy of Burgundy in 1030. He was installed as ROBERT I "le Vieux" Duke of Burgundy in 1032 by his brother King Henri I.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. EUDES de France ([1013]-Germigny-des-Prés, near Sully, Loiret 15 May [1057/59]).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum names (in order) "Hugonem qui cognominatus est Magnus, Henricum, Robertum, Odonem" as the four sons of King Robert and Constance[209].
2 CONT
2 CONT He allied himself with Eudes II Comte de Blois in the war against his brother Henri I King of France 1034-1041. He was defeated and imprisoned at Orléans.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his release, he fought for the king in Normandy, but was defeated in 1054 at Mortemer. Orderic Vitalis records the war between the Normans and "Henricum regem" in 1054 when "Odonem fratrem suum" was defeated by "Roberti Auce
2 CONC nsis comiitis et Rogerii de Mortuomari" who led the Norman forces "apud Mortuum-mare"[210].
2 CONT
2 CONT He owned land near Bellême[211].
2 CONT
2 CONT The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Denis records the death "Id Mai" of "Odo Roberti regis filius"[212].
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. ADELA de France (-Messines 8 Jan 1079, bur Messines, Benedictine monastery).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "soror…regis Henrici Adela" as wife of "Balduino Insulano"[213]. The Genealogiæ Scriptoris Fusniacensis names "Alam comitissam Flandrensem" as the daughter of King Robert[214]. She
2 CONC  is named as daughter of King Robert in a manuscript whose attribution to Orderic Vitalis is disputed, which also refers to her marriage[215].
2 CONT
2 CONT Kerrebrouck mentions her betrothal to Duke Richard "très jeune" but does not cite the primary source on which this is based[216].
2 CONT
2 CONT "Richardus Nortmannorum dux" agreed grants of property to "Adela" on the occasion of their marriage by charter dated Jan 1026, which does not specify her parentage[217]. Her father gave her the seigneurie of Corbie as her dowry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ctss de Contenance.
2 CONT
2 CONT She founded the Benedictine monastery at Messines near Ypres.
2 CONT
2 CONT The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Denis records the death "VI Id Jan" of "Adelaidis comitissa"[218].
2 CONT
2 CONT Betrothed (Jan 1027) to RICHARD III Duke of Normandy, son of RICHARD I Duke of Normandy & his first wife Judith de Bretagne ([1001]-6 Aug 1027).
2 CONT
2 CONT m (Amiens 1028) BAUDOUIN de Flandre, son of BAUDOUIN IV "le Barbu/Pulchrae Barbae" Count of Flanders & his first wife Ogive de Luxembourg ([1012/13]-Lille 1 Sep 1067, bur Lille, Saint-Pierre).
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 1035 as BAUDOUIN V “le Pieux/Insulanus” Count of Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was regent of France for his nephew Philippe I King of France 1060-1066/67.
2 CONT
