2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Northern France:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORTHERN%20FRANCE.htm#Elisabethdied1131
2 CONT
2 CONT HUGUES "le Maisné" de France, son of HENRI I King of France & his second wife Anna Iaroslavna of Kiev (1057-Tarsus 18 Oct 1102, bur Tarsus, Church of St Paul).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Liber Modernorum Regum Francorum names (in order) "Philippum, Hugonem atque Rotbertum" as the three sons of King Henri and his wife Anna[1325].  
2 CONT
2 CONT William of Tyre records "dominus Hugo Magnus" as brother of Philippe I King of France[1326].  He succeeded as Comte de Vermandois et de Valois, by right of his wife.  He left France in Aug 1096 as head of the contingent of his bro
2 CONC ther Philippe I King of France which left on the First Crusade[1327].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Alexeiad names "a certain Hugh, brother of the king of France" when recording that he "sent an absurd message to the emperor proposing that he should be given a magnificent reception" after arriving in Constantinople[1328].  H
2 CONC e was shipwrecked off Durazzo, but accompanied to Constantinople by the Byzantine admiral Manuel Butumites[1329].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert of Aix records that "Hugonem Magnum fratrem regis Franciæ, Drogonem et Clareboldum" were held in chains in prison by the emperor at Constantinople but were released after the intervention of "Baldewinus Hainaucorum comes et
2 CONC  Heinricus de Ascha" who were sent as envoys by Godefroi de Bouillon[1330].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert of Aix records that "Hugo, Drogo, Willelmus Carpentarius et Clareboldus" joined the army of Godefroi de Bouillon after their release from captivity in Constantinople, dated to end 1096[1331].  He took part in the siege of A
2 CONC ntioch in 1098.  He was sent on a mission to Emperor Alexios I, but was surprised in a Turkish ambush near Nikaia but escaped with his life[1332].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He returned to France after the capture of Antioch in 1098 to raise another army which he led as part of the second wave of the First Crusade, leaving France in Mar 1101.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert of Torigny records the death in 1102 of "Hugo Magnus apud Tarsum"[1333].  He died from wounds received fighting the Turks near Tarsus in Asia Minor[1334].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (after 1067) as her first husband, ADELAIS Ctss de Vermandois, de Valois et de Crépy, daughter and heiress of HERIBERT [IV] Comte de Vermandois & his wife Adelais de Valois ([1065]-28 Sep [1120/24]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Genealogiæ Scriptoris Fusniacensis names "Adelaide comitissa Veromandensium" as wife of "Hugonem Magnum"[1335].  Her husband left her as regent in Vermandois when he left on crusade.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She married secondly (1103) as his first wife, Renaud de Clermont.  "Adela…Viromandorum comitissa, filius…meus Radulphus" renounced their claim to certain serfs in favour of the abbey of Compiègne Saint-Corneille, with the consent
2 CONC  of "filiorum meorum Radulphi, Henrici, Symonis", by charter dated 1114[1336].  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1117, Louis VI "le Gros" King of France restored to her the county of Amiens which had been usurped by Thomas de Marle[1337].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte Hugues & his wife had nine children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Mathilde de Vermandois (married as second wife Raoul, Seigneur de Baugency)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Agnes de Vermandois (b. c.1085, d. after 1127, m. as second wife Bonifacio di Saluzzo, Marchese del Vasto)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Constance de Vermandois (d. after 1118, m. Godefroi de la Ferte-Gaucher, Vicomte de Meaux)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Isabelle/Elisabeth de Vermandois (b. before 1088, d. 17 February 1131, buried Lewes, OUR ANCESTOR, m. firstly Robert de Beaumont, Comte de Meulan, secondly William de Warenne, Earl of Surrey, OUR ANCESTOR)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Raoul I "le Vaillant" de Vermandois (b. c.1094, d. 13 October 1152, buried St-Arnould in Crepy, succeeded as Comte de Vermandois)
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Henri de Vermandois (d. 1130, Seigneur de Chaumont-en-Vexin)
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Simon de Vermandois (d. 10 February 1148 in Seleukia returning from second crusade, buried Cistercian Abbey of Notre-Dame-de-l'Assomption, Ourscamp)
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Guillaume de Vermandois (d. after 1096, parentage uncertain)
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. Beatrix de Vermandois (d. after 1144, married Hugues IV, Seigneur de Gourney-en-Bray)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Per www.whitesvill.net/Ruth.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh was the first prince to leave on the First Crusade (except for Peter the Hermit's 'Peasant's Crusade' earlier that year).  He set out for Italy in August 1096
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Not to be confused with:
2 CONT
2 CONT Humbert II, surnamed the Fat, was Count of Savoy from 1080 until his death in 1103. He was the son of Amadeus II of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Gisela of Burgundy, daughter of William I, Count of Burgundy, and had 7 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Amadeus III of Savoy 
2 CONT
2 CONT William, Bishop of Liège 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide, (d. 1154), married to Louis VI of France 
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes, (d. 1127), married to Archimbald VI, lord of Bourbon 
2 CONT
2 CONT Humbert 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reginald 
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy, abbey of Namur 
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Amadeus II Count of Savoy Succeeded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Amadeus III 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh I (1053 – October 18, 1101), called Magnus or the Great, was a younger son of Henry I of France and Anne of Kiev and younger brother of Philip I. He was in his own right Count of Vermandois, but an ineffectual leader and sold
2 CONC ier, great only in his boasting. Indeed, Steven Runciman is certain that his nickname Magnus (greater or elder), applied to him by William of Tyre, is a copyist's error, and should be Minus (younger), referring to Hugh as younger 
2 CONC brother of the King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT In early 1096 Hugh and Philip began discussing the First Crusade after news of the Council of Clermont reached them in Paris. Although Philip could not participate, as he had been excommunicated, Hugh was said to have been influen
2 CONC ced to join the Crusade after an eclipse of the moon on February 11, 1096.
2 CONT
2 CONT That summer Hugh's army left France for Italy, where they would cross the Adriatic Sea into territory of the Byzantine Empire, unlike the other Crusader armies who were travelling by land. On the way, many of the soldiers led by f
2 CONC ellow Crusader Emicho joined Hugh's army after Emicho was defeated by the Hungarians, whose land he had been pillaging. Hugh crossed the Adriatic from Bari in Southern Italy, but many of his ships were destroyed in a storm off the
2 CONC  Byzantine port of Dyrrhachium.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh and most of his army was rescued and escorted to Constantinople, where they arrived in November of 1096. Prior to his arrival, Hugh sent an arrogant, insulting letter to Eastern Roman Emperor Alexius I Comnenus, according to 
2 CONC the Emperor's biography by his daughter (the Alexiad), demanding that Alexius meet with him:
2 CONT
2 CONT     "Know, O King, that I am King of Kings, and superior to all, who are under the sky. You are now permitted to greet me, on my arrival, and to receive me with magnificence, as befits my nobility."[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexius was already wary of the armies about to arrive, after the unruly mob led by Peter the Hermit had passed through earlier in the year. Alexius kept Hugh in custody in a monastery until Hugh swore an oath of vassalage to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Crusaders had successfully made their way across Seljuk territory and, in 1098, captured Antioch, Hugh was sent back to Constantinople to appeal for reinforcements from Alexius. Alexius was uninterested, however, and Hug
2 CONC h, instead of returning to Antioch to help plan the siege of Jerusalem, went back to France. There he was scorned for not having fulfilled his vow as a Crusader to complete a pilgrimage to Jerusalem, and Pope Paschal II threatened
2 CONC  to excommunicate him. He joined the minor Crusade of 1101, but was wounded in battle with the Turks in September, and died of his wounds in October in Tarsus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and children
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Adele of Vermandois, the daughter of Herbert IV, Count of Vermandois and Alice, Countess of Valois. They had nine children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Matilda(1080-1130), married Ralph I of Beaugency
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Beatrice (1082-after1144), married Hugh III of Gournay
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ralph I (1085-1152)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Elizabeth of Vermandois, Countess of Leicester (1085-1131)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Constance (1086-??), married Godfrey de la Ferté-Gaucher
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Agnes (1090-1125), married Boniface of Savone
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Henry (1091-1130), Lord of Chaumont en Vexin
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Simon (1093-1148)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * William (c. 1094-c.1096)
2 CONT
