2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh I, called "Magnus," or "the Great," was a younger son of King Henry I of France and Anne of Kiev and younger brother of King Philip I. He was in his own right Count of Vermandois, but he was an ineffectual leader and soldier,
2 CONC  great only in his boasting. Indeed, Steven Runciman is certain that his nickname Magnus (greater or elder), applied to him by William of Tyre, is a copyist's error, and should be Minus (younger), referring to Hugh as younger brot
2 CONC her of the King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh married Adele of Vermandois, the daughter of Herbert IV of Vermandois and Adele of Valois.They had nine children, including two of our ancestors.
2 CONT
2 CONT In early 1096 Hugh and Philip began discussing the First Crusade after news of the Council of Clermont reached them in Paris. Although Philip could not participate, since he had been excommunicated, Hugh was said to have been infl
2 CONC uenced to join the Crusade after an eclipse of the moon on February 11, 1096.
2 CONT
2 CONT That summer Hugh's army left France for Italy, where they would cross the Adriatic Sea into territory of the Eastern Roman (Byzantine) Empire, unlike the other Crusader armies who were traveling by land. On the way, many of the so
2 CONC ldiers led by fellow Crusader Emicho joined Hugh's army after Emicho was defeated by the Hungarians, whose land he had been pillaging. Hugh crossed the Adriatic from Bari in Southern Italy, but many of his ships were destroyed in 
2 CONC a storm off the Byzantine port of Dyrrhachium.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh and most of his army was rescued and escorted to Constantinople, where they arrived in November of 1096. Prior to his arrival, Hugh sent an arrogant, insulting letter to Eastern Roman Emperor Alexios I Komnenus (also our ance
2 CONC stor), according to the Emperor's biography by his daughter (the Alexiad), demanding that Alexios meet with him:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Know, O King, that I am King of Kings, and superior to all, who are under the sky. You are now permitted to greet me, on my arrival, and to receive me with magnificence, as befits my nobility."
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexios was already wary of the armies about to arrive, after the unruly mob led by Peter the Hermit had passed through earlier in the year. Alexios kept Hugh in custody in a monastery until Hugh swore an oath of vassalage to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Crusaders had successfully made their way across Seljuk territory and, in 1098, captured Antioch, Hugh was sent back to Constantinople to appeal for reinforcements from Alexios. Alexios was uninterested, however, and Hug
2 CONC h, instead of returning to Antioch to help plan the siege of Jerusalem, went back to France. There he was scorned for not having fulfilled his vow as a Crusader to complete a pilgrimage to Jerusalem, and Pope Paschal II threatened
2 CONC  to excommunicate him. He joined the minor Crusade of 1101, but was wounded in battle with the Turks in September, and died of his wounds in October in Tarsus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh was our ancestor through two distinct descent lines--through his daughter Elizabeth (Isabel) and through his daughter Beatrice, each of whom was independently our ancestor.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_of_Vermandois for more information.
2 CONT
