2 CONT
2 CONT PHILIPPE de France, son of HENRI I King of France & his third wife Anna Iaroslavna of Kiev (1052-Château de Melun, Seine-et-Marne 30 Jul 1108, bur Abbaye de Saint Benoît-sur-Loire[252]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Liber Modernorum Regum Francorum names (in order) "Philippum, Hugonem atque Rotbertum" as the three sons of King Henri and Anna[253].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the birth in 1052 of "rex futurus regis
2 CONC  Francorum Henrici filius ex Anna filia Georgii regis Sclavonum"[254].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was consecrated associate-king 23 May 1059, at the cathedral of Notre-Dame de Reims.  His father entrusted his education to his uncle Baldwin V Count of Flanders, who became regent until 1066/67.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 1060 as PHILIPPE I King of France.  Consecrated 25 Dec 1071 at Laon, again 16 May 1098 at Tours, and for a fourth time 25 Dec 1100 at Reims.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Foulques IV "le Rechin" Comte d'Anjou ceded Château-Landon and Gâtinais to him in 1069, in return for the king's recognition of his accession as count[255].  King Philippe pursued this policy of expanding his territories, adding C
2 CONC orbie in 1074, acquiring part of Vermandois on the death of Raoul Comte de Vermandois in 1074, invading Vexin in 1077, and taking possession of Bourges in 1100[256].  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1071, after ineffectively helping Arnoul III Count of Flanders against his uncle Robert, the latter made peace with King Philippe and arranged the king's marriage to his stepdaughter.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Regum Francorum Monasterii Sancti Dionysii records the death "apud Milidunum IV Kal Aug" of King Philippe and his burial "in ecclesia sancti Benedicti super Ligerim in pago Aurelianensi"[257].  The necrology of the ab
2 CONC bey of Saint-Germain-des-Prés records the death "III Kal Aug" of "Philippus rex Francorum"[258].  The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Denis records the death "III Kal Aug" of "Philippus rex"[259]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Betrothed ([1055/59]) to JUDITH [Maria/Sophia] of Germany, daughter of Emperor HEINRICH III King of Germany & his second wife Agnès de Poitou ([1054]-14 Mar [1092/96], bur Admont Abbey).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gesta Hungarorum records that King András forced the marriage of "Salomoni regi" and "Henricus imperator…Sophiam suam filiam", specifying that she had earlier been betrothed to "filio regis Franciæ"[260].  This could only refe
2 CONC r to the future Philippe I King of France as it is unlikely that the emperor's daughter would have been betrothed to his younger brother.  This betrothal is not corroborated in the western European primary sources so far consulted
2 CONC . 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (1072, repudiated 1092) BERTHA of Holland, daughter of FLORIS I Count of Holland & his wife Gertrud of Saxony[-Billung] ([1058]-Montreuil-sur-Mer, Pas-de-Calais 30 Jul 1093).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Liber Modernorum Regum Francorum records the marriage of "filiam ducis Frisiæ" and "rex Philippus"[261].  The Historia Francorum names "filiam Florentii ducis Frisonum Bertam" as wife of King Philippe[262].  The Chronologia Jo
2 CONC hannes de Beke names (in order) "Theodricum et Florencium…et Machtildim" as children of Count Floris & his wife, specifying that "Machtildim" married "Philippus rex Francie" after the death of her father which indicates that "Mach
2 CONC tildim" in this text is an error for Bertha[263].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her marriage was arranged as part of the settlement under which her future husband recognised her stepfather as Count of Flanders[264].  
2 CONT
2 CONT She was repudiated after King Philippe abducted Bertrade de Montfort from her husband, and was sent to Montreuil[265]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (Paris 1092, before 27 Oct) as her second husband, BERTRADE de Montfort, fifth wife of FOULQUES IV “le Réchin” Comte d’Anjou, daughter of SIMON [I] de Montfort-l'Amaury & his third wife Agnès d’Evreux (-Fontevrault end-
2 CONC 1115/1116, bur church of the priory of Hautes-Bruyères, Saint-Rémy-l’Honoré, Yvelines).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The De Genere Comitum Flandrensium, Notæ Parisienses names "Fulconi Richin Andegavensi comiti uxorem suam nomine Bertradam" as second wife of King Philippe, specifying that the king abducted her from her first husband after repudi
2 CONC ating his first wife[266].  William of Tyre records this marriage[267].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Pope Urban II at the Council of Autun excommunicated the king 16 Oct 1094, confirmed at the Council of Clermont 18/28 Nov 1095[268].  The church finally admitted the validity of the marriage after the Council of Paris 2 Dec 1104[2
2 CONC 69].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Orderic Vitalis alleges that Bertrade tried to poison her stepson Louis so her own sons could succeed to the throne[270].  "Fulco iunior Andegavensium comes Fulconis comitis filius" donated property to the abbey of Fontevraud with
2 CONC  the consent of "Bertrade regina matre meo, Philipo fratre meo" by charter dated to [1109/1112/13][271]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Philippe I & his first wife had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  CONSTANCE de France ([1078]-14 Sep 1126[272]).  Orderic Vitalis names "Ludovicum-Tedbaldum et Constantiam" as the children of Philippe I King of France and his wife "Bertrandam, Florentii Frisiorum ducis filiam"[273].  The His
2 CONC toria Regum Francorum Monasterii Sancti Dionysii names "Ludovicum regem et filiam unam Constanciam" as children of "Philippus rex [et] uxorem sororem Roberti Flandrensis comitis", specifying that Constance married firstly "Hugoni 
2 CONC Trecharum comiti", from whom she was separated for consanguinity, and secondly "Boamundo apud Carnotho"[274].  Orderic Vitalis confirms her parentage and her two marriages[275], recording 1106 as the year of her second marriage in
2 CONC  an earlier passage[276].   
2 CONT
2 CONT An early sign of possible difficulties in Constance's first marriage is shown by the charter dated 1102 under which "Constantia, Philippi regis Francorum filia…Hugonis comitis Trecensium coniux legitima" donated property to the ab
2 CONC bey of Molesme[277], the suggestion being that the reference to "coniux legitima" indicates that her husband may have had another "unofficial" relationship at this time.  "Hugo comes Campanie Teotbaldi comitis filius" donated prop
2 CONC erty to the abbey of Molesme by charter dated 2 Apr 1104, which names "frater meus Odo comes, Constantiam regis Francorum filiam necnon et comitissam Adelaidem uxorem fratris mei comitis Stephani nepotes…" and is subscribed by "Te
2 CONC otbaldus puer filius Stephani comitis nepos huius comitis Hugonis"[278].  
2 CONT
2 CONT William of Tyre names her, and her father, when he records her (second) marriage[279].  Suger's Vita Ludovici records the marriage of "Antiochenum principem Boamundum" and "domini Ludovici…sororem Constantiam" at Chartres, mention
2 CONC ing her previous marriage to "comitem Trecensem Hugonem"[280].  The Lignages d'Outremer name "Costance la fille le roy de Franche" as wife of "Beymont" son of "Robert Guichart qui conquest Puille"[281].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her second marriage was arranged by Adela Ctss de Blois while Prince Bohémond was in France canvassing support against Byzantium.  After her second marriage, she remained with her husband in Apulia and never visited Palestine[282]
2 CONC .  
2 CONT
2 CONT She was regent for her son in Italy after the death of her second husband[283].  She claimed the title "Queen" as daughter of the king of France.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Romoaldi Annales record that "regina Constancia" was captured by "comite Alexandro et Grimoaldo Barense in Umenatia civitate" and taken to Bari in Aug, dated to 1119[284].  The Annales Ceccanenses record that "reginam Boamundi
2 CONC " was freed from Bari in 1120, after the intercession of Pope Calixtus II[285].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ([1093/95], annulled Soissons 25 Dec 1104 on grounds of consanguinity[286]) as his first wife, HUGUES I de Blois Comte de Troyes, son of THIBAUT III Comte de Blois & his third wife Alix de Crépy-Valois (-Palestine 14 Jun
2 CONC  1126).  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (Chartres [25 Mar/26 May] 1106) BOHEMOND I Prince of Antioch, son of ROBERT “Guiscard” Duke of Apulia and Calabria [Sicily] & his first wife Alberada di Buonalberga (1052-Canosa di Puglia, Apulia 6/7 Mar 1111, bur Cathe
2 CONC dral of Canosa di Puglia).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  LOUIS THIBAUT de France (Paris end 1081-Château Bethizy near Paris 1 Aug 1137, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Regum Francorum Monasterii Sancti Dionysii names "Ludovicum regem et filiam unam Constanciam" as children of "Philippus rex [et] uxorem sororem Roberti Flandrensis comitis"[287].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 1108 as LOUIS VI "le Gros" King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. HENRI de France (1083-young).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum names (in order) "Ludowicum et filiam Constantiam [atque Henricum]" as the children of King Philippe and "filiam Florentii ducis Frisonum Bertam"[288]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Philippe I & his second wife had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  PHILIPPE de France ([1093]-[2 Sep] after 1133).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Regum Francorum Monasterii Sancti Dionysii names "Philippum et Florum et filiam unam" as children of "Philippus rex [et] Fulconi Rechin Andagavorum comiti uxorem"[289].  His parentage is also recorded by Orderic Vital
2 CONC is[290].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded as Seigneur de Montlhéry in 1104 by right of his wife.  
2 CONT
2 CONT His half-brother installed him as Comte de Mantes and Seigneur de Mehun-sur-Yèvre in [1104].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Suger's Vita Ludovici records the rebellion of "regis Ludovici Philippus frater" against his brother, supported by "Amalricus de Monte Forti…avunculus eius" and "Fulco comes Andegavensis postea rex Hierosolymitanus frater eius", a
2 CONC nd the confiscation of his castles of Montlhéry and Mantes[291].  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Fulco iunior Andegavensium comes Fulconis comitis filius" donated property to the abbey of Fontevraud with the consent of "Bertrade regina matre meo, Philipo fratre meo" by charter dated to [1109/1112/13][292].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The necrology of Saint-Germain L'Auxerrois records the death "IV Non Sep" of "Philippus frater Ludovici regis"[293], which may refer to Philippe Comte de Mantes.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1104) ELISABETH de Montlhéry Dame de Montlhéry, daughter and heiress of GUY [III] “Troussel” Seigneur de Montlhéry et de Chevreuse & his wife Mabile [Adelais] --- (-after 3 Mar 1141).  The Chronica Regum Francorum records the b
2 CONC etrothal of "unus illorum Regis illegitimus ex comitissa Andegavensi" and "filiam…Milonis de Montlehery"[294].  The dating clause of a charter dated to [1106/07] refers to the first year in which "Philippus filius Philippi regis F
2 CONC rancorum" married "Helizabeth filiam Guidonis Trosselli"[295]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. FLEURI [Florus] de France ([1095]-after 1119).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Regum Francorum Monasterii Sancti Dionysii names "Philippum et Florum et filiam unam" children of "Philippus rex [et] Fulconi Rechin Andagavorum comiti uxorem"[296].  His parentage is also recorded by Orderic Vitalis[
2 CONC 297].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Living in Anjou with his mother in 1117.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Seigneur de Nangis, by right of his wife.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m --- de Nangis, daughter & heiress of --- de Nangis.  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Fleuri & his wife had one child.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. CECILE de France ([1097]-after 1145).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Regum Francorum Monasterii Sancti Dionysii names "Philippum et Florum et filiam unam" as children of "Philippus rex [et] Fulconi Rechin Andagavorum comiti uxorem", specifying that the (unnamed) daughter married "Tanch
2 CONC redus Anthiochenus"[299].  Her parentage is recorded by William of Tyre, who also records her two marriages[300].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her first marriage was arranged while Bohémond I Prince of Antioch was visiting the French court seeking support against Alexios I Emperor of Byzantium.  She sailed for Antioch end 1106[301].  
2 CONT
2 CONT While dying, Prince Tancred made Pons de Toulouse promise to marry his wife[302].  Albert of Aix records the marriage at Tripoli of "Punctus filius Bertrannus de Tripla" and "uxorem Tancredi, quæ filia erat regis Franciæ", dated t
2 CONC o [1115] from the context[303].  William of Tyre refers to the wife of the count of Tripoli as uterine sister of Foulques King of Jerusalem and names her[304].  She claimed Jebail as her dower, but was eventually satisfied with Ch
2 CONC astel Rouge and Arzghan[305].  
2 CONT
2 CONT She became Lady of Tarsus and Mamistra, in Cilician Armenia, in 1126[306].  "Cecilia comitissa" donated property for the souls of "domini mei Poncii comitis…et filii mei Raimundi comitis" to the church of the Holy Sepulchre, Jerus
2 CONC alem by charter dated 1139[307].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (late 1106) TANCRED Prince of Tiberias, Regent of Antioch, son of ODO [Guillaume] “le Bon” Marquis & his wife Emma de Hauteville (-12 Dec 1112).  He succeeded in 1111 as Prince of Antioch.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (Tripoli 1112) PONS Count of Tripoli, son of BERTRAND Comte de Toulouse and Tripoli & his second wife Hélie de Bourgogne [Capet] ([1096]-executed near Pilgrim Castle, near Tripoli Mar 1137). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. EUSTACHIE de France (-1143).  
2 CONT
2 CONT She and her husband are named by Kerrebrouck who cites no primary source on which this is based[308].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Founded the Abbaye de Yerres.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m JEAN Seigneur d'Etampes et de Corbeil, son of HUGUES de Breteuil Seigneur du Puiset et d’Etampes & his wife ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Philippe had one [probably illegitimate] child [by an unknown mistress]: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. EUDES de France (-1096).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the death in 1096 of "Odo frater Ludovici Grossi de alia matre"[309].  According to Europäische Stammtafeln[310], Eudes was the son of King Philippe & his first wife but the prim
2 CONC ary source on which this is based has not been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Kerrebrouck[311], Eudes was the son of King Philippe's second marriage but there seems little time for a third child to have been born to Bertrade de Montfort during the first three years of her marriage.  It is there
2 CONC fore more likely that he was an illegitimate son of King Philippe. 
2 CONT
