2 TITL Guillaume Bras-de-Fer 1012-1046
1 NOTE William Iron Arm[1] (born before 1010 - died 1046) was a Norman adventurer, founder of the fortunes of the Hauteville family. One of twelve sons of Tancred of Hauteville, he journeyed to the Mezzogiorno with his younger brother Dr
2 CONC ogo in the first half of the eleventh century (c.1035), in response to requests for help made by fellow Normans under Rainulf Drengot, count of Aversa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1038 and 1040, he and other Normans fought in Sicily along with the Lombards as mercenaries for the Byzantine Empire against the Saracens. It was there that he won his nickname Iron Arm, by single-handedly killing the emir
2 CONC  of Syracuse during a sally at the siege of Syracuse. When the Greek general Giorgio Maniace publicly humiliated the Salernitan leader, Arduin, the Lombards withdrew from the campaign, along with the Normans and the Varangian Guar
2 CONC d contingent. After Maniace was recalled to Constantinople, the new catapan of Italy, Michael Doukeianos, appointed Arduin the ruler of Melfi. Melfi, however, soon joined other Apulian Lombards in a revolt against Byzantine rule, 
2 CONC in which they were supported by William and the Normans. The Byzantines, however, managed to buy off the nominal leaders of the revolt - first Atenulf, Prince of Benevento, and then Argyrus. In September 1042, the Normans elected 
2 CONC their own leader, ignoring Arduin. The revolt, originally Lombard, had become Norman in character and leadership.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was elected by Normans as their "count" after the defection of Argyrus. He and the other leaders, chief among them Drogo and Peter, petitioned Guaimar IV, Prince of Salerno, for recognition of their conquests. They receive
2 CONC d the lands around Melfi as a fief and proclaimed Guaimar "Duke of Apulia and Calabria". At Melfi in 1043, Guaimar divided the region (except for Melfi itself) into twelve baronies for the benefit of the Norman leaders: Asclettin 
2 CONC received Acerenza, Tristan received Montepeloso, Hugh Tubœuf received Monopoli, Peter received Trani, and Drogo received Venosa. William himself, predominant among the Norman leaders, received the lordship of Ascoli. He was marrie
2 CONC d to Guida, daughter of Guy, duke of Sorrento, and niece of Guaimar.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his reign, William and Guaimar began the conquest of Calabria in 1044 and built the great castle of Stridula, probably near Squillace. In 1045, he was defeated near Taranto by Argyrus. He died in early 1046 and was succeede
2 CONC d by his brother Drogo.
2 CONT
2 CONT His titles were never confirmed by the Holy Roman Emperor. Drogo would be legally called "Count of the Normans in all Apulia and Calabria" (Comes Normannorum totius Apuliae e Calabriae), and so William is usually titled likewise.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT Ghisalberti, Albert (ed). Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani: II Albicante - Ammannati. Rome, 1960. 
2 CONT Gwatkin, H.M., Whitney, J.P. (ed) et al. The Cambridge Medieval History: Volume III. Cambridge University Press, 1926. 
2 CONT Norwich, John Julius. The Normans in the South 1016-1130. Longmans: London, 1967. 
2 CONT Chalandon, Ferdinand. Histoire de la domination normande en Italie et en Sicilie. Paris, 1907. 
2 CONT Gravett, Christopher, and Nicolle, David. The Normans: Warrior Knights and their Castles. Osprey Publishing: Oxford, 2006. 
2 CONT Beech, George. A Norman-Italian Adventurer in the East: Richard of Salerno. 1993. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Notes
2 CONT ^ Guillaume Bras-de-fer in French, Guglielmo Braccio di Ferro in Italian and Gugghiermu Vrazzu di Ferru in Sicilian. 
