2 CONT
2 CONT 6.         ONFROI de Hauteville ([1015/20]-spring 1057, bur Monastery of Santissima Trinità, Venosa).  
2 CONT -----
2 CONT - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humphrey_of_Hauteville /!\ 
2 CONT
2 CONT Humphrey of Hauteville[1] (c. 1010 – August 1057), surnamed Abagelard, was the Count of Apulia and Calabria from 1051 to his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Humphrey was probably the youngest son of Tancred of Hauteville by his first wife Muriel. Some sources make Geoffrey and Serlo his younger brothers. He is usually said to have accompanied his elder brothers William and Drogo to th
2 CONC e Mezzogiorno circa 1035 on the basis of Goffredo Malaterra; however, he may have arrived later, in 1044, during the reign of his elder brother William. Around 1047, he was granted the lordship (or countship) of Lavello by his bro
2 CONC ther Drogo, whom he succeeded as Count of Apulia in August 1051. In his early years in Lavello, he employed a young Richard Drengot, who was later to serve him against the pope. In 1053, Humphrey received his three brothers, Geoff
2 CONC rey and their half-brothers Mauger and a younger William, on their arrival in Italy. He granted Mauger the Capitanate and William the Principate.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reign began amid the troubles which had ended his brother's. Humphrey vigorously punished the insitgators of his brother's assassination, especially the principal murderer. Many Norman knights were in rebellion and pillaging p
2 CONC apal lands. Guaimar IV of Salerno supported Humphrey's succession, but he was soon assassinated. Pope Leo IX organised a coalition against the Normans and marched south. The pope's forces and those of the Normans fought the Battle
2 CONC  of Civitate near Civitate sul Fortore on 18 June 1053. Humphrey led the armies of the Hautevilles (assisted by his younger half-brother Robert Guiscard) and Drengots (assisted by Richard Drengot) against the combined forces of th
2 CONC e Papacy and the Holy Roman Empire. The Normans destroyed the papal army and captured the pope, whom they imprisoned in Benevento, which they had been authorised by the emperor to capture in 1047. They finally released him on 12 M
2 CONC arch 1054. Leo died soon after.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the aftermath of Civitate, the Normans under Humphrey took advantage of the severely weakened papacy to further their conquest. He took Oria, Nardò, and Lecce by the end of 1055. Robert Guiscard, the hero of Civitate, meanwhile
2 CONC  conquered Minervino, Otranto, and Gallipoli before Humphrey sent him back to Calabria in fear of his growing power and influence. Upon his death in 1057 (or 1056 according to some sources), Humphrey was succeeded as count by Robe
2 CONC rt. Humphrey had given Guiscard the guardianship of his young sons, but Guiscard confiscated their inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Relatives
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Relatives
2 CONT
2 CONT Humphrey's wife was called a "sister of the Duke of Sorrento" by Amatus of Montecassino. This would make her Gaitelgrima, a daughter of Guaimar III of Salerno. It is often stated that his wife was Gaitelgrima, the widow of his bro
2 CONC ther Drogo, however this is impossible[2]. Humphrey had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Abagelard, born after 1044 and died in Greece in 1081
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Herman, born after 1045 and died in Byzantium in 1097
2 CONT
2 CONT We know that Humphrey also had at least one daughter because of a story told by Amatus. Abelard was fleeing Robert Guiscard with "Gradilon, the husband of his sister" in 1078 when in revolt against his uncle. Gradilon was captured
2 CONC  by the Guiscard's troops near Trevico in the summer of 1079 and blinded.
2 CONT
