2 CONT
2 CONT '''Crawford, Francis Marion'''. (1900). [https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Rulers_of_the_South/fWrxgyWCFtkC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22Judith+of+Évreux%22&pg=PA205&printsec=frontcover ''The Rulers of the South: Sicily, Calabria, Ma
2 CONC lta''], Vol 2. New York: Macmillan Co. pp. 205-6, 391.
2 CONT >“Once Messina was secured, the brothers returned to the mainland, Roger to his base at Mileto in Calabria. Here he was married to Judith d’Évreux, daughter of a cousin of William the Conqueror, described as “beautiful and of exce
2 CONC llent parentage”.[6] It was a match that went back several years. It did not prevent him returning shortly afterwards to Sicily. Here he was welcomed by the Christian citizens of Troina, in the interior of the island, a city Roger
2 CONC  made his temporary base.”
2 CONT >In Sicily, “Roger learned that the lovely daughter of William Evreux, with whom he is said to have fallen in love on his way to Italy, had arrived in Calabria and was ready to marry him. Judith of Evreux was the great granddaught
2 CONC er of Richard the First of Normandy, and had just escaped from that country with her sister and half-brother, the latter having for some reason incurred the dangerous wrath of William, who was soon to be called the Conqueror. Judi
2 CONC th is now believed to have been the same person as Eremberga, and to have adopted the latter name when she left the convent in which she was brought up.“
2 CONT >“1061 — Count Roger marries Judith, daughter of William of Evreux, at Mileto.”
2 CONT >“1089 — Judith, wife of Count Roger dies.”
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Byfield, Ted, and Paul Stanway'''. (2004). [https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Quest_for_the_City_A_D_740_to_1100_P/68qCEMEZ6gwC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22Judith+of+Évreux%22&pg=PA242&printsec=frontcover ''The Quest for the City
2 CONC  A.D. 740-1100: Pursuing the Next World, They Founded this One'']. Edmonton, Alberta, Canada: Christian History Project.
2 CONT >'''p. 242''' – “Duke William, it seems, had seriously quarreled with his half-brother, the abbot of St. Evreux, a monastery renowned for the magnificence of its music. The abbot and his monks fled for Italy, taking with him three
2 CONC  children for whom the abbot was godfather. One of the was Judith, about seventeen years old. Duke Robert made them all welcome in Italy. The abbot established a new monastery there, and in one of its first services, he married Ju
2 CONC dith of Evreux to Roger Hauteville. The wedding music, people said, was magnificent.”
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Houben, Hubert'''. (2002). [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Roger_II_of_Sicily/Duwowbx1vuQC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22Judith+of+Évreux%22&pg=PA225&printsec=frontcover ''Roger II of Sicily: A Ruler Between East and West'']. Transl
2 CONC ated by Diane Milburn and Graham A. Loud. Cambridge University Press, 2002. 
2 CONT >“For Malaterra, the Count of Sicily [Roger II], represented the ideal model of a knight: ‘He was very handsome, tall, well proportioned, extremely eloquent, clever in decision making, farsighted in his plans, friendly and affable
2 CONC  with everyone, very strong, and fierce in battle.’ He was married three times. The count had more than ten children by his first two wives, the two Normans, Judith of Evreux and Eremburga of Mortain. When he was about fifty in 10
2 CONC 89/90, he married Adelaide del Vasto, who was barely fifteen.”
2 CONT
