1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andronikos_Doukas_Palaiologos
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in c. 1083/85, Andronikos was a son of the sebastos George Palaiologos and his wife Anna Doukaina.[1][2] His father was the son of the first known member of the Palaiologos line, became a distinguished general and was among t
2 CONC he most important supporters of Emperor Alexios I Komnenos (r. 1081–1118),[3] while his mother was the sister of Alexios' empress-consort, Irene Doukaina.[4] Andronikos himself was named after his maternal grandfather Andronikos D
2 CONC oukas, which according to Byzantine custom probably suggests that he was the second-born son (his older brother Nikephoros bearing the paternal grandfather's name); Andronikos seems also to have preferred his mother's surname of D
2 CONC oukas, by which he is referred to in the sources.[2][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Like his father, he held the court dignity of sebastos, but his early life is obscure. P. Gautier suggested that he is to be identified with the logothetes ton sekreton Andronikos Doukas, active under Alexios I, possibly after 110
2 CONC 9. The only definitive information about his career is that he served as governor of Thessalonica. The Timarion, a satirical dialogue placed in the city, alludes to him without naming him, while an act preserved in the Docheiariou
2 CONC  monastery records the "pansebastos sebastos Andronikos Doukas" serving as "doux and praetor" of Thessalonica in January/February 1112.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos died young of heart failure, predeceasing his parents. His death is placed c. 1115/18.[1][4] According to Demetrios Polemis, he was survived by his wife—Polemis surmises that this was a daughter of the porphyrogennete Z
2 CONC oe Doukaina, youngest daughter of Constantine X Doukas and wife of Adrianos Komnenos—and was possibly the father of the megas hetaireiarches George Palaiologos (who may alternatively have been his brother),[7] but Jean-Claude Chey
2 CONC net and Jean-François Vannier believe that he had no offspring.[8] The court poet Nicholas Kallikles wrote an epitaph and four other poems in his honour,[9][10] and his parents were later buried in the same tomb following their de
2 CONC ath.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT   Family  ( https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Palaiologos )
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/George-Palaiologos/6000000007297857008 George] had a younger brother, Nicholas. Through his marriage to Anna Doukaina, he had four sons:[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT *Nikephoros, one of the ancestors of the Palaiologos family that became the Empire's ruling dynasty after 1261. He had issue:
2 CONT **Michael
2 CONT ***Alexios, megas doux. Married to Irene Komnene.
2 CONT ****Andronikos Palaiologos, megas domestikos
2 CONT *'''Andronikos, doux of Thessalonica'''. Married to Komnene, granddaughter of Constantine X Doukas.
2 CONT *Michael, a sebastos
2 CONT *Alexios, married Anna Komnene Doukaina and he is the other ancestor of the imperial house of Palaiologos. He had issue:
2 CONT **George Palaiologos, megas hetaireiarches
2 CONT ***Alexios despot. Married to Irene Angelina (daughter of Alexios III Angelos).
2 CONT ****Theodora Palaiologina, married to Andronikos Palaiologos, megas domestikos
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------
