1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Michael Taronites (Greek: Μιχαήλ Ταρωνίτης) was a Byzantine aristocrat and brother-in-law of Emperor Alexios I Komnenos. He was involved in a conspiracy against him and was banished in 1094. https://en.wikipedia.or
2 CONC g/wiki/Michael_Taronites
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT * Kazhdan, Alexander, ed. (1991). The Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium. Oxford and New York: Oxford University Press. ISBN 0-19-504652-8.
2 CONT * Skoulatos, Basile (1980). Les personnages byzantins de l'Alexiade: Analyse prosopographique et synthèse [The Byzantine Personalities of the Alexiad: Prosopographical Analysis and Synthesis] (in French). Louvain-la-Neuve: Nauwela
2 CONC erts.
2 CONT * Varzos, Konstantinos (1984). Η Γενεαλογία των Κομνηνών [The Genealogy of the Komnenoi] (PDF) (in Greek). A. Thessaloniki: Centre for Byzantine Studies, University of Thessaloniki.
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BYZANTINE%20NOBILITY.htm#MikahelTaronitesdiedafter1094
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         MIKHAEL Taronites [I] (-[12 Mar] after [1094]).  Nikeforos Bryennios records that "Manuel" (Comnenos) was captured by the Turks "cum duobus sororem suarum viris, Melisseno et Taronita"[801].  The Alexeiad records, in a 
2 CONC passage dateable to 1081, that "Taronites who add married the emperor´s sister Maria" was created protosébastos and protovestarios by Emperor Alexios I, and "not long afterwards…promoted to the rank of panhypersébastos with the ri
2 CONC ght to sit with the Cæsar"[802].  The Codinus Curopalatis records that Emperor Alexios awarded the title panhypersébastos to "Michaelem Taronitem sororis coniugem"[803].  The Alexeiad records that "Michael Taronites, his sister´s 
2 CONC husband" was "also sent into exile" by the emperor, in a passage dateable to [1093/93], and his assets confiscated for alleged involvement in the plot of Nikeforos Diogenes against Emperor Alexios I[804].  The list of obituaries o
2 CONC f Empress Eirene Doukas's family records the death "12 Mar, monk Michael Eumathius"[805], which Morris Bierbrier suggests may refer to Mikhael Taronites[806].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ([1060/65]) MARIA Komnene, daughter of IOANNES Komnenos, kuropalates and domestikos & his wife Anna Dalassena ([1044/49]-[18 Aug] ----).   Nikeforos Bryennios names (in order) "Maria, Eudocia et Theodora" as the three daug
2 CONC hters of "Ioanni" and his wife Anna[807].  Nikeforos Bryennios records the marriage of "Ioannes…Comnenus curopalates…maior…natu [filia] Maria" and "Taronitæ Michaeli"[808].  Her origin is also deduced from the Alexeiad naming "Mik
2 CONC hael" as the husband of the niece of the Komnenoi brothers, although the text does not name her or her daughter[809].  The relationship is clarified in a later passage in the same source which records that "Taronites…had married t
2 CONC he emperor's sister Maria"[810].  It is not known whether Maria was older or younger than her brother Isaakios.  The date of her marriage is estimated approximately based on her supposed birth date.  She became a nun as ANNA.  The
2 CONC  list of obituaries of the monastery of Christ Philanthropos, founded by Empress Eirene Doukas, records the death 18 Aug of "kyras Marias tis autadelfou tou basileos"[811].  This entry is assumed to relate to Maria, sister of Empe
2 CONC ror Alexios I, but it is not an ideal match as it is surprising that it does not record her monastic name.  No indication has been found of the year of Maria’s death.  
2 CONT
2 CONT  Mikhael Taronites & his wife had [four] children: 
