1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Historically, tracking figures like '''Theodora Batatzaina''' (or Vatatzina) alongside Emperor '''Manuel I Komnenos''' requires separating verified primary history from crowdsourced speculation:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''The Identity Confusion:''' The profile merges or misattributes elements of Byzantine nomenclature. While the Batatzes (Vatatzes) family became prominent later—particularly during the Empire of Nicea (such as Emperor John III 
2 CONC Doukas Vatatzes)—individuals bearing these names in the 12th century often suffer from "pedigree collapse" or speculative attachment in amateur genealogy platforms.
2 CONT * '''The Manuel I Komnenos Connection:''' Emperor Manuel I Komnenos (reigned 1143–1180) did have notable royal mistresses and relatives named Theodora—most famously his niece Theodora Komnene (who fled with and bore children to An
2 CONC dronikos Komnenos)—rather than a documented consort from the Batatzes family of this specific timeline. 
2 CONT * '''The Chronological and Family Puzzle:''' Attributing a birth circa 1162 and death around 1191 with an offspring holding a high court title like ''sebastokrator Alexios'' points to the exact type of unvetted synthesis that gene
2 CONC rates historical friction. Without contemporaneous primary chronicles (like Niketas Choniates) explicitly linking a Theodora Batatzaina to these specific maternity and partnership roles, these connections remain speculative geneal
2 CONC ogical constructions.
