1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agnes_of_France,_Byzantine_Empress
2 CONT --------
2 CONT Agnes of France (1171–1240), who was betrothed to Alexius II Comnenus (1180–1183) but married (1) Andronicus I Comnenus (1183–1185); (2) Theodore Branas (1204).
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agnes_of_France_(Byzantine_empress)
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes of France (1171 – after 1204) was a daughter of Louis VII of France by his third wife Adèle of Champagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a younger half-sister of Marie de Champagne, Alix of France, Marguerite of France and Alys, Countess of the Vexin. She was a younger full sister of Philip II of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Betrothal and marriage 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Her later life 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Agnes of France in historical legend and in modern fiction 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Sources 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 Bibliography 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Betrothal and marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In early 1178, Philip, Count of Flanders visited Constantinople on his way back from the Holy Land. The Eastern Roman Emperor Manuel I Komnenos, who had already entertained Louis VII in Constantinople at Christmas 1147 during the 
2 CONC Second Crusade, was perhaps finally convinced by Philip that France would be a desirable ally in Western Europe. Over the winter of 1178-1179 an Imperial embassy accompanying Philip, and led by the Genoese Baldovino Guercio,[1] wa
2 CONC s sent to the French court to secure a match between Agnes and Alexios, the only son and heir apparent of Manuel by his second wife Maria of Antioch. This or some similar marriage alliance had been favoured by Pope Alexander III a
2 CONC s early as 1171.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT It was not uncommon for princesses, when a future marriage had been agreed, to be brought up in their intended husband's family; this, indeed, is why Agnes probably never met her elder sister Alys, who lived in the Kingdom of Engl
2 CONC and from the age of about nine, when her marriage to the future Richard I of England was agreed on (though this marriage never took place). Agnes took ship in Montpellier, bound for Constantinople, at Easter 1179. At Genoa the flo
2 CONC tilla increased from 5 to 19 ships, captained by Baldovino Guercio.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT On arrival in Constantinople in late summer 1179 Agnes was received with lavish festivities. She was greeted with an oration from Eustathios, former Master of the Rhetors and archbishop of Thessalonica.[4] She was perhaps now pres
2 CONC ented with an elaborate volume of welcoming verses by an anonymous author, sometimes called the Eisiterion.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to William of Tyre, Agnes was eight on her arrival at Constantinople, while Alexios was thirteen. William got Alexios's age wrong (he was born on 14 September 1169)[5] and there is no other source for Agnes's year of bir
2 CONC th. If she was in fact eight, she was at least three years too young for marriage, according to most 12th century views.[6] However, William of Tyre, who was present at the ceremony, seems to describe it as a full wedding (matrimo
2 CONC nii legibus ... copulare); in this he is followed by some other non-Byzantine sources and by many modern authors.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT The ceremony took place in the Trullos Hall, in the Great Palace, on 2 March 1180. Agnes was officially renamed Anna. Eustathios of Thessalonica produced a speech to celebrate the occasion, whose title in the manuscript is Oration
2 CONC  on the Public Celebrations of the Betrothal of the Two Royal Children.[8] This ceremony came approximately one month after the wedding of Alexios's half-sister Maria Porphyrogenita to Renier of Montferrat, conducted by the Patria
2 CONC rch of Constantinople, Theodosios.
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 24, 1180, Manuel died and Alexios succeeded him as Emperor. He was too young to rule unaided; his mother, Maria of Antioch, exercised more influence in affairs of state than Alexios or Anna.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1183 Maria of Antioch was dispaced by a new power behind the throne, Andronikos I Komnenos. Andronikos was a first cousin of Manuel and was known to have harbored imperial ambitions for himself. He is believed to have arranged 
2 CONC the deaths by poisoning of Maria Porphyrogenita and her husband Renier; he certainly imprisoned, and soon afterwards executed, Maria of Antioch.[9] Andronikos was crowned co-ruler with Alexios; then, in October of the same year, h
2 CONC e had Alexios strangled. Anna was now 12, and the approximately 65-year-old Andronikos married her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos had previously been married (his first wife's name is unknown). He had had sexual relationships with two nieces (Eudokia Komnene and Theodora Komnene) and with Philippa of Antioch. Philippa was a daughter of Constance o
2 CONC f Antioch and her first husband and consort Raymond of Poitiers; she was also a sister of Maria of Antioch and thus maternal aunt of Alexios. Andronikos had two sons by his first wife; he also had a young son and daughter from his
2 CONC  affair with Theodora. His eldest son, Manuel already had a son of his own, the future Alexios I of Trebizond.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna was Empress consort for two years, until the deposition of Andronikos in September 1185. In an attempt to escape the popular uprising that ended his rule, Andronikos fled from Constantinople with Anna and his mistress (known 
2 CONC only as Maraptike). They reached Chele, a fortress on the Bithynian coast of the Black Sea, where they tried to take ship for the Crimea. Their ship was prevented from sailing by contrary winds. Andronikos was eventually captured 
2 CONC and returned to the capital,[10] where he was tortured and killed on September 12, 1185.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Her later life
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna survived his fall and is next heard of in 1193, when she is said by a Western chronicler to have become the lover of Theodore Branas,[11] a military leader who fought on the Empire's northern frontier. They did not at first m
2 CONC arry, perhaps because by marrying a commoner she would have lost her dowry.
2 CONT
2 CONT They remained together and eventually married, at the urging of the Latin emperor Baldwin I of Constantinople, in summer 1204.[12] Theodore Branas continued to fight for the Latin Empire, and is last heard of in 1219, by which tim
2 CONC e Agnes has already disappeared from the historical record. They had at least one daughter, who married Narjot de Toucy.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Agnes of France in historical legend and in modern fiction
2 CONT
2 CONT The crusader Robert of Clari, writing only 25 years after the event, is clear about the rich entourage that accompanied Agnes to Constantinople:
2 CONT
2 CONT then the king arrayed his sister very richly and sent her with the messengers to Constantinople, and many of his people with her ... When they were come, the emperor did very great honor to the damsel and made great rejoicing over
2 CONC  her and her people ... 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert then betrays his ignorance of a period which for him was already legendary in attributing the embassy to Agnes's brother, Philip II of France. In fact Philip did not succeed to the French throne until 18 September 1180.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes is the subject of the historical novel Agnes of France (1980) by Greek writer Kostas Kyriazis (1920 - ). The novel describes the events of the reigns of Manuel, Alexios and Andronikos through her eyes. She is also part of th
2 CONC e cast of the sequels Fourth Crusade (1981) and Henry of Hainaut (1984). All three have been in print in Greece since their first edition.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes of France (Byzantine empress)
2 CONT
2 CONT Capetian dynasty
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 1171 Died: after 1204 
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal titles 
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Antioch Byzantine Empress consort
2 CONT
