1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodore_Komnenos_Doukas
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodore Komnenos Doukas or Theodore Comnenus Ducas (Greek: Θεόδωρος Κομνηνός Δούκας, Theodōros Komnēnos Doukas), ruler of Epirus from 1215 to 1230 and of Thessalonica from 1224 to 1230, died c. 1253.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Born about 1180/85, Theodore was a legitimate son of the sebastokratōr John Doukas and of Zoe Doukaina. He was thus a first cousin of the emperors Isaac II Angelos and Alexios III Angelos, and half-brother to the founder of the Ep
2 CONC irote principality, Michael I Komnenos Doukas. Initially in the service of Theodore I Laskaris of Nicaea, Theodore joined his half-brother Michael I in Epirus in c. 1210. When Michael was murdered, Theodore took his place and emba
2 CONC rked on a policy of aggressive expansion after allying himself with Serbia and the Albanian clans. Taking advantage of the temporary weakness of Bulgaria and the Kingdom of Thessalonica, Theodore seized most of Macedonia (with Ohr
2 CONC id) and Thessaly in c. 1216. In 1217, when the new Latin Emperor Peter II of Courtenay attempted to cross through Epirus to reach his lands, Theodore defeated and captured him. In 1220 he took Beroia, and in 1221 Serres and Drama,
2 CONC  tightening the noose around Thessalonica. In 1224 completed his conquest of the Kingdom of Thessalonica by taking its capital. Elated by his success, Theodore arranged for his coronation as emperor in 1225 or 1227 by the autoceph
2 CONC alous archbishop of Ohrid, Demetrios Chomatianos. Theodore's forces advanced through the Aegean coast of Thrace and in 1225 seized Adrianople and the surrounding portions of Thrace from the Nicaeans. Worried by the alliance of Iva
2 CONC n Asen II of Bulgaria with the Latin Empire of Constantinople, Theodore broke his treaty with Ivan Asen and invaded Bulgaria with a large army reinforced by western mercenaries in 1230. Allegedly affixing the text of the broken tr
2 CONC eaty to one of his spears as a flag, Ivan Asen II rallied his troops and defeated Theodore in the Battle of Klokotnitsa on March 9, 1230. Theodore was captured and remained a prisoner in the Bulgarian capital Tărnovo for seven yea
2 CONC rs. At some point during his captivity he became involved in a conspiracy and was blinded. Theodore's lands were divided between Ivan Asen II (who took over Thrace, Macedonia, and Albania), Theodore's brothers Manuel (who took The
2 CONC ssalonica) and Constantine (who took Acarnania), and Theodore's nephew Michael II (who took Epirus). In 1237 Theodore was released from captivity by Ivan Asen II who married his daughter Irene. Theodore recovered Thessalonica by c
2 CONC hasing out his brother Manuel and entrusted the city to his son John Komnenos Doukas and retired to Vodena. From here he attempted to unify the various members of his family against the encroachments of John III Doukas Vatatzes of
2 CONC  Nicaea, who was determined to intervene in Thessalonica. After the death of Ivan Asen II in 1241, Emperor John III invited Theodore to a conference in which he arrested him and in 1242 he marched on Thessalonica with Theodore in 
2 CONC tow. Theodore was sent in to negotiate with his son and convince him to accept demotion to the rank of despotes and to recognize the suzerainty of Nicaea. In 1246 John III overthrew Theodore's younger son Demetrios Angelos Doukas 
2 CONC and annexed Thessalonica. In 1252 he had Theodore arrested and sent him into exile in Nicaea, where he died c. 1253.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Family
2 CONT
2 CONT By his wife Maria Petraliphaina (sister of the sebastokratōr John Petraliphas) he had four children:
2 CONT *Anna Komnene, who married King Stefan Radoslav of Serbia
2 CONT *John Komnenos Doukas, who succeeded as ruler of Thessalonica in 1237
2 CONT *Irene Komnene, who married Emperor Ivan Asen II of Bulgaria
2 CONT *Demetrios Angelos Doukas, who succeeded as ruler of Thessalonica in 1244
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------
