2 CONT Fariburz ibn Sallar (Persian: فریبرز بن سالار‎), better simply known as Fariburz I (فریبرز), was the sixteenth Shah of Shirvan, ruling from 1063 to 1096. His reign saw many major political balance changes in Caucasus, including ex
2 CONC pansion by the Seljuqs. He was considered a ruler with great diplomatic skills, and his kingdom extended from Mughan to Kumuk and Alania.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Clash with the Shaddadids Hostilities with people of Derbent Clash with the Turks The rebellion of the son of Giorgi and the events that happened after Dispute with Alp Arslan, the invasion of Qum and the betrayal of Guzhdaham The
2 CONC  loss of Derbent, the accession of Malik-Shah I, the Shaddadid-Shirvanian alliance and the invasion of Arghar
2 CONT
2 CONT Fariburz later died in ca. 1096 and was succeeded by his son Manuchihr II. Although Fariburz was during his late reign a nominal vassal of Seljuqs, he minted his coins in his own name.[12] According to the Persian medieval poet Kh
2 CONC aqani, Fariburz was the first Shirvanshah ever to make a pilgrimage to Mecca.[13] His vizier was a Daylamite named Baha al-Din al-Kakui, whose family was descended from the military leader Makan ibn Kaki and held the vizier office
2 CONC  for several centuries.[14]
