2 CONT
2 CONT '''DAVIT''', son of MUHAMMAD Mughis ud-Din Turkan Shah of Erzerum & his wife Rusudan I Queen of Georgia (-1293).  He was sent to the Great Khan in 1243.  Believed by Georgian nobles to have disappeared two years later, they procla
2 CONC imed Davit, son of King Giorgi IV, as King of Georgia.  The Georgian Chronicle (18th century) records that, after the death of Queen Rusudan, the "fils de notre roi Lacha…David" was located, brought back to Georgia, and sent to th
2 CONC e Great Khan where his first cousin Davit, son of Queen Rusudan, was also found and both were recognised as king[647].  However, Davit emerged again and was recognised by the Great Khan as junior ruler of Georgia in 1249.  He esta
2 CONC blished himself at Kutais in 1258 as DAVIT VI "Narin/the Clever" King of Imerati.  The Georgian Chronicle (18th century) records the death of Davit after the death of his son Vakhtang)[648].  Brosset’s assessment of dates, after r
2 CONC eviewing other sources, dates the death of “[le] roi Narin-Dawith” to 1293[649]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly --- [Tornikaina], illegitimate daughter of [--- & his mistress --- Tornikaina] (-before 1268).  Pachymeres refers to "imperator…fratris…propriam coniugem" who had "filiam ex alio…naturalem sed illegitimam prolem" w
2 CONC ho married "Mepe Iberiæ Davidi"[650].  It is not clear from the passage which of Emperor Mikhael VIII's brothers is indicated.  If it refers to Ioannes, she was the daughter of his wife --- Tornikaine, assuming that the text is ac
2 CONC curate.  She is shown in Europäische Stammtafeln[651] as the daughter of Ioannes by his second marriage.  If the latter is correct, she must have been a child bride.  The Georgian Chronicle (18th century) records that the wife of 
2 CONC King Davit was "la reine, fille du grand Paléologue, souverain de Constantinople et de la Grèce"[652]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly (1268) ---.  The name of Davit's second wife is not known. 
