1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuqaq
2 CONT
2 CONT Tuqaq or Tuqaq beg (دوقاق دمور یالیق‎ or Duqaq (Turkish : Dukak bey; Turkmen : Dukak beg or Dukak Demir ýaýly; full name: Duqaq Temur Yalig) was a subashi (commander-in-chief) in Oghuz Yabgu State[1] and the father of Seljuq, epon
2 CONC ymous founder of the Seljuk dynasty. His great-grandson Tughril beg would establish Great Seljuk Empire in the 11th century.[2] "Tuqaq Temur Yalig" literally means "Iron bow" or "with an iron bow" in old Turkic language.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much is known about Tuqaq or his early activities, information is sparse and dubious, most of which come from sources written later, especially after the establishment of the Seljuk Empire or from oral tradition. It is widely 
2 CONC believed, however, that he and his famous descendants were from the Qiniq tribe of the Oghuz Turks[3]. His father's name was Kerequchi, who was a renowned local blacksmith or master of making tents (yurts). Somewhat reliable sourc
2 CONC es also tell us that at the latter stages of his life, Tuqaq was in the military service of the Oghuz Yabgu State and held important position among the Oghuz Turks. His nickname "Iron bow" tells us that he most probably was not an
2 CONC  ordinary soldier. In Oghuz culture, arrow and bow are considered as a "sign of sovereignty" and considering Duqaq's nickname, he was a commander-in-chief of the Oghuz army[4] or probably a powerful statesman, who most definitely 
2 CONC had great power and influence in Oghuz Yabgu State. Affiliations Persian epic "Maliqnamah" (Book of Kings) mentions a warrior called Tuqaq who was in the service of the Khazar Khagan. Probably, Tuqaq served the Khazars until a dis
2 CONC mal decline of their state, which intensified under the pressure of Cumans,[5] and then made alliance with the Oghuz Yabgu state with which he stayed for the rest of his life. 
