2 CONT Helena (c. 1158 – 25 May 1199), wife of Duke Leopold V of Austria. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret (Margit) (1162 – ?), born posthumously; wife firstly of Isaac Macrodukas and secondly of András, Obergespan of Somogy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Konge av Ungarn 1141-1162.
2 CONT
2 CONT Geza seiret i kamp mot russere og greker. I hans regjeringstid utvandret tallrike Sachsere til Ungarn. Han lot dem i 1143 bosette seg i grevskapeet Scepuze (Szepes, Zeps) og flere områder i Transylvanien, hvor deres etterkommere l
2 CONC evere ennu idag.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tekst: Tore Nygaard
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilder:
2 CONT
2 CONT Erich Brandenburg: Die Nachkommen Karls des Grossen, Leipzig 1935. N. de Baumgarten: Généalogie et Mariage occidenteaux des Rurikides Russes du Xe au XIIIe Siècle. Mogens Bugge: Våre forfedre, nr. 1153. Bent og Vidar Billing Hanse
2 CONC n: Rosensverdslektens forfedre, side 18.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Géza II (Hungarian: II. Géza, Croatian: Gejza I, Slovak: Gejza II), (1130, Tolna – 31 May 1162), King of Hungary and Croatia (1141–1162). He ascended the throne as a child and during his minority the kingdom was governed by his mo
2 CONC ther. He was one of the most powerful monarchs of Hungary, who could intervene successfully in the internal affairs of the neighbouring countries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Géza was the eldest son of King Béla II of Hungary and his wife, Helena of Raška. He was only a baby when his mother introduced him and his brother, Ladislaus to the barons assembled in Arad in order to persuade them to massacre h
2 CONC er husband's opponents.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was crowned three days after his father's death on 13 February 1141. As he was still a minor, his mother served as regent of the kingdom helped by her brother, Beloš. She faced challenges from Boris, the son of King Coloman's a
2 CONC dulterous queen, who disputed Géza's claim to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne of Kiev (c. 1130 – c. 1193), daughter of Grand Prince Mstislav I of Kiev and his second wife, Liubava Dmitrievna
2 CONT
2 CONT King Stephen III of Hungary (1147 – 4 March 1172). 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Béla III of Hungary (1148 – 23 April 1196). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth (c. 1149 – after 1189), wife of Duke Frederick of Bohemia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Géza (c. 1150 – before 1210). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Arpad, died young. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Odola (1156 – 1199), wife of Duke Sviatopluk of Bohemia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena (c. 1158 – 25 May 1199), wife of Duke Leopold V of Austria. 
2 CONT
