2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT SVIATOSLAV, son of IGOR [Ingvar] of Kiev & his wife Olga --- ([935/40]-killed in battle [Mar/May] 972).
2 CONT
2 CONT The De administrando imperio names "Sphendosthlabus Ingor Russiæ principis filius"[45].  The Primary Chronicle names Svyatoslav as son of Olga[46].
2 CONT
2 CONT His birth date is estimated on the assumption that he was a young adult when the De administrando imperio was compiled, before the death of Emperor Konstantinos VII in 959.  According to the Primary Chronicle he "was but a child" 
2 CONC in 946[47].
2 CONT
2 CONT At an early age, Sviatoslav´s father appears to have established him in the northern town of Gorodishche, which seems to indicate that a claim to the overlordship of the northern Scandinavian settlements.  The place is called "Nem
2 CONC ogardas" in the De administrando imperio[48], which could be a corruption of Novgorod.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father as SVIATOSLAV I Grand Prince of Kiev, under the regency of his mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kiev was besieged by the Pechenegs in 962[49].  Ruling alone by the mid-960s, Prince Sviatoslav launched a major attack against the Khazars in 965, using the Pechenegs as allies[50].  He conquered the entire middle Volga area and 
2 CONC took control of the commercial centres of Sarkel and Ityl[51].
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav invaded the territory of the Bulgars along the Danube in 967, having been invited to do so by Emperor Nikephoros Phokas, and established a base at Pereiaslavets on the Danube delta[52].  It is not clear whether Pereiasl
2 CONC avets was the same place as Preslava, the Bulgarian capital, as Franklin & Shepherd appear to assume[53] or different, which appears to be the basis on which Fine writes[54].  Faced with the perceived threat of invasion by Sviatos
2 CONC lav, Emperor Ioannes Tzimisces marched into Bulgaria, captured the capital, and negotiated Sviatoslav's withdrawal.
2 CONT
2 CONT During Sviatoslav's absence in Bulgaria, the Pechenegs raided as far as Kiev.  Fine points out that according to the Primary Chronicle the Bulgarians summoned the Pechenegs to attack Kiev, without help from Byzantium[55].  On Svia
2 CONC toslav´s return journey to Kiev while crossing the Dnieper river in Spring 972, he was attacked and killed by the Pecheneg leader Kuria who reputedly made his skull into a ceremonial cup covered with gold[56].  This represents a c
2 CONC urious echo of the report in Paulus Diaconus according to which the skull of Alboin King of the Lombards in Pannonia was allegedly made into a drinking cup after he was defeated and killed by Cunimund King of the Gepids in 567[57]
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT m (before [960]) --- [of Hungary], daughter of [TORMAS Prince of Hungary & his wife ---].
2 CONT
2 CONT The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.   She was known as PREDSLAVA in Russia.  Europäische Stammtafeln[58] suggests that Predslava was the possible daughter of Tormas but the bas
2 CONC is for this speculation is not known.  Her marriage date is estimated from the estimated date of birth of her son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): ESFIR, daughter of ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is named as Sviatoslav´s mistress in Europäische Stammtafeln[59].  The primary source which confirms her parentage and relationship with Sviatoslav has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (2): MALUSHA [Malfred], sister of DOBRINYA (Ben notes: Dobrinya is a male), daughter of MALK of Lyubech & his wife --- (-1002).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Malusha, stewardess of Olga and sister of Dobrinya (naming their father Malk of Lyubech), as mother of Svyatoslav's son Vladimir[60].
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Sviatoslav & his wife had one child:
