1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT (Ben M. Angel notes: Malusha was never "Queen of Russia" or even "Grand Princess of Rus". She lost her position as housekeeper to Grand Princess Olga after becoming pregnant by her son, Svyatoslav, after which we have no idea what
2 CONC  position as a servant she was placed. She was Grand Prince Vladimir's mother, but this afforded her no official position, even after Grand Princess Olga's death.)
2 CONT
2 CONT (Ben M. Angel further notes: There is absolutely no evidence given in any reliable source that indicates that Malusha came from Sweden, Bulgaria, or the red planet Mars. Please either provide verifiable primary sources or reliable
2 CONC  analysis from primary sources for such assertions, or leave them to your personal family tree. Thank you.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Matuscha ???, levde i år 959.
2 CONT
2 CONT Matuscha ??? hadde sammen med Storfyrste Svjatoslav I av Kiev. Født omkring 942. Død 972, sønnen:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Storfyrste Vladimir den Hellige av Novgorod. Død 15.07.1015.
2 CONT
2 CONT Matuscha var husholderske hos Olga, mor til Svjatoslav, og var hans konkubine. 1)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1). Mogens Bugge: Våre forfedre, se nr. 146. Bent og Vidar Billing Hansen: Rosensverdslektens forfedre, side 90.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid (covering Sviatoslav, his wife, and concubines Esfir and Malusha):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#_Toc198014251
2 CONT
2 CONT SVIATOSLAV, son of IGOR [Ingvar] of Kiev & his wife Olga --- ([935/40]-killed in battle [Mar/May] 972).
2 CONT
2 CONT The De administrando imperio names "Sphendosthlabus Ingor Russiæ principis filius"[45].  The Primary Chronicle names Svyatoslav as son of Olga[46].  His birth date is estimated on the assumption that he was a young adult when the 
2 CONC De administrando imperio was compiled, before the death of Emperor Konstantinos VII in 959.  According to the Primary Chronicle he "was but a child" in 946[47].
2 CONT
2 CONT At an early age, Sviatoslav´s father appears to have established him in the northern town of Gorodishche, which seems to indicate that a claim to the overlordship of the northern Scandinavian settlements.  The place is called "Nem
2 CONC ogardas" in the De administrando imperio[48], which could be a corruption of Novgorod.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father as SVIATOSLAV I Grand Prince of Kiev, under the regency of his mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kiev was besieged by the Pechenegs in 962[49].
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruling alone by the mid-960s, Prince Sviatoslav launched a major attack against the Khazars in 965, using the Pechenegs as allies[50].  He conquered the entire middle Volga area and took control of the commercial centres of Sarkel
2 CONC  and Ityl[51].
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav invaded the territory of the Bulgars along the Danube in 967, having been invited to do so by Emperor Nikephoros Phokas, and established a base at Pereiaslavets on the Danube delta[52].  It is not clear whether Pereiasl
2 CONC avets was the same place as Preslava, the Bulgarian capital, as Franklin & Shepherd appear to assume[53] or different, which appears to be the basis on which Fine writes[54].
2 CONT
2 CONT Faced with the perceived threat of invasion by Sviatoslav, Emperor Ioannes Tzimisces marched into Bulgaria, captured the capital, and negotiated Sviatoslav's withdrawal.  During Sviatoslav's absence in Bulgaria, the Pechenegs raid
2 CONC ed as far as Kiev.  Fine points out that according to the Primary Chronicle the Bulgarians summoned the Pechenegs to attack Kiev, without help from Byzantium[55].
2 CONT
2 CONT On Sviatoslav´s return journey to Kiev while crossing the Dnieper river in Spring 972, he was attacked and killed by the Pecheneg leader Kuria who reputedly made his skull into a ceremonial cup covered with gold[56].  This represe
2 CONC nts a curious echo of the report in Paulus Diaconus according to which the skull of Alboin King of the Lombards in Pannonia was allegedly made into a drinking cup after he was defeated and killed by Cunimund King of the Gepids in 
2 CONC 567[57].
2 CONT
2 CONT m (before [960]) --- [of Hungary], daughter of [TORMAS Prince of Hungary & his wife ---].
2 CONT
2 CONT The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.   She was known as PREDSLAVA in Russia.  Europäische Stammtafeln[58] suggests that Predslava was the possible daughter of Tormas but the bas
2 CONC is for this speculation is not known.  Her marriage date is estimated from the estimated date of birth of her son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): ESFIR, daughter of ---.  She is named as Sviatoslav´s mistress in Europäische Stammtafeln[59].  The primary source which confirms her parentage and relationship with Sviatoslav has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (2): MALUSHA [Malfred], sister of DOBRINYA, daughter of MALK of Lyubech & his wife --- (-1002).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Malusha, stewardess of Olga and sister of Dobrinya (naming their father Malk of Lyubech), as mother of Svyatoslav's son Vladimir[60].
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Sviatoslav & his wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.   IAROPOLK Sviatoslavich ([960]-murdered Kiev [980]).
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 972 as IAROPOLK I Grand Prince of Kiev.  The Primary Chronicle records that, after he was attacked in Kiev by his half-brother Vladimir, Iaropolk fled to Rodnia near Pecheneg territory.  He was murdered 
2 CONC when he returned to Kiev to try to negotiate with Vladimir[62].
2 CONT
2 CONT m (972) ---.  The Primary Chronicle records that Yaropolk had a Greek wife who had been a nun, brought home by his father[64].  She was the mistress of her husband´s brother VLADIMIR I "Velikiy/the Great" Grand Prince of Kiev.  Th
2 CONC e Primary Chronicle records that Vladimir, later Grand Prince of Kiev, had intercourse with his brother's [Yaropolk's] wife "a Greek woman"[65].
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Sviatoslav had one illegitimate child by Mistress (1) Esfir:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. OLEG Sviatoslavich (-killed [976/77], bur Vruchiy).
2 CONT
2 CONT His father designated him in 972 as ruler in Dereva[67].  After a dispute with his brother Iaropolk over hunting rights, the latter attacked and defeated Oleg, who was pushed from the bridge leading into Ovruch, and later buried a
2 CONC t Vruchiy[68].
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Sviatoslav had one illegitimate child by Mistress (2) Malusha:
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  VLADIMIR Sviatoslavich ([960]-Berestov 15 Jul 1015).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Yaropolk, Oleg and Vladimir as grandsons of Olga[70].  The Primary Chronicle names Malusha, stewardess of Olga and sister of Dobrinya (naming their father Malk of Lyubech), as mother of Svyatoslav's son
2 CONC  Vladimir[71].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded in [980] as VLADIMIR I "Velikiy/the Great" Grand Prince of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
