2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Antioch
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Antioch (1127-1163) was the Princess regnant of the principality of Antioch (a crusader state) from 1130 to her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance was the only daughter of Bohemund II of Antioch by his wife Alice, princess of Jerusalem. She became princess of Antioch when she was only four-years-old, under the regency of Baldwin II of Jerusalem (1130-1131) and Fulk
2 CONC  of Jerusalem (1131-1136). Her mother Alice did not want the principality to pass to Constance, preferring to rule in her own name. Alice attempted to ally with the Muslim atabeg of Mosul, Zengi, offering to marry Constance to a M
2 CONC uslim prince, but the plan was foiled by Alice's father Baldwin, who exiled her from Antioch. In 1135 Alice attempted once again to take control of the principality, and sought a husband for Constance in Manuel Comnenus, at that t
2 CONC ime the heir to the Byzantine throne. Fulk exiled her again and re-established the regency for Constance. In 1136, while still a child, Constance was married to Raymond of Poitiers, whom the noble supporters of the regency had sec
2 CONC retly summoned from Europe; Alice was tricked into believing Raymond was going to marry her, and, humiliated, left Antioch for good when the marriage was performed. From this union three children were born:
2 CONT
2 CONT Bohemund III of Antioch, who succeeded her in 1163
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Antioch (1145-1182), married (rechristened as "Xena") to Manuel I Comnenus
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippa of Antioch, mistress to Andronicus I Comnenus
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1149, Raymond died in the battle of Inab and Constance remarried in 1153 to Raynald of Chatillon, who also became co-ruler of Antioch. Constance had one daughter from Raynald:
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes (1154-1184), married king Bela III of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT According to some sources[1], Constance and Raynald had another daughter, Jeanne, possibly the second wife of Boniface I of Montferrat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raynald was captured in 1160 and spent the next sixteen years in a prison in Aleppo. A dispute arose between Constance and her son, Bohemund, when Bohemund tried to seize power in Antioch. A riot broke out, and Constance was exile
2 CONC d from the city. She died in 1163.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Antioch (1127 – 1163) was the only daughter of Bohemund II of Antioch by his wife Alice, princess of Jerusalem. She was also Princess regnant of the Principality of Antioch (a crusader state) from 1130 to her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT She became princess of Antioch when she was only four-years-old, under the regency of Baldwin II of Jerusalem (1130-1131) and Fulk of Jerusalem (1131-1136).
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother Alice did not want the principality to pass to Constance, preferring to rule in her own name. Alice attempted to ally with the Muslim atabeg of Mosul, Zengi, offering to marry Constance to a Muslim prince, but the plan 
2 CONC was foiled by Alice's father Baldwin, who exiled her from Antioch. In 1135 Alice attempted once again to take control of the principality, and sought a husband for Constance in Manuel Comnenus, at that time the heir to the Byzanti
2 CONC ne throne. Fulk exiled her again and re-established the regency for Constance. In 1136, while still a child, Constance was married to Raymond of Poitiers, whom the noble supporters of the regency had secretly summoned from Europe;
2 CONC  Alice was tricked into believing Raymond was going to marry her, and, humiliated, left Antioch for good when the marriage was performed. From this union three children were born:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Bohemund III of Antioch, who succeeded her in 1163
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Maria of Antioch (1145-1182), married (rechristened as "Xena") to Manuel I Comnenus
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Philippa of Antioch, mistress to Andronicus I Comnenus
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1149, Raymond died in the Battle of Inab and Constance remarried in 1153 to Raynald of Chatillon, who also became co-ruler of Antioch. Constance had one daughter from Raynald:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Agnes of Antioch (1154-1184), married king Bela III of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT According to some sources believe that Constance and Raynald had another daughter, Jeanne, possibly the second wife of Boniface I of Montferrat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raynald was captured in 1160 and spent the next sixteen years in a prison in Aleppo. A dispute arose between Constance and her son, Bohemund, when Bohemund tried to seize power in Antioch. A riot broke out, and Constance was exile
2 CONC d from the city. She died in 1163.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
