2 CONT 2.         '''SILVESTRE''' (-after 1166).  "Geoffroi de Raguse, fils du comte Roger" is named with his three sons in a charter dated 1120[38].  Chalandon says that Silvestre de Marsico was the son of "Geoffroi de Raguse, fils du c
2 CONC omte Roger I"[39].  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  He was invested as Conte di Marsico in [1150] by Roger I King of Sicily.  Hugo Falcandus names "Count Silvester" describing him as "
2 CONC the most timid of men" and one of the nobles in Sicily whose survival was attributable to their unwillingness to move against chancellor Maio[40].  The "Catalogus Baronum", dated to [1168], records "Guillelmus filius Henrici de Sa
2 CONC ncto Severino" holding "demanium eius de Sancto Severino…de Principatu", with "milites XIII et augmentum eius milites XIV…una…inter feudum et augmentum milites XXVIII", adding in a later passage that "comes Silvester tenet eium in
2 CONC  servitio"[41].  The same source records "comes Silvester de Marsico" holding fees "in demanio Marsicum…Roccettam…et Dianum…et Salam…" in "de Marsico", with "militum XXXVI et augmentum eius milites LXXII"[42].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ---.  The name of Silvestre's wife is not known.  Silvestre & his wife had four children: 
