1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Charter by which Petronila abdicated in favour of her son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Petronila Ramírez (1135 – 15 October 1174), whose name is also spelled Petronilla or Petronella (Aragonese and Catalan: Peronella), was Queen of Aragon from 1137 until 1164. She was the daughter and successor of Ramiro II and Agne
2 CONC s of Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Petronila came to the throne through special circumstances. Her father, Ramiro, was bishop of Barbastro-Roda when his brother, Alfonso I, died heirless in 1134. As king, Ramiro received a papal dispensation to abdicate from his mo
2 CONC nastic vows in order to secure the succession to the throne. King Ramiro the Monk, as he is known, married Agnes, daughter of Duke Wiliam IX of Aquitaine and Gascony, and through her produced an heiress, Petronila. At two years ol
2 CONC d, Petronila was bethrothed to Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona. Immediately thereafter, Ramiro abdicated in favour of Petronilla and Ramon Berenguer and returned to monastic life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Petronila married Ramon Berenguer in 1150. Upon his death, Petronila renounced the crown of Aragon in favour of her eldest son, Ramon, who, in compliment to the Aragonese, changed his name to Alfonso. Her son was the first ruler o
2 CONC f both Aragon and Catalonia (where he is known as Alfons I) thereby establishing the dynastic union between the two countries that lasted until the Crown of Aragon was dissolved in 1707. Alfonso was crowned king in 1162, and on 18
2 CONC  July 1164, Petronila abdicated. She died in Barcelona in 1174 and was buried at Barcelona cathedral; her tomb was lost.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Bisson, Thomas N. (2000). The Medieval Crown of Aragon: A Short History. Oxford: Clarendon Press.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Chaytor, Henry John. (1933). A History of Aragon and Catalonia. London: Methuan Publishing.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT EUROPEAN QUEENS AND EMPRESSES
2 CONT
2 CONT and women who acted as regents of Kingdoms and Empires from the year BCE 1200
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.guide2womenleaders.com/womeninpower/European-Queens.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT 1137-63  Queen Regnant Petronilla I of Aragón (Spain)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1163-69 Regent of Arágon and Barcelona
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded father, Ramiro II the Monk. She married Count Berenguer IV of Barcelona, who did not become joint-regent. In 1163 she abdicated in favour of  her son, Alfonso II. and continued as his regent, and even after he came of ag
2 CONC e she continued to control the state affairs. Alfonso later named himself king of Aragon and Cataluña. She lived (1136-73/74).
2 CONT
2 CONT Petronila de Aragón (Huesca, 29 de junio de 1136 - Barcelona, 15 de octubre de 1173).[1] Reina de Aragón entre 1157 y 1164 y condesa de Barcelona entre 1162 y 1164.[2] [3] Hija de Ramiro II el Monje e Inés de Poitou.
2 CONT
2 CONT Del matrimonio con Ramón Berenguer IV tuvo a:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * El infante Pedro de Aragón (1152- antes de 1158), al que se alude como nasciturus (hijo que va a nacer) en un testamento dado por Petronila de Aragón el 4 de abril de 1152 estando en los trabajos del parto junto a Barcelona 
2 CONC («in partu laborans, apud Barchinonam»). Murió antes de 1158, quizá poco después del alumbramiento.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * El infante Alfonso II de Aragón (1157–1196), rey de Aragón y conde de Barcelona.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * El infante Ramón Berenguer IV de Provenza (1158–1181), conde de Cerdaña y de Provenza.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * La infanta Dulce de Aragón (1160–1198), casada en 1175 con el rey Sancho I de Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * El infante Sancho de Aragón y Barcelona (1161–1223), conde de Cerdaña, de Provenza y de Rosellón.
2 CONT
2 CONT Murió en Barcelona el 15 de octubre de 1173.
2 CONT
