1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tzachas '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Tzachas''' (Greek: Τζαχᾶς), also known as '''Chaka Bey''' (Turkish: Çaka Bey)[dn 1] was an 11th-century Seljuk Turkish military commander who ruled an independent state based in Smyrna (present-day Izmir). Originally in Byzanti
2 CONC ne service, he rebelled and seized Smyrna, much of the Aegean coastlands of Asia Minor and the islands lying off shore in 1088–91. At the peak of his power, he even declared himself Byzantine emperor, and sought to assault Constan
2 CONC tinople in conjunction with the Pechenegs. In 1092, a Byzantine naval expedition under John Doukas inflicted a heavy defeat on him and retook Lesbos, while in the next year he was treacherously slain by his son-in-law Kilij Arslan
2 CONC  I. Smyrna and the rest of Tzachas' former domain were recovered by the Byzantines a few years later, in ca. 1097.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Life
2 CONT * 2 See also
2 CONT * 3 Footnotes
2 CONT * 4 References
2 CONT * 5 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT =Life=
2 CONT
2 CONT Very little is known about his life, and that mostly from only one source, the Alexiad of the Byzantine princess Anna Komnene, daughter of Emperor Alexios I Komnenos (r. 1081–1118). He is also mentioned in the 13th-century Danishm
2 CONC endname, but it is not a very reliable source due to the semi-legendary nature of its material.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Alexiad, Tzachas was originally a raider, who was taken as a prisoner by the Byzantines during the reign of Nikephoros III Botaneiates (ruled 1078–81). Tzachas entered Byzantine service and advanced rapidly throug
2 CONC h imperial favour, receiving the title of protonobilissimus and rich gifts.[1][2] However, when Alexios I Komnenos deposed Botaneiates in 1081, Tzachas lost his position and fled Byzantium.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT From ca. 1088 on, he used his base at Smyrna to wage war against the Byzantines. Employing Christian craftsmen, he built a fleet, with which he captured Phocaea and the eastern Aegean islands of Lesbos (except for the fortress of 
2 CONC Methymna), Samos, Chios and Rhodes. A Byzantine fleet under Niketas Kastamonites was sent against him, but Tzachas defeated it in battle.[2][3] Some modern scholars have speculated that his activities during this time may have bee
2 CONC n in conjunction, and perhaps even coordination, with two contemporary Byzantine Greek rebels, Rhapsomates in Cyprus, and Karykes in Crete.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1090/91, the Byzantines under Constantine Dalassenos recovered Chios.[2][4] Undeterred, Tzachas rebuilt his forces, and resumed his attacks, even proclaiming himself emperor (basileus) and seeking to conclude an alliance agains
2 CONC t Alexios I with the Pechenegs in Thrace for a joint attack on Constantinople.[1][2] In 1092, Dalassenos and the new megas doux, John Doukas, were sent against Tzachas, and attacked the fortress of Mytilene on Lesbos. Tzachas resi
2 CONC sted for three months, but finally had to negotiate a surrender of the fortress. During his return to Smyrna, Dalassenos attacked the Turkish fleet, which was almost destroyed.[2][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT In spring 1093, Tzachas attacked the port of Abydos in the Sea of Marmara. Alexios I called upon the Sultan of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum Kilij Arslan I (r. 1092–1107), who was married to Tzachas's daughter and was thus his son-i
2 CONC n-law, to attack Tzachas from the rear. The Sultan advanced to Abydos, where, at the pretext of inviting Tzachas to a banquet, he had his father-in-law murdered.[2][6] However, in ca. 1097 a "Tzachas"—possibly the original Tzachas
2 CONC ' son—is reported as still holding Smyrna when the Byzantine army under John Doukas recaptured the city.[1][2][7]
2 CONT
2 CONT =See also=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seljuq_campaigns_in_the_Aegean '''Seljuq campaigns in the Aegean''']
2 CONT
2 CONT =Footnotes=
2 CONT
2 CONT # The Turkish form of "Tzachas" does not appear in any historical documents. The name "Çaka" or "Çaka Bey" prevailed especially in modern Turkey, after Akdes Nimet Kurat used it in his work Çaka: Orta Zamanda İzmir ve Yakınındaki 
2 CONC Adaların Türk Hakimi, Istanbul, 1936, ... yüksek siyasî ve askerî görüş sahibi olarak büyük önem taşıyan bu bey'in adının gerçek söylenişi henüz tamamen kesinliğe kavuşmuş değildir. Bu hususta şimdiye kadar üç ihtimal ileri sürülm
2 CONC üştür: Çaka, Çağa, Çakan. AN Kurat'ın bunu «Çaka» kabûl ederek eserini de «Çaka Bey» diye adlandırması, özellikle memleketimizde Çaka şeklinin yaygınlaşmasına yol açmıştır denebilir. (Tarih Dergisi, Cilt 20, İstanbul Üniversitesi 
2 CONC Edebiyat Fakültesi, İbrahim Horoz Basımevi, 1983, p. 56.)
2 CONT
