1 NOTE {geni:about_me} from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella,_Queen_of_Armenia '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Isabella I'''[1] (Armenian: Զապել), also '''Isabel I'''[2][3][4] or '''Zabel I''',[5] ( 27 January 1216/ 25 January 1217 – 23 January 1252) was the queen regnant of Cilician Armenia (1219–1252).[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was proclaimed queen under the regency of Adam of Baghras.[1][6] But he was assassinated; and Constantine of Baberon (of the Hethumian family) was nominated as guardian.[6] At this juncture, Raymond-Roupen, grandson of Roupen 
2 CONC III (the elder brother of Isabella’s father, King Leo I)[5] set up a claim to the throne of Cilician Armenia; but he was defeated, captured, and executed.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine of Barbaron was soon convinced to seek an alliance with Prince Bohemond IV of Antioch,[2] and he arranged a marriage between the young princess and Philip,[6] a son of Bohemond IV.[2] Philip, however, offended the Arme
2 CONC nians’ sensibilities, and even despoiled the royal palace, sending the royal crown to Antioch; therefore, he was confined in a prison in Sis (now Kozan in Turkey), where he died, presumably poisoned.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT The unhappy young Isabella was forced to marry Constantine of Barbaron’s son, Hethum; although for many years she refused to live with him, but in the end she relented.[1] The apparent unification in marriage of the two principal 
2 CONC dynastic forces of Cilicia (i.e., the Roupenids and the Hethumids) ended a century of dynastic and territorial rivalry and brought the Hethumids to the forefront of political dominance in Cilician Armenia.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT >''The lawful heiress of the empire, Isabella, governed the country together with her husband, and led a pious, religious life. She was blessed for her good deeds and exemplary life by many children, the numerous offsprings of a f
2 CONC amous race.''
2 CONT
2 CONT >—Vahram of Edessa: ''The Rhymed Chronicle of Armenia Minor''[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Early years
2 CONT * 2 Wife of Philip of Antioch
2 CONT * 3 Wife of Hethum of Barbaron
2 CONT * 4 Marriages and children
2 CONT * 5 Footnotes
2 CONT * 6 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT =Early years=
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella was the only child of King Leo I by his second wife, Sybille of Cyprus.[5] She was betrothed to Andrew, the third son of King Andrew II of Hungary in 1218, but the betrothal later broken in favor of a more advantageous Ru
2 CONC ssian marriage of her bridegroom.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT King Leo I died on May, 1219.[5] On his death-bed, he named Isabella as his heiress;[1] and released the barons from their oath of allegiance to his great-nephew, Raymond-Roupen.[4] But the claim of his five-year-old daughter was 
2 CONC contested by Raymond-Roupen and by John of Brienne.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella emerged as the favourite of the ruling Armenian nobles and thus she was proclaimed queen by acclamation and placed under the regency of Adam of Baghras.[2] But Adam of Baghras was murdered after a few months;[1] and the r
2 CONC egency passed to the only remaining influential Armenian house, that of the Hethumian family whose head was Constantine of Barbaron.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Brienne’s claim was based on his marriage to Leo I’s older daughter Rita (Stephanie).[3] Pope Honorius III recognized John of Brienne’s claim that his wife or her son should succeed.[1] John of Brienne received the Pope’s 
2 CONC permission to leave the Crusade and visit Cilician Armenia in February, 1220.[1] But as he prepared to sail for Cilicia his Armenian wife died; and when their small son died a few weeks later, John of Brienne had no further claim 
2 CONC on the Armenian throne.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond-Roupen laid claim to the throne by virtue of lineage through his mother Alice, the niece of King Leo I.[2] Moreover, he had long been considered as King Leo I’s heir.[3] Raymond-Roupen approached the crusaders at Damietta 
2 CONC in 1219 for support in claiming Cilician Armenia, and was able to return in 1221 with some of them and promises from the Papal legate Pelagius.[3] Raymond-Roupen found some Armenian support in and around Tarsus, notably Vahram, th
2 CONC e castellan of Corycus.[3] Together they conquered from Tarsus to Adana, but then met reverses and were forced to retire to Tarsus where Raymond-Roupen was captured and ended his days in prison in 1222;[3] his infant daughters ret
2 CONC ired with their mother to Cyprus.[1] This event left Isabella the sole and largely incontestable heir to her father’s throne.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Wife of Philip of Antioch=
2 CONT
2 CONT Cilician Armenia, weakened by wars and in need of strong ally, found a temporary solution in a tie with the Principality of Antioch:[3] the regent suggested that Prince Bohemond IV should send his fourth son, Philip, to marry Isab
2 CONC ella, insisting only that the bridegroom should join the separated Armenian Church.[1] Philip agreed to adopt the Armenian faith, communion and customs and to respect the privileges of all nations in Cilician Armenia.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip married Isabell at Sis in June 1222, and he was accepted as prince-consort.[3] The joint-rule of Isabel and Philip lasted only a short while; Philip’s disdain for the Armenian ritual, which he had promised to respect, and h
2 CONC is marked favoritism to the Latin barons angered the Armenian nobility.[4] Philip spent as much time as possible in Antioch.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT When it was rumored that Philip wanted to give the crown and throne to Antioch, Constantine of Barbaron led a revolt (at the end of 1224).[3] Philip and Isabella were seized at Tall Hamdun (today Toprakkale in Turkey) on their way
2 CONC  to Antioch, and taken back to Sis where Philip was imprisoned, and probably poisoned at the beginning of 1225.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of her husband, Isabella decided to embrace a monastic life,[2] and fled to Seleucia.[4] She sought refuge with the Hospitallers.[4] The latter were unwilling to give her up to Constantine of Barbaron but feared the p
2 CONC owerful regent; they eased their conscience by selling him the fortress with Isabella in it.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bohemond IV, in anger, determined on war, although such a conflict had been expressly forbidden by the pope as harmful for all Christendom.[3] Bohemond IV called in as ally the sultan at Iconium, Kai-Qobad I, and ravaged upper Cil
2 CONC icia in 1225.[3] Constantine of Barberon arranged for the regent of Aleppo, Toghril, to advance on Antioch.[1] When the latter attacked Baghras, Bohemond IV had to return to his own lands.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Wife of Hethum of Barbaron=
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella was forced into marriage with Constantine of Barbaron’s son who was subsequently crowned King Hetum I in Tarsus in June 1226.[2] She is said to have refused to consummate the marriage for several years.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT >''In the year 675 AE /1226/ the Armenian princes, together with the Catholicos, Lord Constantine, assembled and enthroned Hethum, son of Constantine, bailli of the Armenians, and also gave him /as a wife/ Isabel, King Leo’s daugh
2 CONC ter. Thereafter there was peace in the House of the Armenians, and year by year they strived for the heights.''
2 CONT
2 CONT >—Smbat Sparapet: ''Chronicle''[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine of Barbaron now thought it wise to reconcile Armenia with the Papacy: loyal messengers were sent in the name of the young couple to the Pope and to the Emperor Frederick II.[1] Although Bohemond IV and later his son, B
2 CONC ohemond V attempted to persuade the Pope to arrange a divorce between Isabella and Hethum, but both he and King Henry I of Cyprus were specifically forbidden by Rome to attack the Armenians.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage was legalized by Rome in 1237.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT >''The queen being near the end of her life, and staying in a place called Ked, she heard a voice from heaven, crying aloud,'' «come my dove, come my love, thy end is near.» ''She felt joyful on this happy vision, imparted it to t
2 CONC he bystanders, and died in the Lord; her body was brought to the grave by a large assembly of the priesthood and laid in consecrated earth.''
2 CONT
2 CONT >—Vahram of Edessa: ''The Rhymed Chronicle of Armenia Minor''[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was buried in the monastery of Trazarg.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Marriages and children=
2 CONT
2 CONT # 25 January 1221 – 24 January 1222: Philip of Antioch (? – Sis, 1225/1226)[5]
2 CONT # 14 May 1226: Hethum I, king of Cilician Armenia (1215 – 28 October 1270)[5]
2 CONT * Euphemia[1] (? – 1309), the wife of Julian of Sidon (? – 12 January 1275/ 11 January 1276)[10]
2 CONT * Maria (? – Nicosia, after 1310), the wife of Guy of Ibelin (1235/1240 – after 1270)[10]
2 CONT * Sybilla (? – 1290), the wife of Prince Bohemond VI of Antioch (c. 1237 – May/ 11 July 1275)[10]
2 CONT * Rita (? – ?), the wife of Constantine of Servantikar[10]
2 CONT * Leo II, king of Cilician Armenia ( 24 January 1236/ 23 January 1237 – 6 February 1289)[10]
2 CONT * Thoros (1244 – 24 August 1266)[10]
2 CONT * Isabella (? – c. 1268)[10]
2 CONT
