1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''aka as Otelindis of the Nordmark'''. (See posts to media tab for proof of existence)
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000221645921940&size=large}}
2 CONT ''Tribes in the Northern and Billung marches at the time of the Lutici uprising of 983''
2 CONT
2 CONT Question: was Bernard her father the Duke of Lüneburg, this could explain the shield he holds
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000221649277826&size=large}}
2 CONT Was he the Duke of Lüneburg or the Principality of Lüneburg (dexter), his wife from Lower Saxony (sinister)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT OTELHILDIS von der NORDMARK, aka "Othelhilde", "Otehilde", "Wettin", "Othilde", "Othehildis", "Othelindis", "Othelhildis", "de Nordmark", "van den Noordmark", "van Holland", "Zwanehilde", was born c. 985, in Schweinfurt, Niederfra
2 CONC nken, Bayern, Deutschland, died 31 Mar 1044, in Quedlinburg, Harz, Sachesen-anhalt, Deutschland, Othelhildis was the daug. of Bernard, margrave of the Nordmark and Vladimirovna Rurkides, Von Kiew, the wife of Dirk III, count of We
2 CONC stfriesland. Othelhildis was the mother of de Ghent; Dirk IV van Holland, Count Of Frisia, Floris I, count of Friesland, Bertrada von Holland and Suanehilde (Swanehilde) van Holland. Othelhildis was the sister of Bernard II Graf v
2 CONC on Haldensleben, Markgraf von der Nordmark; Konrad von Haldensleben; Thietburga von der Nordmark; Wuilhelm Markgraf von der Nordmark; OTTO and 1 other . Otelhildis was a Princess.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''MARKGRAFEN der NORDMARK'''
2 CONT BERNHARD (-[1044/51]).  "Bernhardo" is named as son of "Teoderici ducis et marchionis" in the Annalista Saxo, which also specifies that he was avunculus of Dietrich Graf von Merseburg[12].  He was appointed as BERNHARD Markgraf de
2 CONC r Nordmark in 1009.  m [firstly] ---.  The name and origin of Bernhard´s [first] wife is not known.  [m secondly or Mistress (1):] ---, a Slav.  The Annales of Lambert von Hersfeld record in 1057 “Otto frater Wilhehelmi marchionis
2 CONC , sed matrimonio impari, matre...Slavia”[13].  It is not clear from this passage whether Otto´s mother was the second wife or the mistress of Bernhard.  Baumgarten states that the wife of Markgraf Bernhard was --- Vladimirovna, il
2 CONC legitimate daughter of Vladimir Sviatoslavich "Velikiy/the Great" Grand Prince of Kiev & his mistress ---.  He cites a single German secondary source, dated to 1722, but this in fact makes no mention of Bernhard´s wife[14].  The a
2 CONC uthority for his statement is therefore unknown and it is preferable to assume that it is incorrect until further information comes to light.  If Baumgarten is correct, this person could have been the unknown second wife/mistress 
2 CONC of Bernhard who is described as “Slavia” by Lambert von Hersfeld.  As the birth of Bernhard´s son by his second wife/mistress can be dated to [1040], this daughter would have been one of the youngest children of Vladimir (who died
2 CONC  in 1015) if this is the correct parentage for Bernhard´s wife/mistress.  Markgraf Bernhard & his [first] wife had [five] children: 
2 CONT a)         THIETBURGA (-1018).  The primary source which confirms her parentage has not yet been identified.  Nun. 
2 CONT b)         WILHELM (-near Prizlawa 10 Sep 1056).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  He succeeded in [1051] as WILHELM Markgraf der Nordmark.  He was killed fighting the Slavs. 
2 CONT c)         KONRAD .  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.   Graf von Haldensleben. 
2 CONT -        GRAFEN von HALDENSLEBEN. 
2 CONT d)         ODA .  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.  m CHRISTIAN Graf, son of [GEBHARD [I] Graf von Querfurt/BURCHARD [I] Graf von Querfurt] & his wife ---. 
2 CONT e)         [OTHELINDIS (-in Saxony 9 Mar [1043/44], bur in Saxony).  The Annales Egmundani name "Othelhildis comitissa" as wife of "Theodericus comes quartus [Hollandensium]" but do not give her origin[15].  According to Europäisc
2 CONC he Stammtafeln[16], the wife of Count Dirk III was the daughter of Bernhard I Markgraf der Nordmark Graf von Haldensleben, but the primary source on which this is based has not so far been identified.  The Chronologia Johannes de 
2 CONC Beke records the marriage of "Theodericus…tercius…comes Hollandie" and "Utilhildim filiam…ducis Saxonie"[17].  The corresponding duke of Saxony would have been the Billung Duke Bernhard I (who died in 1011).  However, if Duke Bern
2 CONC hard was Othelindis's father, the wife of Count Dirk III's son Count Floris would have been his first cousin on his mother's side, which seems unlikely to be correct.  "Theodericus…Holtlandensis comes…Florentii filius" recites the
2 CONC  ownership history of properties claimed by the church of Utrecht in Holland, including donations by "Theodericus tertius, predicti Arnulfi filius, cum coniuge sua Othelhildis", by spurious charter dated 26 Jul 1083, probably writ
2 CONC ten in [1130][18].  The Annales Egmundani record the death in 1044 of "Othilhildis comitissa"[19].  The Chronologia Johannes de Beke records that "Othilhildis…comitissa" returned to Saxony after her husband died, and that she died
2 CONC  there "1043 VII Id Mai" and was buried there[20].  Beke's Egmondsch Necrologium records the death "1043 VII Id Mar" of "Othelhildis comitissa rediens ad Saxoniam"[21].  m DIRK III Count of Holland, son of ARNULF Count of Holland 
2 CONC & his wife Liutgard de Luxembourg (-27 May 1039).] 
2 CONT '''Internal Sourcing:'''
2 CONT [12] Annalista Saxo 1010. 
2 CONT [13] Lamberti Annales, 1057, MGH SS V, p. 158. 
2 CONT [14] Baumgarten, N. de 'Généalogies et mariages occidentaux des Rurikides Russes du X au XIII siècles´, Orientalia Christiana Vol. IX - 1, No. 35, May 1927 (reprint, Pont. Institutum Orientalium Studiorum, Rome) (“Baumgarten (1927
2 CONC )”), p. 8, citing Eccardus Origines serenis. fam. Anhaltinæ, p. 496, which presumably indicates Eccard, Johann Georg (1722) Historia genealogica principum Saxoniae superioris, qua recensentur stemma Witichindeum, origines familiae
2 CONC  Saxonicae regnantis ... nec non origines familiae Anhaltinae. Accedunt appendices loco origines sabaudicae; stemma desidenianum genuinum; origo domu (Leipzig, Gleditsch). 
2 CONT [15] Annales Egmundani, MGH SS XVI, p. 444. 
2 CONT [16] ES II 2. 
2 CONT [17] Bruch, H. (ed.) (1973) Chronologia Johannes de Beke (The Hague), 39a, p. 73, available at < http://www.inghist.nl/Onderzoek/Projecten /KroniekVanJohannesDeBekeTot1430/latijn> (31 Aug 2006). 
2 CONT [18] Koch (1970) Oorkondenboek van Holland en Zeeland Part I, nr. 88, Latin text and English translation available at <http://www.keesn.nl/sources/en_start.htm> (31 Aug 2006), information supplied by Kees Nieuwenhuijsen, in a priv
2 CONC ate email to the author dated 31 Aug 2006. 
2 CONT [19] Annales Egmundani 1044, MGH SS XVI, p. 447. 
2 CONT [20] Chronologia Johannes de Beke 43, p. 81. 
2 CONT [21] Beka's Egmondsch Necrologium, in Oppermann, O. (1933) Fontes Egmundenses (Utrecht), p. 107. 
2 CONT '''Source:''' Cawley, Charles. “ BRANDENBURG.” Fmg.ac, 2025, fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BRANDENBURG/OTHELINDIS.htm#. Accessed 9 Oct. 2025.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Research Notes:'''
2 CONT 1. Parentage Disputed Caution Advised
2 CONT m OTHELINDIS, daughter of --- According to Europäische Stammtafeln], she was Othelindis von Haldensleben, daughter of Bernhard I Markgraf der Nordmark Graf von Haldensleben, but the primary source on which this is based has not ye
2 CONC t been identified.
2 CONT 2. Notes on Othilde of Saxony, of the Northern March, of Haldensleben
2 CONT There is no certainty about her origins. If she was a daughter of Duke Bernhard I of Saxony, then her son Floris married his first cousin. Bernard I of Brandenburg, the Margrave of the Northern March, is also mentioned as her fath
2 CONC er.
2 CONT 3. Person note:
2 CONT <b>m</b> <b>OTHELINDIS</b>, daughter of --- (-in Saxony 9 Mar [1043/44], bur in Saxony).  
2 CONT The <i>Annales Egmundani</i> name "<i>Othelhildis comitissa</i>" as wife of "<i>Theodericus comes quartus [Hollandensium]</i>" but do not give her origin<b>[312]</b>.  
2 CONT According to <i>Europäische Stammtafeln</i><b>[313]</b>, she was <b>Othelindis von Haldensleben</b>, daughter of Bernhard I Markgraf der Nordmark Graf von Haldensleben, but the primary source on which this is based has not yet bee
2 CONC n identified.  
2 CONT The <i>Chronologia Johannes de Beke</i> records the marriage of "<i>Theodericus…tercius…comes Hollandie</i>" and "<i>Utilhildim filiam…ducis Saxonie</i>"<b>[314]</b>.  
2 CONT The corresponding duke of Saxony would have been the Billung Duke Bernhard I (who died in 1011). 
2 CONT However, if Duke Bernhard was Othelindis's father, the wife of Count Dirk III's son Count Floris would have been his first cousin on his mother's side, which seems unlikely to be correct.  
2 CONT "<i>Theodericus…Holtlandensis comes…Florentii filius</i>" recites the ownership history of properties claimed by the church of Utrecht in Holland, including donations by "<i>Theodericus tertius, predicti Arnulfi filius, cum coniug
2 CONC e sua Othelhildis</i>", by spurious charter dated 26 Jul 1083, probably written in [1130]<b>[315]</b>.  
2 CONT The <i>Annales Egmundani</i> record the death in 1044 of "<i>Othilhildis comitissa</i>"<b>[316]</b>.  
2 CONT The <i>Chronologia Johannes de Beke</i> records that "<i>Othilhildis…comitissa</i>" returned to Saxony after her husband died, and that she died there "<i>1043 VII Id Mai</i>" and was buried there<b>[317]</b>.  
2 CONT Beke's <i>Egmondsch Necrologium</i> records the death "<i>1043 VII Id Mar</i>" of "<i>Othelhildis comitissa rediens ad Saxoniam</i>"<b>[318]</b>. 
2 CONT 4. She died on November 18th of an unknown year. Her grave was discovered during an archaeological investigation in Egmond. A physical-anatomical examination revealed that she must have been between 65 and 70 years old at the time
2 CONC  of her death. She therefore significantly outlived her husband.
2 CONT 5. She was Abbess of Meteln & Herford from the GEDCOM
2 CONT 6. In Medlands under the B.      DUKES in SAXONY 973-1106, BERNHARD Billung, son of HERMANN Billung dux in Saxony, we find Bernhardt II but he does NOT have a daughter named Otelhildis.
2 CONT -BERNHARD (after 990-29 Jun 1059, bur Lüneburg St Michael).  The Annalista Saxo names "duce Bernhardo, filius eius Bernhardus" when recording his succession to his father in 1011 as BERNHARD II Herzog in Sachsen[329].  As "Bernard
2 CONC  Duke of Westfalia", he signed the 1013 document of Heinrich II King of Germany under which the king renewed his settlement of a dispute over Gandersheim, listed first among the lay signatories[330].
2 CONT [329] Annalista Saxo 1010. 
2 CONT [330] D H II 255, p. 293. 
2 CONT Source: Cawley, Charles. “SAXONY DUKES.” Fmg.ac, 2025, fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SAXONY.htm#BernhardIdied1011B. Accessed 9 Oct. 2025.
2 CONT (Curator Note: this is imported because the CoA of the Dukes of Westfalia was a while horse on a gules background , reflective of the sinister side of Bernard’s CoA!)
2 CONT 7. EILIKA ([1000/05][1095]-10 Dec after [1055/56]).  The Annalista Saxo names "Eilica…filia marchionis Heinrici de Suinvorde" as wife of "Bernhardus iunior"[1096].  The necrology of Lüneburg records the death "10 Dec" of "Eila duc
2 CONC issa"[1097].  m ([1020]) BERNHARD II Duke in Saxony, son of BERNHARD I Duke in Saxony [Billung] & his wife Hildegard von Stade (after 990-29 Jun 1059, bur Lüneburg St Michael). 
2 CONT 8. Othelendis (of Haldensleben), Countess -9 March 1044
2 CONT Possibly daughter of Margrave Bernhard I of the Nordmark, Brandenburg Eric: Plate 5, Page 11
2 CONT 9. There are notes that Otelhildis was from the GRAFEN von KATLENBURG, but I think this is conflation with a lady named OTHILDIS von Katlenburg, daughter of Dietrich I von Stade
2 CONT
