2 CONT
2 CONT Géza of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Géza (c. 945 – 997), Grand Prince of the Magyars (before 972 - 997)[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT Géza was the son of Taksony of Hungary, Grand Prince of the Magyars and his Pecheneg or Bulgar wife[2]. Géza's marriage with Sarolt, the daughter of Gyula of Transylvania, was arranged by his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his father's death (before 972), Géza followed him as Grand Prince of the Magyars. Shortly afterwards, a Benedictine monk of the Abbey of Sankt Gallen, Bruno, who had been ordained Bishop of the Magyars, arrived to his court
2 CONC  where he baptised Géza. His Christian name was Stephen (Hungarian: István).
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Géza probably never became a convinced Christian, during his rule Christianity began to spread among the Magyars. According to Thietmar of Merseburg, Géza continued to worship pagan gods; a chronicle claims that when he w
2 CONC as questioned about this he stated he is rich enough to sacrifice to both the old gods and the new one.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 973, twelve illustrious Magyar envoys, whom probably Géza had assigned, participated in the Diet held by Otto I, Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 983, when Henry II, Duke of Bavaria rebelled against the then child Otto III, Holy Roman Emperor, the Magyars occupied Melk. However, Melk was reoccupied, already in 985, by Leopold I, Margrave of Austria. When Henry II lead hi
2 CONC s armies to the Vienna Basin in 991, the Magyars were obliged to evacuate the territories West of the Leitha (Hungarian:Lajta) River.
2 CONT
2 CONT Géza arranged the marriage of his son Stephen I of Hungary to Giselle of Bavaria, the daughter of Henry II. He started the construction of the Abbey of Pannonhalma.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT before 972: Sarolt, a daughter of kangar Gyula of Transylvania (? – after 997)
2 CONT
2 CONT #Judith (? – after 988), wife of the future King Boleslaw I of Poland
2 CONT #Margareth (? – after 988), wife of the future Tsar Gavril Radomir of Bulgaria
2 CONT #King Stephen I of Hungary (967/969/975 – 15 August 1038)
2 CONT #Maria (? – after 1026), wife of Otto Orseolo, Doge of Venice
2 CONT #Gizella (? – ?), wife of the future King Samuel Aba of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Kristó, Gyula - Makk, Ferenc: Az Árpád-ház uralkodói (IPC Könyvek, 1996)
2 CONT
2 CONT Korai Magyar Történeti Lexikon (9-14. század), főszerkesztő: Kristó, Gyula, szerkesztők: Engel, Pál és Makk, Ferenc (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1994)
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ His name was possibly "Gyeücsa" or "Gyécsa" in Old Hungarian.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ The Gesta Hungarorum mentions that Géza's father married a woman "of the territories of the Cumans", but the Cumans had not crossed the Volga River before the 11th century.
