2 CONT from MedLands
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/HUNGARY.htm#Gezadied997A '''GÉZA 970-997''' ]
2 CONT
2 CONT GÉZA, son of TAKSONY Prince of Hungary & his wife --- [Pss of the Kumans] ([940/45]-1 Feb 997).  The Chronicon Varadiense names "dux Geisa, pater B. Stephani, secundus…Michael dux" as the two sons of "Toxin"[275].  The Kronika Węg
2 CONC iersko-Polska names "Geyzan, Mychlemum et caluum Ladislaum" as the three sons of "Thoxon"[276].  The Gestis Hungarorum Liber names "Geysam, quantum ducem Hungarie" as son of "dux Tocsun"[277].  He succeeded his father in [970] as 
2 CONC Prince of Hungary.  He sent ambassadors to the court of Emperor Otto I, with whom he established friendly relations.  Géza was baptised in 974 as ISTVÁN [Stephen] by priests sent by Pilgrim Bishop of Passau, although he appears to
2 CONC  have adopted Christianity more for political expediency than religious conviction as he never renounced his pagan beliefs entirely, declaring himself, according to Macartney, "rich enough to afford two gods" (although this allege
2 CONC d quote may represent an inaccurate report of comments by Thietmar, see below)[278].  He continued to use his pre-baptismal name Géza.  He centralised Magyar government, based at Esztergom, where his bodyguard consisted of Bavaria
2 CONC n knights.  The alliance with Bavaria was confirmed after the accession in 985 of Duke Heinrich II, and sealed by the marriage of Duke Heinrich's daughter to Géza's heir in 996[279].  Thietmar names "Deuvix" as father of King Istv
2 CONC án, describing him as "very cruel…when becoming a Christian…he turned his rage against his reluctant subjects [and] sacrificed both to the omnipotent God and to various false gods.  When reproached by his priest for doing so, he m
2 CONC aintained that the practice had brought him great wealth and power"[280].  The Chronicon Posoniense records the death in 998 of "Geyza princeps Ungarorum"[281].
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ([967], repudiated shortly after 975) SAROLT of Transylvania, daughter of GYULA Prince of Transylvania & his wife --- ([954]-after 988).  The Gestis Hungarorum Liber names "una…Caroldu et altera Saroltu" as the two daugh
2 CONC ters of "Geula", specifying that the Sarolt was mother of "sancti regis Stephani"[282].  Thietmar names "Beleknegini, the name means beautiful lady in Slavonic" as wife of "Deuvix", commenting that she "drank immoderately and rode
2 CONC  a horse like a warrior" adding that "once in a fit of anger she killed a man"[283].  The primary source which confirms her name and parentage has not yet been identified.  She had been baptised into the Orthodox faith by Bishop H
2 CONC ierotheos at her father's court[284].  Her marriage may have been arranged by her father to build an alliance against the more powerful Bulgars[285].
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([985]) as her second husband, ADELAJDA [Adleta] of Poland, widow of his brother MIHÁLY of Hungary Duke between March and Gran, daughter of [ZIEMOMYSŁ Duke in Poland] & his [second wife ---] ([950/60]-after 997).  The A
2 CONC nnales Kamenzenses record that "Mesco…rex Polanorum…sororem…Atleydem" married "Iesse rex Ungarie" by whom she was mother of "Stephanum regem Ungarie"[286].  The Breve chronicon Silesiæ names "Adilheidem" as sister of "primo dux Me
2 CONC sco", adding that she married "Jesse rex Ungarie" and that she was the mother of "Stephanum regem Ungarie" born in 975[287].  The Kronika Węgiersko-Polska records that "Iesse" married "sororem Meschonis ducis…Athleitam", adding th
2 CONC at she was a Christian and converted her husband to Christianity[288].  The primary source which confirms her first marriage has not yet been identified.   According to Europäische Stammtafeln[289], Adelajda was the daughter not s
2 CONC ister of Mieszko I Prince of Poland, although the primary source on which this is based has not yet been identified.  If this is correct, she was an otherwise unrecorded daughter by his first wife (name not known), assuming that P
2 CONC rince Mieszko's marriage to Dobroslawa of Bohemia is correctly dated to 965.  Adelajda's birth date range is estimated from the supposed dates of birth of her two sons by her first husband (before his death in [976/78]) and of her
2 CONC  three known daughters by her second marriage after [985].  The date range appears chronologically more consistent with her having been the daughter, rather than sister, of Mieszko, but this would be in direct contradiction to the
2 CONC  sources quoted above.  If she was Mieszko's sister, it is likely that they did not share the same mother, assuming that the estimated birth dates of Mieszko and Adelajda are both accurate.  It is probable that her second marriage
2 CONC  was arranged in accordance with the Magyar tradition that the oldest male relative should marry the widow of a deceased relative (originally polygamously) and take care of his children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Géza & his first wife had [three] children:
