0 @N6000000002188110591@ NOTE Romanos III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Romanos III Argyros or Romanus III Argyrus (Greek: ??µa??? G? ???????, Romanos III Argyros), (968 – April 11, 1034) was Byzantine emperor (November 15, 1028 - April 11, 1034). His last name is Greek for "Silver".
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Life 
1 CONT 2 Family 
1 CONT 3 Bibliography 
1 CONT 4 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT Romanos Argyros was the son of an unnamed member of the Argyros family and a great-grandson of Emperor Romanos I. Under Basil II, Romanos served as judge, and under Constantine VIII he became prefect of Constantinople. Romanos att
1 CONC racted the attention of Constantine VIII, who forced him to divorce his wife (sending her into a monastery) and to marry the emperor's daughter Zoe. The marriage took place on November 12, 1028, and three days later Constantine VI
1 CONC II died, leaving Romanos III as emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT The new emperor showed great eagerness to make his mark as a ruler, but was mostly unfortunate in his enterprises. He spent large sums upon new buildings and in endowing the monks, and in his endeavour to relieve the pressure of t
1 CONC axation disorganized the finances of the state. Idealizing Marcus Aurelius, Romanos aspired to be a new "philosopher king", and similarly desired to imitate the military prowess of Trajan.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Romanos III Argyros - severely ill - dies inside the palace in 1034. In the picture, Romanos in a bath where he dies, from the Chronicle of John Skylitzes.In 1030 he resolved to retaliate upon the incursions of the Muslims on the
1 CONC  eastern frontier by leading a large army in person against Aleppo, but by allowing himself to be surprised on the march sustained a serious defeat at Azaz near Antioch. Though this disaster was retrieved by the capture and succes
1 CONC sful defence of Edessa by George Maniakes in 1032 and by the defeat of a Saracen fleet in the Adriatic, Romanus never recovered his popularity.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a member of the aristocracy, Romanos III abandoned his predecessors' curtailment of the privileges of the nobility and reduced their taxes, at the same time allowing peasant freeholders to fall into a condition of serfdom. In a
1 CONC  vain attempt to reduce expenditure, Romanos limited his wife's expenses, which merely exacerbated the alienation between the spouses.
1 CONT
1 CONT At home Romanos III faced several conspiracies, mostly centered around his sister-in-law Theodora, as in 1029 and 1030. Although he survived these attempts on the throne, his early death in 1034 was supposed to have been due to po
1 CONC ison administered by his wife, though it has also been alleged that he was drowned in a bath on his wife's orders.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT By his first wife Helena, Romanos III Argyros had a daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT (unnamed) Argyra, who was engaged to Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor. 
1 CONT By his second wife Zoe, Romanos III had no children.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Bibliography
1 CONT (primary source) Michael Psellus, Chronographia. 
1 CONT The Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium, Oxford University Press, 1991. 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT Wikimedia Commons has media related to: 
1 CONT Romanus IIIRomanus coinage 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Constantine VIII Byzantine Emperor
1 CONT 1028–1034 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Michael IV 
