0 @N6000000000945065067@ NOTE Fyrste av Pereyaslavl fra 1054. 
1 CONT Fyrste av Chernigiv 1077-78.
1 CONT Storfyrste av Kijev [Kiev] i 1077 og 1078 - 1093.
1 CONT Vsevolod var 2. gang gift med Oda av Tyskland.
1 CONT Vsevolod støttet sin bror Izyaslev i Kijev mot den tyrkiske folkegruppen Polovsti  (kumaner,
1 CONT Polouzianer/Kipcakser] og troneraneren Vseslav I Bryacheslav. Han holdt seg utenfor da
1 CONT deres bror, Svyatoslav, tok tronen i Kijev (1073-76), men støttet igjen sin bror, Izyaslav, når
1 CONT denne returnerte i 1077. Han flyttet så til Chernigiv. Men deres nevø Oleg Svyatoslavish, en
1 CONT sønn til den tidligere herskeren i Chernigiv, var ikke villig til å kompromisse. Han brakte inn en
1 CONT polovtsi-arme for å angripe Vsyevolod i 1078. Vsyevolod flyktet til Kijev hvor hans bror Izyaslav
1 CONT raskt organiserte en redningsarme.  Når så Izyaslav døde i slaget mot Oleg utenfor Chernigiv,
1 CONT flyttet Vsyevolod til Kijev. I et familiekompromiss overtok Oleg Chernigiv.
0 @N6000000002187645651@ NOTE Vsevolod I of Kiev
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Vsevolod I Yaroslavich (Ukrainian and Russian: ???????? I ?????????), (1030 – 13 April 1093) ruled as Grand Prince of Kiev from 1078 until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early life 
1 CONT 2 Reign 
1 CONT 3 Children 
1 CONT 4 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT He was the fourth and favourite son of Yaroslav I the Wise by Ingigerd Olafsdottir.
1 CONT
1 CONT To back up an armistice signed with the Byzantine Empire in 1046, his father married him to a daughter of Emperor Constantine IX. The couple had a son, the future Vladimir Monomakh.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon his father's death in 1054, he received in appanage the towns of Pereyaslav, Rostov, Suzdal, and the township of Beloozero which would remain in possession of his descendants until the end of Middle Ages. Together with his el
1 CONC der brothers Iziaslav and Sviatoslav he formed a sort of princely triumvirate which jointly waged war on the steppe nomads, polovtsy, and compiled the first East Slavic law code. In 1067 Vsevolod's Greek wife died and he soon marr
1 CONC ied a Kypchak princess. She brought him another son, who drowned after the Battle of the Stugna River, and two daughters, one becoming a nun and another, Eupraxia of Kiev, marrying Emperor Henry IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign
1 CONT Upon Sviatoslav's death in 1076, Vsevolod inherited the Kievan throne, but ceded it to the banished Iziaslav in return for his patrimony of Chernigov. But Iziaslav died 2 years later, and Vsevolod took the Kievan throne yet again.
1 CONC  Vsevolod was versed in Greek learning and spoke 5 languages. Since he lost most of his battles, his eldest son, Vladimir Monomakh, a grand and famous warrior, did most of the fighting for his father. Last years of his reign were 
1 CONC clouded by grave illness, and Vladimir Monomakh presided over the government.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT Vsevolod and his first wife had only one known son:
1 CONT
1 CONT Vladimir II Monomakh (1053 - 19 May 1125). 
1 CONT Vsevolod and his second wife had five known children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Rostislav Vsevolodovich (1070 - 26 May 1093). Drowned while retreating from the Battle of the Stugna River. 
1 CONT Eupraxia of Kiev (1071 - 20 July 1109). Married Henry IV, Holy Roman Emperor. 
1 CONT Catherine Vsevolovna (d. 11 August 1108). A nun. Her date of death is recorded in the Primary Chronicle. 
1 CONT Maria Vsevolodovna (d. 1089). 
1 CONT Anna Vsevolodovna (d. [[3 November], 1112). Abbess. Visited Constantinople in 1089. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Sviatoslav II Prince of Kiev and Chernigov Succeeded by
1 CONT Sviatopolk II 
1 CONT
1 CONT
