0 @N6000000004533651482@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Robert Guiscard (i.e. "the resourceful") (c. 1015-1085) was the mostremarkable of the Norman adventurers who conquered southern Italy andSicily.
1 CONT
1 CONT From 999 to 1059 the Normans were pure mercenaries, serving eitherGreeks or Lombards, and then Sergius of Naples, by installing theleader Rainulf in the fortress of Aversa in 1029, gave them theirfirst pied-à-terre and they began 
1 CONC an organized conquest of the land.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1035 there arrived William Iron-Arm and Drogo, the two eldest sonsof Tancred of Hauteville, a petty noble of Coutances in Normandy. Thetwo joined in the organized attempt to wrest Apulia from the Greeks,who by 1040 had lost mos
1 CONC t of that province. In 1042 Melfi was chosenas the Norman capital, and in September of that year the Normanselected as their count William Iron-Arm who was succeeded in turn byhis brothers Drogo, "Comes Normannorum totius Apuliae 
1 CONC e Calabriae",and Humphrey, who arrived about 1044. 1047 saw the arrival of Robert,the sixth son of Tancred of Hauteville. His tall stature, blondecolouring, blue eyes, and powerful voice.
1 CONT
1 CONT Guiscard soon rose to distinction. The Lombards turned against theirallies and Leo IX determined to expel the Norman freebooters. The armywhich he led towards Apulia in 1053 was, however, overthrown atCivitate on the Fortore by th
1 CONC e Normans united under Humfrey, Guiscardand Richard of Aversa. In 1057 Robert succeeded Humfrey as count ofApulia and, in company with Roger his youngest brother, carried on theconquest of Apulia and Calabria, while Richard conque
1 CONC red theprincipality of Capua.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Papacy, foreseeing the breach with the emperor over investitures,now resolved to recognize the Normans and secure them as allies.Therefore at Melfi, on August 23, 1059, Nicholas II invested Robertwith Apulia, Calabria, and Sic
1 CONC ily, and Richard with Capua. Guiscard"by Grace of God and St Peter duke of Apulia and Calabria and futurelord of Sicily" agreed to hold by annual rent of the Holy See and tomaintain its cause.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the next twenty years he made an amazing series of conquests.Invading Sicily with Roger, the brothers captured Messina (1061) andPalermo (1072). Bari was reduced (April 1071) and the Greeks finallyousted from southern Italy. Th
1 CONC e territory of Salerno was alreadyRobert's; in December 1076 he took the city, expelling its Lombardprince Gisulf, whose sister Sikelgaita he had married. The Normanattacks on Benevento, a papal fief, alarmed and angered Gregory V
1 CONC II,but pressed hard by the emperor, Henry III, he turned again to theNormans, and at Ceprano (June 1080) reinvested Robert, securing himalso in the southern Abruzzi, but reserving Salerno.
1 CONT
1 CONT Guiscard's last enterprise was his attack on the Greek Empire, arallying ground for his rebel vassals. He contemplated seizing thethrone of the Basileus and took up the cause of Michael VII, who hadbeen deposed in 1078 and to whos
1 CONC e son his daughter had been betrothed.He sailed with 16,000 men against the empire in May 1081, and byFebruary 1082 had occupied Corfu and Durazzo, defeating the emperorAlexis before the latter (the Battle of Dyrrhachium, October 
1 CONC 1081). Hewas, however, recalled to the aid of Gregory VII, besieged in SanAngelo by Henry IV (June 1083).
1 CONT
1 CONT Marching north with 36,000 men he entered Rome and forced Henry toretire, but an émeute of the citizens led to a three days' sack of thecity (May 1084), after which Guiscard escorted the pope to Rome. Hisson Bohemund, for a time m
1 CONC aster of Thessaly, had now lost the Greekconquests. Robert, returning to restore them, occupied Corfu andKephalonia, but died of fever in the latter on July 15 1085, in his70th year. He was buried in S. Trinità at Venosa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Guiscard was succeeded by Roger "Borsa," his son by Sikelgaita;Bohemund, his son by an earlier Norman wife Alberada, being set aside.At his death Robert was duke of Apulia and Calabria, prince of Salernoand suzerain of Sicily. His
1 CONC  successes had been due not only to hisgreat qualities but to the "entente" with the Papal See. He createdand enforced a strong ducal power which, however, was met by manybaronial revolts, one being in 1078, when he demanded from 
1 CONC the Apulianvassals an "aid" on the betrothal of his daughter. In conquering suchwide territories he had little time to organize them internally. Inthe history of the Norman kingdom of Italy Guiscard remainsessentially the hero and
1 CONC  founder, as his nephew Roger II is thestatesman and organizer.
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT F. Chalandon, Histoire de la domination normande en Italie et enSicile (Paris, 1907)
1 CONT L. von Heinemann, Geschichte der Normannen in Unteritalien (Leipzig1894)
1 CONT Graham Loud, The Age of Robert Guiscard (ISBN 0582045290)
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the public domain 1911Encyclopædia Britannica.
