0 @N6000000002188091692@ NOTE Roger Borsa
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Roger Borsa (1060/1061 – February 22, 1111) was the son and successor of Robert Guiscard, the Norman conqueror of Southern Italy and Sicily. His mother was Sikelgaita, an imposing warrior Lombard noblewoman. Roger was not as adept
1 CONC  as Robert Guiscard, and most of his reign was spent in feudal anarchy. His ambitious mother arranged for Roger to succeed his father in place of Robert Guiscard's eldest son by another wife, Bohemund of Taranto. According to John
1 CONC  Julius Cooper, 2nd Viscount Norwich:
1 CONT
1 CONT . . . Roger—nicknamed Borsa, the purse, from his early-ingrained habit of counting and recounting his money. He was a weak and hesitant thirteen-year-old who gave the impression that a childhood spent with Robert and Sichelgaita h
1 CONC ad been too much for him. 
1 CONT In 1073, Sichelgaita had Roger proclaimed heir after Guiscard fell ill at Trani. Roger's cousin Abelard was the only baron to dissent from the election of Roger, claiming that he was the rightful heir to the duchy. Roger accompani
1 CONC ed his father on a campaign to Greece in 1084. He was still in Greece when his father died on 17 July 1085 at Corfu. While Bohemond was supposed to inherit the Greek possessions and Roger the Italian ones, it was Bohemund who was 
1 CONC in Italy (Salerno) and Roger in Greece (Bundicia) at the time of the Guiscard's passing.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roger rejoined his mother on Cephalonia, his absence causing panic and confusion with his army, according to Goffredo Malaterra. The two quickly returned to the peninsula and with the support of Roger I of Sicily, his uncle, was r
1 CONC ecognised as duke in September. His Lombard heritage also made him a more attractive candidate than his Norman half-brother, who had fled to Capua. With the support of Jordan I of Capua, he rebelled against his brother and took Or
1 CONC ia, Otranto, and Taranto. Roger, however, made peace in March 1086 and the brothers acted as effective co-rulers. In late Summer 1087, Bohemond renewed the war with the support of some of his brother's vassals. He surprised and de
1 CONC feated Roger at Fragneto and retook Taranto. Though described as a powerful warrior (he took the cities of Benevento, Canosa, Capua, and Lucera by siege), Roger Borsa was never able to check Bohemund's power or bring him under his
1 CONC  control. The war was finally resolved by the mediation of Pope Urban II and the award of Taranto and other possessions to Bohemund. Roger also granted him Cosenza and other holdings he desired allodially. In 1089 Roger Borsa was 
1 CONC officially invested with the duchy of Apulia by Pope Urban II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Borsa planned to urbanise the Mezzogiorno by granting charters to various towns and encouraging urban planning. In 1090, he and Urban encouraged Bruno of Cologne, founder of the Carthusian Order to accept election to the archbisho
1 CONC pric of Reggio.
1 CONT
1 CONT In May 1098, at the request of his first cousin once removed, Prince Richard II of Capua, Borsa and his uncle Count Roger I of Sicily began the siege of Capua, from which the prince had long ago been exiled as a minor. In exchange
1 CONC  for his assistance, the duke received the homage of Richard, though he seems to have made no use of it, for Richard's successors paid no heed to Roger Borsa's overlordship. Capua fell after forty days of notable besieging, for Po
1 CONC pe Urban II had come to meet Roger of Sicily and Archbishop Anselm of Canterbury had come to meet the pope.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1092, Roger Borsa married Adela, the daughter of Robert I, Count of Flanders, and widow of Canute IV of Denmark. He was succeeded by their son William. However William proved to be as weak a ruler as his father, and the domain 
1 CONC was ultimately inherited by a cousin, Roger II of Sicily.
1 CONT
1 CONT One of the prime sources for Roger's reign is the work of William of Apulia, who dedicated his chronicle to Guiscard and his son.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Robert Guiscard Duke of Apulia
1 CONT 1085–1111 Succeeded by:
1 CONT William II 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Norwich, John Julius. The Normans in the South 1016-1130. Longmans: London, 1967. 
1 CONT Norwich, John Julius. The Kingdom in the Sun 1130-1194. Longman: London, 1970. 
1 CONT Matthew, Donald. The Norman Kingdom of Sicily. Cambridge University Press: 1992. 
