0 @N6000000002187893063@ NOTE Mihailo Vojislav
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mihailo of the House of Voislav was the ruler of Duklja as Grand Prince (1050-1077) and King (1077-1082).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Mihailo Voislav, the greatest ruler of Duklja on a fresco in the Church of St. Michael in Ston: He was crowned as King of Slavs and became known as the Ruler of Tribals and SerbsHe was the first king of Duklja, under whom it was t
1 CONC he first Serbian state to achieve more widespread international recognition. Originally, Mihailo (Michael) appeared to have shared power (or perhaps been "the first among equals") with his four brothers. An early threat by a break
1 CONC away rebellion in Trebinje was faced by coordinated action of the brothers, and the agreement that bound them in so doing, brokered by their mother, is perhaps the oldest known treaty in Serbian history.
1 CONT
1 CONT While in no imminent danger from that side, Mihailo found it favorable to further strengthen ties with Byzantium around 1052, gaining a patrician title and marriage to a Greek princess in the process. This might have implied titul
1 CONC ar recognition of Constantinople's authority, but no real concessions on his part. It corresponded to the current balance of forces, and bought some 20 years of peace and prosperity to his land.
1 CONT
1 CONT Matters started to change after 1071, the year of Byzantium's key Asian debacle at Manzikert, as well as of the loss of south Italy to the Normans. Following the Slavic uprising in Macedonia, Mihailo broke his neutrality and sent 
1 CONC off troops under his son Bodin to aid the rebels, upon their request. Despite initial successes, the rebellion failed toward the end of 1072, with Bodin captured and only much later saved. After this, Mihailo begins looking for su
1 CONC pport westward - to the Pope. This came as a result of his alienation from the Byzantines, but also from a desire to instate an independent archbishopric within his realm, and finally to obtain a royal title. In the aftertmath of 
1 CONC the Church schism of 1054, Pope Gregory VII had an interest in bestowing these on rulers in the rift area, and Mihailo was granted one, sometime prior to 1077. Thereafter, Duklja (Zeta) is referred to as a kingdom, until its reduc
1 CONC tion in the following century.
1 CONT
1 CONT Having sealed ties with the Normans through marriage of his heir Bodin, Mihailo died in 1081, after a rule of 30 or so years. He left us St. Michael's Church in Ston, north of Dubrovnik, a small church following mostly an early By
1 CONC zantine style, which contains the oldest known fresco portrait of a Balkan Slavic ruler.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stephen Dobroslav I Voislav Grand Prince
1 CONT 1050–1082 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Constantin Bodin of Voislav 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT none King of Duklja
1 CONT 1077–1082 
1 CONT
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Mihailo Vojislavici, knez Duklje1
1 CONT b. circa 1015, d. circa 1082, #16814
1 CONT Pedigree 
1 CONT       Also called Mihailo I of Zeta.2 Ban of Duklja.3 A member of ruling the house of Duklja, usually called Vojislavici (descendants of Voislav).1 Also called Michael of Duklja. Mihailo Vojislavici, knez Duklje was born circa 101
1 CONC 5. He was the son of Stefan Vojislav, knez Duklje and N. N. Kometopoulos.4 He was married twice, once to a niece of an undetermined Byzantine Emperor.4 He married Neda Dukljanina, daughter of knez Duklje Jovan Vladimir Dukljanina 
1 CONC and Teodora Kosara Kometopoulos; His 1st.5,2 He had seven sons by his first wife whose names were these: Vladimir, Priaslav, Sergius, Deria, Gabriel, Miroslav and Bodin.5 King of Duklja in (Montenegro), Balkan Peninsula, between 1
1 CONC 047 and 1081.4,1 He received the kingdom only after his mother's death, for so long as his mother lived, he and his brothers were only called princes (knesii) [knesius=knez in Slavonic].5 He found it favorable to further strengthe
1 CONC n ties with Byzantium around, gaining a patrician title and marriage to a Greek princess in the process, in 1052.6 He married N. N. of Byzantium in 1052; His 2nd.5,7 He four further sons by his 2nd wife, a relative of the Greek em
1 CONC peror, namely Dobroslav, second Petrislav, third Nicephorus, fourth Theodorus.5 He broke his neutrality towards Byzantium, now beset by troubles to the east, and sent off troops under his son Bodin to aid the rebel Slavs in Macedo
1 CONC nia, upon their request, in 1071.6 He is not satisfied with the election of Slavac and seceded from the Croatian state and proclaimed himself the autonomous ruler of South Croatia in 1074.3 He was granted a royal title by Pope Gre
1 CONC gory VI, who in the aftertmath of the Church schism of 1054 had an interest in bestowing these on rulers in the rift area, before 1077.4,6 He added Zahumlje (a large Bosnian territory) to his lands after 1077.4 He died circa 1082.
1 CONC  He died after he had reigned for 35 years.4,5 . He was entombed with great ceremony in the monastery of the Martyr Saints Sergius and Bacchus.5  
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT      Children of Mihailo Vojislavici, knez Duklje and Neda Dukljanin
1 CONT   Vladimir Vojislavici+ 
1 CONT   knez Duklje Konstantin Bodin Vojislavici   b. c 1034? 
1 CONT
1 CONT      Child of Mihailo Vojislavici, knez Duklje and N. N. of Byzantiu
1 CONT   Petrislav, Grand Zupan of Rassa+   b. c 1034?, d. c 1082 
1 CONT
