0 @N6000000002187918606@ NOTE Kavadh II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Kavadh II (Siroes), King of Persia, son of Khosrau II (590–628), was raised to the throne in opposition to his father in February 628, after the great victories of the Emperor Heraclius (610–641). He put his father and eighteen br
1 CONC others to death, began negotiations with Heraclius, but died after a reign of a few months.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Khosrau II Sassanid Ruler
1 CONT 628 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ardashir III
0 @N6000000002187757868@ NOTE Romanus I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Romanus I Lecapenus (Romanos I Lakapenos, 870 - 948), who shared the throne of the Byzantine Empire with Constantine VII and exercised all the real power from 919 to 944, was admiral of the Byzantine fleet on the Danube River when
1 CONC , hearing of the defeat of the army at the Battle of Anchialus (917), he resolved to sail for Constantinople.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the marriage of his daughter Helena to Constantine he was first proclaimed basileopator ("father of the emperor") in 919 and soon after, crowned colleague of his son-in-law. His reign was uneventful, except for an attempt to
1 CONC  check the accumulation of landed property. It was terminated by his own sons, Stephen and Constantine, who in 944 carried him off to the Prince's Islands and compelled him to become a monk. Instead of taking power for themselves,
1 CONC  his sons then recognized Constantine VII as sole emperor. Romanus died in 948.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Bibliography
1 CONT Runciman, Steven. The Emperor Romanus Lecapenus and his Reign. Cambridge: University Press, 1990. (Originally published 1929.) ISBN 0-521-35722-5 
0 @N6000000003949868642@ NOTE Byzantine emperor who shared the imperial throne with his son-in-law Constantine VII and exercised all real power from 920 to 944. Romanus was admiral of the Byzantine fleet on the Danube when, hearing of the defeat of the army at
1 CONC  Achelous (917), he resolved to sail for Constantinople. Soon after the marriage of his daughter Helena to Constantine he was crowned colleague of his son-in-law. His reign was terminated by his own sons, Stephen and Constantine, 
1 CONC who in 944 compelled him to become a monk.
1 CONT He had 3 of his sons crowned co-emperors.  Among his most important allies was General John Kourkouas, who won many important victories against the Abbasids.
1 CONT His reign also saw a number of revolts inside the empire. Especially in Chaldia, the Peloponnesos and in southern Italy were the conflicts hard, but all were eventually subdued.
1 CONT He managed to mend the poor relationship with the Pope in Rome as it had become during the reign of Leo 6. He reinstated Nicholas Mysticus as Patriarch in Constantinople.   Romanus involved himself much in juridical matters. He in
1 CONC troduced laws that aimed at protecting small landowners against the ambitions fo the large estates. Instead, taxes were increased for large landowners.
0 @N6000000002188110538@ NOTE Patriarch Theophylaktos of Constantinople
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Theophylaktos Lekapenos or Theophylactus Lecapenus (Greek: Te?f??a?t?? ?a?ap????, Theophylaktos Lakapenos), (917–February 27, 956), Patriarch of Constantinople from February 2, 933 to his death in 956.
1 CONT
1 CONT Theophylaktos was the youngest son of Emperor Romanos I Lekapenos by Theodora. Romanos I planned to make his son Patriarch as soon as Nicholas Mystikos died in 925, but two minor patriarchates and a two-year vacancy passed before 
1 CONC Theophylaktos was considered old enough to discharge his duties as patriarch (still only 16 years old). Theophylaktos was the third patriarch of Constantinople to be the son of an emperor and the only one to have become patriarch 
1 CONC during the reign of his father. His patriarchate of just over 23 years was unusually long, and his father had secured the support of Pope John XI for his elevation to the patriarchate. Apart from the bastard eunuch Basil, Theophyl
1 CONC aktos was the only male member of the Lekapenoi to survive the family's fall from power in 945.
1 CONT
1 CONT Theophylaktos supported his father's policies and pursued ecclesiastical ecumenicalism, keeping in close contact with the patriarchates of Alexandria and Antioch. He sent missionaries to the Magyars, trying to help the efforts of 
1 CONC imperial diplomacy in the late 940s. At about the same time, Theophylaktos advised his nephew-in-law Emperor Peter I of Bulgaria on the new Bogomil heresy. Theophylaktos introduced theatrical elements to the Byzantine liturgy, som
1 CONC ething which was not universally supported by the conservative clergy around him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Theophylaktos' detractors describe him as an irreverent man primarily interested in his huge stable of horses, who was ready to abandon the celebration of mass in Hagia Sophia to be present at the foaling of his favorite mare. And
1 CONC  turned the churches of Hagia Sophia and Hagia Eirini to whorehouses. Perhaps ironically, Theophylaktos died after falling from a horse in 956.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Tryphon Patriarch of Constantinople
1 CONT 933–956 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Polyeuctus 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT The Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium, Oxford University Press, 1991. 
1 CONT Warren Treadgold, A History of the Byzantine State and Society. Stanford University Press, 1997. 
