0 @N6000000002187757792@ NOTE Constantine VIII
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantine VIII (in Greek Konstantinos VIII, written ???sta?t???? ?') (960 – November 15, 1028), Byzantine emperor (December 15, 1025 – November 15, 1028) was the son of the Emperor Romanus II and Theophano; the younger brother o
1 CONC f the eminent Basil II, who died childless and thus left the rule of the Byzantine Empire in his hands.
1 CONT
1 CONT Technically, Constantine became co-emperor with his brother when Basil assumed the throne in 976 at the age of 18, but during the 49 years that Basil II ruled, his brother had little role in the affairs of state even though Basil 
1 CONC was often away from Constantinople fighting wars. Constantine is said to have spent most of this time living in luxury and with little or no responsibility.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Basil died on December 15, 1025, Constantine finally became sole emperor, although he ruled for less than three years before his own death on November 15, 1028.
1 CONT
1 CONT Physically Constantine was tall and magnificent where Basil had been short and stocky, and was graceful. He was a superb horseman. By the time he became emperor, he had chronic gout and could hardly walk. His reign was a disaster 
1 CONC because he lacked in courage. He reacted to every challenge with cruelty. He ordered the execution or mutilation of hundreds of innocent men. Constantine carried on as he always had: hunting, feasting, and enjoying life - and avoi
1 CONC ded state business as much as possible. He was poor at appointing officials. Within months, the land laws of Basil II were dropped under pressure from the Anatolian aristocracy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Like his brother, Constantine died without a male heir. The empire thus passed to his daughter Zoë and a long line of her husbands and children, none of whom distinguished themselves.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Bibliography
1 CONT Michael Psellus the Younger. Chronographia. 
