0 @N6000000000437181301@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Grand Prince Name Suffix:<NSFX> I, Of Kiev
0 @N6000000000945084157@ NOTE Familien var en av de mest ansette slekter i Øvre Schwaben og var i slekt med 
1 CONT Hohenstauferne.
1 CONT Deres navneborg reiste seg over landsbyen syd for Ehingen. De haadde greve- og
1 CONT herskapsbesittelser i Berg (Ehingen) og Schelklingen, uten tvil også i Wartstein, og fikk i
1 CONT begynnelsen av det 13. århundre også det statelige markgrevskapet Burgau.
1 CONT Familiens sikre genealogi begynner med Poppo. I Zweifalten klosters nekrolog som antagelig
1 CONT ble påbegynt i Popos sønns eller sønnesønns tid, kalles han greve av Berg.
1 CONT Han er med sin hustru begravet i klostret.
0 @N6000000000945065107@ NOTE Storfyrste av Kijev [Kiev] 1054-68, 1069-73 og 1076-78. 
1 CONT Izyaslav [Yziaslav] levde som landflyktig i Polen i 1068-69 og 1073-76.
1 CONT Han ble drept i 1078.
0 @N6000000000945049656@ NOTE Hertug av Polen 1080 - 1102. 
1 CONT Vladislaw [Ladislaus] overtok regjeringen i 1080 efter at hans bror Boleslaus II hadde blitt jaget
1 CONT bort.
1 CONT Da hans første hustru, Judith, døde kort tid etter Boleslaus III's fødsel, ble forholdet til Böhmen
1 CONT bittert, og Polen måtte senere betale tributt. Han søkte da støtte hos keiser Heinrich IV.
1 CONT Senere inngikk han ekteskap med keiserens søster, Jutta, men ekteskapet ble barnløst.
1 CONT Heldigst var han i sine tog mot Pommern 1081-90 og 1092-99, men ellers var hans styre urolig.
1 CONT Den opprørske adel innkalte hans uekte sønn Zbigniev mot ham. I kampen seiret Vladislaw,
1 CONT og Zbigniev ble en tid holdt fanget.
1 CONT I 1097 ble landene delt i tre, hvorav han selv beholdt de viktigste borgene, men han ga
1 CONT Zbigniev og sin ekte sønn, Boleslaus, hver en del.
1 CONT Ettter hans død seiret Boleslaus III over Zbigniev og blindet ham.
0 @N6000000002187645573@ NOTE Iziaslav of Kiev
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Iziaslav Yaroslavich (1024-1078), Kniaz' (Prince) of Turaw, Velikii Kniaz (the Grand Prince) of Kiev (since 1054), the oldest son of Yaroslav I the Wise. Iziaslav was one of the authors of "Pravda Yaroslavichey" - a part of the fi
1 CONC rst Russian legal code called Russkaya Pravda.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1043 his father Velikii Kniaz (Grand Prince) Yaroslav made an agreement with King Casimir I of Poland that recognized Cherven as part of Kiev. The agreement was sealed with a double marriage—Casimir to Dobronega, Yaroslav's sis
1 CONC ter; and Iziaslav to Gertrude, Casimir's sister. From this marriage was born Iziaslav's son Yaropolk. His other son, Sviatopolk, was illegitimate.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a result of the popular uprising in 1068, Iziaslav was deposed and fled to Poland. In 1069 he retook Kiev with the help the Polish army, however, he was ousted again by his brothers in 1073. Iziaslav turned to German emperor, P
1 CONC olish king and the Pope for help on several occasions. In 1077 he succeeded in retaking Kiev once again, but soon died in an internecine war against princes Oleg Svyatoslavich and Boris Vyacheslavich.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Yaroslav I Prince of Kiev and Turaw
1 CONT {{{years}}} Succeeded by:
1 CONT Vseslav of Polotsk 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Vseslav of Polotsk Prince of Kiev and Turaw
1 CONT {{{years}}} Succeeded by:
1 CONT Sviatoslav II of Kiev 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sviatoslav II of Kiev Prince of Kiev and Turaw
1 CONT {{{years}}} Succeeded by:
1 CONT Vsevolod I of Kiev 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Martin, Janet. Medieval Russia, 980-1584 (Cambridge Medieval Textbooks) 
0 @N6000000002187639761@ NOTE Wladislaus I Herman of Poland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladislaus I Herman (Polish: Wladyslaw I Herman, also seen as Ladislaus, Ladislas or Vladislav) (1040-1102), duke of Poland, was the son of Casimir I of Poland, duke of Poland. He was the brother of Boleslaus II, the Bold, King of
1 CONC  Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT He took the Polish throne in 1079 when Boleslaus II died, but resigned the royal title and supported Henry IV in a bid to restore peace.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladislaus Herman was first married to Judith of Bohemia, then to Judith of Swabia, the daughter of Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladislaus and Judith of Swabia had a daughter named Agnes of Gandersheim.
0 @N6000000003051234213@ NOTE [From "The Great Dynasties", retrieved 13 Aug 07]
1 CONT Boleslaw was expelled in a later revolt and replaced by his weak brother, Wladyslaw Herman (b. 1043; d. 1102), ruled 1079 - 1102. Wladyslaw distanced himself from any involvement in the east and it is during this period that the R
1 CONC uthenians colonised the lands of the Dniester and the San and we see the growth of Halicz.
0 @N6000000003051240769@ NOTE He was deposed 1068-1069, 1073-1076.
0 @N6000000003949868777@ NOTE The Laurentian Chronicle describes Izyaslav thusly: He "was a man fair of appearance and imposing in stature, not malicious in temper, but a hater of injustice and a lover of rectitude. In him there was no guile, for he was a simp
1 CONC le man who did not render evil for evil." His reign was one of the most troubles of all the Yaroslavichi in Kyiv. He was driven from the city twice, regaining his throne each time with the aid of other's troops. Finally, he was th
1 CONC e only one of the Rurikid princes to die in battle with his relatives. The menacing clouds of an imminent storm were gathering on the horizon of the Asiatic steppes. The Polovtsi came up into the Pereyaslavl region in 1055 but Pri
1 CONC nce Vsyevolod Yaroslavich came to terms with them and they threatened Russia only in 1061. The Chronicle derides the morays of the Polovtsi; they ate raw meat, drank the blood of the freshly slaughtered herds, and also drank the m
1 CONC are's milk. In 1061 under the command of their Prince Sekala, they suddenly burst into regions of Russia, attacked Vsyevolod, thrashed him and returned to the Don with their loot. From this event begins the period of misery. The p
1 CONC eople's attention was focused on a comet and many other unexplained occurrences.
0 @N6000000004533651133@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladislaus I on Jan Matejko's paintingWladislaus I Herman (PolishWladyslaw I Herman, also seen as Ladislaus, Ladislas or Vladislav)(1040-1102), duke of Poland, was the son of Casimir I of Poland, dukeof Poland. He was the brother 
1 CONC of Boleslaus II, the Bold, King ofPoland.
1 CONT
1 CONT He took the Polish throne in 1079 when Boleslaus II died, but resignedthe royal title and supported Henry IV in a bid to restore peace.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladislaus Herman was first married to Judith of Bohemia, then toJudith of Swabia, the daughter of Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladislaus and Judith of Swabia had a daughter named Agnes ofGandersheim.
