1 CONT Anne of Kiev or Anna Yaroslavna (between 1024 and 1032 ? 1075), daughter of Yaroslav I of Kiev, and his wife Ingegarde of Sweden, was the queen consort of France, as the wife of Henry I and regent for her son Philip I. After the d
1 CONC eath of his first wife, Matilda, King Henry searched the courts of Europe for a suitable bride, but could not locate a princess who was not related to him within illegal degrees of kinship. At last he sent an embassy to distant Ki
1 CONC ev, which returned with Anne (also called Agnes or Anna). Anne and Henry were married at the cathedral of Reims, May 19, 1051. They had three sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip (May 23, 1052 ? July 30, 1108). Anne is credited with bringing the name Philip to the Western Europe. She imported this Greek name (Philippos, from philos (love) and hippos (horse), meaning "the one that love horses") from 
1 CONC her Eastern Orthodox culture.
1 CONT Hugh (1057 ? October 18, 1102), called the Great or Magnus, later Count of Crépi, who married the heiress of Vermandois and died on crusade in Tarsus, Cilicia.
1 CONT Robert (c.1055 ? c.1060)
1 CONT For six years after Henry's death in 1060, she served as regent for Philip, who was only seven at the time. She was the first queen of France to serve as regent. Her co-regent was Count Baldwin V of Flanders. Anne was a literate w
1 CONC oman, rare for the time, but there was some opposition to her as regent on the grounds that her mastery of French was less than fluent.
1 CONT
1 CONT A year after the king's death, Anne, acting as regent, took a passionate fancy for Count Ralph III of Valois, a man whose political ambition encouraged him to repudiate his wife to marry Anne in 1062. Accused of adultery, Ralph's 
1 CONC wife appealed to Pope Alexander II, who excommunicated the couple. The young king Philip forgave his mother, which was just as well, since he was to find himself in a very similar predicament in the 1090s. Ralph died in September 
1 CONC 1074, at which time Anne returned to the French court. She died in 1075, was buried at Villiers Abbey, La-Ferte-Alais, Essonne and her obits were celebrated on September 5.
0 @N6000000003495155016@ NOTE
1 CONT Princess Anne of Kiev1 (F)
1 CONT b. circa 1024, d. circa 1075, #103174
1 CONT Pedigree
1 CONT Last Edited=19 Jun 2005
1 CONT
1 CONT      Princess Anne of Kiev was born circa 1024. She was the daughter of Jarislaus I of Kiev, Grand Duke of Kiev.1 She married Raoul Crepi.2 She married Henri I, Roi de France, son of Robert II, Roi de France and Constance d'Arles,
1 CONC  on 29 January 1044.1 She died circa 1075. 
1 CONT      Princess Anne of Kiev gained the title of Princess Anne of Kiev.
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Princess Anne of Kiev and Henri I, Roi de France
1 CONT Philippe I, Roi de France+ b. c 1052, d. 29 Jul 11081
1 CONT Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois+ b. 1057, d. 18 Oct 11022
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT 1. [S45] Marcellus Donald R. von Redlich, Pedigrees of Some of the Emperor Charlemagne's Descendants, volume I (1941; reprint, Baltimore, Maryland, U.S.A.: Genealogical Publishing Company, 2002), page 63. Hereinafter cited as Pedi
1 CONC grees of Emperor Charlemagne, I.
1 CONT 2. [S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 64. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anne of Kiev
1 CONT Princess Anne of Kiev or Anna Yaroslavna (b. between 1024 and 1032 - 1075), daughter of Yaroslav I, and his wife Ingegarde of Sweden, was Queen consort of France, as the wife of King Henri I. After the death of his first wife, Mat
1 CONC hilde, King Henri had searched the courts of Europe for a suitable bride, but could not locate a princess who was not related to him within illegal degrees of kinship. At last he sent an embassy to distant Kiev, who returned with 
1 CONC Anne (also called Agnes or Anna). Anne and Henri were married at the cathedral of Reims, May 19 1051. They had three sons:
1 CONT • Philippe I, King of France (May 23, 1052 - July 30 1108. Anne is credited with bringing the name Philippe into the French dynasty. She imported this Greek name (Philo: Love, Hippo: Horse, the one that love horses) from her orien
1 CONC tal Christian Orthodox cultural background.
1 CONT • Hugues ("Magnus"), later Count of Crépi (1057 - October 18 1102), who married the heiress of Vermandois and died on crusade in Tarsus, Cilicia.
1 CONT • Robert (ca 1055 - ca 1060)
1 CONT For six years after his death in 1060, she served as regent for their son, Philip, who was 7 at the time of his father's death. She was the first queen of France to serve as regent. Her co-regent was Count Baldwin V of Flanders. A
1 CONC nne was known as a literate woman, rare for the time, but there was some opposition to her as Regent on the grounds that her mastery of French was less than fluent.
1 CONT A year after the King's death, Anne, acting as Regent, took a passionate fancy for Count Raoul III of Valois and of the Vexin, a man whose political ambition encouraged him to repudiate his wife to marry Anne in 1062. Accused of a
1 CONC dultery, Raoul's wife appealed to Pope Alexander II, who excommunicated Raoul and Anne. The young king Philippe forgave his mother, which was just as well, since he was to find himself in a very similar predicament in the 1090s. R
1 CONC aoul died in September 1074, at which time Anne returned to the French court. She died in 1075, was buried at Villiers Abbey, La-Ferte-Alais, Essonne, France and her obits were celebrated on September 5.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Bauthier, Robert-Henri. Anne de Kiev reine de France et la politique royale au Xe siècle, revue des Etudes Slaves, Vol. 57, 1985
1 CONT This entry is from Wikipedia, the leading user-contributed encyclopedia. It may not have been reviewed by professional editors (see full disclaimer)
