0 @N6000000002187828014@ NOTE Eufemia of Kiev
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eufemia of Kiev (? – 4 April 1139), Queen Consort of Hungary.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eufemia was the daughter of Grand Prince Vladimir II of Kiev and his second wife whose name and descendance is unknown. She was married to King Coloman of Hungary around 1112. However, her husband, who had been suffering from a se
1 CONC rious disease, caught her in adultery and immadiately sent her back to Kiev. Eufemia gave birth to her son, Boris Conrad in her father's court, but the son was never recognised by King Coloman
1 CONT
1 CONT Afterwards, she lived in a monastery near Kiev till her death.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and child
1 CONT # c. 1112: King Coloman of Hungary (c. 1070 – 3 February 1116)
1 CONT
1 CONT Boris Conrad (1113 – 1155/1156)[1] 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Soltész, István: Árpád-házi királynék (Gabo, 1999) 
1 CONT Kristó, Gyula - Makk, Ferenc: Az Árpád-ház uralkodói (IPC Könyvek, 1996) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ He was never acknowledged as Coloman's son because of his mother's adultery. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Felicia of Sicily Queen Consort of Hungary
1 CONT c.1112–c.1113 Succeeded by
1 CONT ? 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Felicia of Sicily Queen Consort of Croatia
1 CONT c.1112–c.1113 Succeeded by
1 CONT ? 
