0 @N6000000000945068622@ NOTE Tancred, som levde i 1031, var en normannisk ridder. Han bodde på borgen Hauteville 
1 CONT de Guichard nær Cautances.
1 CONT Tancred var far til 12 sønner, hvorav 10 dro til Syditalien i 1037. Her hadde franske
1 CONT normanner i langobardisk tjeneste under Rainulf av Aversa grunnet et selvstendig grevskap.
1 CONT Først gikk hans sønner i grekernes tjeneste mot sarasenerne, men da de kun fikk svik til
1 CONT gjengjeld, begynte kampen mot grekerne.
1 CONT Over de land de erobret, hersket først den eldste
1 CONT broren, Wilhelm I Jernarmen, som greve av Apulia. Hans brødre Drogo (1046-51) og Humfred
1 CONT (1051-56) etterfulgte ham, og Humfred tvang pave Leo IX til å gi normannerne de land i len
1 CONT som var tatt fra grekerne og maurerne. Robert Guiscard styrte så (1056-85) i stedet for
1 CONT Humfreds umyndige barn. Roberts bror, Roger I, erobret Sicilien, mens dennes sønn, Roger II,
1 CONT forenet Nedre Italien og Sicilien til kongeriket Sicilien.
1 CONT Han var første gang gift med Muriella.
0 @N6000000002322298341@ NOTE The wars fought by the Hauteville family contributed to a steady reduction of
1 CONT Muslim and Byzantine power in the region. In their conquered territories the
1 CONT Hauteville descendants established strong states that were organized
1 CONT according to hierarchical feudal norms. Freedom of religious worship was
1 CONT widely permitted. Tancred's three sons were among the Norman knights who
1 CONT flocked to southern Italy early in the 11th century. Serving as mercenaries
1 CONT at first, they soon began to seize lands for themselves.
0 @N6000000002187891661@ NOTE Tancred of Hauteville
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Tancred of Hauteville was an eleventh-century Norman petty lord about whom little is known. His historical importance comes entirely from the accomplishments of his sons and later descendants. He was a minor noble near Coutances i
1 CONC n the Cotentin, but it is not even certain which of the three villages called Hauteville he held, though Hauteville-la-Guichard is most often cited. Various legends later arose about him which have no supporting contemporary evide
1 CONC nce.
1 CONT
1 CONT He had 12 sons by his two wives, and several daughters, almost all of whom left Normandy for southern Italy and acquired some prominence there.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his first wife Muriel he had five sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT William Iron-Arm, count of Apulia (1042-1046) 
1 CONT Drogo, count of Apulia (1046-1051) 
1 CONT Humphrey, count of Apulia (1051-1057) 
1 CONT Geoffrey, count of Loritello 
1 CONT Serlo (stayed in Normandy), lord of Hauteville (1041) 
1 CONT According to the Italian chronicler of the Norman feats in the south, Amatus of Montecassino, Tancred was a morally upright man who would not carry on a sinful relationship and so remarried, being unable also to live out his life 
1 CONC in perfect celibacy. By his second wife Fressenda (or Fredesenda) he had seven sons and at least one daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Guiscard, count of Apulia (1057), then duke of Apulia and Sicily (1059-1085) 
1 CONT Mauger, count of the Capitanate (1056-1059) 
1 CONT William (Willerm or Guillerm, Willermus in Latin), count of the Principate 
1 CONT Aubrey (Alberic or Alvared, Alveredus in Latin; sometimes called Alvred or Alfred) (stayed in Normandy) 
1 CONT Humbert (Hubert) (stayed in Normandy) 
1 CONT Tancred (stayed in Normandy) 
1 CONT Roger, count of Sicily from 1062 
1 CONT Fressenda, who married Richard I (dead in 1078), count of Aversa and prince of Capua 
