1 CONT Humphrey, count of Apulia (1051-1057)
1 CONT Geoffrey, count of Loritello
1 CONT Serlo (stayed in Normandy), lord of Hauteville (1041)
1 CONT According to the Italian chronicler of the Norman feats in the south, Amatus of Montecassino, Tancred was a morally upright man who would not carry on a sinful relationship and so remarried, being unable also to live out his life 
1 CONC in perfect celibacy. By his second wife Fressenda (or Fredesenda) he had seven sons and at least one daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Guiscard, count of Apulia (1057), then duke of Apulia and Sicily (1059-1085)
1 CONT Mauger, count of the Capitanate (1056-1059)
1 CONT William (Willerm or Guillerm, Willermus in Latin), count of the Principate
1 CONT Aubrey (Alberic or Alvared, Alveredus in Latin; sometimes called Alvred or Alfred) (stayed in Normandy)
1 CONT Humbert (Hubert) (stayed in Normandy)
1 CONT Tancred (stayed in Normandy)
1 CONT Roger, count of Sicily from 1062
1 CONT Fressenda, who married Richard I (dead in 1078), count of Aversa and prince of Capua
0 @N6000000003949736782@ NOTE "At Hautville-la-Guichard, eight miles or so north-east of Coutances, the ruins of an ancient castle marked until recently what was once the home of tancred, the father of the conquerors of Apulia, and the grandfather of Roger II.
1 CONC  of Naples and Sicily. Tancred was a knight of small patrimony, possessing apparently a single manor. He was born in the latter half of the tenth century at a time when only two generations separated the Normans, now becoming Gall
1 CONC icised in blood, physique, and culture, from their Norse ancestors who, heathens and barbarians, had sailed with Rollo up the Seine and shared the duchy of Normandy with him. Many legends testify to the tremendous physical strengt
1 CONC h and personal address of the old Tancred, the worthy founder of a great race. Poor as he was in possessions, he imparted courage, strength, and high gifts to twelve sons who grew up round him and found the patrimony too narrow fo
1 CONC r them. They were the product of two marriages: Muriella was the mother of William, Drogo, Humfrey, Geoffrey, and Serlo; a second wife, Fredesinda or Fressenda, bore him Robert afterwards called Guiscard, Mauger, another William, 
1 CONC Alveredus or Auvray, Tancred, Humbert, and Roger. Of the twelve, eight sought their fortunes in Italy. Five proved to be conquerors of the first rank."
0 @N6000000004533664247@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Tancred of Hauteville was the Norman noble, about whom little isknown; his historical importance comes entirely from theaccomplishments of his sons and later descendants. He was a minornoble near Coutances in the Cotentin in Norma
1 CONC ndy, but it is not evencertain which of the 3 villages called Hauteville he held. Variouslegends later arose about him which have no supporting contemporaryevidence.
1 CONT
1 CONT He had 12 sons by his two wives, and several daughters, almost all ofwhom left Normandy for southern Italy and acquired some prominencethere.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his first wife Muriel he had 5 sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT William Iron-Arm, count of Apulia 1042-1046 (d. 1046)
1 CONT Drogo, count of Apulia 1046-1051 (d. 1051)
1 CONT Humphrey, count of Apulia 1051-1057 (d. 1057)
1 CONT Godfrey, count of Loritello
1 CONT Serlo (stayed in Normandy)
1 CONT By his second wife Fressenda he had 7 sons and at least 1 daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Guiscard, count of Apulia(1057),duke of Apulia,Calabria,andSicily(1059-1085)
1 CONT Mauger
1 CONT William, count of the Principate (d. 1080)
1 CONT Alfred(or Alvred) (stayed in Normandy)
1 CONT Hubert (stayed in Normandy)
1 CONT Tancred
1 CONT Roger I of Sicily,count of Sicily in 1062
1 CONT Fredesenda, who married Richard, count of Aversa and Prince of Capua
1 CONT For the Tancred who participated in the First Crusade, who issometimes also called Tancred of Hauteville, see Tancred, Prince ofGalilee. For the Tancred who was King of Sicily in the late 12thcentury, see Tancred of Sicily.
1 CONT
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tancred_of_Hauteville"
