0 @N6000000002187891630@ NOTE Mauger of Hauteville
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mauger of Hauteville (also Latin Malgerius or Italian Maugerio) was a younger (probably the second) son of Tancred of Hauteville by his second wife, Fressenda. He travelled to the Mezzogiorno with his brother William and his elder
1 CONC  half-brother Geoffrey around 1053, though some sources indicate him coming later, c.1056.
1 CONT
1 CONT He soon distinguished himself and was invested with the county of the Capitanate soon after his arrival (either 1053 or 1057) by Humphrey, his half brother and count of Apulia, but he did not long survive to hold it. According to 
1 CONC Goffredo Malaterra, he died in 1054, though other chroniclers have him dying in 1057 or as late as 1060, after assisting his elder full brother Robert Guiscard, Humphrey's successor, in an expedition against the new army of the By
1 CONC zantine Emperor Constantine X, sent to recover Langobardia. Whenever he died, his fief went to William, who passed it to Geoffrey out of fraternal affection (according to Malaterra).
0 @N6000000002187891768@ NOTE William of the Principate
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT William of Hauteville (c. 1027-1080) was one of the younger sons of Tancred of Hauteville by his second wife Fressenda. He is usually called Willermus instead of Wilelmus in Latin annals and so is often called Guillerm instead of 
1 CONC Guillaume in French. He left Normandy around 1053 with his elder half-brother Geoffrey and full brother Mauger.
1 CONT
1 CONT He participated in the Battle of Civitate in the years of his arrival and was received cordially by his half-brother Humphrey, the reigning count of Apulia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1055, he distinguished himself in the taking of the castle of San Nicandro, which formed the nucleus of his county of the Principate, with which he was invested by Humphrey in 1056. In 1058, he married Maria, the daughter of Gu
1 CONC y, Duke of Sorrento and brother of Guaimar IV of Salerno. He inherited all Guy's lands in the principality of Salerno and fought with Guaimar's successor, Gisulf II, whose lands he ate away at until little was left but Salerno its
1 CONC elf. He also inherited the Capitanate from Mauger, who died between 1054 and 1060. That last possession he gave to Geoffrey, out of fraternal love, Malaterra informs us.
1 CONT
1 CONT He invited his landless youngest brother Roger to join him, promising him half of all he owned, save his wife and children. He aided Roger against their elder brother Robert Guiscard, who had succeeded Humphrey, and gave him the c
1 CONC astle of Scalea, at Catanzaro. He fought against Robert later when Robert came to the aid of Gisulf in order to receive in marriage Gisulf's sister Sichelgaita. In 1067, the Council of Melfi excommunicated him, along with Turgis d
1 CONC e Rota and Guimond de Moulins, for stealing property from the church of Alfano I, Archbishop of Salerno. Later that year, he travelled to Salerno to reconcile with Pope Alexander II.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to some sources, he died in 1080, though others have him living into the twelfth century (to 1104, 1113, or 1117) and participating in the Guiscard's Byzantine campaigns and being present at the Battle of Durazzo, Octobe
1 CONC r 1081. He was buried in the church of the Santissima Trinità in Venosa.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Goffredo Malaterra. The Deeds of Count Roger of Calabria and Sicily and of Duke Robert Guiscard his brother. 
1 CONT Norwich, John Julius. The Normans in the South 1016-1130. Longmans: London, 1967. 
1 CONT Ghisalberti, Albert (ed). Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani: II Albicante – Ammannati. Rome, 1960. 
