0 @N6000000002187821173@ NOTE Anna Dalassena
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna Dalassena (1025-1102) was an important Byzantine noblewoman who rose to the position of an Empress-Mother during the reign of her son Alexios I Komnenos.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna was the daughter of Alexius Charon, the Imperial lieutenant in Italy, and the daughter of Adrian Dalassenus. As her mother's family was more illustrious then her father's, Anna retained her mother's family name throughout her
1 CONC  life, even after she had married.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna married Johannes Komnenos, whose brother Isaac became Emperor in 1057.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her younger son Alexios I Komnenos rose to the throne after vicissitudes of politics. Alexius was for many years under the strong influence of her éminence grise. She is described as a wise and immensely able politician and acted 
1 CONC as regent during the absences of her son.
1 CONT
1 CONT After acceeding to the throne, Alexios crowned his mother Empress Augusta, an honour commonl given to the Emperor's wife, Irene Doukaina in this case.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna was the effective administrator of the Empire during the long absences of Alexios in war campaigns: she was constantly at odds with her daughter-in-law Irene and had assumed total responsibility for the upbringing and educati
1 CONC on of her granddaughter Anna Komnene.
0 @N6000000003051155227@ NOTE Having shared with her son the sovereign power for a while, she noticed his declining patience with her and withdrew to a convent where she died.
1 CONT She probably was a displaced mother-in-law.
0 @N6000000003949935393@ NOTE Anna Dalassena was an important Byzantine noblewoman who rose to position of sort of Empress-Mother during the reign of her son Emperor Alexius I.
1 CONT She married Johannes Komnenos, a nephew of Emperor Isaac I Comnenus (Emperor from 1057 to 1059).
1 CONT Her younger son Alexius rose to the throne after vicissitudes of politics. Alexius was for many years under the strong influence of her eminence grise. She is described as a wise and immensely able politician.
1 CONT She was in a uniquely irregular fashion, crowned as Empress Augusta by her son the emperor, instead of the rightful claimant to the title, Alexius' wife Irene.
1 CONT Anna Dalassena was the effective administrator of the Empire during Alexius' long absences in war campaigns: she was constantly at odds with her daughter-in-law Irene Ducaena and had assumed total responsibility for the upbringing
1 CONC  and education of her granddaughter Anna Comnena.  Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_Dalassena"
1 CONT "It may cause some surprise that my father the Emperor had raised his mother to such a position of honor, and that he had handed complete power over to her. Yielding up the reins of government, one might say, he ran alongside her 
1 CONC as she drove the imperial chariot...
1 CONT
1 CONT My father reserved for himself the waging of wars against the barbarians, while he entrusted to his mother the administration of state affairs, the choosing of civil servants, and the fiscal management of the empire's revenues and
1 CONC  expenses. One might perhaps, in reading this, blame my father's decision to entrust the imperial government to the gyneceum. But once you understood the ability of this woman, her excellence, her good sense, and her remarkable ca
1 CONC pacity for hard work, you would turn from criticism to admiration.
1 CONT
1 CONT For my grandmother really had the gift of conducting the affairs of state. She knew so well how to organize and administer that she was capable of governing not only the Roman Empire but also every other kingdom under the sun....S
1 CONC he was very shrewd in seizing on whatever was called for, and clever in carrying it out with certitude. Not only did she have an outstanding intelligence, but her powers of speech matched it. She was a truly persuasive orator, in 
1 CONC no way wordy or long-winded...
1 CONT
1 CONT She was ripe in years when she ascended the imperial throne, at the moment when her mental powers were at their most vigorous...
1 CONT
1 CONT As for her compassion toward the poor and the lavishness of her hand toward the destitute, how can words describe these things? Her house was a shelter for her needy relatives, and it was no less a haven for strangers....Her expre
1 CONC ssion, which revealed her true character, demanded the worship of the angels but struck terror among demons...
1 CONT The breaking of the Doukas clan by a league of their enemies was largely a female accomplishment, as two of their foremost enemies were Eudokia Makroembolitissa and Anna Dalassene. The latter and her enemy, Caesar John Doukas, wer
1 CONC e the primary exemplars of the new dynastic thinking, whereas Nikephoros Botaneiates attempted to stand alone. And of the two great families, their large numbers of young military men and their matriarch's skill at marriage allian
1 CONC ces brought the victory to the Komnenoi - they absorbed the Doukai and went on to create a century. "The coup of 1081", she writes, "carries the stamp of Anna Dalassene all over it".
1 CONT Anna Dalassene was the "ultimate mother of Byzantium": she was breaking new ground in being vested with responsibility for the entire civil administration, but this was acceptable because unlike Eirene the Athenian, she never atte
1 CONC mpted to set her son aside. Here Hill
1 CONT points out that ambition need not be personal and individual - if this is ignored, Anna becomes only a "self-betraying victim of patriarchy".  On the contrary, she achieved everything she could have dreamed of, an undisputed co-em
1 CONC perorship.
1 CONT Anna Dalassene was interested in monks, especially Christodoulos, who was able to ask for all sort of property rights and get them, but these were not necessarily clients proper. She founded the Pantepoptes.
1 CONT In consequence, Anna has been accused of collusion with patriarchy; she says the usual things about beautiful, soft and gentle women. However, if we look at what the women actually do, we see a different story. Her set-pieces cont
1 CONC rast with the reality of female power that she shows in operation. She is a bit defensive about her mother and grandmother - she admires them, but has to take account of male prejudice. Other feminists' criticism of Anna reflects 
1 CONC their own ideology: it is anachronistic to condemn Anna for not being some kind of modern family-despising woman in a society where family and politics are the same thing. Since the family was the empire and Anna was the first-bor
1 CONC n heir, she thought, why not be emperor herself? But Alexios had wrought too well, and her mother had to rely on changing her father's mind instead of the kind of
1 CONT independent maternal politics practised by Anna Dalassene.
