0 @N6000000002187829781@ NOTE Iziaslav II of Kiev
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Iziaslav II Mstislavich (??????? II ?????????? in Russian) (c. 1097-1154), Prince of Vladimir and Volyn (1134—1142), Pereyaslavl (1143-1145), Velikiy Kniaz (Grand Prince) of Kiev (1146—1154), oldest son of Mstislav Vladimirovich, 
1 CONC Kniaz' (Prince) of Novgorod.
1 CONT
1 CONT His first wife was a German princess, the daughter of Conrad III of Germany. She died in 1151. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Mstislav II of Kiev 
1 CONT Yaroslav II of Kiev 
1 CONT Yaropolk, Prince of Shumsk 
1 CONT Eudoxia, married King Mieszko III of Poland. 
1 CONT Iziaslav's second wife was a daughter of King Demetre I of Georgia, but they were married for only a few months in 1154 before his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Igor II Grand Prince of Kiev Succeeded by:
1 CONT George I 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT George I Grand Prince of Kiev Succeeded by:
1 CONT George I 
0 @N6000000002188101196@ NOTE Emperor Gaozu of Tang
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Gaozu of Tang 
1 CONT Birth and death: 566–Jun. 25, 635 
1 CONT Family name: Li (?) 
1 CONT Given name: Yuan (?) 
1 CONT Courtesy name (?): Shude (??) 
1 CONT Dates of reign: Jun. 18, 618¹–Sept. 4, 626² 
1 CONT Dynasty: Tang (?) 
1 CONT Temple name: Gaozu (??) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (short) Emperor Shenyao³ (????) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (full) Emperor Shenyao Dasheng
1 CONT Daguang Xiao4
1 CONT ????????? 
1 CONT General note: Dates given here are in the Julian calendar.
1 CONT They are not in the proleptic Gregorian calendar. 
1 CONT ——— 
1 CONT 1. Was already in control of Chang'an and de facto master of China
1 CONT since December 12, 617 
1 CONT 2. Abdicated in favor of his son, and was granted the title
1 CONT Taishang Huang (???), a title reserved for retired emperors. 
1 CONT 3. Given in 674 
1 CONT 4. Given in 754 
1 CONT Emperor Gaozu of Tang China (566 - June 25, 635), born Li Yuan, was the founder of the Tang Dynasty of China, and the first emperor of this dynasty from 618 to 626. Under the shortlived Sui dynasty, Li Yuan was the governor in the
1 CONC  area of modern-day Shanxi province, and was based in Taiyuan, Shanxi.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Establishment of the Tang Dynasty
1 CONT In 615, Li Yuan was assigned to garrison Lunghsi. He gained much experience by dealing with the Gokturks of the north and was able to pacify them. Li Yuan was also able to gather support from these successes and, with the disinter
1 CONC gration of the Sui dynasty in July of 617, Li Yuan - urged on by his second son Li Shimin (later Taizong emperor) - rose in rebellion. Using the title of "Great Chancellor" (???), Li Yuan installed a puppet child emperor, Emperor 
1 CONC Gong, but eventually removed him altogether and established the Tang Dynasty in 618.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign as Emperor
1 CONT Li Yuan's reign was concentrated on uniting the empire under the Tang. Aided by his son, Li Shimin, he defeated all the other contenders, including Li Gui, Dou Jiande, Wang Shichong, Xue Ju and Liu Wuzhou. By 628, the Tang Dynasty
1 CONC  had succeeded in uniting all of China. On the homefront, Li Yuan recognized the early successes forged by the Sui dynasty and strived to emulate most of its policies, including the equal distribution of land amongst its people, a
1 CONC nd he also lowered taxes. He abandoned the harsh system of law set about during the Sui dynasty as well as reforming the judicial system. These acts of reform paved the way for the reign of Emperor Taizong, which ultimately pushed
1 CONC  Tang China to the height of its power.
1 CONT
1 CONT Li Yuan abdicated in 626 in favour of his son, Taizong emperor after the Xuan Wu Gate incident (?????) when Taizong had to kill his two immoral brothers who were attempting to usurp the throne. Li Yuan lived on as "Grand Emperor" 
1 CONC (Taishang Huang) until his death in 635.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT (none) Emperor of the Tang Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Taizong 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Gong of Sui Emperor of China 
0 @N6000000002188193597@ NOTE Shirin
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Shirin (? – 628 a.d.) (Persian: ?????) was an Armenian princess and wife of the Sassanid Persian Shahanshah (king of kings), Khosrau II. In the revolution after the death of Khosrau's father Hormizd IV, the General Bahram Chobin t
1 CONC ook power over the Persian empire. Shirin fled with Khosrau to Syria where they lived under the protection of Byzantine emperor Maurice. In 591, Khosrau returned to Persia to take control of the empire and Shirin was made queen. S
1 CONC he used her new influence to support the Christian minority in Iran, but the political situation demanded that she do so discreetly. Initially she belonged to the Church of the East, the so-named Nestorians, but later she joined t
1 CONC he monophysitic western-Syrian church. After conquering Jerusalem in 614, the Persians supposedly captured the cross of Jesus and brought it to their capital Ctesiphon, where Shirin took the cross in her palace.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] In Persian Literature
1 CONT After the fall of Khosrau, Firdausi remembered Shirin in his epic, the Shahnama. Around 1180 the Persian poet Nezami wrote of her alleged love for the master builder Farhad in his epic Chosroes and Shirin. This story grew to be a 
1 CONC myth with Shirin and Farhad being symbols of pure, unrequited love.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the myth Shirin and Khosrau fell in love by seeing each other in portraits, but their first meeting was long delayed. Before Shirin agreed to marry Khusraw, there were obstacles to be overcome, including the vanquishi
1 CONC ng of Farhad, a rival for Shirin’s affections.
1 CONT
1 CONT The long standing myth spread to other ethnic literature, living on even as far as Europe with Goethe’s West-oestlicher Divan. Shirin is also mentioned in Scheherazade's The Book of One Thousand and One Nights on the 390th night w
1 CONC ith the story of Kosrau and Shirin with a fisherman.
1 CONT
1 CONT “ The romance of Khusraw and Shirin is a classic text of Persian literature. It tells the story of the courtship of the pre-Islamic king Khusraw Parviz and the Armenian princess Shirin. The poet Nizami created the romance for a lo
1 CONC cal ruler in north-west Iran in 1184 and from the 15th century manuscripts containing the poem were often illustrated with miniature paintings. [1] ” 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT Khosrau Parviz 
1 CONT Sassanid Empire 
1 CONT Chosroes and Shirin 
1 CONT Nezami 
1 CONT Sarkash 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Wilhelm Baum: Shirin: Christian - Queen - Myth of Love, 2004 (orig. German: Schirin. Christin - Königin - Liebesmythos. Eine spätantike Frauengestalt - Historische Realität und literarische Wirkung, 2003). 
1 CONT ^ The Romance of Khusraw and Shirin Illustrated by Riza’ ‘Abbasi (c. 1565 - 1635) 
1 CONT Gianroberto Scarcia: Scirin. La Regina dei Magi, Ed. Jaca Book, Milano, 2004. 
0 @N6000000001354427278@ NOTE Yazdegerd III (d. 651, Merv, Sasanian Empire), the last king of the Sasanian dynasty (reigned 632-651), the son of Shahryar and a grandson of Khosrow II.
1 CONT A mere child when he was placed on the throne, Yazdegerd never actually exercised power. In his first year the Arab invasion began, and in 636/637 the Battle of al-Qadisiyah on one of the Euphrates canals decided the fate of the e
1 CONC mpire. His capital, Ctesiphon, was occupied by the Arabs, and Yazdegerd fled into Media, where his generals unsuccessfully attempted to organize resistance. After the Battle of Nahavand (642), in which Sasanian forces were badly d
1 CONC efeated, Yazdegerd sought refuge in one district after another, until at last he was slain at Merv. The Parsis--Zoroastrians who immigrated to western India on the advent of Islam--still use the old Persian calendar and continue t
1 CONC o countthe years from Yazdegerd's accession. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, YAZDEGERD]
0 @N6000000002187918539@ NOTE Yazdgerd III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdgerd III (Persian: ?????? ???, "made by God"), last king of Sassanid dynasty, a grandson of Khosrau II (590–628), who had been murdered by his son Kavadh II of Persia in 628, and was raised to the throne in 632 after a series 
1 CONC of internal conflicts.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdgerd III reigned as a youth and never truly exercised authority. In his first year the Arab invasion of Persia began, and in 636 the Battle of al-Qadisiyyah decided the fate of the Persian empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arabs occupied Ctesiphon, and the young King fled into Media. Yazdgerd III fled from one district to another, until at last he was murdered at Merv in 651. The Parsees, who use the old Persian calendar, continue to count the years
1 CONC  from his accession (era of Yazdgerd, beginning June 16, 632).
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdgerd III's daughter Shahr Banu married Husayn ibn Ali, Muhammad's grandson, and gave birth to the fourth Shia Imam, Ali Zayn al Abidin. His son Pirooz fled to China.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT
