0 @N6000000000945071902@ NOTE Keiser av Bysants 959 - 963. 
1 CONT Den østromerske keiser protesterte mot Otto ?den Store?'s keiserverdighet, som i sin tid
1 CONT mot Karl ?den Store?'s, og sa til hans utsending at det rett og slett var en skandale at en
1 CONT saksisk barbar våget å legge seg til en så opphøyet titel. For å fremtvinge en anerkjennelse,
1 CONT rykket Otto da inn i de østromerske besittelsene i Syd-Italia, men samtidig tilbød han forsoning
1 CONT ved å foreslå ekteskap mellom sin og Adelheis unge sønn Otto og en bysantisk prinsesse. Som
1 CONT Ottos utsending dro biskop Liutprand til Konstantinopel for å fremføre frieriet. Men den stolte
1 CONT østromerske keiseren var mektig forarget over at tyskerne hadde rykket inn på hans område
1 CONT og ville slett ikke høre tale om noen dynastisk forbindelse. Liutprand ble behandlet som en
1 CONT spion og fikk ikke engang foretrede for keiseren. Da han langt om lenge likevel ble mottatt ved
1 CONT hoffet, begynte keiseren å spørre ham ut om Ottos stridskrefter. Da biskopen hadde svart på
1 CONT spørsmålene, tordnet keiseren: ?Du lyver! Din herres krigere duger hverken til å ride eller til å
1 CONT kjempe til fots. De store skjoldene de bruker og de tunge panserne og hjelmene, for ikke å
1 CONT snakke om de lange sverdene, gjør begge deler umulig, og? - la han hånlig til - ?drikk og
1 CONT fråtsing hindret dem også, for de har gjort buken til sin gud; motet deres er bare en rus og
1 CONT tapperheten drukkenskap. Forresten har din herre ingen flåte. Jeg alene er mektig til sjøs. Jeg
1 CONT skal angripe ham med mine skip, ødelegge havnebyene hans og alt land langs elvene. Og
1 CONT hvem skulle vel kunne motstå meg til lands, jeg, som har like mange krigere, som det finnes
1 CONT bølger i havet og stjerner på himmelen!?
1 CONT Da Liuprand ville svare ham, ble han hysjet ned og hånet med ordene: ?Dere er slett
1 CONT ikke romere men langobarder!? I stedet for å oppta dette som en fornærmelse, ga biskopen til
1 CONT svar at de østromerske keisere kunne føre sine aner tilbake til brodermorderen Romulus. ?Vi
1 CONT langobarder og andre germanere? - fortsatte han - ?forakter romernavnet i den grad at vi
1 CONT bruker det som skjellsord når vi er fortørnet på noen. For med navnet romer forbinder vi alt som
1 CONT tenkes kan av nedrighet, feighet, griskhet, lystenhet, løgnaktighet, ja over hodet alle laster.?
1 CONT Følgen ble naturligvis at keiseren i sin vrede befalte Liutprand å gå tilbake til sitt losji,
1 CONT hvor han ble behandlet så dårlig at han ble alvorlig syk. Han var mer død enn levende da han
1 CONT omsider ble satt på fri fot og fikk lov til å reise hjem igjen. Men selv da ble han utsatt for
1 CONT forferdelige trusler og fornærmelser.
1 CONT Romanos regjerte svakt. Dog ble Kreta erobret fra maurerne under hans regjeringstid.
1 CONT De ble også overvunnet i Lilleasien og Syria.
0 @N6000000002187757813@ NOTE Romanos II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Romanos II or Romanus II (Greek: ??µa??? ??, Romanos II), (938 – 963) succeeded his father Constantine VII as Byzantine emperor in 959 at the age of twenty-one, and died, poisoned, it was believed, by his wife Theophano, in 963.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Life 
1 CONT 2 Family 
1 CONT 3 References 
1 CONT 4 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT Romanos II was a son of Emperor Constantine VII and Helena Lekapene, the daughter of Emperor Romanos I. Named after his maternal grandfather, Romanos was married, as a child, to Bertha, the illegitimate daughter of Hugh of Arles, 
1 CONC King of Italy. On April 6, 945, after the fall of the Lekapenoi, Constantine VII associated his son Romanos on the throne. With Hugh out of power in Italy and dead by 947, and Bertha herself dead in 949, Romanos secured the promis
1 CONC e from his father that he would be allowed to select his own bride. Romanos' choice fell on an innkeeper's daughter named Anastaso, whom he married in 956 and renamed Theophano.
1 CONT
1 CONT In November 959 Romanos II succeeded his father on the throne, among rumors that he or his wife had sped up the end of Constantine VII by poison. Romanos carried out a virtual purge of his father's courtiers and replaced them with
1 CONC  his own friends and those of his wife. Among the persons removed from court were the Empress Mother, Helena, and her daughters, all of them being relegated to a monastery. Nevertheless, many of Romanos' appointees were able men, 
1 CONC including his chief adviser, the eunuch Joseph Bringas.
1 CONT
1 CONT The pleasure-loving sovereign could also leave military matters in the adept hands of his generals, in particular the brothers Leo and Nikephoros Phokas. In 960 Nikephoros Phokas was sent to recover Crete from the Muslims. After a
1 CONC  difficult campaign and the 9-month siege of Candia, Nikephoros successfully re-established Byzantine control over the entire island in 961. Following a triumph celebrated at Constantinople, Nikephoros was sent to the eastern fron
1 CONC tier, where he conquered Cilicia and even Aleppo in 962. In the meantime Leo Phokas and Marianos Argyros had countered Magyar incursions into the Byzantine Balkans.
1 CONT
1 CONT After a lengthy hunting expedition Romanos II took ill and died on March 15, 963. Rumor attributed his death to poison administered by his wife Theophano. Romanos II's reliance on his wife and on bureaucrats like Joseph Bringas ha
1 CONC d resulted in a relatively capable administration, but built up resentment among the nobility, which was associated with the military.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT Romanos II probably never consummated his first marriage to Bertha of Italy. By his second wife Theophano, he had at least three children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Basil II 
1 CONT Constantine VIII 
1 CONT Anna, who married Vladimir I of Kiev. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT The Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium, Oxford University Press, 1991. 
1 CONT George Ostrogorsky, History of the Byzantine State, 1969. 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
0 @N6000000009304914995@ NOTE #Générale##Générale#Profession : Empereur de Byzance de 948 à 963.
0 @N6000000002187891853@ NOTE Stephen Ladislaus I of Serbia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Stephen Ladislaus I (Stefan Vladislav I, ?????? ????????? I) (died after 1264) was Serb king from 1233 up to 1242, son of Stefan Prvovencani and Eudocia Angelina, and son-in-law of Bulgarian tzar Ivan Asen II. He overthrew his old
1 CONC er brother, king Radoslav and succeeded with help from his father-in-law. He transferred body of Saint Sava from Trnovo to Mileševa. After death of his father-in-law, during Tatar invasion in Middle Europe he was overthrown by his
1 CONC  younger brother Uroš, who gave him to rule Zeta.
1 CONT
1 CONT His marriage with Beloslava, daughter of Ivan Asen II issued two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Desa 
1 CONT anonymous daughter, who married a Balkan nobleman. 
1 CONT See also: List of Serbian monarchs - History of Serbia
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stefan Radoslav
1 CONT  House of Nemanjic
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stefan Uroš I
1 CONT
0 @N6000000002187891844@ NOTE Stefan Radoslav
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan Radoslav (?????? ????????) (around 1192-1234) was king of Serbia from 1228 to 1233.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the eldest son of Stefan Prvovencani and Eudocia Angelina. His maternal grandparents were Alexius III Angelus and Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamaterina.
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded his father in 1228. As ruler he was blind and relied on his father-in-law Theodore I Ducas, despot of Epirus (1215-1230). When his father-in-law was beaten by the Bulgarians, Radoslav could no longer remain in power: 
1 CONC the nobility dethroned him and placed his younger brother Vladislav on the throne . Rejecting his family, Radoslav ended his life as a monk.
1 CONT
1 CONT His marriage with Anna, daughter of Theodore I Ducas, was childless.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: List of Serbian monarchs - History of Serbia
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stefan Prvovencani
1 CONT  House of Nemanjic
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stefan Vladislav I
1 CONT
