0 @N6000000001783566381@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Vladimir was a pagan at the beginning of his reign, which was at first devoted to consolidating his territories into a unified Russian state. By the early 10th century, however, Kievan Rus had established close commercial and cult
1 CONC ural ties with the Byzantine Empire, an Orthodox Christian state.  He converted in 988 to Orthodox Christianity and made Orthodoxy the official religion of Kievan Rus.  Vladimir's choice of Orthodox Christianity, rather than the L
1 CONC atin church (Roman Catholicism) or Islam, had an important influence on the future of Russia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Vladimir's choice between the Christian and Islamic faiths was said to have been heavily influenced by the fact that he enjoyed the consumption of alcoholic beverages.  The Christian faith allowed for this and the Islamic faith di
1 CONC d not.  Hence, Validimir chose Christianity.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT St. Vladimir married in  to  Rogneda von Polotzk, a Nun and a daughter of Rognald of Polotzk, in 980.  Rogneda died in 1002.  St. Vladimir and Rogneda the Nun had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Yaroslav I the Wise, Grand Prince of Kiev
1 CONT Vissavald of Kiev
1 CONT Iasaslav, Prince of Polotzk
1 CONT Mtsislav, Grand Prince Tschernigow
1 CONT Premislava
1 CONT Sviataslav
1 CONT Sudislav, Prince of Pskow
1 CONT Wizeslau, Prince of Novgorod
1 CONT
1 CONT St. Vladimir married after 1011 to Malfreda of Bohemia, a daughter of Kuno, Count of Ohningen, by Richilde who was a daughter of Otto I the Great, King of Germany. They had a daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT Dobroniega who married in 1038 to King Casimir I, King of Poland.
