0 @N6000000000437678441@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> I, of Hungary
0 @N6000000002187805494@ NOTE Solomon of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Solomon (Hungarian: I. Salamon) (1053 – 1087), king of Hungary (1057-1081).
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Childhood 
1 CONT 2 King of Hungary 
1 CONT 3 Struggle for the throne 
1 CONT 4 Conspiracy and failure 
1 CONT 5 Marriages 
1 CONT 6 Sources 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Childhood
1 CONT
1 CONT Solomon was the first son of king Andrew I of Hungary and Anastasia, a daughter of Grand Duke Yaroslav I the Wise of Kiev. Before his birth, his uncle, Bela was the designated heir to the crown, who was governing the Tercia pars r
1 CONC egni (i.e. one third of the kingdom). However, Solomon's birth changed the situation radically, because king Andrew decided to ensure his son's inheritance and he had Solomon crowned in 1057.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the child's coronation, duke Bela left the royal court. In September 1058, when king Andrew concluded peace with king Henry IV of Germany, the child Solomon was engaded to the German ruler's sister, Judith who was six years 
1 CONC older then her fiancé. In 1059, according to the chronicles, Andras called back Bela to his court, and placed before him a crown and a sword, representing royal and ducal power, respectively, and asked Bela to take his choice. Kno
1 CONC wing that choosing the crown would mean his life, Bela instead selected the sword. Shortly afterwards, Bela fled to Poland and returned followed by Polish troops placed at his disposal by king Boleslaw II of Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT When king Andrew was informed on his brother's return, he sent his wife and children to Melk in Austria. Afterwards, the king lost two battles against his brother, and died before 6 December 1060, when Bela I was crowned. Solomon 
1 CONC lived in the imperial court three years, during which his followers tried to acquire assistance against his uncle, but it was only in August 1063 when the Imperial Diet decided in Mainz to send troops to Hungary. King Bela I died 
1 CONC in an accident before the imperial army entered to the kingdom, and his three sons (Géza, Ladislaus and Lampert) left for Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] King of Hungary
1 CONT After his cousins' escape, Solomon returned to Hungary where he was crowned again and he married the German king's sister in September 1063 in Székesfehérvár. When the German army left Hungary, his three cousins came back followed
1 CONC  by Polish troops. The parties, however, wanted to avoid the civil war; therefore they accepted the mediation services of the bishops, and they made an agreement on 20 January 1064 in Gyor. Under the agreement Géza, Ladislaus and 
1 CONC Lampert accepted Solomon's rule, and they received their father's former duchy, i.e. the one third of Hungary. Following the conclusion of the peace, the king and his cousins celebrated Easter together in Pécs. However, when a fir
1 CONC e broke out, the two parties accused the other's followers of incerdiarism. The bishops had to intervene again in order to appease the king and the dukes.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the next years Solomon and his cousins collaborated successfully. In 1067 they lead an army together to provide assistance to the dukes' brother-in-law, king Dmitar Zvonimir of Croatia against Venice. In 1068, when the Pecheneg
1 CONC s had overrun the territories of Transylvania, Solomon and the dukes went together against them and they won a victory at Chirales (Kerlés). In 1071 Solomon and the dukes lead a campaign against the Byzantine Empire and laid siege
1 CONC  to the fortress of Belgrade (Nándorfehérvár). The siege lasted two months, and the Greek commander surrendered the fort to the dukes not to the king. Moreover, duke Géza denied to hand over the king's share of the booty and set t
1 CONC he Greek captives free without the king's permission. Having the Byzantine troops reoccupied Belgrade in the next year, Solomon and duke Géza lead their armies together against the Greeks, but Géza left his two brothers behind, be
1 CONC cause he was worrying about that the king's partisans would try to occupy their duchy during their absence. The campaign was a total failure, because the king and the duke were not able to cooperate during the siege any more.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Struggle for the throne
1 CONT During 1073 both the king and his cousing were preparing for the coming struggle; Solomon sent his envoys to his brother-in-law, king Henry IV of Germany, while his cousins were seeking the help of their Polish and Czech relatives
1 CONC . In the beginning of 1074, before the Polish and Czech troops arrived, Solomon lead his armies against the dukes' territory and defeated duke Géza on 26 February at Kemej. However, after the arrival of the reinforcement from Pola
1 CONC nd and Bohemia, the dukes' armies started a counter-attack and they won a decisive victory over Solomon's troops on 14 March in the Battle of Mogyoród.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his defeat, Solomon run to the Western borders of Hungary and sought help from the German king, whose supremacy he accepted, while Géza was declared king, supported by the Hungarian tradition which preferred seniority. In Au
1 CONC gust 1074, the imperial troops invaded the Northern part of the kingdom and advanced till Vác, but the German king was obliged to return to his domain because of the Saxons' uprising. King Solomon could only maintain his rule over
1 CONC  the counties (megye) of Moson and Bratislava (Pozsony).
1 CONT
1 CONT He could beat off the troops Géza sent to siege Bratislava (Pozsony) and Géza, who became more and more ill, was thinking of his abdication and the acceptance of Solomon's rule. But after Géza's death on 25 April 1077, his followe
1 CONC rs proclaimed his brother, Ladislaus king. In 1079 king Ladislaus I managed to occupy the fortress of Moson from Solomon's adherents, and he also repealled the German king's troops. Solomon also lost the support of the clergy when
1 CONC  pope Gregory VII, because of his alliance with the German king, acknowledged the legitimacy of Ladislaus' rule.
1 CONT
1 CONT Salomon had to realize that he did not have any chance to win over Ladislaus and he abdicated in 1081. In exchange for the acceptance of Ladislaus' rule, Solomon received extensive landholdings.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Conspiracy and failure
1 CONT Solomon, however, did not give up his ambitions and tried to plot against the king, but he was betrayed and Ladislaus had him imprisoned. On 15 August 1083, on the occasion of the canonization of Stephen I, the first king of Hunga
1 CONC ry, Solomon was released. His release was attributed to the newly canonized Saint Stephen's intercession whose coffin could only be oppened when Solomon retrieved his freedom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Solomon left for Regensburg, where he tried to obtain military assistance from the German king, but his attempts failed. Afterwards, following a long wandering, Solomon made an alliance with Kuteshk, the leader of a Pecheneg (bese
1 CONC nyo) tribe settled down in the territory of the future principality of Moldavia whose daughter he married, in spite of the fact that his first wife was still alive (although she had left Solomon years before). Solomon promised to 
1 CONC hand over parts of the kingdom of Hungary in exchange for his new father-in-laws' military assistance. In 1085, Solomon lead the Pecheneg troops against Hungary, but king Ladislaus I defeated them.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1087 Solamon took part in the Pechenegs' campaign against the Byzantine Empire and he was killed in a battle near Hadrianopolis. Later chronicles claimed that Solomon escaped from the battlefield and he went to Pula where he li
1 CONC ved as a monk.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages
1 CONT #1. 1063: Judith of Swabia (c. 1054 – c. 1092/1096), daughter of Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor and his second wife, Agnes de Poitou
1 CONT
1 CONT #2. 1084/1085: (bigamiously) Unnamed daughter of Kuteshk, leader of a Pecheneg tribe
1 CONT
1 CONT (Salomon did not father any children.)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Kristó Gyula - Makk Ferenc: Az Árpád-ház uralkodói (IPC Könyvek, 1996) 
1 CONT Korai Magyar Történeti Lexikon (9-14. század), foszerkeszto: Kristó Gyula, szerkesztok: Engel Pál és Makk Ferenc (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1994) 
1 CONT Magyarország Történeti Kronológiája I. – A kezdetektol 1526-ig, foszerkeszto: Benda Kálmán (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1981) 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Béla I King of Hungary
1 CONT 1063-1074 Succeeded by
1 CONT Géza I 
