1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh and most of his army was rescued and escorted to Constantinople, where they arrived in November of 1096. Prior to his arrival, Hugh sent an arrogant, insulting letter to Eastern Roman Emperor Alexius I Comnenus, according to 
1 CONC the Emperor's biography by his daughter (the Alexiad), demanding that Alexius meet with him: "Know, O King, that I am King of Kings, and superior to all, who are under the sky. You are now permitted to greet me, on my arrival, and
1 CONC  to receive me with magnificence, as befits my nobility." Alexius was already wary of the armies about to arrive, after the unruly mob led by Peter the Hermit had passed through earlier in the year. Alexius kept Hugh in custody in
1 CONC  a monastery until Hugh swore an oath of vassalage to him.
1 CONT After the Crusaders had successfully made their way across Seljuk territory and, in 1098, captured Antioch, Hugh was sent back to Constantinople to appeal for reinforcements from Alexius. Alexius was uninterested, however, and Hug
1 CONC h, instead of returning to Antioch to help plan the siege of Jerusalem, went back to France. There he was scorned for not having fulfilled his vow as a Crusader to complete a pilgrimage to Jerusalem, and Pope Paschal II threatened
1 CONC  to excommunicate him. He joined the minor Crusade of 1101, but was wounded in battle with the Turks at Heraclea in June, and died of his wounds in October in Tarsus.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He married Adele of Vermandois, the daughter of Herbert IV of Vermandois and Adele of Valois.They had eight children:
1 CONT 1. Count Raoul I of Vermandois
1 CONT 2. Henry, senior of Chaumont-en-Vexin, (d. 1130).
1 CONT 3. Simon, Bishop of Noyon
1 CONT 4. Elizabeth de Vermandois, married
1 CONT 1. Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Leicester;
1 CONT 2. William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey
1 CONT 5. Matilde de Vermandois, married Raoul I of Beaugency
1 CONT 6. Constance de Vermandois, married Godefroy de la Ferte-Gaucher
1 CONT 7. Agnes de Vermandois, married Bonifacio, Marchese del Vasto. Mother of Adelaide del Vasto.
1 CONT 8. Beatrix de Vermandois, married Hugh III of Gournay-en-Bray
1 CONT 9. Emma de Vermandois, married Ralph de Gael, 1st Earl of Norfolk
1 CONT
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois1 (M)
1 CONT b. 1057, d. 18 October 1102, #103173
1 CONT Pedigree
1 CONT Last Edited=7 Feb 2005
1 CONT
1 CONT      Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois was born in 1057. He was the son of Henri I, Roi de France and Princess Anne of Kiev.2,3 He married Aelis de Vermandois, Comtesse de Vermandois, daughter of Heribert V, Comte de
1 CONC  Vermandois et de Valois and Adele de Crépi, circa 1080.2 He died on 18 October 1102 in Tarsus. 
1 CONT      Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois was a member of the House of Capet. Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois also went by the nick-name of Hugh 'le Grand'.3 He gained the title of Comte de Vermandois. H
1 CONC e gained the title of Comte de Valois.
1 CONT
1 CONT Child of Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois and Aelis de Vermandois, Comtesse de Vermandois
1 CONT Elizabeth de Vermandois+ d. 17 Feb 11311
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT 1. [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extin
1 CONC ct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume VII, page 526. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
1 CONT 2. [S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 64. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession.
1 CONT 3. [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume XII/2, page 829.
