0 @N6000000000437070243@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> I, Of France "The Fair" ruled 1060- 1108 First six years of his reign were spent under the regency of his motherand uncle king of France (1060-1108), son and successor of Henry I. He enla
1 CONC rged, by arms and by diplomacy, his small royal domain. In order to prevent the union of England and Normandy under a single ruler, he consistently supported Robert II of Normandy (Robert Curthose). In spite of his efforts, royal 
1 CONC power remained weak. Philip's practice of simony and his consequent opposition to the reforms of Pope Gregory VII brought him into conflict with the Holy See. Among the issues were simony and control of marriage policy, an issue f
1 CONC ueled by Philip's private life. Philip repudiated his first wife, Bertha, daughter of the countof Holland, and married, over the opposition of the Roman Catholic Church, Bertrada of Montfort, wife of Count Fulk of Anjou, while bot
1 CONC h Bertha and Fulk werestill living. Philip, excommunicated by popes Urban II and Paschal II, remained defiant until 1104. In his last years his son, Louis VI, ruled for him.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Any additions, comments, and especially corrections are welcome.  Contact Scottat: [ scott_williams@hotmail.com ] or [ scott_williams@libero.it ]@S157@
0 @N6000000003051295819@ NOTE [From Wikipedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_I_of_France]
1 CONT Philip I (French: Philippe Ier) (May 23, 1052 ? July 29, 1108) was King of France from 1060 to 1108.
1 CONT
1 CONT A member of the Capetian Dynasty, Philip I was born on May 23, 1052, the son of Henry I (1031?60) and Princess Anne of Kiev (1024?75). His name (derived from the Greek Philippos, "lover of horses") was rather exotic for Western Eu
1 CONC rope at the time; it was bestowed upon him by his mother from Kiev. Although he was crowned King at the age of seven, until age fourteen in 1066 his mother acted as Regent, the first Queen ever to do so. Her co-regent was Baldwin 
1 CONC V of Flanders (1036?67).
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip I's first marriage was in 1072 to Bertha, daughter of Floris I, Count of Holland (1055 ? July 30, 1094). Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance (1078?c. 1125) married to Hugh of Troyes (Hugh I, count of Champagne) before 1097 and then, after her divorce, to Bohemund I of Antioch in 1106.
1 CONT King Louis VI of France (December 1, 1081 ? August 1, 1137)
1 CONT Although the marriage produced the necessary heir, Philip I fell in love with Bertrade de Montfort, the wife of Count Fulk IV of Anjou. He repudiated Bertha and married Bertrade on May 15, 1092. In 1094, he was excommunicated by H
1 CONC ugh, bishop of Lyon for the first time; after long silence, Pope Urban II (1088?99) repeated the excommunication at the council of Clermont in November 1095. Several times the ban was lifted as Philip I promised to part with Bertr
1 CONC ade, but he always returned to her, and after 1104, the ban was not repeated. In France, the King was opposed by Bishop Ivo of Chartres, a famous canonist.
1 CONT
1 CONT The children of Philip I and Bertrade were:
1 CONT
1 CONT - Philippe, Comte de Mantes (living 1123)
1 CONT - Fleury, seigneur de Nagis (living 1118)
1 CONT - Cécile, married Tancred, Prince of Galilee; married secondly Pons of Tripoli
1 CONT Philip I appointed Alberic, the first Constable of France in 1060. A great part of Philip I's reign, like his father's, was spent putting down revolts by his power-hungry vassals. In 1077, he made peace with William I of England (
1 CONC Duke of Normandy) who gave up attempting the conquest of Brittany. In 1082, Philip I expanded his empire with the annexation of Vexin, then in 1100, he took control of Bourges.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was during Philip I's time that the First Crusade was launched in 1095 which he at first did not personally support because of his conflict with Urban II (1088?99). The Pope would not have allowed him to participate anyway, as 
1 CONC he had reaffirmed Philip I's excommunication at the Council of Clermont before he called for the Crusade. Philip I's brother Hugh of Vermandois, however, was a major participant.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Philip I died in the castle at Melun on July 29, 1108 and was buried at the monastery of Saint-Benoît-sur-Loire ? and not in St Denis with almost all Capetian Kings. According to Abbot Suger:
1 CONT
1 CONT "...King Philippe daily grew feebler. For after he had abducted the Countess of Anjou, he could achieve nothing worthy of the royal dignity; consumed by desire for the lady he had seized, he gave himself up entirely to the satisfa
1 CONC ction of his passion. So he lost interest in the affairs of state and, relaxing too much, took no care for his body, well-made and handsome though it was. The only thing that maintained the strength of the state was the fear and l
1 CONC ove felt for his son and successor. When he was almost sixty, he ceased to be king, breathing his last breath at the castle of Melun-sur-Seine, in the presence of the [future king] Louis... They carried the body in a great process
1 CONC ion to the noble monastery of St-Benoît-sur-Loire, where King Philippe wished to be buried; there are those who say they heard from his own mouth that he deliberately chose not to be buried among his royal ancestors in the church 
1 CONC of St. Denis because he had not treated that church as well as they had, and because among so many noble kings his own tomb would not have counted for much."
1 CONT He was succeeded by his son, Louis VI whose succession was, however, not uncontested.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Genealogiae Comitum Flandriae
