0 @N6000000001783211517@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip I, called the Amorous[1] or the Fat, was King of France from 1060 to his death. His reign, like that of most of the early Direct Capetians, was extraordinarily long for the time. The monarchy began a modest recovery from th
1 CONC e low it reached in the reign of his father and he added to the royal demesne the Vexin and Bourges.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip was the son of Henry I and Anne of Kiev. His name was of Greek origin, being derived from Philippos, meaning "lover of horses". It was rather exotic for Western Europe at the time and was bestowed upon him by his Eastern Eu
1 CONC ropean mother. Although he was crowned king at the age of seven, until age fourteen (1066) his mother acted as regent, the first queen of France ever to do so. Her co-regent was Baldwin V of Flanders.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip first married Bertha, daughter of Floris I, Count of Holland, in 1072. Although the marriage produced the necessary heir, Philip fell in love with Bertrade de Montfort, the wife of Count Fulk IV of Anjou. He repudiated Bert
1 CONC ha (claiming she was too fat) and married Bertrade on 15 May 1092. In 1094, he was excommunicated by Hugh, Archbishop of Lyon, for the first time; after a long silence, Pope Urban II repeated the excommunication at the Council of 
1 CONC Clermont in November 1095. Several times the ban was lifted as Philip promised to part with Bertrade, but he always returned to her, and after 1104, the ban was not repeated. In France, the king was opposed by Bishop Ivo of Chartr
1 CONC es, a famous jurist.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip's children with Bertha were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance, married Hugh I of Champagne before 1097 and then, after her divorce, to Bohemund I of Antioch in 1106
1 CONT Louis (December 1, 1081 - August 1, 1137)
1 CONT Henry (b.1083) (died young)
1 CONT Eudes (1087-1096)
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip's children with Bertrade were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippe, Comte de Mantes (living 1123)
1 CONT Fleury, seigneur de Nagis (living 1118)
1 CONT Cecile of France, married Tancred, Prince of Galilee; married secondly Pons of Tripoli
1 CONT
1 CONT It was at the aforementioned Council of Clermont that the First Crusade was launched. Philip at first did not personally support it because of his conflict with Urban II. The pope would not have allowed him to participate anyway, 
1 CONC as he had reaffirmed Philip's excommunication at the said council. Philip's brother Hugh of Vermandois, however, was a major participant.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip died in the castle of Melun and was buried per request at the monastery of Saint-Benoît-sur-Loire - and not in St Denis among his forefathers. He was succeeded by his son, Louis VI, whose succession was, however, not uncont
1 CONC ested. According to Abbot Suger:
1 CONT
1 CONT “ … King Philip daily grew feebler. For after he had abducted the Countess of Anjou, he could achieve nothing worthy of the royal dignity; consumed by desire for the lady he had seized, he gave himself up entirely to the satisfact
1 CONC ion of his passion. So he lost interest in the affairs of state and, relaxing too much, took no care for his body, well-made and handsome though it was. The only thing that maintained the strength of the state was the fear and lov
1 CONC e felt for his son and successor. When he was almost sixty, he ceased to be king, breathing his last breath at the castle of Melun-sur-Seine, in the presence of the [future king] Louis... They carried the body in a great processio
1 CONC n to the noble monastery of St-Benoît-sur-Loire, where King Philip wished to be buried; there are those who say they heard from his own mouth that he deliberately chose not to be buried among his royal ancestors in the church of S
1 CONC t. Denis because he had not treated that church as well as they had, and because among so many noble kings his own tomb would not have counted for much."
1 CONT wikipedia
0 @N6000000002187734810@ NOTE Philip I of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip I (French: Philippe Ier) (May 23, 1052 – July 29, 1108) was King of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT A member of the Capetian Dynasty, Philippe was born on May 23, 1052, the son of Henri I (1008-1060) and Princess Anne of Kiev (1024 - 1075). His name (derived from the Greek Philippos, "lover of horses") was rather exotic for West
1 CONC ern Europe at the time; it was bestowed upon him by his Russian mother. Although he was crowned king at the age of seven, until 1066 his mother acted as Regent, the first queen ever to do so.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippe's first marriage was in 1072 to Bertha, daughter of Floris I, Count of Holland (1055-July 30, 1094). Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance (1078 - c. 1125) married to Hugh of Troyes (Hugh I, count of Champagne) before 1097 and then, after her divorce, to Bohemund I of Antioch in 1106. 
1 CONT King Louis VI of France (December 1, 1081 - August 1, 1137) 
1 CONT Although the marriage produced the necessary heir, Philippe fell in love with Bertrade de Montfort, the wife of Count Fulk IV of Anjou. He repudiated Bertha and married Bertrade on May 15, 1092. In 1094, he was excommunicated by H
1 CONC ugh, bishop of Lyon for the first time; after long silence, Pope Urban II repeated the excommunication at the council of Clermont in November 1095. Several times the ban was lifted as Philippe promised to part with Bertrade, but h
1 CONC e always returned to her, and after 1104, the ban was not repeated. In France, the king was opposed by Bishop Ivo of Chartres, a famous canonist.
1 CONT
1 CONT The children of Philippe and Bertrade were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippe, Comte de Mantes (living 1123) 
1 CONT Fleury, seigneur de Nagis (living 1118) 
1 CONT Cécile, married Tancred, Prince of Galilee; married secondly Pons of Tripoli 
1 CONT Philippe appointed Alberic, the first Constable of France in 1060. A great part of Philippe's reign, like his father's, was spent putting down revolts by his power-hungry vassals. In 1077, he made peace with William I of England (
1 CONC Duke of Normandy) who gave up attempting the conquest of Brittany. In 1082, Philippe expanded his empire with the annexation of Vexin, then in 1100, he took control of Bourges.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was during Philippe's time that the First Crusade was launched in 1095 which he at first did not personally support because of his conflict with Urban II. Urban would not have allowed him to participate anyway, as he had reaffi
1 CONC rmed Philippe's excommunication at the Council of Clermont before he called for the Crusade. Philippe's brother Hugh of Vermandois, however, was a major participant.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Philippe I died in the castle at Melun on July 29, 1108 and was buried at the monastery of Saint-Benoît-sur-Loire -- and not in St Denis with almost all Capetian kings. According to Abbot Suger:
1 CONT
1 CONT "...King Philippe daily grew feebler. For after he had abducted the Countess of Anjou, he could achieve nothing worthy of the royal dignity; consumed by desire for the lady he had seized, he gave himself up entirely to the satisfa
1 CONC ction of his passion. So he lost interest in the affairs of state and, relaxing too much, took no care for his body, well-made and handsome though it was. The only thing that maintained the strength of the state was the fear and l
1 CONC ove felt for his son and successor. When he was almost sixty, he ceased to be king, breathing his last breath at the castle of Melun-sur-Seine, in the presence of the [future king] Louis... They carried the body in a great process
1 CONC ion to the noble monastery of St-Benoît-sur-Loire, where King Philippe wished to be buried; there are those who say they heard from his own mouth that he deliberately chose not to be buried among his royal ancestors in the church 
1 CONC of St. Denis because he had not treated that church as well as they had, and because among so many noble kings his own tomb would not have counted for much." 
1 CONT He was succeeded by his son, Louis VI whose succession was, however, not uncontested.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Genealogiae Comitum Flandriae 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry I King of France
1 CONT 1060–1108 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Louis VI 
