0 @N6000000005598936391@ NOTE Philip I (French: Philippe Ier) (May 23, 1052 - July 29, 1108) was Kin g of France from 1060 to 1108.  A member of the Capetian Dynasty, Phil ip I was born on May 23, 1052, the son of Henry I (1031-60) and Prince ss Anne of Kiev (
1 CONC 1024-75). His name (derived from the Greek Philippos , "lover of horses") was rather exotic for Western Europe at the time ; it was bestowed upon him by his mother from Kiev. Although he was cr owned King at the age of seven, unti
1 CONC l age fourteen in 1066 his mother acted as Regent, the first Queen ever to do so. Her co-regent was Bald win V of Flanders (1036-67).  As his father and grandfather, Philippe support the 'pax dei' or 'Turce / Peace of God', but he
1 CONC  opposed refor m for the church.  His policy of insisting on making money out of inve stiture made him unpopular with the clergy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippe enlarged, by arms and by diplomacy, his small royal domain. Philippe has been judged more severly by his contemporaries and recen t historians than other members of the Capatian dynasty.  In his time , he was styled as 't
1 CONC he fat monarch reviled for his gormandising, his sensuality and his greed' as well as being also 'a practical man and a realist'.  He is also described as a rather weak King.  Pope Gregor y VII denounced him as "a tyrant possessed
1 CONC  by the devil, ... a perjure r and a robber."  He has also been castigated by history for not preve nting the Norman conquest of William the Conqueror.  Philip did recogn ize how dangerous William the Conqueror was to the peace of
1 CONC  the contin ent, but he was too young at the time to prevent, or stop William fro m his intentions regarding England.  He was able to obtain the fortres s called the Tower of Montlheny by arranging a marriage between his so n Phil
1 CONC ip (by Bertrada de Montfort.  He showed no favor to the Gregoria n movement for church reform.  He did have frequent brushs with the ch urch, over the then burning issue of investiture.  He did not involve himself in the first cru
1 CONC sade, but did not oppose it.  During Philippe' s reign, in addition to the royal bishoprics held by his father, he ac quired the archbishopric of tours, the Sees of Terouanne & Amiens in t he province of Rheims and Chalon-sur-Saon
1 CONC e in the province of Lyon.
1 CONT
1 CONT In order to prevent the union of England and Normandie under a single ruler, he consistently supported Robert II of Normandie (Robert Curtho se).  He developed the technique of encouraging his vassels to fight a mong themselves.  
1 CONC In spite of his efforts, royal power remained weak .  In about 1079, Philip encouraged the grown son of William the Conqu eror, Robert Curthose to rebel against his father, then supported him when he did.  Philip's practice of sim
1 CONC ony and his consequent oppositio n to the reforms of Pope Gregory VII brought him into conflict with th e Holy See. Among the issues were simony and control of marriage polic y, an issue fueled by Philip's private life. Philip rep
1 CONC udiated his fir st wife, Bertha, daughter of the count of Holland, and married, over t he opposition of the Roman Catholic Church, Bertrada of Montfort, wif e of Count Fulk of Anjou, while both Bertha and Fulk were still living . 
1 CONC Philip, excommunicated by popes Urban II and Paschal II, remained de fiant until 1104.  Philip was of a tranquil nature and hated war.  Des pite this, he had to fight a number of battles against William I and W illiam II of Englan
1 CONC d.In his last years his son, Louis VI, ruled for hi m.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip I, King of France from 1060 to 1108, was born in 1052/3, and di ed in July 1108 in Meulan, buried at Abbaye St-Benoit-sur-Loire. Henr y I, father of Philip I., died in 1060, having crowned his son, Philip , although he was 
1 CONC only a child. Philip was only eight years old at th e time of his father's death. One of the young king's uncles, Baldwin , Count of Flanders, became the regent. Anne of Kiev, refused to be re gent. Later she was abducted by Raoul
1 CONC  of Crepy, lived with him as hisw ife and married him when his wife died. Widowed a second time, she los t her title as Queen and was henceforth referred to as the Queen Mothe r. Of Baldwin's regency little is known, although it a
1 CONC ppears to havebe en fairly lacking in incident, but Burgundy, over which Robert ruled, took advantage of the situation to assert its independence from theFre nch crown; this was to occur frequently in the course of the various B u
1 CONC rgundian dynasties. He married (1) Bertha of Holland (Frise), born ab out 1055, died in 1093, daughter of Florent (Florenz) I, Count ofHolla nd. They were divorced in 1091.
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1070, Philippe had acquired the Norman Vexin as part of the French realm.  Also in about 1070, when Baldwin VI of Flanders died, Robert o f France (Robert Frisan) threatened to claim the patrimony.  Baldwins wife Richilda threa
1 CONC tened to throw Robert into prison and he appealed t o Philippe for help.  But with a single bribe Richilda convinced Phili ppe to not intervene on Robert's behalf.  On 22 February 1071, Philipp e took up arms against Robert of Fra
1 CONC nce (Robert Frisian) and defeated him at the battle of Cassels.  Philip I's first marriage was in 1072 t o Bertha, daughter of Floris I, Count of Holland (1055 - July 30, 1094 ). Their children were: Constance (1078 - ca. 1125) ma
1 CONC rried to Hugh o f Troyes (Hugh I, count of Champagne) before 1097 and then, after her divorce, to Bohemund I of Antioch in 1106; King Louis VI of France (De cember 1, 1081 - August 1, 1137). Although the marriage produced the n ec
1 CONC essary heir, Philip I fell in love with Bertrade de Montfort, the wi fe of Count Fulk IV of Anjou.He repudiated Bertha and married Bertrad e on May 15, 1092. In 1094, he was excommunicated by Hugh, bishop of L yon for the first ti
1 CONC me; after long silence, Pope Urban II (1088 - 1099 ) repeated the excommunication at the council of Clermont in November 1095. Several times the banwas lifted as Philip I promised to part wit h Bertrade, but he always returned to 
1 CONC her, and after 1104, the ban wa s not repeated. In France, the King was opposed by Bishop Ivo of Chart res, a famous canonist.
1 CONT
1 CONT The children of Philip I and Bertrade were: Philippe, Comte de Mantes( living 1123); Fleury, seigneur de Nagis (living 1118); Cécile, marrie d Tancred, Prince of Galilee; married secondly Pons of Tripoli, Phili p I appointed Alber
1 CONC ic, the first Constable of France in 1060. A greatp art of Philip I's reign, like his father's, was spent putting down rev olts by his power-hungry vassals. In 1077, he made peace with William I of England (Duke of Normandy) who g
1 CONC ave up attempting the conquestof Brittany. In 1082, Philip I expanded his empire with the annexation o f Vexin, then in 1100, he took control of Bourges.  It was during Phil ip I's time that the First Crusade was launched in 1095 
1 CONC which he at fi rst did not personally support because of his conflict with UrbanII (1 088 - 1099). The Pope would not have allowed him to participate anyway , as he had reaffirmed Philip I's excommunication at the Council of Cl er
1 CONC mont before he called for the Crusade.  The first crusade was all t o the good for Philip as he was able to rid himself of many of his mor e turbulent subjects.  Philip I's brother Hughof Vermandois, however, was a major participa
1 CONC nt.  King Philip I died in the castle at Melun o n July 29, 1108 and was buried at the monastery of Saint-Benoît-sur-Lo ire - and not in St Denis with almost all Capetian Kings. According t o Abbot Suger:
1 CONT
1 CONT "...King Philippe daily grew feebler. For after he had abducted the Co untess of Anjou, he could achieve nothing worthy of the royal dignity ; consumed by desire for the lady he had seized, he gave himself up en tirely to the sati
1 CONC sfaction of his passion. So he lost interest in thea ffairs of state and, relaxing too much, took no care for his body, wel l-made and handsome though it was. The only thing that maintained the strength of the state was the fear a
1 CONC nd love felt for his son and succe ssor. When he was almost sixty, he ceased to be king, breathing hislas t breath at the castle of Melun-sur-Seine, in the presence of the [fut ure king] Louis... They carried the body in a great p
1 CONC rocession to the noble monastery of St-Benoît-sur-Loire, where King Philippe wishedto b e buried; there are those who say they heard from his own mouth that h e deliberately chose not to be buried among his royal ancestors in th e
1 CONC  church of St. Denis because he had not treated that church as well a s they had, and because among so many noble kings his own tomb would n ot have counted for much."  He was succeeded by his son, Louis VIwhos e succession was, h
1 CONC owever, not uncontested.
