0 @N6000000002187757420@ NOTE Coloman of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Coloman (Hungarian: Könyves Kálmán, Slovak and Croatian: Koloman) (1070 – February 3, 1116) was King of Hungary from 1095 to 1116.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Géza I of Hungary and Zsófia (Sophia) von Looz.
1 CONT
1 CONT László I wanted Kálmán's brother Álmos to succeed him as King of Hungary. (According to the chronicles, Kálmán may have had a physical deformity, which would have made him unfit to be king per medieval beliefs about such things, a
1 CONC lthough this deformity may be a later falsification of this appearance as in the case of England's Richard III, as the chronicles reflected the image of Coloman created by his successors, who were in fact descendants of his blinde
1 CONC d younger brother Álmos). Not wanting to abandon his eldest nephew completely, he wished instead to make him a bishop. Kálmán was accordingly sent abroad to acquire his subsequently famous learning, which earned him the appellatio
1 CONC n "Könyves", literally "bookish" (usually translated "the Booklover"). (At the time, this was not a compliment. History has been kind to Kálmán, however, so modern histories are more apt to translate the nickname as "The Wise" or 
1 CONC similar.)
1 CONT
1 CONT The exact circumstances of how Kálmán acquired the throne after László's death are unknown; among other difficulties, he may have had to get papal dispensation, because ordained clergy could not become king. (The sources are uncle
1 CONC ar on whether Kálmán was actually ordained. His later laws show that he had no problem with married clergy, so his eventual marriages are no evidence in this matter.)
1 CONT
1 CONT László died before he could fulfill his promise of leading a Crusade. Kálmán did not find it necessary to fulfill the promise of his predecessor, but did concede at first to let the crusading armies go through Hungary. When the fi
1 CONC rst such army (the First Crusade in 1096) proceeded to pillage its way through the country, he put greater and greater restrictions on subsequent armies, such as taking hostages and mustering his own army to guard the progress. Th
1 CONC ese actions did not endear him to contemporary chroniclers; descriptions of an ugly, hairy, crosseyed man are certainly exaggerated.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kálmán changed Hungary's foreign policy: while László I. had asked for the Holy Roman Emperor's help (instead of the pope's) when waging war on Croatia, Kálmán wanted to stay on good terms with the Holy See. This didn't prevent hi
1 CONC m from subjugating Croatia, nor from conquering Dalmatia ahead of the similarly-inclined Venetians. The pope eventually acceded him the right of appointing bishops.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kálmán's court was a center of learning and literature. Bishop Hartvik's Life of St. Stephen, a chronicle of Hungary, the shorter of the extant Legends of St. Gellért, and several collections of laws all stem from his reign. One o
1 CONC f his most famous laws was half a millennium ahead of its time: De strigis vero quae non sunt, nulla amplius quaestio fiat (As for the matter of witches [more exactly "strigas", which isn't exactly the same as "witch"], there is n
1 CONC o such thing, therefore no further investigations or trials are to be held).
1 CONT
1 CONT Álmos made several attempts to take over Kálmán's throne, but all were unsuccessful. After repeatedly forgiving his wayward brother, Kálmán was finally forced to bring justice against him in 1115, although even then he commuted th
1 CONC e familial death sentence required by law to the sentence of blinding Álmos and his young son Béla.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kálmán died February 3, 1116. He was buried in Székesfehérvár, next to St. Stephen.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married twice, first in 1097 to Felicia (called Busila in Hungary), daughter of Roger I of Sicily. They had three children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Zsófia (Sophia) 
1 CONT István (II) (1102-1131) 
1 CONT László (1101-1112) 
1 CONT Felicia died in 1102, and a second marriage was arranged in 1104 between Kálmán and Eufemia of Kiev, daughter of Prince Vladimir II of Kiev. However a few years later she was caught in adultery and immediately divorced and sent ba
1 CONC ck to her father. Eufemia bore a son in Kiev, named Boris Conrad, in 1112. After Stephen II died in 1131, Boris became a contender for the throne, but because Kálmán had refused to acknowledge Boris as his son, the Hungarian magna
1 CONC tes ignored him and gave the Hungarian throne to a cousin, the blinded son of Álmos, Béla.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ladislaus I King of Hungary Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stephen II 
0 @N6000000002176661971@ NOTE He succeeded his uncle Landislas I in 1095. Coloman had his own brother,
1 CONT Almos, and the infant son of Almos blinded to secure the throne for his own
1 CONT son Stephen II (r. 1116-1131), perhaps Hungary's worse king. The blinded boy,
1 CONT whom his father's friends had brought up in secrecy, Bela II (r. 1131-1141)
1 CONT and his eldest son, Geza II (r. 1141-1161), ruled thereafter unchallenged.
1 CONT But the succession of Geza II's son, Stephen III (r. 1161-1165) was disputed
1 CONT by two of Stephen's uncles, Ladislas II (1162-63)and Stephen IV (1161-1173).
0 @N6000000002240801904@ NOTE He succeeded his uncle Landislas I in 1095. Coloman had his own brother,
