0 @N6000000000437040850@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Prince Name Suffix:<NSFX> Ii, Of Antioch
0 @N6000000002187639314@ NOTE Bohemund II of Antioch
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  Bohemund II Guiscard (1108-1131) was the Prince of Antioch between 1111 and 1131 and Prince of Taranto from 1111 to 1128. He was the son of the founder of the principalities, Bohemund I, and princess Constance of France (daughter
1 CONC  of Philip I).
1 CONT
1 CONT When his father Bohemund I died, absent from Antioch, Bohemund II was a child living in Apulia. His cousin Tancred took over the regency of Antioch until he died in 1112; it then passed to Roger of Salerno, with the understanding 
1 CONC that he would relinquish it to Bohemund whenever the latter arrived. Roger, however, was killed at the Battle of Ager Sanguinis in 1119, and the nobles of Antioch invited King Baldwin II of Jerusalem to govern the Principality. In
1 CONC  October 1126, after his eighteenth birthday, Bohemund II finally came from Apulia to Antioch, leaving his lands in Italy to his cousin William II, Duke of Apulia. As part of his agreement to come to Antioch, he also married Baldw
1 CONC in II's daughter Alice. According to Matthew of Edessa Baldwin supposedly also promised him the crown of Jerusalem, but Matthew might be confusing Alice with her elder sister Melisende of Jerusalem, who also married a westerner, F
1 CONC ulk V of Anjou, around the same time.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1127 Bohemund besieged and captured Kafartab, killing all the inhabitants. He also attacked Shaizar, and Usamah ibn-Munqidh supposedly met the prince himself in battle (and frightened him off, if Usamah is to be believed). The 
1 CONC next years of his rule were marked by conflicts with Joscelin I of Edessa and skirmishes in the northern border. Both Bohemund and Joscelin attacked Aleppo individually, but refused to cooperate in a larger siege against the city.
1 CONC  Roger of Salerno had given away territory to Joscelin, but Bohemund did not consider these donations legitimate as they had been made without his authority, even though he had been a minor at the time. The dispute came to open co
1 CONC nflict between Antioch and Edessa, with Joscelin allying with the Muslims against Bohemund. The Latin Patriarch of Antioch placed an interdict over the County of Edessa. In 1128 Baldwin II marched north to mediate in the dispute, 
1 CONC and Joscelin abandoned his claims. Meanwhile, the atabeg Zengi consolidated his power over Aleppo and Mosul and the crusaders would never again have a chance to impose their authority over Aleppo.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the dispute was settled, Bohemund joined Baldwin II in attacking Damascus but the crusaders were defeated at the Battle of Marj es-Suffar. Bohemund then turned to the north to recover Anazarbus and other territories lost to 
1 CONC the Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia. Leo I of Armenia allied with the Danishmends against him, and Bohemund's army was lured into an ambush in February of 1131. Bohemund died in the struggle, and his blond head was embalmed, placed in
1 CONC  a silver box, and sent as a gift to the caliph.
1 CONT
1 CONT From his marriage to Alice, only one daughter, Constance of Antioch survived. Alice took over the regency of Antioch for two-year-old Constance, until Baldwin II forced her to relinquish it to Joscelin. Both Baldwin II and Josceli
1 CONC n died some months later.
1 CONT
1 CONT William of Tyre describes him as "rather tall and of fine figure. He had blond hair and well-made features. His whole bearing plainly showed the prince to those who did not know him. His conversation was agreeable and easily won t
1 CONC he favor of those who listened to him. He was of a generous nature and, like his father, truly magnificent."
1 CONT
1 CONT Usamah ibn-Munqidh calls him ibn-Maymun, the "son of Bohemund."
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Steven Runciman, A History of the Crusades, vol. II: The Kingdom of Jerusalem. Cambridge University Press, 1952 
1 CONT Fulcher of Chartres, A History of the Expedition to Jerusalem, 1095-1127, trans. Francis Rita Ryan, ed. Harold S. Fink, 1969. 
1 CONT William of Tyre, A History of Deeds Done Beyond the Sea, trans. E.A. Babcock and A.C. Krey. Columbia University Press, 1943 
1 CONT Philip K. Hitti, trans., An Arab-Syrian Gentleman and Warrior in the Period of the Crusades; Memoirs of Usamah ibn-Munqidh (Kitab al i'tibar). New York, 1929 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Bohemund I Prince of Taranto
1 CONT 1088–1128 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Roger II 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Bohemund I Prince of Antioch
