0 @N6000000002187891686@ NOTE William Iron Arm
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT William Iron Arm[1] (born before 1010 – died 1046) was a Norman adventurer, founder of the fortunes of the Hauteville family. He was a son of Tancred of Hauteville, who, along with his younger brother Drogo, journeyed to the Mezzo
1 CONC giorno in the first half of the eleventh century (c.1035) in response to calls of aid from fellow Normans under Rainulf Drengot, count of Aversa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Between 1038 and 1040, he fought in Sicily. It was there that he won his nickname Iron Arm, by single-handedly killing the emir of Syracuse during a sally at the siege of Syracuse while fighting for the Greeks. When the Greek gene
1 CONC ral Giorgio Maniace publicly humiliated the Salernitan leader, Arduin, the Lombards, along with their mercenary Normans and the Varangian Guard contingent, left. Maniace was consequently disgraced by the emperor in Constantinople 
1 CONC and the new catepan of Italy, Michael Doukeianos, appointed Arduin ruler at Melfi. The Normans of William followed him and soon they were all in revolt with the Apulian Lombards. First Atenulf, Prince of Benevento, and then Argyru
1 CONC s, the nominal leaders of the revolt, were bought off by the Greeks and the Normans elected their own leader, ignoring Arduin. The revolt, originally Lombard, had become Norman in character and leadership.
1 CONT
1 CONT In September 1042, William was elected by Normans as their "count" after the defection of Argyrus. He and the other leaders, chief among them Drogo and Peter, petitioned Guaimar IV, Prince of Salerno, for recognition of their conq
1 CONC uests. They received the lands around Melfi as a fief and proclaimed Guaimar "Duke of Apulia and Calabria". At Melfi in 1043, Guaimar divided the region (except for Melfi itself) into twelve baronies for the benefit of the Norman 
1 CONC leaders: Asclettin received Acerenza, Tristan received Montepeloso, Hugh Tubœuf received Monopoli, Peter received Trani, and Drogo received Venosa. William himself, predominant among the Norman leaders, received the lordship of As
1 CONC coli. He was married to Guida, daughter of Guy, duke of Sorrento, and niece of Guaimar.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his reign, William and Guaimar began the conquest of Calabria in 1044 and built the great castle of Stridula, probably near Squillace. In 1045, he was defeated near Taranto by Argyrus. He died in early 1046 and was succeede
1 CONC d by his brother Drogo.
1 CONT
1 CONT His titles were never confirmed by the Holy Roman Emperor. Drogo would be legally called "Count of the Normans in all Apulia and Calabria" (Comes Normannorum totius Apuliae e Calabriae), and so William is usually titled likewise.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Guillaume Bras-de-fer in French, Wilhelm Eisenarm in German, Guglielmo Braccio di Ferro in Italian and Gugghiermu Vrazzu di Ferru in Sicilian. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Ghisalberti, Albert (ed). Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani: II Albicante – Ammannati. Rome, 1960. 
1 CONT Gwatkin, H.M., Whitney, J.P. (ed) et al. The Cambridge Medieval History: Volume III. Cambridge University Press, 1926. 
1 CONT Norwich, John Julius. The Normans in the South 1016-1130. Longmans: London, 1967. 
1 CONT Chalandon, Ferdinand. Histoire de la domination normande en Italie et en Sicilie. Paris, 1907. 
1 CONT Gravett, Christopher, and Nicolle, David. The Normans: Warrior Knights and their Castles. Osprey Publishing: Oxford, 2006. 
1 CONT Beech, George. A Norman-Italian Adventurer in the East: Richard of Salerno. 1993. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT History of the Norman World. 
1 CONT Leeds Medieval History Texts in Translation. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT none Count of Apulia and Calabria
1 CONT 1042 – 1046 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Drogo 
