0 @N6000000002187893222@ NOTE Drogo of Hauteville
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Drogo of Hauteville[1] (c.1010 – 10 August 1051) succeeded his brother, William Iron Arm, with whom he arrived in southern Italy c.1035, as the leader of the Normans of Apulia.
1 CONT
1 CONT With his brother, he took part in the campaign of the Byzantine catepan George Maniaches in Sicily (1038) and then in the campaign against the Greeks of Apulia with the Lombard prince Guaimar IV of Salerno. In 1042, the Normans el
1 CONC ected William as count at Melfi and Drogo received Venosa from Guaimar in a twelve-part division of the conquered territory. In 1044–1045, Drogo was warring on behalf of his brother in Apulia. He seized Bovino from the Greeks.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1046, William died and Drogo and Peter of Trani were the premier candidates for his replacement. Drogo, supported by Guaimar, a good friend of William's, was duly elected as his brother's successor. The foundations of a Hautevi
1 CONC lle dynasty were set. Drogo was then approached by Ranulf Trincanocte, Count of Aversa, to reconcile him with Guaimar, which he did. In that year, he entered Apulia again and defeated the catepan Eustathios Palatinos near Taranto.
1 CONC  His brother Humphrey, who had arrived a short while earlier, even forced Bari to conclude a treaty with the Normans. In 1047, Drogo captured Benevento with the permission of the Holy Roman Emperor. It turned out to be a watershed
1 CONC  year. First, he received a daughter of Guaimar III, named either Gaitelgrima or Altrude, as wife and secondly, the Emperor Henry III confirmed him in his position and gave him the titular Dux et magister Italiae comesque Normanno
1 CONC rum totius Apuliae et Calabriae, translating "Duke and Master of Italy and Count of the Normans of all Apulia and Calabria," the first legitimate comital title for the Normans of Melfi. Henry also divested Guaimar of the principal
1 CONC ity of Capua and gave it to the old prince, Pandulf IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his reign, his half-brother Robert Guiscard arrived in the Mezzogiorno (c.1047). Drogo remained close in alliance to Guaimar and assisted him against Pandulf after the latter's reinstallation. However, he could not control 
1 CONC his barons and stem the tide of brigandage and feudal warfare paralysing the Mezzogiorno. He had to send away both his own younger brother Robert and the newly-arrived Richard Drengot, who attacked him and was captured. Guaimar se
1 CONC cured Richard's release late in 1047 or early in 1048. In the latter year, Drogo commanded an expedition in the valley of Crati, near Cosenza, and into Calabria. He distributed the conquered territories in Calabria and granted Rob
1 CONC ert Guiscard a castle at Scribla to guard the entrances. In 1050, he had to aid Guaimar in putting down the rebellion of one Guillaume Barbote.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1051, Pope Leo IX forced a promise from Drogo that he would stop the Norman pillaging, but he was assassinated later that year (probably by a Byzantine conspiracy led by Argyrus) at Montoglio and was succeeded by his younger br
1 CONC other Humphrey after a brief interregnum.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his marriage, he had a son, Richard, who joined the First Crusade. Richard's son Roger was later regent of the principality of Antioch.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Called Dreux or Drogon de Hauteville in French and Drogone d'Altavilla in Italian. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Ghisalberti, Albert (ed). Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani: II Albicante – Ammannati. Rome, 1960. 
1 CONT Gwatkin, H.M., Whitney, J.P. (ed) et al. The Cambridge Medieval History: Volume III. Cambridge University Press, 1926. 
1 CONT Norwich, John Julius. The Normans in the South 1016-1130. Longmans: London, 1967. 
1 CONT Chalandon, Ferdinand. Histoire de la domination normande en Italie et en Sicilie. Paris, 1907. 
1 CONT Gravett, Christopher, and Nicolle, David. The Normans: Warrior Knights and their Castles. Osprey Publishing: Oxford, 2006. 
1 CONT Beech, George. A Norman-Italian Adventurer in the East: Richard of Salerno. 1993. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT History of the Norman World. 
1 CONT Leeds Medieval History Texts in Translation. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT William I Count of Apulia and Calabria
1 CONT 1046–1051 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Humphrey 
0 @N6000000002188091711@ NOTE Humphrey of Hauteville
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Humphrey of Hauteville (d. 1057), called Onfroi de Hauteville in French and Umfredo d'Altavilla in Italian, succeeded his older brother Drogo as count of Apulia and Calabria in 1051. He was previously lord of Lavello. He is also u
1 CONC sually said to have accompanied his brothers to the Mezzogiorno c. 1035, however, he may have arrived later, in 1044, during the reign of his eldest brother William.
1 CONT
1 CONT By far the biggest event of his reign was the Battle of Civitate, fought near Civitate sul Fortore. Humphrey led the armies of the Hautevilles (along with his younger half-brother and heir, Robert Guiscard) and Drengots (along wit
1 CONC h Richard, count of Aversa) against the combined forces of the papacy and the Holy Roman Empire. The papal army was destroyed and the pope, Leo IX, was captured and imprisoned in Benevento.
1 CONT
1 CONT Humphrey's inheritance passed to his younger brother of the half blood and hero of Civitate, Robert Guiscard upon his death in 1057. He gave Guiscard the guardianship of his young sons, but Guiscard confiscated their inheritance. 
1 CONC Within two years, Guiscard would raise the county to the status of a duchy.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married to Gaitelgrima, a daughter of Prince Guaimar III of Salerno, former wife of his elder brother Drogo. Humphrey had two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Abagelard (Abélard or Abailardo), born after 1044 and died in Greece in 1081 
1 CONT Herman, born after 1045 and died in Byzantium in 1097 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Drogo Count of Apulia
1 CONT 1046–1051 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Robert Guiscard 
0 @N6000000002188091720@ NOTE Geoffrey of Hauteville
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Geoffrey of Hauteville (also Gottfried, Godfrey, Goffredo, or Gaufrido) was the second youngest son of Tancred of Hauteville by his first wife Muriella. He joined his brothers in the Mezzogiorno around 1053, arriving with his half
1 CONC -brothers Mauger and William. He was certainly present at the Battle of Civitate in that year.
1 CONT
1 CONT In that year, Humphrey, his brother the count of Apulia, gave Mauger and William the Capitanate and the Principate, respectively, with the title of count. When Mauger died later that decade (in 1054, according to Goffredo Malaterr
1 CONC a), the county passed to William, who gave it to Geoffrey. In 1059, his brother Robert Guiscard, Humphrey's successor over Geoffrey, who was older, but had not been in the south as long, helped him quell a revolt in the Capitanate
1 CONC . He also ruled the region around Loritello, where his son Robert was invested as count, and he expanded his domains into those of the pope, conquering Gissi in the Abruzzi. His death is a matter of confusion. The Breve Chronicon 
1 CONC Northmannicum states, on the authority of Goffredo Malaterra, that he died in 1063, but the chronicler apparently confused the many Geoffreys of the period. He probably died circa 1071.
1 CONT
1 CONT He had been married in Normandy and he had three sons from that union: the aforementioned Robert; Ralph, who inherited Catanzaro; and William, who inherited Tiriolo. In the Mezzogiorno, he married, like his eldest brother William 
1 CONC Iron Arm, a niece of the Prince Guaimar IV of Salerno, Theodora of Cappaccio, daughter of Pandulf, lord of Cappaccio, Guaimar's brother. From this second marriage was born at least one son, named Tancred, who was alive in 1103 and
1 CONC  1104. He also had a son, of unknown parentage, named Drogo or Tasso[1].
1 CONT
1 CONT It seems that Ralph participated in the Battle of Hastings (1066) and obtained thereby a fief in Wiltshire before 1086 (the time of the Domesday Book), thus founding the English branch of his illustrious family.
0 @N6000000002187631557@ NOTE Geoffrey, Count of Conversano
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Geoffrey the Elder (died after 1085), a nephew of Robert Guiscard through one of his sisters, was the count of Conversano, Brindisi, and Nardò from 1068 to his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Goffredo Malaterra, Geoffrey conquered most of his lands (castra) with his own energy (sua strenuitate) and without the help of the Guiscard. Thus, when, in 1067, the Guiscard demanded homage for the castle of Montepe
1 CONC loso, Geoffrey refused. Robert brought him to heel in the subsequent war and Geoffrey did homage. Among the other lands Geoffrey had conquered from the Byzantines were Polignano and Monopoli.
1 CONT
1 CONT Geoffrey joined his cousins Abelard and Herman, his brother Robert, and Henry, Count of Monte Sant'Angelo in the rebellion of 1079-1082, while the Guiscard was away fighting the Byzantine Empire in the Balkans. His brother died in
1 CONC  July 1080 and Abelard a year later. While he was besieging Oria, a city on the Via Appia between Brindisi and Taranto, Robert returned at Otranto and began to march towards him. Geoffrey, though he had numerous troops, fled anywa
1 CONC y.
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1083, Geoffrey was reconciled to Robert Guiscard and accompanied him on his final Byzantine expedition. He last appears in 1085 and left three sons and a daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Robert
1 CONT     * Alexander, Count of Conversano
1 CONT     * Tancred, Count of Brindisi
1 CONT     * Sybilla, married Robert Curthose, duke of Normandy
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Lexikon de Mittelalters.
1 CONT     * Norwich, John Julius. The Normans in the South 1016-1130. Longmans: London, 1967.
