0 @N6000000002187757270@ NOTE Béla II of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Béla II of Hungary, "The Blind" (Hungarian: Vak Béla) (1110-1141) of the Arpad dynasty was King of Hungary from 1131 until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT His father Prince Álmos led a rebellion against his brother King Coloman of Hungary, and as a result of this he and Béla were blinded and forced to flee to Constantinople. Béla was recalled by Coloman's son and successor Stephen I
1 CONC I, who, lacking offspring of his own, designated him his successor. Because Béla was blind, his wife Ilona and brother-in-law Beloš played a large role in governing his kingdom. Shortly after taking the throne, Ilona ordered the m
1 CONC assacre of the men she considered responsible for her husband's blinding.
1 CONT
1 CONT Béla's reign was notable for his foreign policy - his sister Hedwig was married to a son of Leopold III of Austria and another sister to Sobeslav I of Bohemia, thereby allying Hungary with two previously inimical states. In 1136, 
1 CONC Béla managed to recover part of Dalmatia from Venetian control, and sent an expedition into Bosnia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Béla's entire reign was overshadowed by a conflict with Boris, a son of Coloman of doubtful legitimacy, in which Boris was supported by Poland and Russia. In 1132 Boleslaus III of Poland led a campaign with Russian and Polish troo
1 CONC ps on Boris' behalf. Boleslaus and Boris were defeated near the Sajó River, but Boris was to prove a persistant claimant for a number of years to come.
1 CONT
1 CONT Béla died from the effects of an overindulgence of alcohol on February 13, 1141.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT Béla married Ilona of Serbia, daughter of Uros I Nemanjic, in 1127. They had six children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Géza II 
1 CONT László II 
1 CONT István IV 
1 CONT Álmos, died young 
1 CONT Zsófia, a nun 
1 CONT Erzsébet, married Mieszko III of Poland 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Engel, Pat. Realm of St. Stephen : A History of Medieval Hungary, 2001 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stephen II King of Hungary
1 CONT
1 CONT 1131–1141 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Geza II 
0 @N6000000003949871854@ NOTE He was blinded, together with his father, on the orders of his uncle King Kálmán and took refuge in the monastery of Pécsvárad[480].  He was appointed heir to the throne by his first cousin King István II in [1129][481].  He succe
1 CONC eded in 1131 as BÉLA II "the Blind" King of Hungary, crowned 28 Apr 1131, one of the rare exceptions of succession to a throne by a blind person in the Balkan region.  King Béla was under the influence of his domineering wife who 
1 CONC took an active part in the government of the country.
