0 @N6000000002187805654@ NOTE Malusha
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Malusha (Ukrainian and Russian: ??????) was a housekeeper and concubine of Sviatoslav I of Kiev. According to Slavonic chronicles, she was the mother of Vladimir the Great and sister of Dobrynya. The Norse sagas describe Vladimir'
1 CONC s mother as a prophetess who lived to the age of 100 and was brought from her cave to the palace to predict the future.
1 CONT
1 CONT As the chronicles are silent on the subject of Malusha's pedigree, 19th-century Russian historians devised various theories to explain her parentage and name. An archaeologist Dmitry Prozorovsky believed that Malusha was the daugh
1 CONC ter of Mal, a Drevlyan leader. A prominent chronicle researcher and linguist Alexei Shakhmatov considered Malusha to be the daughter of Mstisha Sveneldovich, son of a Kievan voyevoda Sveneld. He believed that the name Malusha was 
1 CONC a slavinized version of a Scandinavian name Malfried. Another Russian historian Dmitry Ilovaisky came to an opposite conclusion that the Slavic name Malusha was turned into a Scandinavian Malfried. Ukrainian historian Mykhailo Hru
1 CONC shevsky criticized both of these versions.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Primary Chronicle records that a certain Malfried died in 1000. This record follows that of Rogneda's death. Since Rogneda was Vladimir's wife, historians assume that Malfried was another close relative of the ruling prince, p
1 CONC referably his wife or mother.
0 @N6000000003635701645@ NOTE [1558] WSHNGT.ASC file (Geo Washington Ahnentafel) # 34883433, or Malfreda
