0 @N6000000001783211415@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis VI called the Fat (French: le Gros), was King of France from 1108 until his death (1137). Chronicles called him "roi de Saint-Denis". The first member of the House of Capet to make a lasting contribution to the centralizing 
1 CONC institutions of royal power,[1] Louis was born in Paris, the son of Philip I and his first wife, Bertha of Holland. Almost all of his twenty-nine-year reign was spent fighting either the "robber barons" who plagued Paris or the No
1 CONC rman kings of England for their continental possession of Normandy. Nonetheless, Louis VI managed to reinforce his power considerably and became one of the first strong kings of France since the division of the Carolingian Empire.
1 CONC  His biography by his constant advisor Abbot Suger of Saint Denis renders him a fully-rounded character to the historian, unlike most of his predecessors.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his youth, Louis fought the duke of Normandy, Robert Curthose, and the lords of the royal demesne, the Île de France. He became close to Suger, who became his adviser. He succeeded his father on Philip's death on July 29, 1108.
1 CONC  Louis's half-brother prevented him from reaching Rheims and so he was crowned on August 3 in the cathedral of Orléans by Daimbert, Archbishop of Sens. The archbishop of Reims, Ralph the Green, sent envoys to challenge the validit
1 CONC y of the coronation and anointing, but to no avail.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1104 he married Lucienne de Rochefort - the marriage was annulled.  They had one child:
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabelle (c.1105 - before 1175), married (ca 1119) William of Vermandois, seigneur of Chaumont
1 CONT
1 CONT In  1115 he married Adélaide de Maurienne (1092-1154).  They had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip (1116 - October 13, 1131), King of France (1129-31), not to be confused with his brother of the same name; died from a fall from a horse.
1 CONT Louis VII (1120 - November 18, 1180), King of France
1 CONT Henry (1121-75), archbishop of Reims
1 CONT Hugues (born ca 1122
1 CONT Robert (ca 1123 - October 11, 1188), count of Dreux
1 CONT Constance (ca 1124 - August 16, 1176), married first Eustace IV, count of Boulogne and then Raymond V of Toulouse.
1 CONT Philip (1125-61), bishop of Paris. not to be confused with his elder brother.
1 CONT Peter of France (ca 1125-83), married Elizabeth, lady of Courtenay
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis VI died on August 1, 1137, at the castle of Béthisy-Saint-Pierre, nearby Senlis and Compiègne, of dysentery caused by his excesses, which had made him obese. He was interred in Saint Denis Basilica. He was succeeded on the t
1 CONC hrone by his son Louis VII, called "the Younger," who had originally wanted to be a monk.
0 @N6000000002319313749@ NOTE Louis VI, the Fat (born 1081, ruled 1108-37), was the first important
1 CONT king of the Capetian line. This line sprang from Hugh Capet, who became
1 CONT king in 987. Louis the Fat was a great fighter, a great hunter, and a
1 CONT great eater. At 46 he became too fat to mount a horse, but he remained
1 CONT the embodiment of warlike energy. His great task was to reduce to order
1 CONT the petty nobles of the royal domain, who could truly be called robber
1 CONT barons. When Louis came to the throne, every lord of a castle robbed at
1 CONT will and it was not safe for even the king to pass along the road.
1 CONT Twenty years of hard fighting were necessary to remedy this condition,
1 CONT but in the end, law and order prevailed. So that such evils might not
1 CONT recur, every castle that was captured was destroyed or given to
1 CONT faithful followers.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
1 CONT
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT    Louis VI (1081-1137), king of France (1108-1137). Almost his entire
1 CONT reign
1 CONT   was spent in subduing the robber barons, who preyed on the environs of
1 CONT   Paris but were finally forced to yield to royal authority. For some
1 CONT 20 years
1 CONT    during the period from 1109-1135, Louis waged war against Henry I,
1 CONT the
1 CONT   Norman king of England, and against Henry's son-in-law, Holy Roman
1 CONT  Emperor Henry V. Louis greatly strengthed the royal power in France.
