0 @N6000000002248181606@ NOTE Louis Vi, though stout, was an active warrior and fought steadily to increase the prestige of the monarchy.  He consolidated his position in his own lands and received a striking tribute when Duke William X of Aquitaine bequeathed
1 CONC  to him his daughter and heiress Eleanor, who was promptly married to the future Louis VII.  It seemed as though the Kings of France might be on the way to real power.  Eleanor, however, presented Louis with nothing but daughters 
1 CONC and was suspected of infidelity; in 1152 Louis divorced her, and she promptly married Henry II, Duke of Normandy and soon to be King of England, the most powerful vassal of Louis - to whom she bore more sons than he could easily c
1 CONC ontrol.  A great opportunity had miscarried, but Louis reacted well in face of the new threat.  Henry was constantly reminded that the French monarch was his overlord for all lands south of the Channel, and Louis skilfully fomente
1 CONC d the discords among Henry's unruly family.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Heraldry of the Royald Families of Europe by Jirí Louda & Michael Maclagan
1 CONT
1 CONT pg 123
0 @N6000000003051255189@ NOTE [Weis 97] Crusader
1 CONT
1 CONT [From Wikipedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_VI_of_France]
1 CONT Louis VI the Fat (French: Louis VI le Gros) (December 1, 1081 ? August 1, 1137) was King of France from 1108 to 1137.
1 CONT
1 CONT A member of the Capetian Dynasty, Louis VI was born in Paris, the son of Philip I of France (1060?1108), and Bertha of Holland (1055?94).
1 CONT
1 CONT He married in 1104: 1) Lucienne de Rochefort ? the marriage was annulled.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married in 1115: 2) Adélaide de Maurienne (c.1100-54)
1 CONT
1 CONT Their children:
1 CONT 1) Philippe (1116 - October 13, 1131), King of France (1129-31)
1 CONT 2) Louis VII (1120 - November 18, 1180), King of France
1 CONT 3) Henri (1121-74), archbishop of Reims
1 CONT 4) Hugues (c.1122-?)
1 CONT 5) Robert (c.1123 - October 11, 1188), count of Dreux
1 CONT 6) Constance (c.1124 - August 16, 1176), married first Eustace IV, count of Boulogne and then Raymond V of Toulouse.
1 CONT 7) Philippe (1125-61), bishop of Paris
1 CONT 8) Pierre (c.1126-83), married Elizabeth, lady of Courtenay
1 CONT Almost all of his 29 year reign was spent fighting either the "robber barons" who plagued Paris, or the English. Nonetheless, Louis VI managed to reinforce his power considerably and endeared himself to the working classes of Fran
1 CONC ce.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis VI died on August 1, 1137 and is interred in Saint Denis Basilica.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded on the throne by his son Louis VII (1137-80).
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT Suger, Abbot of Saint Denis,. The Deeds of Louis the Fat. Translated with introduction and notes by Richard Cusimano and John Moorhead. Washington, DC : Catholic University of America Press,1992. (ISBN 0813207584)
1 CONT Suger, Abbot of Saint Denis,. The Deeds of Louis the Fat. Translated by Jean Dunbabin (this version is free, but has no annotations)
0 @N6000000003949776898@ NOTE King of France from 1108 to 1137, was born in 1078 or 1081, he was called Thibaud at birth, and died in August 1137 at Chateau Bethizy near Paris, buries at St. Denis. At his baptism he was given the name Louis, in accordance with
1 CONC  Carolingian tradition. The only son of Bertha, Philip I.'s first wife, he was soon the object of persecution by Bertrade of Anjou (Montfort), the mistress whom the king had married while repudiating the legitimate queen. Betrade 
1 CONC went so far as trying to have one of her sons substituted for Louis, the legitimate heir, and it is to Philip's credit that he absolutely opposed this plan. But because of the family conflicts, Philip abandoned his son as early as
1 CONC  1098, then sent him off to the wars in the Vexin to be rid of him. Eventually he acceded to the throne in 1108, a mature and hardened young man. He was the first Capetian who, though designated, was not crowned during his father'
1 CONC s lifetime. In 1115 he married in 1104 but divorced in 1108 (1) Lucienne, born about 1088, daughter of Guy I de Rochefort. They had a daughter, Isabelle. Louis married in Paris in 1115 (2) Adelaide (Alice) of Savoy and of Maurienn
1 CONC e, born in 1092 and who died in 1154, daughter of Count Hubert (Humbert) II of Savoy-Maurienne.
