0 @N6000000000945068714@ NOTE Fyrste av Benevento 982 - 1016. 
1 CONT Fyrste av Capua 1009 - 1016 som Pandulf III.
1 CONT Da hans far døde i 968-69 ble Pandulf fordrevet fra Benevento av sin farbror Pandulf I.
1 CONT Men da broren Landulf V av Capua døde i mai 993 og dennes sønn, Pandulf II av Capua, som
1 CONT døde 13.08.1014 var mindreårig, regjerte vår Pandulf II. Han hadde etter farbrorens død i
1 CONT 981 fått Benevento tilbake.
0 @N6000000003051273657@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Pandulf II of Benevento", retrieved 13 Oct 07]
1 CONT Pandulf II the Old (died August 1014) was the prince of Benevento from 981 and prince of Capua (as Pandulf III) from 1008 or 1009 to his death, the son of Landulf III who was co-prince between 959 and 968. Pandulf was first associ
1 CONC ated as co-prince (in Capua) in 977.
1 CONT
1 CONT On his father's death, Pandulf was marginalised by his uncle, the reigning Pandulf Ironhead, who gave Capua and Benevento to his eldest son Landulf IV on his death in 981. That year, however, Landulf IV was forced to divide his pr
1 CONC incipality for the first time since 910. Benevento was given to Pandulf. In May 987, he associated his son Landulf with him in the tradition of the Capuan dynasty begun by Atenulf I.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 999, Otto III visited the shrine at the Sanctuary of Monte Sant'Angelo on Monte Gargano. On his return through Benevento, he signed a diploma in favour of the monastery of S. Sofia on 11 March. S. Sofia was the familial foundat
1 CONC ion of Pandulf's line and probably acted as their mausoleum. For reasons unknown, Otto and Pandulf had a falling out in 1000, possibly over the relics of Saint Bartholomew, patron saint of Benevento, to whom Otto had constructed a
1 CONC  new church on the Isola Tiberina?San Bartolomeo all'Isola?just recently. According to the Annales Beneventani, Otto rex cum magno exercitu obsedit Benevento: "King Otto with a large army besieged Benevento." Nothing, however, cam
1 CONC e of it, except perhaps the yielding of certain relics (possibly the skin of Bartholomew).
1 CONT
1 CONT Also in that millennial year, the Capuans ousted their imperialist prince, Adhemar, and invited Landulf di Sant'Agata, Pandulf's brother, to be their new prince. In 1003, a rebellion led by Adelfer, Count of Avellino, ousted Pandu
1 CONC lf and his son from Benevento. The princes did not remain exiled for long, however. In 1005, we find them ruling from their capital again. The revolt was a bad sign, though. Civil unrest was rising in the principality.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1007, Landulf of Capua died and Pandulf succeeded him alongside his son, Pandulf II of Capua. In 1011, 1012, or 1013, Pandulf's eponymous grandson, Pandulf III, the son of Landulf, was associated with the rule of Benevento. The
1 CONC reafter, Pandulf the elder recedes from the pages of history until his death is recorded in 1014.
1 CONT
1 CONT Children
1 CONT - Gaitelgrima, married Guaimar III of Salerno
1 CONT - Maria, married Sergius II of Amalfi
1 CONT - Landulf V of Benevento
1 CONT - Pandulf IV of Capua
1 CONT - Atenulf, Abbot of Montecassino
0 @N6000000000945068662@ NOTE Gemma var grevinne av Teano. 
1 CONT Hennes datter, Sikilgaita, kan antas å være født ca. 1040. Gemma kan da være født
1 CONT ca. 1015.
0 @N6000000003051245069@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "John II of Salerno", retrieved 13 Oct 07]
1 CONT John II (died between 994 and 998), called the Accursed, son of Lampert of Spoleto, was the count of the palace of Salerno in 980 and acting regent for Prince Pandulf II. He was pushed out with the prince by the duke of Amalfi, Ma
1 CONC nso, in 981. The rule of the Amalfitan and his son John was oppressive and the local populace rose in revolt and elected the Spoletan John prince in 983, expelling Manso.
1 CONT
1 CONT He tried, through appointing his scribe Toto as advocate, to control the monastery of San Massimo and her property, but failed. Together with his wife Sichelgaita, he founded S. Maria de Domno and put it directly under the authori
1 CONC ty of the archdiocese of Salerno. Under its first abbot, Radoald, it was very successful, though John's attempts to control religion in his principality were less so. In January 984, John associated his son Guido with him, but Gui
1 CONC do died in 988. Between January and March 989, he associated his next son Guaimar, who succeeded him. He left other sons in Pandulf, Lambert, John, and Peter.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to a legend related by Peter Damian, there was an eruption of Mount Vesuvius and John exclaimed that surely it was an omen foretelling the death of some rich man, who would surely end up in hell. The next day, John was f
1 CONC ound dead in the arms of a prostitute. Perhaps this is the basis for his epithet "accursed".
0 @N6000000000945068690@ NOTE Greve av Spoleto 947 - 999. 
1 CONT Greve av Salerno 981 - 999.
1 CONT Johan (Giovanno) var toskansk av fødsel og opprinnelig greve av Spoleto. Han ble
1 CONT antagelig greve av Salerno da han i 947 ble kalt til hersker over guvernementet Salerno ved å
1 CONT avlegge et offentlig løfte etter at hans forgjenger hadde flyktet. Som medregent hadde han i 5
1 CONT år sin eldste sønn, Johan II, som døde i 988. Deretter delte han regjeringen med sin annen
1 CONT sønn, Waimar IV. Fra hans regjeringstid vet man kun å fortelle at pave Benedikt VII opprettet
1 CONT en erkebispestol i Salerno.
