0 @N6000000002187757167@ NOTE Constance of Antioch
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance of Antioch (1127-1163) was the ruler of the principality of Antioch (a crusader state) from 1130 to her death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance was the only daughter of Bohemund II of Antioch by his wife Alice, princess of Jerusalem. She became princess of Antioch when she was only four-years-old, under the regency of Baldwin II of Jerusalem (1130-1131) and Fulk
1 CONC  of Jerusalem (1131-1136). Her mother Alice did not want the principality to pass to Constance, preferring to rule in her own name. Alice attempted to ally with the Muslim atabeg of Mosul, Zengi, offering to marry Constance to a M
1 CONC uslim prince, but the plan was foiled by Alice's father Baldwin, who exiled her from Antioch. In 1135 Alice attempted once again to take control of the principality, and sought a husband for Constance in Manuel Comnenus, at that t
1 CONC ime the heir to the Byzantine throne. Fulk exiled her again and re-established the regency for Constance. In 1136, while still a child, Constance was married to Raymond of Poitiers, whom the noble supporters of the regency had sec
1 CONC retly summoned from Europe; Alice was tricked into believing Raymond was going to marry her, and, humiliated, left Antioch for good when the marriage was performed. From this union three children were born:
1 CONT
1 CONT Bohemund III of Antioch, who succeeded her in 1163 
1 CONT Maria of Antioch (1145-1182), married (rechristened as "Xena") to Manuel I Comnenus 
1 CONT Philippa of Antioch, mistress to Andronicus I Comnenus 
1 CONT In 1149, Raymond died in the battle of Inab and Constance remarried in 1153 to Raynald of Chatillon, who also became co-ruler of Antioch. Constance had one daughter from Raynald:
1 CONT
1 CONT Agnes (1154-1184), married king Bela III of Hungary 
1 CONT Raynald was captured in 1160 and spent the next sixteen years in a prison in Aleppo. A dispute arose between Constance and her son, Bohemund, when Bohemund tried to seize power in Antioch. A riot broke out, and Constance was exile
1 CONC d from the city. She died in 1163.
1 CONT
1 CONT  This biography of a member of a Middle Eastern royal house is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Bohemund II Prince of Antioch
1 CONT 1153–1160
1 CONT (with Raymond and Raynald) Succeeded by:
1 CONT Bohemund III 
0 @N6000000002248200718@ NOTE Constance became princess at the age of four years old.  Her mother Alice did not want the principality to pass to Constance, preferring to rule in her own name.  Alice attempted to ally with the Muslim atabeg of Mosul, Zengi, off
1 CONC ering to marry Constance to a Muslim prince, but the plan was foiled by Alice's father Baldwin, who exiled her from Antioch.  In 1135 Alice attempted once again to take control of the principality, and sought a husband for Constan
1 CONC ce in Manuel Comnenus, at that time the heir to the Byzantine throne.  Fulk exiled her again and re-established the regency for Constance.  In 1136, while still a child, Constance was married to Raymond of Poitiers, whom the noble
1 CONC  supporters of the regency had secretly summoned from Europe;  Alice was tricked into believing Raymond was going to marry her, and humiliated, left Antioch for good when the marriage was performed.  
1 CONT From this union three children were born:
1 CONT
1 CONT Bohemund III of Antioch, who succeeded her in 1163
1 CONT Maria of Antioch (1145-1182), married (rechristened as "Xena") to Manuel I Comnenus
1 CONT Philippa of Antioch, mistress to Andronicus I Comnenus
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1149, Raymond died in the battle of Inab and Constance remarried in 1153 to Raynald of Chatillon, who also became co-ruler of Antioch.
1 CONT Constance had one daughter from RAynald:
1 CONT Agnes (1154-1184), married king Bela III of Hungary
1 CONT
1 CONT According to some sources, Constance and Raynald had another daughter, Jeanne, possible the second wife of Boniface I of Monferrat.
1 CONT
1 CONT Raynald was captured in 1160 and spent the next sixteen years in a prison in Aleppo.  A dispute arose between Constance and her son, Bohemund, when Bohemund tried to seize power in Antioch.  A riot broke out, and Constance was exi
1 CONC led from the city.  She died in 1163.
0 @N6000000003949871743@ NOTE Baldwin III, King of Jerusalem, was anxious to marry off his cousin, Constance, to some powerful baron who would undertake the defense of Antioch.  But she was not anxious to remarry.  As regent for her son Bohemond, she was perfe
1 CONC ctly happy enjoying her freedom and took a mischievous pleasure in annoying her cousin, the king by refusing the suitors he proposed.  Having made up er mind to marry only a man she loved, she finally made her choice and Baldwin w
1 CONC as so impatient to see her married off that he gave his consent as soon as he heard that she had finally made a choice.  Her choice scandalized the nobility of the entire country and caused a furor in ecclesiastical cirles.  The m
1 CONC an was Reynaud, the penniless son of an Angevin family with no great nobility.  ("The Crusades" by Zoe Oldenbourgh, 1966, p 343-345)  
0 @N6000000004533182376@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Constance Guiscard (1127-1163) was the ruler of the principality ofAntioch (a crusader state) from 1130 to her death.
