0 @N6000000002187837154@ NOTE Roman the Great
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman the Great or Roman Mstislavich (c. 1150-1205) was the Prince of Novgorod, Volhynia, and Halych.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman was the son of Mstislav II of Kiev. During 1168-1170 he reigned in the Great Novgorod. From 1170 to 1199 he ruled in Volodymyr-Volynsky, which he inherited from his father. In 1187, he made an unsuccessful attempt to wrestle
1 CONC  from the boyars the principality of Halych. In 1197 he married Anna, by whom he had four children, including Danylo of Halych.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman Mstislavich gained nation-wide fame by his victorious campaign against Polovtsi in 1197-1198, 1201, and 1204. In 1199, he became Prince of Halych, uniting Halichina and Volhynia lands in the mighty principality of Halych-Vol
1 CONC hynia. In 1202, he also captured Kiev, uniting all South-west Ruthenia under his rule. Prominent as a warlord, Roman the Great was also generally considered a wise politician. He lived mostly in peace with Hungary and Poland, but 
1 CONC had uneasy relationships with several other neighbouring states. He perished near the Polish city Zawichost during a march against Prince Leszek the White of Lesser Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ingvar I Grand Prince of Kiev Succeeded by:
1 CONT Rostislav II 
0 @N6000000002187825310@ NOTE Mieszko III the Old
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  Mieszko III the Old (Polish: Mieszko III Stary; 1121? – March 13, 1202), of the Piast Dynasty, was Duke of Greater Poland, 1138-1202, and High Duke of all Poland, with interruptions, 1173-1202.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko was the son of Boleslaw III Wrymouth, Duke of Poland, and Salome of Berg. Mieszko was Boleslaw's third son, and the ducal couple's second.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko was Duke of Greater Poland, 1138-1202, and Duke of Kraków and High Duke of all Poland, 1173-1177, 1190, 1198/1199, 1202.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko married:
1 CONT
1 CONT ca. 1140, Elizabeth of Hungary, daughter of Prince Álmos, brother of the King of Hungary; 
1 CONT ca. 1154, Eudoksia of Kiev, daughter of Izjaslav II, Grand Duke of Kyiv (Kievan Rus'). 
1 CONT Mieszko's children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Odon I of Greater Poland; 
1 CONT Boleslaw of Kujawy; 
1 CONT Mieszko of Kalisz; and 
1 CONT Wladyslaw III Spindleshanks. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Biography
1 CONT In accordance with the testament of Poland's Duke Boleslaw III Wrymouth, Poland was divided into 4-5 hereditary provinces distributed among his sons, with the royal province of Kraków being reserved for the eldest son, who was to 
1 CONC be High Duke of all Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT One of the provinces, Greater Poland, was granted to Boleslaw's third-oldest son, Mieszko (and was subsequenly divided among Mieszko's descendants and successors). In 1173 Mieszko was promoted to the throne of Kraków and became Hi
1 CONC gh Duke of Poland (Latin: dux maximus, dux totius Poloniae); but he was soon expelled from Kraków by his younger brother, Kazimierz II the Just, and from Greater Poland by his son. Mieszko III never abdicated his rights or ambitio
1 CONC ns, and returned to Kraków four times before he died in 1202.
0 @N6000000002187829077@ NOTE Wladyslaw III Spindleshanks
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladyslaw III Spindleshanks (Polish: Wladyslaw III Laskonogi; 1165? – 1231) was Duke of the province of Greater Poland and High Duke of all Poland, overlord of other Dukes, 1202-1206 and 1227-1228, during which periods he also was
1 CONC  Duke of Cracow, the duchy customarily held by the overlord.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladyslaw became overlord of Poland in place of his cousin Leszek the White who was deposed. Wladyslaw then was himself deposed in favor of Leszek from the overlordship. Only after twenty years, and a couple of reigns in between, 
1 CONC he succeeded to get restored.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladyslaw was the son of Mieszko III the Old and his successor in their family part-realm of Greater Poland.
0 @N6000000000437345922@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Armenia
0 @N6000000002188294134@ NOTE Leo I, King of Armenia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Leo I the Magnificent
1 CONT ????? ? ???????? 
1 CONT Prince, then King 
1 CONT
1 CONT Predecessor Roupen III 
1 CONT Successor Isabella 
1 CONT Dynasty House of Roupen 
1 CONT
1 CONT Leo I or Leon I (Armenian: ????? ? ????????, Eastern tansliteration: Levon Metsagorts, Western trans.: Levon Medzakordz; 1150 – 2[1] or 5 May 1219), called the Magnificent, was the Prince of Cilicia, as Leo II, from 1187 and first
1 CONC  King of Cilicia from 1199 until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Lord of the Mountains 
1 CONT 2 First King of Lesser Armenia 
1 CONT 3 Notes 
1 CONT 4 References 
1 CONT 5 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Lord of the Mountains
1 CONT Leo became "Lord of the Mountains" (ruler of Cilician Armenia) in 1187, his brother Ruben III of Armenia resigning the crown to him shortly before his death. He was initially the 'Regent and Tutor' for his nieces, Alice and Philip
1 CONC pa, but later he set them aside and had himself crowned on January 6, 1199.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 3 February 1189 Leo married with a lady called Isabelle. Her origins are unclear (her surname "of Austria", indicate a possibly German origin); the only certain about her is she was the daughter of a brother of Sybille, third w
1 CONC ife of the Prince Bohemund III of Antioch. The marriage only produced a daughter, Stephanie (also called Rita), born after 1195 and later married John of Brienne. With the onset of the Third Crusade, he opened negotiations with Fr
1 CONC ederick Barbarossa to receive the title of King of Armenia, but Frederick's drowning at the Saleph River in Cilicia forestalled the plan. This represented a significant shift in Armenian policy, which had traditionally looked towa
1 CONC rds the Byzantine empire for authority. Leo is also said to have attended the wedding of Richard the Lionhearted in Cyprus as a groomsman in 1191.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1194, Leo seized the castle of Bagras, on the northern border of the Principality of Antioch, which had been captured by Saladin in 1189. Bohemund III of Antioch and the Knights Templar, its original owners, demanded its return
1 CONC . Leo lured Bohemund to Bagras under pretense of a parley, captured him, and imprisoned him in Sis. Under duress, Bohemund was compelled to cede the Principality to Leo. Bohemund was subsequently released through the mediation of 
1 CONC Henry II of Champagne, but was forced to abandon all claims to the suzerainity of Armenia. In addition, the two entered into a marital alliance in 1195. Bohemond's son Raymond married Alice of Armenia, the daughter of Ruben III.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] First King of Lesser Armenia
1 CONT Leo continued to appeal to the new Emperor, Henry VI, and to Pope Celestine III for a royal title. These efforts were successful, and on January 6, 1199, he was crowned King of Armenia by the papal legate, Conrad of Wittelsbach, A
1 CONC rchbishop of Mainz, in the Church of Holy Wisdom at Tarsus. He was nominally an Imperial vassal, but soon abandoned any acknowledgement of fealty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Strife had by now broken out in Antioch; Raymond of Antioch had died in 1197, leaving a posthumous son, Raymond-Roupen of Antioch, heir to the Principality through primogeniture. However, Raymond's younger brother Bohemund, alread
1 CONC y Count of Tripoli, vigorously disputed the succession, and ejected his own father from the city in 1199. However, Leo intervened against him and restored Bohemund III to the Principality; but on his death in 1201, the younger Boh
1 CONC emund was recognized as Prince of Antioch as Bohemund IV. Leo attempted to capture Antioch in 1203 and place Raymond-Roupen on the throne, but he was defeated by the Knights Templar, who favored Bohemund.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1206, the Catholicos John of Sis accused the Queen Isabelle of adultery. Leo put to death several members of his wife's suite and personally attacked her. Their marriage was annulled and Isabelle was imprisoned on 28 January in
1 CONC  the fortress of Vaghka. She was poisoned there in 1207.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Cyprus on 28 January 1210, Leo married secondly with the princess Sybilla of Lusignan, daughter of King Amalric II of Jerusalem, who bore him a daughter, Isabella or Zabel, on 27 January 1216. In Leo's later years he suffered f
1 CONC rom deformed hands and feet, probably a symptom of gout.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leo continued to support the claims of Raymond-Roupen, who had married Helvis of Lusignan, another daughter of Amalric II (by his first wife), in 1210, and had him crowned "King Junior" of Armenia on August 15, 1211. However, Bohe
1 CONC mund's allies, including the Knights Templar, and the Ayyubid emir of Aleppo Az-Zahir always forestalled attempts to eject Bohemund from the citadel of Antioch, although Leo several times entered the city. In 1216, with the aid of
1 CONC  the Knights Hospitaller, Leo was finally able to capture Antioch and install Raymond-Roupen as prince. Having established Raymond-Roupen with Antioch, Leo decided that his daughter Isabella, rather than Raymond-Roupen, should suc
1 CONC ceed him in Armenia. On his death on May 2, 1219 Isabella became queen, but Raymond-Roupen was ejected from Antioch and came to contest the crown.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leo was a powerful ruler, remembered by chroniclers as "Leo the Magnificent". His court at Sis was cultured and he was a supporter of calligraphy and the arts. He allied himself with the Knights Hospitaller and the Teutonic Knight
1 CONC s, but drove the Knights Templar from his kingdom. He fostered commerce with Venice and Genoa, opening his ports at Ayas and Corycos to the world. The Assizes of Antioch were adopted as the law of the kingdom, and translated into 
1 CONC Armenian. Also he captured Herakleia (Now Eregli or Konya Ereglisi) and Larende (Now Karaman) from Anatolian Seljuks in 1211 and held these cities for five years.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ "Dying on 2 May 1219 without male heirs, Leo II had this daughter Isabel (Zabel) proclaimed queen the day before." Toumanoff, p. 633 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Boase, T. S. R. (1978). The Cilician Kingdom of Armenia. Edinburgh: Scottish Academic Press. ISBN 0-7073-0145-9.  
1 CONT Chahin, M. (2001). The Kingdom of Armenia: A History, 2nd ed., Richmond, Surrey: Curzon. ISBN 0-7007-1452-9.  
1 CONT Rudt de Collenberg, W. H. (1963). The Rupenides, Hethumides and Lusignans: The Structure of the Armeno-Cilician Dynasties. Paris: Klincksieck.  
1 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ARMENIA.htm#_Toc176328582 
1 CONT Toumanoff, C. (1966). "Armenia and Georgia", Cambridge Medieval History, vol. IV.  
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT Greeks, Crusaders and Moslems — Rise of Leon II (Kurkjian's History of Armenia, Chs. 28-29) 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Leo I, King of Armenia
1 CONT House of Roupen 
1 CONT Regnal titles 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Roupen III King of Armenia
1 CONT 1187–1219 Succeeded by
1 CONT Isabella 
0 @N6000000002187918554@ NOTE Pirooz
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Pirooz ("the winner") was son of Yazdgerd III the last Sassanid king of Persia. Most of what is known of Pirooz is written by his son, prince Narsieh.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Pirooz was very young at the time of the reign of his father Yazdgerd and never exercised the monarch power of the Sassanids. After the Arab conquest of Persia, Pirooz and much of the monarch family escaped through the Pami
1 CONC r mountains in what is now Tajikistan and arrived at Tang China, which was more supportive of the Sassanids.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Old Book of Tang (???), Pirooz (???) asked for military help from Tang China against the Arabs in 661. The Tang court created the Governorate of Persia (?????) at what is now Zaranj, Afghanistan, with Pirooz as go
1 CONC vernor. Between 670 and 674, Pirooz arrived personally at the Tang court and was given the title of Youwuwei Jiangjun (?????, "Martial General and Guard of the Right [Flank]"). In 678, the Deputy Minister for Personnel (????) of t
1 CONC he Tang Court, Pei Xingjian (???) was ordered to escort Pirooz back to Persia. Pei Xingjian got as far as Suiye (??; near modern Tokmok, Kyrgyzstan) before returning, while Pirooz was forced to spend over 20 years in Tuhuoluo (???
1 CONC ; likely Bactra or Tokharistan) with several thousand followers. In 708 Pirooz arrived at the Tang court again and was given the title of Zuoweiwei Jiangjun (????? Awe-inspiring General and Guard of the Left [Flank]). He died soon
1 CONC  after, while some of his followers survived him.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the New Book of Tang (???), Pirooz died after receiving the title Youwuwei Jiangjun. It was then Pirooz's son, Narsieh (???), a hostage at the Tang court, who was escorted by Pei Xingjian westwards to Persia in 679 (n
1 CONC ot 678). Like the Old Book of Tang, Pei got as far as Suiye, and Narsieh (not Pirooz) then spent 20 years in Tuhuoluo. Finally, it was again Narsieh and not Pirooz that received the title of Zuoweiwei Jiangjun.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Old Book of Tang
1 CONT     * New Book of Tang
1 CONT     * China page
1 CONT     * Iranian.com
1 CONT     * Iranian.com
1 CONT
