0 @N6000000000337194259@ NOTE (Research):Igor Encyclopædia Britannica Article    born c. 877 died 945, Dereva region [Russia]   also called Ingvar grand prince of Kiev and presumably the son of Rurik, prince of Novgorod, who is considered the founder of the dy
1 CONC nasty that ruled Kievan Rus and, later, Muscovy until 1598. Igor, successor to the great warrior and diplomat Oleg (reigned c. 879-912), assumed the throne of Kiev in 912.   Depicted as a greedy, rapacious, and unsuccessful prince
1 CONC  by the 12th-century The Russian Primary Chronicle, Igor in 913-914 led an expedition into Transcaucasia that ended in total disaster for his forces. He also conducted two expeditions against Byzantium (941 and 944), but many of h
1 CONC is ships were destroyed by "Greek fire," and the treaty that he finally concluded in 944 was less advantageous to Kiev than the one obtained by Oleg in 911. Igor did manage to extend the authority of Kiev over the Pechenegs, a Tur
1 CONC kic people inhabiting the steppes north of the Black Sea, as well as over the East Slavic tribe of Drevlyane. When he went to Dereva (the land of the Drevlyane located in the region of the Pripet River) to collect tribute (945), h
1 CONC owever, his attempt to extort more than the customary amount provoked the Drevlyane into rebelling and killing him.
0 @N6000000000337408527@ NOTE Weis, p. 205:  Grand Prince of Kiev
0 @N6000000000945068552@ NOTE Storfyrste av Kijev [Kiev] 913 -  945. 
1 CONT Igor etterfulgte Oleg som hersker over ?Kievan Rus?. Ifølge en russisk middelalderkrønika
1 CONT (Beretningen om forgangne tider [påbegynt 1037-39]), var Igor sønn til Rurik. Han regnes som
1 CONT den virkelige grunderen av det russiske fyrstedømmet. Kijev var rikets sentrum og omliggende
1 CONT slaviske stammer ble beskattet i skinn og penger.
1 CONT Hans ekspedisjon i 941 mot Konstantinopel ble slått ned av grekerne. Et nytt angrep i 944
1 CONT førte til forhandlinger og en kommersiell avtale med Bysants i 945.
1 CONT Igors hustru, senere Sankt Olga, ble regent for deres sønn, Svyatoslav, etter hans død.
1 CONT Samtidig som det i Europa på 900- og 1000-tallet var en utvikling i gang mot store
1 CONT territorialstater med grunnlag i romersk og germansk tradisjon, utviklet det seg et helt nytt
1 CONT kraftsentrum i den østlige delen av Europa. Det var Russland som vokste frem på slavisk
1 CONT grunn, oppblandet med et sterkt kulturinnslag fra det østromerske rike, det vil si Bysants.
1 CONT Mellom disse maktgrupperingene forsøkte enkelte mindre statsformasjoner å gjøre seg
1 CONT gjeldende. De viktigste av dem var Böhmen, Mähren, Polen og Ungarn, og de store
1 CONT maktkoalisjonene søkte å utvide sin innflytelse over disse områdene.
1 CONT I Novgorod kontrollerte rus-høvdingen Rurik ca. 862-79 de nordlige delene av
1 CONT østerleden, mens stammefrendene hans, Askold og Dir, i Kijev hadde kontrollen over rutens
1 CONT sørlige avsnitt. Da Rurik døde, var sønnen Igor (Ingvar) ennå et barn. Hans slektning og
1 CONT formynder Oleg (Helge) overtok ledelsen (880-912) og begynte å utvide slektens maktsfære.
1 CONT Ved svik fikk han myrdet Askold og Dir og okkuperte Kijev, som nå ble Russlands hovedstad,
1 CONT ?de russiske byers mor?. Da han senere inntok Smolensk, kontrollerte han et rike som strakte
1 CONT seg langs handelsleden fra Finskebukta og Ladoga til Dnepr's nedre løp, der nomadestammen
1 CONT petsjenegerne lå i bakhold ved elvestrykene og ventet på de reisende. Særlig i vest lyktes det
1 CONT Oleg å underkue endel slaviske stammer med våpenmakt. En liten skandinavisk overklasse
1 CONT av krigere og storkjøpmenn hadde ledelsen i et multinasjonalt samfunn som foruten slaver
1 CONT også innbefattet finskbeslektede folkeslag. Rikets fremste handelsbyer var etter alder og rang
1 CONT disse: Kijev, Tsjernigov, Pereyaslav, Novgorod og Smolensk.
1 CONT Fordi det lyktes å holde petsjenegerne borte fra Dnepr's nedre løp, og som en følge av
1 CONT angrepet på Bysants i 907, ble ferdselsveiene for fjernhandelen åpnet på ny, og med
1 CONT handelen kom også fruktene av den sørlige høykultur til Russland. Det unge Russland
1 CONT opplevde en tid preget av kraftig utvikling.
1 CONT Oleg døde omkring 912, muligens så sent som i 922 ifølge ?Novgorod Lietopis?.
1 CONT I Igors regjeringstid fortsatte den indre stabiliseringen av riket, særlig fordi stadig flere slaver
1 CONT begynte å nå opp til ledende stillinger, samtidig som varjagerne i stadig større utstrekning
1 CONT begynte å smelte sammen med den slaviske majoriteten. De ?russiske? representantene i de
1 CONT første avtaler med Bysants hadde skandinaviske navn, men etter Igors korstog i 941 og 944
1 CONT fantes det et ganske sterkt innslag av slavere blant forhandlerne om forretningsavtaler.
1 CONT Kort tid etter hjemkomsten fra kampanjen mot Bysants, ble Igor drept i kamp med den slaviske
1 CONT Drevlianstammen nær byen Iskorosten i et forsøk på å samle inn  en tvungen ekstraskatt.
1 CONT Dereva var den viktigste byen i denne folkegruppens område.
0 @N6000000001783438285@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Although said to be less capable than leaders before and after him, Igor nontheless carried the conquests of Kievian Rus further.  He undertook campaigns against Persia, Constantinople and Bulgaria.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Igor married in 903 to St. Olga, who was born circa 890 and died in 969.  Igor and Olga had the following children:
1 CONT Svyatoslav I, Grand Prince of Kiev
1 CONT Halgu (or Oleg)
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Igor died in 945, murdered by the Drevlanes from the town of Korosten .
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://www.robertsewell.ca/kiev.html>
0 @N6000000002319313997@ NOTE Acceded: 924
1 CONT He succeeded his father in 877 at age two. Oleg, a kinsman of Rurik, ruled as
1 CONT regent for Iror. Prince Oleg, realizing the value of the Kiev region, had the
1 CONT Varangian rulers killed in 882 and united Novgorod and Kiev, with Kiev as the
1 CONT capital. He went on to extend Russian rule by subduing neighboring tribes,
1 CONT and led his raiders as far south as Constantinople where he concluded a
1 CONT commercial treaty with Byzantium in 911, the first authentically dated event
1 CONT in Russian history. Igor assumed power in 912, succeeded by widow Olga 945.
0 @N6000000002322271092@ NOTE Acceded: 924
1 CONT He succeeded his father in 877 at age two. Oleg, a kinsman of Rurik, ruled as
1 CONT regent for Iror. Prince Oleg, realizing the value of the Kiev region, had the
1 CONT Varangian rulers killed in 882 and united Novgorod and Kiev, with Kiev as the
1 CONT capital. He went on to extend Russian rule by subduing neighboring tribes,
1 CONT and led his raiders as far south as Constantinople where he concluded a
1 CONT commercial treaty with Byzantium in 911, the first authentically dated event
1 CONT in Russian history. Igor assumed power in 912, succeeded by widow Olga 945.
0 @N6000000002187805679@ NOTE Igor, Grand Prince of Kiev
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Igor (Old East Slavic: ?????, Old Norse: Ingvar, Ukrainian: ????) was a Varangian ruler of Kievan Rus from 912 to 945. Very little is known about him from the Primary Chronicle. It has been speculated that the chroniclers chose no
1 CONC t to enlarge on his reign, as the region was dominated by Khazaria at that time. That he was Rurik's son is also questioned on chronological grounds.
1 CONT
1 CONT He twice besieged Constantinople, in 941 and 944, and in spite of his fleet being destroyed by Greek fire, concluded with the Emperor a favourable treaty whose text is preserved in the chronicle. In 913 and 944, the Rus plundered 
1 CONC the Arabs in the Caspian Sea during the Caspian expeditions of the Rus, but it's not clear whether Igor had anything to do with these campaigns.
1 CONT
1 CONT Drastically revising the chronology of the Primary Chronicle, Constantine Zuckerman argues that Igor actually reigned for three years, between summer 941 and his death in early 945. He explains the epic 33-year span of his reign i
1 CONC n the chronicle by its author's faulty interpretation of Byzantine sources.[1] Indeed, none of Igor's activity are recorded in the chronicle prior to 941.
1 CONT
1 CONT Igor was killed[2] while collecting tribute from the Drevlians in 945 and revenged by his wife, Olga of Kiev. The Primary Chronicle blames his death on his own excessive greed, indicating that he was attempting to collect tribute 
1 CONC a second time in a month. As a result, Olga changed the system of tribute gathering (poliudie) in what may be regarded as the first legal reform recorded in Eastern Europe.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ Zuckerman, Constantine. On the Date of the Khazars' Conversion to Judaism and the Chronology of the Kings of the Rus Oleg and Igor. A Study of the Anonymous Khazar Letter from the Genizah of Cairo. // Revue des études byzantines
1 CONC . 1995. 53. Pp. 237–270. 
1 CONT ^ Leo the Deacon describes how Igor met his death: "They had bent down two birch trees to the prince’s feet and tied them to his legs; then they let the trees straighten again, thus tearing the prince’s body apart."[1] 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Oleg Prince of Kiev Succeeded by
1 CONT Olga 
