0 @N6000000002187690271@ NOTE Taksony of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Taksony (? – before 972), High Prince of the Magyars (c. 955 - before 972).
1 CONT
1 CONT Taksony was the son of Zoltán (Zaltas), the fourth son of Árpád, the second High Prince of the Magyars. The Gesta Hungarorum mentiones that his mother was a daughter of Menmarót, the local military leader in the region of Bihar (R
1 CONC omanian: Biharea) at the time of the Hungarian occupation (Honfoglalás). Taksony married a Pecheneg or Bulgar woman[1].
1 CONT
1 CONT In 947, Taksony lead a raid to Italy till Apulia, and King Berengar II of Italy had to buy the peace by paying a large amount of money to him and his followers. He might have taken part in the Battle of Lechfeld (Hungarian: augsbu
1 CONC rgi csata) where King Otto I of Germany won a decisive victory over the Magyars. After the defeat the Magyars stopped their raids (Hungarian: kalandozások) in Western Europe, but the Magyars began to pillage the Byzantine Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Taksony became the High Prince of the Magyars just or shortly after the Battle of Lechfeld, but his authority must have been only nominal over some regions of the Carpathian Basin inhabited by the Magyars. During his rule a large 
1 CONC number of Pechenegs and Khalyzians immigrated to the territory of the future Hungary.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 963, Pope John XII ordained the first Roman Catholic missionary bishop, Zacheus for the Magyars[2], but he probably never visited them.
1 CONT
1 CONT Taksony arranged the marriage of his son Géza to Sarolt, the daughter of Gyula of Transylvania.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
1 CONT # c. 945: Unnamed woman "of the territories of the Cumans"
1 CONT
1 CONT Géza of Hungary High Prince of the Magyars (c. 945 – 997) 
1 CONT Michael (Mihály), Duke between Morava (March) and Esztergom (Hron or Gran) (– ca 978 or bef. 997). Married Adelajda of Poland (– aft. 997), daughter or sister of Mieszko I of Poland 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Lines: 243-3, 243-4. 
1 CONT Kristó Gyula - Makk Ferenc: Az Árpád-ház uralkodói (IPC Könyvek, 1996) 
1 CONT Korai Magyar Történeti Lexikon (9-14. század), foszerkeszto: Kristó Gyula, szerkesztok: Engel Pál és Makk Ferenc (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1994) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ The Gesta Hungarorum mentiones that he married a woman "of the territories of the Cumans", but the Cumans had not cross the Volga River before the 11th century. 
1 CONT ^ Liutprand of Cremona: Liber de rebus gestis Ottonis magni imperatoris. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Fajsz High Prince of the Magyars
1 CONT c. 955 - before 972 Succeeded by
1 CONT Géza 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Named after the last pagan ruling prince, Taksony is also a small village by the "small" (kis) Danube about 20 km south of Budapest, Hungary. It is known for its many natural springs and tranquil scenery and serves as a haven for 
1 CONC fishermen, boaters and summer vacationers.
0 @N6000000003051316232@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Taksony of Hungary", retrieved 19 Oct 07]
1 CONT Taksony (c. 931 - 970) was the fejedelem (ruling prince) of Hungary between 955 and 970. There are few details on his life. His father was Zoltan of Hungary.
1 CONT
1 CONT Taksony's policies were a change from the previous habit of Hungarian leaders of conducting rapid raids on neighbouring countries, as he was probably present at the Battle of Lechfeld, where the Hungarian army was defeated and los
1 CONC t its leader Bulcsú. Relations with the Byzantine Empire progressively deteriorated, possibly due to propaganda from the Holy Roman Emperor Otto which depicted the Hungarians as ungodly. In the second half of his reign, Taksony in
1 CONC creasingly pursued campaigns against the Byzantine Empire. Although Taksony asked the Pope to send a bishop to Hungary (a request which was thwarted by Otto), he didn't follow an open policy for the spreading of Christianity withi
1 CONC n his realm.
1 CONT
1 CONT Taksony arranged the marriage of his son Géza to Sarolt, the daughter of Gyula of Transylvania. He also had a son called Michael, father of Vazul.
