0 @N6000000002187699133@ NOTE Géza of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Géza (c. 945 – 997), High Prince of the Magyars (before 972 - 997)[1].
1 CONT
1 CONT Géza was the son of Taksony, High Prince of the Magyars and his Pecheneg or Bulgar wife[2]. Géza's marriage with Sarolt, the daughter of Gyula of Transylvania, was arranged by his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his father's death (before 972), Géza followed him as High Prince of the Magyars. Shortly afterwards, a Benedictine monk of the Abbey of Sankt Gallen, Bruno, who had been ordained Bishop of the Magyars, arrived to his court 
1 CONC where hi baptised Géza. His Christian name was Stephen (Hungarian: István).
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Géza probably never became a convinced Christian, during his rule Christianity began to spread among the Magyars. According to Thietmar of Merseburg Géza continued to worship pagan gods; a chronicle claims that when he wa
1 CONC s questioned about this he stated he is rich enough to sacrifice to both the old gods and the new one.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 973, twelfe illostriuous Magyar envoys, whom probably Géza had assigned, participated in the Diet held by the Emperor Otto I.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 983, when Duke Henry the Quarrelsome of Bavaria rebelled against the child Emperor Otto III, the Magyars occupied Melk. However, Melk was reoccupied, already in 985, by Leopold I, Margrave of Austria. When Henry the Quarrelsome
1 CONC  lead his armies to the Vienna Basin in 991, the Magyars were obliged to evacuate the territories West of the Leitha (Lajta) River.
1 CONT
1 CONT Géza arranged the marriage of his son Stephen to Giselle, the daughter of Duke Henry the Quarrelsome. He started the construction of the Abbey of Pannonhalma.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
1 CONT # before 972: Sarolt, a daughter of Gyula of Transylvania (? – after 997)
1 CONT
1 CONT Unnamed daughter (? – after 988), wife of the future King Boleslaw I of Poland 
1 CONT Unnamed daughter (? – after 988), wife of the future Tsar Gavril Radomir of Bulgaria 
1 CONT King Stephen I of Hungary (967/969/975 – 15 August 1038) 
1 CONT Unnamed daughter (? – after 1026), wife of Otto Orseolo, Doge of Venice 
1 CONT Unnamed daughter (? – ?), wife of the future King Samuel Aba of Hungary 
1 CONT Married Adelajda of Poland, his brother's wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Line 243-4. 
1 CONT Kristó, Gyula - Makk, Ferenc: Az Árpád-ház uralkodói (IPC Könyvek, 1996) 
1 CONT Korai Magyar Történeti Lexikon (9-14. század), foszerkeszto: Kristó, Gyula, szerkesztok: Engel, Pál és Makk, Ferenc (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1994) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ His name was possibly "Gyeücsa" or "Gyécsa" in Old Hungarian. 
1 CONT ^ The Gesta Hungarorum mentiones that his father married a woman "of the territories of the Cumans", but the Cumans had not cross the Volga River before the 11th century. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Taksony High Prince of the Magyars
1 CONT before 970-997 Succeeded by
1 CONT Stephen I (Vajk) 
0 @N6000000003635711376@ NOTE [3282] WSHNGT.ASC file (Geo Washington Ahnentafel) # 139486328 = 34935064
0 @N6000000003949721835@ NOTE Although still a pagan when he became ruler, the alliance concluded between the Holy Roman Empire and Byzantium in 972 forced Géza to convert to Christianity in order to secure a lasting peace for Hungary. He turned to the Holy Ro
1 CONC man Emperor Otto I, who ordained a Benedictine monk, Bruno of Sankt Gallen, as bishop and sent him to Hungary to baptise Géza (this occurred in 985 according to some sources).  However, although he was mainly accepted as a Christi
1 CONC an ruler it is doubtful that he was a Christian at heart. According to Thietmar of Merseburg he continued to worship pagan gods; a chronicle claims that when he was questioned about this he stated he is rich enough to sacrifice to
1 CONC  both the old gods and the new one.  Although overshadowed by his son, King Stephen I of Hungary, Géza made considerable achievements during his reign. He established centralised rule over the entire country, except for Transylvan
1 CONC ia which remained under the separate authority of the gyula. This allowed him to collect taxes and duties far more successfully than his predecessors and thus increase his personal wealth.
0 @N6000000002240804181@ NOTE The dynasty of Arpad was named after Arpad (d. 907), who was chosen by the
1 CONT seven Hungarian tribes to lead them westward from their dwelling on the Don
1 CONT River in 889. Having crossed the Carpathian Mountains (c. 896), the Hungarians
1 CONT settled on the Pannonian, or Hungarian, Plain and for the next half century
1 CONT raided their neighbors and collected booty. But after their defeat by Emperor
1 CONT Otto I (Battle of Lechfeld, Aug 10, 955) they became less belligerent. Geza,
1 CONT great-grandson of Arpad, established supreme authority & reigned from 972-997.
