0 @N6000000002322328003@ NOTE King of Denmark, 985-1014. He made an expedition against England and in 994
1 CONT extorted a large amount of tribute money. Following a massacre of Danes in
1 CONT England in 1002, he conducted a further series of raids and in 1013 led an
1 CONT invasion to effect a permanent conquest. The fall of London and the flight of
1 CONT the English King Ethelred to Normandy made Sweyn the master of England in
1 CONT 1014. After his death, the English  throne was eventually passed to his son
1 CONT Canute II in 1016. Sweyn "died suddenly" at Gainsborough.
0 @N6000000001745033223@ NOTE From: http://www.camelotintl.com/heritage/sweyn.html:
1 CONT
1 CONT King of Denmark from 986, King of Norway from 995 and King of England (in all but name) in the latter months of 1013.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sweyn was married twice, his first wife, Gunhilda, bore him five children: Gytha, Harald, Cnut, Thyra and Estrith. His second marriage, to Sigfrid, produced no children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sweyn instigated Danish raids on England in the year 1009. This led to Ethelred II offering, in 1012, what would in modern times be known as protection money. This payment was called Danegeld.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon receipt of this Danegeld Sweyn made plans for an all out invasion of England. In 1013, with Sweyn in personal command, his naval forces invaded England.
1 CONT
1 CONT His forces soon overwhelmed the English, whose King had fled to Normandy. Sweyn reigned but briefly though as he died in 1014 at Gainsborough in Lincolnshire as a result of falling from his mount. It is romantically proposed that 
1 CONC he fell as a result of an hallucination that he was in single combat with his most hated of enemies, Edward.
