0 @N6000000002187639750@ NOTE Boleslaus III of Poland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus III the Wrymouth (Polish: Boleslaw III Krzywousty; 1085–1138) was duke of Poland from 1102. He was a son of Duke Wladislaus Herman and Judith of Bohemia, daughter of emperor Henry III. Boleslaus III was thus the brother-
1 CONC in-law of emperor Henry IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT He defeated the Pomeranians at the Battle of Naklo in 1109, and took control of Pomerania between 1119 and 1123, once again regaining Polish access to the Baltic Sea. The government of the local Pomeranians was left in place. He a
1 CONC lso defeated Henry V, Holy Roman Emperor in 1109, in the Battle of Glogów and Battle of Psie Pole. However, in 1135 he became a vassal of Henry's son Lothair II, Holy Roman Emperor, with Pomerania and Rügen as the fief.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus also campaigned in Hungary from 1132 to 1135, but to little effect.
1 CONT
1 CONT First he married Zbyslava, daughter of Sviatopolk II, grand duke of Kiev. They had one son:
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladislaus II the Exile (b. 1105), King of Poland 
1 CONT Second, he married Salome von Berg-Schelklingen, by whom he had 14 children (6 sons + 8 daughters), of which we know 4 sons and 5 daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus IV the Curly (b. 1125) 
1 CONT Mieszko III the Old (b. 1126) 
1 CONT Henry of Sandomierz (b. 1127) 
1 CONT Casimir the Just (b. 1138) 
1 CONT Rycheza of Poland (b. 12 April 1116), married to Grand Duke Volodar 
1 CONT Dobronega of Poland (b. 1128), married Markis Dietrich of Niederlausitz 
1 CONT Gertruda of Poland 
1 CONT Judith of Poland (b. 1132), married Otto I of Brandenburg 
1 CONT Agnes of Poland (b. 1137), married Mstislav II of Kiev 
1 CONT Before his death in 1138 he announced his testament (Boleslaus the Wrymouth's testament) dividing his land between four of his sons. The "senioral principle" established in the testament stated that at all times the oldest member 
1 CONC of the dynasty was to have supreme power over the rest and also control an indivisible "senioral part": a vast strip of land running N-S through the middle of Poland, with Kraków as the main city. The Senior's prerogatives also in
1 CONC cluded control over Pomerania, which was a fief of the Empire. The principle was quickly broken, at which there began a period of almost 200 years of feudal dissolution in Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT    This royalty-related article is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT This biography of a Polish noble is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
0 @N6000000002322323296@ NOTE As Prince of Poland, he introduced his seniorate system, by which the eldest
1 CONT legitimate son received the major part of any royal inheritance. He also
1 CONT converted the Pomeranians to Christianity. The seniorate system, a halfway
1 CONT measure of inheritance distrubution to satisfy all male heirs, ultimately
1 CONT caused dissention leading to the disintegration of the Polish state. Earlier,
1 CONT by 1125, the Polish boundries reached those of Mieszko I's domain. The Polish
1 CONT inheritance was divided amongst his sons (four in all??) upon his death.
0 @N6000000002176666222@ NOTE As Prince of Poland, he introduced his seniorate system, by which the eldest
1 CONT legitimate son received the major part of any royal inheritance. He also
1 CONT converted the Pomeranians to Christianity. The seniorate system, a halfway
1 CONT measure of inheritance distrubution to satisfy all male heirs, ultimately
1 CONT caused dissention leading to the disintegration of the Polish state. Earlier,
1 CONT by 1125, the Polish boundries reached those of Mieszko I's domain. The Polish
1 CONT inheritance was divided amongst his sons (four in all??) upon his death.
0 @N6000000003051245922@ NOTE [From "The Great Dynasties", retrieved 13 Aug 07]
1 CONT Wladyslaw's son, Boleslaw III Krzywousty (the Wry-Mouthed) (b. 1086; d. 1138), Prince 1102 - 1138, was an extremely capable ruler who earned the respect of his people. He exiled his half-brother and co-ruler, Zbigniew (1107) who, 
1 CONC in 1109, with the aid of the Emperor Henry V, attempted to cross the Odra but was thwarted by the rugged resistance of Glogow, when the Germans used hostages obtained during a truce as human shields for their siege towers - to no 
1 CONC avail. Boleslaw defeated the Emperor and the Duke of Bohemia at the battle of Psie Pole, near Wroclaw, 1109, forcing them to renounce all claims to Polish territory. In a series of stubbornly resisted campaigns he also recaptured 
1 CONC Eastern Pomerania (1122) but had to swear fealty to the Emperor Lothar II (1135) in order to regain Dymin and the Island of Rugen. Unfortunately Boleslaw failed to stem the decentralising tendencies undermining the state, nor did 
1 CONC he regain the title of king.
0 @N6000000003403166126@ NOTE [alfred_descendants10gen_fromrootsweb_bartont.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT of Poland; m. (1) Zbyslava. (Weis 147-25).
0 @N6000000003949868425@ NOTE ENCYCLOPÆDIA BRITANNICA, Vol. 18, p. 807  (1086 - 1138), called the Wry-Mouthed, prince of Poland, the son of Wladyslaw I Herman and Judith of Bavaria, succeeded his father in 1102. His early years were troubled continually by the
1 CONC  rivalry of his natural half-brother Zhigniew, who, till he was imprisoned and blinded, involved Boleslaw in contests with Bohemia and the Emperor Henry V. The first of the German wars began in 1109, when Henry, materially assiste
1 CONC d by the Czechs, invaded Silesia. Th Poles avoided an encounter in the open field, but harried the Germans so successfully and defended Glogau so stoutly that the emperor withdrew leaving the plain covered with corpses. The chief 
1 CONC political result of this great disaster was the complete independence of Poland for the next quarter of a century. It was during this respite that Boleslaw devoted himself to the main business of his life - the subjugation of Pome
1 CONC rania with the view of gaining access to the sea. The struggle began in 1109, when Boleslaw inflicted a terrible defeat on the Slavonic but still pagan Pomeranians at Naklo which compelled their temporary submission. In 1120-24 th
1 CONC e rebellion of the vassal Prince Warcislaw of Szczecin again brought Boleslaw into the country, but the resistance was as stout as ever, and only after 18,000 of his followers had fallen and 8,000 more had been expatriated did War
1 CONC cislaw submit to his conqueror. The obstinacy of the resistance convinced Boleslaw that Pomerania must be Christianized before it could be completely subdued; and this important work was partially accomplished by St. Otto, bishop 
1 CONC of Bamberg, an old friend of Boleslaw' father, who knew the Slavonic languages. In his later years Boleslaw waged an unsuccessful war with Hungary and Bohemia. He died in 1138, leaving four sons, between whom the kingdom was divid
1 CONC ed, the eldest son having overlordship of the whole.
0 @N6000000004533651116@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
